### Notes on design & trade-offs

- **Chaining** (`ChainingHashMap`): buckets are arrays; collisions just append. Simple, great for deletion, stable under adversarial input (though degrades to O(n) if every key lands in one bucket). Resizes when `size/capacity > 0.75`.
- **Open addressing** (`OpenAddressingHashMap`): stores entries in a flat array, linear probe on collision. Better cache locality; deletions use **tombstones** (a `Symbol` sentinel) so probe chains stay intact. Rebuilds when load factor or tombstone ratio gets too high.
- **Hashing**: default `defaultHash` is FNV-1a over strings, splitmix for integers, and identity-via-`WeakMap` for objects. You can inject a custom hasher/equals (example: case-insensitive keys).
- **Capacity** is always a power of two, so `index = hash & (capacity-1)` (bitmask) instead of modulo.
- **Equality** uses `Object.is` by default (so `NaN === NaN` and `-0 !== 0` behave as expected).
- **Complexity**: average O(1) get/set/delete; worst-case O(n) under heavy collisions; iteration O(capacity).
- Production code would additionally handle: cryptographically robust randomized hashing (to resist HashDoS), `WeakMap`-backed caches, and typed arrays for primitive keys. Native `Map` is still the recommended choice for real applications.

## Idea

A **hash table** (hash map) stores **key → value** pairs and aims for **O(1) average** lookup/insert/delete.  
Conceptually:

1. A **hash function** turns a key into an integer index.
2. The value is stored in an array (“bucket”) at that index.
3. **Collisions** (two keys → same index) are handled by chaining (linked list per bucket) or open addressing (probing).

In TypeScript/JavaScript you rarely implement one from scratch; the runtime already provides hash-table-like structures. The two main ones are **plain objects** and **`Map`**.

---

## Syntax

### 1. Plain object (string/symbol keys)

```ts
// Typed with an index signature
const scores: Record<string, number> = {};
scores.alice = 10;
scores["bob"] = 7;

// Or a more specific shape
interface UserCache {
  [id: string]: { name: string; age: number };
}
```

### 2. `Map` (any key type, preserves insertion order)

```ts
const cache = new Map<string, number>();
cache.set("alice", 10);
cache.get("alice"); // 10
cache.has("bob");   // false
cache.delete("alice");

// Typed
const byId = new Map<number, { name: string }>();
```

### 3. `WeakMap` (keys must be objects; allows GC)

```ts
const meta = new WeakMap<object, string>();
const obj = {};
meta.set(obj, "metadata");
```

### 4. `Set` (hash set of unique values)

```ts
const seen = new Set<string>();
seen.add("a");
seen.has("a"); // true
```

---

## Typical use cases

| Use case | Best structure | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Fast lookup by string id | Object or `Map` | O(1) average |
| Counting occurrences | `Map<K, number>` | Easy increment |
| Grouping by key | `Map<K, T[]>` | Preserve order, any key |
| Memoization / caching | `Map` or object | Avoid recomputation |
| Deduplication | `Set` | Uniqueness |
| Private metadata on objects | `WeakMap` | No memory leak |

---

## Short examples

### Counting words

```ts
function countWords(text: string): Map<string, number> {
  const counts = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const w of text.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/)) {
    counts.set(w, (counts.get(w) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  return counts;
}
```

### Grouping

```ts
const users = [
  { role: "admin", name: "A" },
  { role: "user", name: "B" },
  { role: "admin", name: "C" },
];

const byRole = new Map<string, string[]>();
for (const u of users) {
  if (!byRole.has(u.role)) byRole.set(u.role, []);
  byRole.get(u.role)!.push(u.name);
}
```

### Memoization

```ts
const memo = new Map<number, number>();
function fib(n: number): number {
  if (n < 2) return n;
  if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)!;
  const v = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
  memo.set(n, v);
  return v;
}
```

### Simple hash-table implementation (educational)

```ts
class SimpleHashTable<K, V> {
  private buckets: [K, V][][] = [];
  private size = 0;

  private hash(key: K): number {
    const s = String(key);
    let h = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      h = (h * 31 + s.charCodeAt(i)) >>> 0;
    }
    return h % this.buckets.length || 1;
  }

  constructor(capacity = 16) {
    this.buckets = Array.from({ length: capacity }, () => []);
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    const idx = this.hash(key);
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    const found = bucket.find(([k]) => k === key);
    if (found) found[1] = value;
    else bucket.push([key, value]);
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    const idx = this.hash(key);
    return this.buckets[idx].find(([k]) => k === key)?.[1];
  }
}
```

---

## Key differences to remember

- **Object keys are coerced to strings** (`{1: "a"}` → key `"1"`). Use `Map` when keys are numbers, objects, or you need insertion order.
- **`Map` is iterable** (`for (const [k,v] of map)`); plain objects need `Object.entries`.
- **Performance**: For large, dynamic key sets, `Map` is generally faster and more predictable than objects.

If you want, I can go deeper into collision strategies, load factor, or how V8 implements objects/Maps internally.

## Buggy version

```ts
class SimpleHashTable {
  private size = 8;
  // BUG: Array.fill([]) evaluates [] once and puts the *same array*
  // reference into every bucket.
  private buckets: Array<Array<[string, number]>> = Array(this.size).fill([]);

  private hash(key: string): number {
    let h = 0;
    for (const c of key) h = (h * 31 + c.charCodeAt(0)) | 0;
    return h >>> 0; // convert to unsigned 32-bit
  }

  set(key: string, value: number): void {
    const idx = this.hash(key) % this.size;
    // Does not remove an old value for the same key, so updates create duplicates.
    this.buckets[idx].push([key, value]);
  }

  get(key: string): number | undefined {
    const idx = this.hash(key) % this.size;
    for (const [k, v] of this.buckets[idx]) {
      if (k === key) return v;
    }
    return undefined;
  }
}
```

## Corrected version

```ts
class SimpleHashTable {
  private size = 8;
  // Each bucket gets its own array instance.
  private buckets: Array<Array<[string, number]>> =
    Array.from({ length: this.size }, () => []);

  private hash(key: string): number {
    let h = 0;
    for (const c of key) h = (h * 31 + c.charCodeAt(0)) | 0;
    return h >>> 0;
  }

  set(key: string, value: number): void {
    const idx = this.hash(key) % this.size;
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];

    // Update in place if the key already exists.
    for (let i = 0; i < bucket.length; i++) {
      if (bucket[i][0] === key) {
        bucket[i][1] = value;
        return;
      }
    }

    bucket.push([key, value]);
  }

  get(key: string): number | undefined {
    const idx = this.hash(key) % this.size;
    for (const [k, v] of this.buckets[idx]) {
      if (k === key) return v;
    }
    return undefined;
  }
}
```

## What was wrong and why

1. **Shared bucket array from `Array.fill([])`**  
   `Array(n).fill([])` creates the inner array *once*, then copies that same reference into every slot. As a result, every bucket is actually the same array. Inserting into bucket `2` also appears in bucket `5`, and the table effectively collapses into one giant bucket. The corrected code uses `Array.from({ length: size }, () => [])`, which invokes the factory function separately for each index.

2. **Updates created duplicate entries**  
   The original `set` always pushed a new `[key, value]` pair. With chaining, multiple entries for the same key can accumulate, and `get` may return an older value depending on which one it encounters first. The corrected `set` scans the bucket and replaces the value when the key already exists.

3. **Collision handling must compare keys, not just positions**  
   The implementation uses separate chaining: each bucket is a list of entries. A correct lookup must compare `k === key`, not assume one value per bucket. The corrected version keeps this comparison.

4. **Hash range needs care**  
   The hash uses signed 32-bit arithmetic, so `% size` could receive a negative number. Converting with `>>> 0` makes the hash unsigned before taking the modulo. A production implementation would also manage load factor and resize/rehash buckets when they become too long.

# Common hash-table pitfalls in TypeScript

Below are the most frequent pitfalls, each with a short explanation and runnable code.

---

## 1. Missing key vs. explicitly `undefined`
With a plain object, reading a missing key and reading a key set to `undefined` look identical.

```ts
const table: Record<string, number | undefined> = { a: undefined };

console.log(table.a);        // undefined
console.log(table.missing);  // undefined

// Safer checks:
console.log(Object.hasOwn(table, "a"));       // true
console.log(Object.hasOwn(table, "missing")); // false
```

**Fix:** use `Map`, `Object.hasOwn`, or `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`.

---

## 2. Prototype pollution when using plain objects
String keys like `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `toString` can collide with inherited properties.

```ts
const dict: Record<string, number> = {};
dict["__proto__"] = 42; // surprising behavior in some engines / TS configs

// Safer dictionary bases:
const safe1 = Object.create(null); // no prototype
const safe2 = new Map<string, number>();
```

---

## 3. Keys are coerced to strings
Numbers, booleans, and objects become strings when used as object keys. Distinct values can collide.

```ts
const obj: any = {};
obj[1] = "one";
obj["1"] = "string one"; // overwrites
console.log(obj[1]); // "string one"

const map = new Map<number | string, string>();
map.set(1, "one");
map.set("1", "string one"); // kept separate
console.log(map.get(1)); // "one"
```

Also note: `NaN` becomes `"NaN"`, and `-0` becomes `"0"` in objects, but `Map` uses `SameValueZero` (so `NaN` matches `NaN`, and `-0` matches `0`).

---

## 4. Object iteration order is not simple insertion order
Integer-like keys are sorted first, then other keys by insertion order.

```ts
const o: any = {};
o.b = 1;
o["2"] = 2;
o.a = 3;
o["1"] = 4;

console.log(Object.keys(o)); // ["1", "2", "a", "b"]

const m = new Map<string | number, number>();
m.set("b", 1);
m.set(2, 2);
m.set("a", 3);
m.set(1, 4);
console.log([...m.keys()]); // ["b", 2, "a", 1] (insertion order)
```

---

## 5. `for...in` includes inherited enumerable properties
`for...in` walks the prototype chain.

```ts
const base = { inherited: 1 };
const child = Object.create(base);
child.own = 2;

for (const k in child) console.log(k); // "own", "inherited"

// Safer:
for (const k of Object.keys(child)) console.log(k); // "own"
for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(child)) console.log(k, v);
```

---

## 6. Type system lies about index access
`Record<string, V>` claims every access returns `V`, even for missing keys.

```ts
const counts: Record<string, number> = {};
const c = counts["definitelyMissing"]; // typed as number, actually undefined

// Better types:
const partial: Partial<Record<string, number>> = {};
const p = partial["x"]; // number | undefined

// Or enable noUncheckedIndexedAccess in tsconfig:
// "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true
```

---

## 7. `in` operator checks the whole prototype chain
`"toString" in obj` is `true` even if you never set it.

```ts
const dict: Record<string, number> = { a: 1 };
console.log("a" in dict);        // true
console.log("toString" in dict); // true (inherited)

console.log(Object.hasOwn(dict, "a"));        // true
console.log(Object.hasOwn(dict, "toString")); // false
```

---

## 8. Object keys are reference-identity for objects in `Map`, not deep equality
Two different object literals are different keys even if contents match.

```ts
const m = new Map<object, string>();
const k1 = { id: 1 };
const k2 = { id: 1 };

m.set(k1, "first");
console.log(m.get(k2)); // undefined

// If you need deep keys, normalize to a string key:
const key = JSON.stringify(k2);
```

---

## 9. Mutating while iterating
Deleting or adding entries during iteration can skip items or throw.

```ts
const m = new Map([["a", 1], ["b", 2], ["c", 3]]);

// Risky:
for (const [k] of m) {
  if (k === "a") m.delete(k); // can affect iteration
}

// Safer: snapshot first
for (const k of [...m.keys()]) {
  if (k === "a") m.delete(k);
}
```

For objects, prefer `Object.keys(obj)` snapshot before mutating.

---

## 10. Falsy default values with `||`
`0`, `false`, `""`, and `NaN` are replaced by the default.

```ts
const scores: Record<string, number> = { a: 0 };

const wrong = scores["a"] || 10; // 10 (wrong)
const right = scores["a"] ?? 10; // 0 (correct)
```

Use `??` (nullish coalescing) when only `null`/`undefined` should trigger defaults.

---

## 11. `delete` vs. assigning `undefined`
`delete` removes the key; assigning `undefined` keeps the key present.

```ts
const o: Record<string, number | undefined> = { a: 1 };
o.a = undefined;

console.log(Object.keys(o)); // ["a"]  (key still present)
console.log(Object.hasOwn(o, "a")); // true

delete o.a;
console.log(Object.keys(o)); // []
```

This matters for serialization, iteration counts, and storage size.

---

## 12. `Map` is not JSON-serializable by default
`JSON.stringify(new Map(...))` produces `{}`.

```ts
const m = new Map([["a", 1], ["b", 2]]);
console.log(JSON.stringify(m)); // "{}"

// Serialize explicitly:
const obj = Object.fromEntries(m);
console.log(JSON.stringify(obj)); // {"a":1,"b":2}

// Rehydrate:
const restored = new Map(Object.entries(JSON.parse('{"a":1,"b":2}')));
```

Also, plain objects drop keys whose value is `undefined`.

---

## 13. Sparse arrays make poor hash tables
Holes behave inconsistently with iteration and length.

```ts
const arr: (number | undefined)[] = [];
arr[1000] = 5; // sparse
console.log(arr.length); // 1001
console.log(arr[0]);     // undefined, but key 0 was never set

// Use Map or Record instead:
const table = new Map<number, number>();
table.set(1000, 5);
```

---

## 14. Performance: rehashing and large object keys
Plain objects and `Map` are optimized for string/symbol keys. Hashing large objects or arrays as keys (via serialization) is expensive and collision-prone.

```ts
// Slow and collision-prone:
const cache = new Map<string, unknown>();
function keyFor(obj: unknown) {
  return JSON.stringify(obj); // O(n), unstable key order issues
}

// Better: use a stable id or WeakMap for object-keyed caches
const cache2 = new WeakMap<object, unknown>();
```

---

## Practical recommendations

- Prefer `Map<K, V>` when keys are not strings, when insertion order matters, or when you need reliable presence checks.
- Prefer `Record<K, V>` / plain objects for small, string-keyed, JSON-friendly dictionaries.
- Enable `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` for safer indexing.
- Use `Object.hasOwn` instead of `hasOwnProperty` or `in`.
- Use `??` for defaults, not `||`.
- Snapshot keys before mutating during iteration.
- Normalize complex keys to stable strings, or use `WeakMap` for object-keyed caches.

## Exercise

**Build a generic hash table with separate chaining, then use it to find the most frequent word in a sentence.**

Requirements:
- `HashTable<K, V>` where `K` is `string | number`.
- Methods: `set(key, value)`, `get(key)`, `delete(key)`, `has(key)`, `size()`, `keys()`.
- Handle collisions with chaining (array of entries per bucket).
- Resize when load factor exceeds `0.75`.
- Use the table in `mostFrequentWord(text)`.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Choose the storage shape
Use an array of buckets. Each bucket is an array of `{ key, value }` entries. This is **separate chaining**: colliding keys live in the same bucket list.

### 2. Hash function
Convert the key to a string and use a simple polynomial rolling hash. Modulo the capacity gives the bucket index. Using `>>> 0` keeps the intermediate value as an unsigned 32-bit integer, avoiding negative indices.

### 3. Basic operations
- `set`: find the bucket; if key exists, update value; otherwise push a new entry and increment count.
- `get`: scan the bucket for the key.
- `delete`: remove the matching entry with `splice`, decrement count.
- `has` and `size` are thin wrappers over existing state.

### 4. Resize / rehash
When `count / capacity > 0.75`, double capacity and re-insert every existing entry. Rehashing is necessary because bucket indices depend on capacity.

### 5. Apply it
Tokenize text, count words with the table, then scan `keys()` to find the highest count.

---

## Final solution

```ts
type Entry<K, V> = { key: K; value: V };

class HashTable<K extends string | number, V> {
  private buckets: Entry<K, V>[][];
  private count = 0;

  constructor(private capacity = 16) {
    this.buckets = Array.from({ length: capacity }, () => []);
  }

  private hash(key: K): number {
    let h = 0;
    const s = String(key);
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      h = (h * 31 + s.charCodeAt(i)) >>> 0;
    }
    return h % this.capacity;
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    const idx = this.hash(key);
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];

    for (const entry of bucket) {
      if (entry.key === key) {
        entry.value = value;
        return;
      }
    }

    bucket.push({ key, value });
    this.count++;

    if (this.count / this.capacity > 0.75) {
      this.resize();
    }
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    const idx = this.hash(key);
    for (const entry of this.buckets[idx]) {
      if (entry.key === key) return entry.value;
    }
    return undefined;
  }

  delete(key: K): boolean {
    const idx = this.hash(key);
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    const pos = bucket.findIndex((e) => e.key === key);
    if (pos === -1) return false;
    bucket.splice(pos, 1);
    this.count--;
    return true;
  }

  has(key: K): boolean {
    return this.get(key) !== undefined;
  }

  size(): number {
    return this.count;
  }

  keys(): K[] {
    const result: K[] = [];
    for (const bucket of this.buckets) {
      for (const entry of bucket) {
        result.push(entry.key);
      }
    }
    return result;
  }

  private resize(): void {
    const oldBuckets = this.buckets;
    this.capacity *= 2;
    this.buckets = Array.from({ length: this.capacity }, () => []);
    this.count = 0;

    for (const bucket of oldBuckets) {
      for (const entry of bucket) {
        this.set(entry.key, entry.value);
      }
    }
  }
}

function mostFrequentWord(text: string): string | undefined {
  const table = new HashTable<string, number>();
  const words = text.toLowerCase().match(/\w+/g) ?? [];

  for (const word of words) {
    table.set(word, (table.get(word) ?? 0) + 1);
  }

  let bestWord: string | undefined;
  let bestCount = 0;

  for (const key of table.keys()) {
    const count = table.get(key)!;
    if (count > bestCount) {
      bestCount = count;
      bestWord = key;
    }
  }

  return bestWord;
}

// --- Quick demo ---
const sentence = "To be or not to be, that is the question.";
console.log(mostFrequentWord(sentence)); // "to" (appears twice)

const ht = new HashTable<string, number>();
ht.set("apple", 3);
ht.set("banana", 7);
console.log(ht.get("banana")); // 7
console.log(ht.has("pear"));   // false
console.log(ht.size());        // 2
ht.delete("apple");
console.log(ht.size());        // 1
```

### Notes
- **Collision handling** is done by scanning the small bucket list; average case remains `O(1)`.
- **Resize threshold** (`0.75`) keeps buckets short and operations fast.
- Keys are compared with `===`, so object keys would need a custom key serializer in a production implementation. For `string | number`, this is sufficient.
- The hash uses `String(key)`, so numeric keys like `42` and string keys `"42"` would collide; that is acceptable for this exercise but worth noting.

## Hash Tables in TypeScript — Interview-Style Answer

### 1. What a hash table is

A **hash table** (hash map) maps **keys → values** with (amortized) **O(1)** lookup, insert, and delete.  
It has three core pieces:

1. **Hash function** — turns a key into an integer index into an array of “buckets.”
2. **Buckets / storage** — an array where each slot holds one or more key/value pairs.
3. **Collision resolution** — what happens when two keys hash to the same bucket.

Common strategies:
- **Chaining** (what we’ll implement): each bucket is a list (or array) of entries. Simple, easy to delete, tolerates high load factors.
- **Open addressing**: probe for an empty slot (linear/quadratic/double hashing). Cache-friendly, but deletion is Trickier.

Other concepts interviewers expect:
- **Load factor** = `size / capacity`. When it exceeds a threshold (e.g., 0.75), **resize** and **rehash**.
- **Amortized O(1)**: most ops O(1), but a resize is O(n); spread over n inserts → O(1) amortized.
- **Worst case O(n)** if every key collides (bad hash or adversarial input).
- A good hash function should be **deterministic, uniform, fast**, and mix bits (e.g., for strings).

---

### 2. Implementation (chaining, generic, with comments)

```ts
/**
 * A simple chained hash map.
 *
 * - Keys are hashed via a user-provided function (defaults to a string-based hash).
 * - Collisions are resolved with chaining (each bucket is an array of entries).
 * - The table resizes when the load factor exceeds a threshold.
 */
export class HashMap<K, V> {
  private buckets: Array<Array<{ key: K; value: V }> | undefined>;
  private _size = 0;
  private capacity: number;
  private readonly loadFactorThreshold: number;
  private readonly hashFn: (key: K) => number;

  constructor(
    initialCapacity = 16,
    loadFactorThreshold = 0.75,
    hashFn?: (key: K) => number
  ) {
    if (initialCapacity <= 0) throw new Error("capacity must be > 0");
    this.capacity = nextPowerOfTwo(initialCapacity);
    this.loadFactorThreshold = loadFactorThreshold;
    this.hashFn = hashFn ?? defaultHash;
    this.buckets = new Array(this.capacity);
  }

  get size(): number {
    return this._size;
  }

  /** Insert or update a value for a key. O(1) amortized. */
  set(key: K, value: V): void {
    if (this._size / this.capacity > this.loadFactorThreshold) {
      this.resize(this.capacity * 2);
    }

    const idx = this.indexFor(key);
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];

    if (!bucket) {
      this.buckets[idx] = [{ key, value }];
      this._size++;
      return;
    }

    // Update existing key in place (no size change).
    for (const entry of bucket) {
      if (equals(entry.key, key)) {
        entry.value = value;
        return;
      }
    }

    bucket.push({ key, value });
    this._size++;
  }

  /** Returns the value or undefined if absent. O(1) average. */
  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    const bucket = this.buckets[this.indexFor(key)];
    if (!bucket) return undefined;
    for (const entry of bucket) {
      if (equals(entry.key, key)) return entry.value;
    }
    return undefined;
  }

  /** Returns true if the key exists. */
  has(key: K): boolean {
    return this.get(key) !== undefined || this.containsKey(key);
  }

  /** Removes a key; returns true if it existed. */
  delete(key: K): boolean {
    const idx = this.indexFor(key);
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    if (!bucket) return false;

    for (let i = 0; i < bucket.length; i++) {
      if (equals(bucket[i].key, key)) {
        bucket.splice(i, 1);
        this._size--;
        if (bucket.length === 0) this.buckets[idx] = undefined;
        return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  /** Clears all entries. */
  clear(): void {
    this.buckets = new Array(this.capacity);
    this._size = 0;
  }

  /** Iterate over all entries. */
  *entries(): IterableIterator<[K, V]> {
    for (const bucket of this.buckets) {
      if (!bucket) continue;
      for (const { key, value } of bucket) {
        yield [key, value];
      }
    }
  }

  // ---------- internals ----------

  private indexFor(key: K): number {
    // Mix + modulo; using power-of-two capacity lets us use bitwise AND.
    const h = this.hashFn(key) >>> 0; // coerce to unsigned 32-bit
    return h & (this.capacity - 1);
  }

  private resize(newCapacity: number): void {
    const oldBuckets = this.buckets;
    this.capacity = newCapacity;
    this.buckets = new Array(this.capacity);
    this._size = 0;

    for (const bucket of oldBuckets) {
      if (!bucket) continue;
      for (const { key, value } of bucket) {
        // Re-insert (rehash) into the new table.
        this.set(key, value);
      }
    }
  }
}

// ---------- helpers ----------

/** Round up to the next power of two (keeps indexFor as a fast bitwise AND). */
function nextPowerOfTwo(n: number): number {
  let p = 1;
  while (p < n) p <<= 1;
  return p;
}

/** Default hash: djb2-style for strings/numbers; objects fall back to identity-ish. */
function defaultHash(key: any): number {
  if (typeof key === "number") return key | 0;
  if (typeof key === "string") {
    let h = 5381;
    for (let i = 0; i < key.length; i++) {
      h = (h * 33) ^ key.charCodeAt(i);
    }
    return h;
  }
  // For objects, use a stable-enough identity-ish hash via toString.
  // In a real implementation you'd require a custom hashFn for objects.
  return defaultHash(String(key));
}

/** Key equality. Primitives use ===; objects use reference equality by default. */
function equals(a: any, b: any): boolean {
  return a === b;
}

/** `has` helper that doesn't conflate `undefined` values with missing keys. */
function containsKey<K, V>(map: HashMap<K, V>, key: K): boolean {
  // We re-check buckets directly to avoid the undefined-value ambiguity.
  const idx = (map as any).indexFor(key);
  const bucket = (map as any).buckets[idx];
  if (!bucket) return false;
  return bucket.some((e: { key: K }) => equals(e.key, key));
}
```

> Note: `has` above is slightly awkward because of the private fields. In a real class you’d just keep an internal `containsKey` method. Below is the cleaner version you’d actually ship.

### Cleaner `has` (put inside the class)

```ts
has(key: K): boolean {
  const bucket = this.buckets[this.indexFor(key)];
  if (!bucket) return false;
  return bucket.some((e) => equals(e.key, key));
}
```

And remove `containsKey` entirely.

---

### 3. Usage example

```ts
const m = new HashMap<string, number>();

m.set("apple", 5);
m.set("banana", 3);
m.set("apple", 7); // update

console.log(m.get("apple")); // 7
console.log(m.has("banana")); // true
console.log(m.size); // 2

m.delete("banana");
console.log(m.has("banana")); // false

for (const [k, v] of m.entries()) {
  console.log(k, v);
}
```

Custom key type:

```ts
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
const points = new HashMap<Point, string>(
  16,
  0.75,
  (p) => (p.x * 31 + p.y) | 0
);
```

---

### 4. Interview talking points (quick checklist)

- **Why power-of-two capacity?** → index via `h & (cap-1)` is faster than `%`.
- **Why resize at 0.75?** → balances memory vs. collision chain length.
- **Why rehash on resize?** → bucket index depends on capacity.
- **Equality vs. hash:** two equal keys must have the same hash; unequal keys may collide.
- **Objects as keys:** need a custom `hashFn` + `equals` (or use `Map`, which uses SameValueZero and an internal hash).
- **Real-world:** JS `Map` is a hash table (or tree-backed in some engines); it handles any key type and avoids the pitfalls above.

### 5. Complexity summary

| Operation | Average | Worst |
|-----------|---------|-------|
| `set`     | O(1) amortized | O(n) |
| `get`     | O(1) | O(n) |
| `delete`  | O(1) | O(n) |
| `resize`  | O(n) (amortized over n inserts) | O(n) |

Space: O(n).

---

If you want, I can also show an **open-addressing** variant (linear probing) or explain how V8/SpiderMonkey implement `Map` under the hood.

When using “hash tables” in TypeScript, the important distinction is between **plain objects** (`Record`, index signatures), **`Map`**, and **`WeakMap`**. Each has different edge cases.

## 1. Plain objects: keys are always strings or symbols

### 1.1 Key coercion causes silent collisions

```ts
const counts: Record<string, number> = {};

counts[0] = 10;
counts["0"] = 20; // overwrites the numeric key
counts[true] = 30;
counts["true"] = 40; // overwrites boolean key
counts[NaN] = 50; // key becomes "NaN"

console.log(counts);
// { "0": 20, "true": 40, "NaN": 50 }
```

**Edge case:** `0`, `"0"`, `false`, `"false"`, etc. all collapse into string keys.

### 1.2 Prototype pollution / inherited properties

```ts
const data: Record<string, number> = {};

data["__proto__"] = 123;
data["constructor"] = 456;
data["toString"] = 789;

// Dangerous: inherited members may be shadowed or unexpected
console.log(Object.keys(data)); // may not include all keys reliably
console.log(({} as any).polluted); // possible pollution in some patterns
```

Safer pattern:

```ts
const safe = Object.create(null) as Record<string, number>;
safe["__proto__"] = 123; // stored as an own data property
console.log(Object.hasOwn(safe, "__proto__")); // true
```

### 1.3 Check existence with `hasOwn`, not truthiness

```ts
const cache: Record<string, number> = { hits: 0 };

// Bad: 0 is falsy
if (cache.hits) {
  console.log("has hits");
}

// Good
if (Object.hasOwn(cache, "hits")) {
  console.log("has hits, value:", cache.hits);
}
```

### 1.4 Symbol keys are not enumerable by `Object.keys`

```ts
const id = Symbol("id");
const obj: Record<PropertyKey, unknown> = {};
obj[id] = 42;

console.log(Object.keys(obj));        // []
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertySymbols(obj)); // [Symbol(id)]
```

---

## 2. `Map`: reference equality and SameValueZero

### 2.1 `NaN` keys collapse, and `+0` / `-0` are equal

```ts
const m = new Map<number, string>();

m.set(NaN, "first");
m.set(NaN, "second"); // overwrites
console.log(m.size); // 1
console.log(m.get(NaN)); // "second"

m.set(0, "zero");
m.set(-0, "negative zero"); // overwrites because SameValueZero
console.log(m.size); // 2 total: NaN + 0
console.log(m.get(-0)); // "negative zero"
```

### 2.2 Object keys are compared by identity, not deep equality

```ts
const byId = new Map<{ id: number }, string>();

const a = { id: 1 };
const b = { id: 1 };

byId.set(a, "A");
console.log(byId.get(b)); // undefined
console.log(byId.get(a)); // "A"
```

If you need value-based lookup, build a key string or use a custom wrapper.

### 2.3 Mutating a key object can make entries “unreachable by value”

```ts
const m = new Map<{ id: number }, string>();

let key = { id: 1 };
m.set(key, "value");

// The key reference stored in Map is still the original object.
key = { id: 1 };

console.log(m.get(key)); // undefined
console.log(m.size);     // 1, but original key is still strongly referenced
```

This is both a correctness bug and a potential memory leak.

### 2.4 Iterating while mutating

Modifying a `Map` during iteration can skip entries or process newly inserted ones unexpectedly.

```ts
const m = new Map([
  [1, "a"],
  [2, "b"],
  [3, "c"],
]);

// Risky: deletion during iteration
for (const [k] of m) {
  if (k === 2) m.delete(1); // deleting an already-visited or upcoming entry can alter traversal
}

// Safer: snapshot first
const entries = Array.from(m.entries());
for (const [k, v] of entries) {
  if (k === 2) m.delete(1);
}
```

Adding entries during iteration may also cause them to be visited if they are inserted after the current cursor position.

### 2.5 Strong references and memory leaks

`Map` keeps keys and values alive as long as the `Map` itself is alive.

```ts
const cache = new Map<object, string>();

function load(obj: object) {
  cache.set(obj, expensiveCompute(obj));
}

// Even if caller drops `obj`, Map retains it.
```

Use `WeakMap` when keys are objects and cache lifetime should follow key lifetime (see below).

### 2.6 `Map` is not JSON-serializable by default

```ts
const m = new Map([["a", 1], ["b", 2]]);

console.log(JSON.stringify(m)); // "{}"

// Convert manually
const json = JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(m));
const restored = new Map(Object.entries(JSON.parse(json)));
```

Nested `Map`s require recursive conversion.

---

## 3. `WeakMap`: object-only keys and no enumeration

### 3.1 Keys must be objects

```ts
const w = new WeakMap<object, number>();

w.set({ x: 1 }, 10); // OK

// Runtime error: Invalid value used as weak map key
// w.set("string" as any, 10);
// w.set(42 as any, 10);
```

At runtime, primitives throw `TypeError`.

### 3.2 No iteration, size, or clearing

```ts
const w = new WeakMap<object, number>();

// w.size;     // does not exist
// w.keys();   // does not exist
// w.clear();   // does not exist in modern engines? Actually clear() was removed

const key = { id: 1 };
w.set(key, 99);

// Once `key` becomes unreachable, entry may be collected.
```

Use `WeakMap` for metadata/caching attached to objects, not for general dictionaries.

### 3.3 WeakMap caching pattern

```ts
const labelCache = new WeakMap<object, string>();

function getLabel(obj: { id: number }): string {
  const cached = labelCache.get(obj);
  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;

  const label = `object-${obj.id}`;
  labelCache.set(obj, label);
  return label;
}
```

This avoids leaking cached values after objects are no longer referenced elsewhere.

---

## 4. TypeScript-specific edge cases

### 4.1 Index signatures are unsound by default

```ts
const counts: Record<string, number> = {};

const value = counts["missing"]; // type is `number`, but runtime is undefined
console.log(value.toFixed(2)); // runtime crash possible
```

Enable `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` in `tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true
  }
}
```

Then:

```ts
const value = counts["missing"]; // number | undefined
```

### 4.2 `Map#get` is correctly typed as `V | undefined`

```ts
const m = new Map<string, number>([["a", 1]]);

const v = m.get("missing"); // number | undefined

if (v !== undefined) {
  console.log(v.toFixed(2));
}

// If 0 is a valid value, do not use truthiness:
const has = m.has("a"); // boolean
```

### 4.3 `Map` constructor input typing

```ts
// Good
const m1 = new Map<string, number>([
  ["a", 1],
  ["b", 2],
]);

// Bad: tuple inference may fail in complex expressions
const pairs = [["a", 1], ["b", 2]] as const;
const m2 = new Map(pairs); // Map<string, number | ...> possibly widened
```

Prefer explicit type parameters:

```ts
const m = new Map<string, number>(pairs as Array<[string, number]>);
```

### 4.4 Readonly and immutability

```ts
type ReadonlyMap<K, V> = ReadonlyMap<K, V>;

function read(map: ReadonlyMap<string, number>) {
  // map.set("x", 1); // compile error
  return map.get("x");
}
```

For plain objects, use `Readonly<Record<string, number>>` or `as const`, but remember that `as const` makes values readonly, not deeply immutable.

### 4.5 Exhaustive handling of union keys

```ts
type Status = "ok" | "error" | "pending";

const labels: Record<Status, string> = {
  ok: "OK",
  error: "Error",
  pending: "Pending",
};

// Exhaustive check helper
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unexpected value: ${x}`);
}

function labelFor(s: Status): string {
  switch (s) {
    case "ok": return labels.ok;
    case "error": return labels.error;
    case "pending": return labels.pending;
    default: return assertNever(s);
  }
}
```

---

## 5. Custom value-equality map (common gap)

JavaScript maps do not support custom equality. If you need deep equality, derive a stable key.

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

const cache = new Map<string, User>();

function keyOf(user: User): string {
  return JSON.stringify({ id: user.id }); // or a canonical serializer
}

function getCached(user: User): User | undefined {
  return cache.get(keyOf(user));
}
```

Be careful: JSON key ordering and undefined values can make keys unstable. For complex cases, use a canonical serialization library or a custom comparator-based structure.

---

## 6. Practical checklist

- Use **`Map`** for dynamic keys, non-string keys, frequent add/remove, or when size matters.
- Use **`WeakMap`** for object-keyed caches/metadata to avoid leaks.
- Use **plain objects** only for static-ish string-key dictionaries; prefer `Object.create(null)` to avoid prototype issues.
- Enable **`noUncheckedIndexedAccess`** and handle `undefined` from lookups.
- Never mutate a `Map`/`Set` while iterating unless you understand traversal rules; snapshot first.
- Treat object keys as identity-based.
- Convert `Map` explicitly when serializing to JSON.
- Use `Object.hasOwn` instead of `hasOwnProperty` in modern code.

This covers the major correctness, memory, typing, and runtime edge cases for hash-table-like structures in TypeScript.

Here is a complete, runnable TypeScript file demonstrating several common and useful **higher-order functions** (HOFs): functions that accept functions as arguments and/or return functions.

Save it as `hof.ts` and run it with:

```bash
npx ts-node hof.ts
# or
npx tsc hof.ts --target es2020 --module commonjs && node hof.js
```

## `hof.ts`

```ts
// hof.ts
// Complete, runnable example of higher-order functions in TypeScript.

type User = { name: string; age: number };

// ---------- Basic HOF building blocks ----------

type Predicate<T> = (value: T) => boolean;
type Mapper<T, U> = (value: T) => U;

/** Returns a new function that filters an array using the given predicate. */
function filter<T>(predicate: Predicate<T>): (items: T[]) => T[] {
  return (items) => items.filter(predicate);
}

/** Returns a new function that maps an array using the given mapper. */
function map<T, U>(mapper: Mapper<T, U>): (items: T[]) => U[] {
  return (items) => items.map(mapper);
}

/** Compose two unary functions: f(g(x)). */
function compose<A, B, C>(
  f: (b: B) => C,
  g: (a: A) => B
): (a: A) => C {
  return (a) => f(g(a));
}

/** Type-safe pipe for 1 to 3 functions. */
function pipe<A, B>(f1: (a: A) => B): (a: A) => B;
function pipe<A, B, C>(f1: (a: A) => B, f2: (b: B) => C): (a: A) => C;
function pipe<A, B, C, D>(
  f1: (a: A) => B,
  f2: (b: B) => C,
  f3: (c: C) => D
): (a: A) => D;
function pipe(...fns: Array<(x: any) => any>) {
  return (x: any) => fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), x);
}

/** Currying for a 2-argument function. */
function curry<A, B, C>(fn: (a: A, b: B) => C): (a: A) => (b: B) => C {
  return (a) => (b) => fn(a, b);
}

/** Memoize a pure function by JSON-serializing its arguments. */
function memoize<Args extends unknown[], R>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => R
): (...args: Args) => R {
  const cache = new Map<string, R>();

  return (...args: Args): R => {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) {
      return cache.get(key)!;
    }
    const result = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}

/** Ensure a function runs only once; later calls return the first result. */
function once<Args extends unknown[], R>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => R
): (...args: Args) => R {
  let called = false;
  let result: R;

  return (...args: Args): R => {
    if (!called) {
      called = true;
      result = fn(...args);
    }
    return result;
  };
}

/** Wrap an async function with retry logic. */
function withRetry<Args extends unknown[], R>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => Promise<R>,
  attempts = 3,
  delayMs = 100
): (...args: Args) => Promise<R> {
  return async (...args: Args): Promise<R> => {
    let lastError: unknown;

    for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
      try {
        return await fn(...args);
      } catch (err) {
        lastError = err;
        if (i < attempts - 1) {
          await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, delayMs));
        }
      }
    }

    throw lastError;
  };
}

// ---------- Demo / usage ----------

function main() {
  const users: User[] = [
    { name: "Ada", age: 30 },
    { name: "Bob", age: 17 },
    { name: "Cara", age: 25 },
  ];

  // 1. filter + map as HOFs
  const adultNames = pipe(
    filter<User>((u) => u.age >= 18),
    map<User, string>((u) => u.name)
  )(users);

  console.log("adultNames:", adultNames); // ['Ada', 'Cara']

  // 2. Currying
  const add = (a: number, b: number) => a + b;
  const increment = curry(add)(1);
  console.log("increment(4):", increment(4)); // 5

  // 3. Composition
  const double = (x: number) => x * 2;
  const square = (x: number) => x * x;
  const doubleThenSquare = compose(square, double);
  console.log("doubleThenSquare(3):", doubleThenSquare(3)); // 36

  // 4. Memoization (classic fibonacci)
  const fib = memoize((n: number): number => {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
  });
  console.log("fib(40):", fib(40)); // 102334155

  // 5. Once
  const initialize = once(() => {
    console.log("  [initialize running once]");
    return 42;
  });
  console.log("first call:", initialize());
  console.log("second call:", initialize());

  // 6. Retry wrapper around a flaky async function
  const flakyFetch = withRetry(
    async (id: number) => {
      if (Math.random() < 0.7) {
        throw new Error("network blip");
      }
      return `data for ${id}`;
    },
    5,
    50
  );

  flakyFetch(7)
    .then((result) => console.log("retried result:", result))
    .catch((err) => console.error("all attempts failed:", err));
}

main();
```

## Key ideas illustrated

| HOF | What it demonstrates |
|-----|----------------------|
| `filter`, `map` | A function returning another function specialized by a predicate/mapper. |
| `compose`, `pipe` | Function combination; `pipe` is usually easier to read left-to-right. |
| `curry` | Transforming a multi-arg function into nested unary functions, enabling partial application. |
| `memoize` | Wrapping a function to cache results by serialized arguments. |
| `once` | Stateful wrapper that guarantees side-effecting initialization happens exactly once. |
| `withRetry` | Async HOF that adds cross-cutting behavior (retries) without changing the core function. |

## Notes on TypeScript specifics

- Generic type parameters (`<T>`, `<Args extends unknown[], R>`) let the HOF preserve input/output types instead of degrading to `any`.
- Rest parameters with `Args extends unknown[]` keep variadic functions type-safe.
- Overload signatures on `pipe` provide strong inference for a small, fixed number of steps; a fully variadic type-safe pipe requires more advanced type-level programming.
- `memoize` uses `JSON.stringify` for keys, which is simple but not safe for functions, `undefined`, or circular objects—swap in a custom key function for production.
- `withRetry` catches errors and rethrows the last one, preserving the original async contract.

## Higher-Order Functions in TypeScript

A **higher-order function (HOF)** is a function that either:

1. **takes one or more functions as arguments**, or
2. **returns a function**,

or both. They let you abstract *behavior*, not just data.

---

### 1. The idea

Instead of hard-coding *what* to do, you pass in *how*.  
Classic example: instead of writing a loop for every transformation, you write one `map` that accepts a transformation function.

```ts
const numbers = [1, 2, 3];

// HOF: takes a function `fn` and applies it to each element
function map<T, U>(arr: T[], fn: (x: T) => U): U[] {
  const out: U[] = [];
  for (const x of arr) out.push(fn(x));
  return out;
}

const doubled = map(numbers, (n) => n * 2); // [2, 4, 6]
```

The function `(n) => n * 2` is the **callback**; `map` is the HOF.

---

### 2. Syntax

#### Typing callbacks

```ts
// A function type alias
type Predicate<T> = (value: T) => boolean;
type Mapper<T, U> = (value: T, index: number) => U;

function filter<T>(arr: T[], pred: Predicate<T>): T[] {
  return arr.filter(pred); // built-in HOF
}
```

#### Returning a function (factory)

```ts
function makeAdder(x: number): (y: number) => number {
  return (y) => x + y;
}

const add5 = makeAdder(5);
add5(3); // 8
```

#### Arrow-function shorthand

```ts
const multiplier = (factor: number) => (n: number) => n * factor;
const times3 = multiplier(3);
times3(4); // 12
```

#### Generic HOFs (preserve types)

```ts
function withLogging<TArgs extends unknown[], TResult>(
  fn: (...args: TArgs) => TResult
): (...args: TArgs) => TResult {
  return (...args) => {
    console.log("calling with", args);
    const result = fn(...args);
    console.log("returned", result);
    return result;
  };
}
```

Using `TArgs extends unknown[]` keeps full argument inference.

---

### 3. Typical use cases

#### A. Collection pipelines (built-in HOFs)

```ts
const users = [
  { name: "Ada", age: 30 },
  { name: "Bob", age: 17 },
];

const adultNames = users
  .filter((u) => u.age >= 18)
  .map((u) => u.name.toUpperCase());
```

`filter`, `map`, `reduce`, `forEach`, `some`, `every`, `find` are all HOFs.

#### B. Event handling / subscriptions

```ts
type Handler<E> = (event: E) => void;

function on<E>(event: string, handler: Handler<E>): () => void {
  // returns an unsubscribe function (another HOF pattern)
  const unsub = () => console.log(`unsub ${event}`);
  return unsub;
}

const off = on<MouseEvent>("click", (e) => console.log(e.clientX));
off(); // unsubscribe
```

#### C. Factories / configuration

```ts
function makeValidator<T>(schema: (x: unknown) => x is T) {
  return (input: unknown): T => {
    if (!schema(input)) throw new Error("invalid");
    return input;
  };
}

const asNumber = makeValidator((x): x is number => typeof x === "number");
asNumber(42); // number
```

#### D. Currying & partial application

```ts
const curry = <A, B, C>(fn: (a: A, b: B) => C) =>
  (a: A) => (b: B) => fn(a, b);

const add = (a: number, b: number) => a + b;
const addFrom = curry(add);
const add10 = addFrom(10);
add10(5); // 15
```

#### E. Composition

```ts
const compose = <T>(...fns: Array<(x: T) => T>) =>
  (x: T) => fns.reduceRight((acc, f) => f(acc), x);

const shout = (s: string) => s.toUpperCase();
const exclaim = (s: string) => s + "!";
const shoutExclaim = compose(shout, exclaim);
shoutExclaim("hi"); // "HI!"
```

#### F. Memoization

```ts
function memoize<TArgs extends unknown[], TResult>(
  fn: (...args: TArgs) => TResult
): (...args: TArgs) => TResult {
  const cache = new Map<string, TResult>();
  return (...args) => {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key)!;
    const r = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, r);
    return r;
  };
}
```

#### G. Middleware / wrappers ( Express-style )

```ts
type Next = () => void;
type Middleware = (req: any, res: any, next: Next) => void;

const logger: Middleware = (req, _res, next) => {
  console.log(req.url);
  next();
};
```

---

### 4. Type-safety tips

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---|---|
| Losing argument inference | Use `...args: TArgs` with `TArgs extends unknown[]` |
| `this` binding issues | Prefer arrow functions or explicit `this` param |
| Overloads on HOFs | Use function overloads on the outer function |
| Callback returns `void` but returns value | TS allows extra returns for `void` callbacks; be aware |

```ts
// Explicit `this` typing
function forEach<T>(this: T[], cb: (this: T, x: T) => void) {
  for (const x of this) cb.call(x, x);
}
```

---

### 5. When *not* to use HOFs

- Hot loops where allocation of closures matters (profile first).
- When readability suffers: deeply nested callbacks ("callback hell"). Prefer `async/await` for async flows.
- When types become unreadable: extract named types.

---

### Summary

Higher-order functions are the backbone of functional-style TypeScript: they let you compose small, typed behaviors into larger ones. The key TypeScript skill is **preserving inference** via generics (`TArgs`, `TResult`) and **function-type aliases**, so callers keep full type safety.

A very common higher-order-function mistake in TypeScript is passing a class method directly as a callback. The function is higher-order because `forEach` takes another function, but the method loses its `this` binding when it is extracted from the object.

### Mistake

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;

  increment() {
    this.count += 1;
  }

  run(values: number[]) {
    // `this.increment` is extracted and passed as a bare function.
    // `forEach` calls it without the Counter instance as `this`.
    values.forEach(this.increment);
  }
}

const counter = new Counter();
counter.run([1, 2, 3]);
console.log(counter.count); // Expected 3, but often breaks or stays 0
```

### Corrected version

Option 1: use an arrow function at the call site:

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;

  increment() {
    this.count += 1;
  }

  run(values: number[]) {
    values.forEach(() => this.increment());
  }
}
```

Option 2: bind the method once:

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;

  increment() {
    this.count += 1;
  }

  run(values: number[]) {
    values.forEach(this.increment.bind(this));
  }
}
```

Option 3: define the method as an arrow-function class field:

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;

  increment = () => {
    this.count += 1;
  };

  run(values: number[]) {
    values.forEach(this.increment);
  }
}
```

### Why the original is wrong

When you write `this.increment`, JavaScript evaluates that expression to a plain function value. It does not automatically remember that it came from `this`. Later, `forEach` invokes that function roughly like:

```ts
callback(value, index, array)
```

So inside `increment`, `this` is not the `Counter` instance. In strict mode, which TypeScript emits by default for modules/classes in many setups, `this` becomes `undefined`. Accessing `this.count` then throws something like:

```text
Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'count')
```

Even where it does not throw, the update may happen on the wrong object or silently fail to affect `counter.count`.

TypeScript also often catches this statically. A method such as `increment(): void` is typed as needing a `this` value of `Counter`. Passing it to `forEach`, whose callback type does not promise to call it with that `this`, can produce an error like:

```text
The 'this' context of type 'void' is not assignable to method's 'this' of type 'Counter'.
```

### Which correction to choose

- `() => this.increment()` is usually clearest and most flexible.
- `.bind(this)` is concise but creates a new function each call unless cached.
- Arrow class fields permanently bind `this`, which is convenient for callbacks, but they are instance properties rather than prototype methods, so they cost slightly more memory per instance and are not ideal when many instances are created.

The underlying lesson: higher-order functions do not preserve the receiver of a method. If a callback depends on `this`, you must explicitly preserve that relationship.

# Common Pitfalls with Higher-Order Functions in TypeScript

Below are the most common pitfalls, each with broken vs. idiomatic code.

## 1. Losing `this` when passing a method as a callback

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;
  inc() { this.count++; }
}

const c = new Counter();
[1, 2, 3].forEach(c.inc); // ❌ `this` is undefined at runtime
```

**Fix** — bind, arrow, or wrap:

```ts
[1, 2, 3].forEach(() => c.inc());
// or
[1, 2, 3].forEach(c.inc.bind(c));
```

---

## 2. Mutating the source inside `map`/`filter`

```ts
const users = [{ name: "a", active: true }, { name: "b", active: false }];
const active = users.map(u => { u.active = true; return u; }); // ❌ mutates original
```

**Fix** — return new objects:

```ts
const active = users
  .filter(u => u.active)
  .map(u => ({ ...u })); // ✅ pure
```

---

## 3. Forgetting to return in a `map` callback

```ts
const doubled = [1, 2, 3].map(n => { n * 2; }); // ❌ returns undefined -> [undefined, undefined, undefined]
```

TS may not catch this if the callback's inferred return is `void`-compatible. Fix with explicit return type:

```ts
const doubled = [1, 2, 3].map((n): number => n * 2);
```

---

## 4. Using `forEach` with async callbacks (unhandled rejections / no await)

```ts
async function saveAll(items: Item[]) {
  items.forEach(async (item) => {
    await db.save(item); // ❌ forEach doesn't await; rejections unhandled
  });
}
```

**Fix** — `for...of` or `Promise.all` + `map`:

```ts
async function saveAll(items: Item[]) {
  await Promise.all(items.map(item => db.save(item)));
}
```

---

## 5. Closure over a loop variable

```ts
const fns: (() => number)[] = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  fns.push(() => i); // ❌ all return 3
}
```

**Fix** — use `let`/`const` (block scope) or capture:

```ts
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  fns.push(() => i); // ✅
}
// or
[0, 1, 2].forEach(i => fns.push(() => i));
```

---

## 6. Boolean callbacks that should be type guards

```ts
function isString(x: unknown): boolean {
  return typeof x === "string";
}

const strs = [1, "a", 2].filter(isString); // ❌ type is (number | string)[], not string[]
```

**Fix** — use a type predicate:

```ts
function isString(x: unknown): x is string {
  return typeof x === "string";
}
const strs = [1, "a", 2].filter(isString); // ✅ string[]
```

---

## 7. Losing inference with intermediate variables / overloads

```ts
const pick = <T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, keys: K[]) => ...;
const f = pick;            // ❌ widened to generic signature, inference often degrades
const g = (o, k) => pick(o, k); // ✅ keeps inference
```

Also avoid splitting HOF pipelines in ways that widen:

```ts
const step1 = items.map(x => x.value); // type: number[]
const step2 = step1.filter(x => x > 0); // fine
// but assigning to `any`/`unknown` mid-pipeline kills narrowing
```

---

## 8. `void` return type confusion

```ts
function run(cb: () => void) {
  const r = cb(); // r is void — can't use it
}
run(() => 42); // allowed, but return discarded
```

If you need the result, type the callback's return explicitly:

```ts
function run<T>(cb: () => T): T {
  return cb();
}
```

Also `Array.prototype.forEach` returns `void` — don't chain after it.

---

## 9. Partial application / currying arity mistakes

```ts
const add = (a: number, b: number) => a + b;
const add5 = add.bind(null, 5);
add5("x"); // ❌ TS may not flag depending on lib; bind typings are loose
```

**Fix** — write an explicit wrapper for type safety:

```ts
const add5 = (b: number) => add(5, b);
```

---

## 10. Creating new function identities in hot paths / render loops

```tsx
// React example
{items.map(item => (
  <Row key={item.id} onClick={() => select(item.id)} /> // ❌ new fn every render -> memo breaks
))}
```

**Fix** — stable handler + id:

```tsx
const handleSelect = useCallback((id: string) => select(id), [select]);
{items.map(item => (
  <Row key={item.id} onClick={handleSelect} data-id={item.id} />
))}
```

Or use a data attribute / closure factory memoized outside render.

---

## 11. `reduce` without initial value on possibly-empty arrays

```ts
const sum = [1, 2, 3].reduce((a, b) => a + b); // type ok, but runtime throws on []
```

TS types `reduce` without initial as `T | undefined`? Actually `reduce` without initial returns `T`, but runtime throws on empty. With initial value it's safe:

```ts
const sum = xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0); // ✅
```

Also beware `reduce` type inference with mixed accumulators — annotate accumulator:

```ts
const map = items.reduce<Record<string, Item>>((acc, item) => {
  acc[item.id] = item;
  return acc;
}, {});
```

---

## 12. Sorting with a comparator that returns boolean

```ts
arr.sort((a, b) => a > b); // ❌ comparator must return number; boolean coerces weirdly
```

**Fix:**

```ts
arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);
// or for strings:
arr.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));
```

---

## 13. Shadowing / stale closures in event handlers

```ts
let count = 0;
const handler = () => console.log(count);
count = 5;
handler(); // 5 — fine, but if count is a *value* captured by value it's stale
```

More subtle with HOF factories:

```ts
function makeAdder(x: number) {
  return (y: number) => x + y; // ✅ captures x by closure (fine)
}
```

Pitfall is capturing a mutable `let` you *intended* to be fixed. Prefer `const` bindings in factories.

---

## 14. Forgetting that `filter`/`map` don't short-circuit

For large arrays, chaining multiple passes is O(n·k). Use a single `reduce` or `for` loop when profiling shows a bottleneck. Also `find`/`some`/`every` short-circuit — prefer them.

---

## 15. Typing a HOF's `this` parameter

```ts
function withLogging<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: T): T {
  return function (this: any, ...args: any[]) { // ❌ loses `this` typing
    console.log("call");
    return fn.apply(this, args);
  } as T;
}
```

Preserve `this` via `ThisParameterType`:

```ts
function withLogging<F extends (this: any, ...args: any[]) => any>(fn: F): F {
  return function (this: ThisParameterType<F>, ...args: Parameters<F>): ReturnType<F> {
    console.log("call");
    return fn.apply(this, args);
  } as F;
}
```

---

### Quick checklist

- Annotate callback return types when inference is loose.
- Prefer pure functions; never mutate inputs.
- Use type predicates (`x is T`) for narrowing filters.
- Await async work explicitly; never `forEach(async ...)`.
- Avoid `bind` for typed partial application; use wrappers.
- Keep HOF chains O(n); short-circuit where possible.

# Exercise

Build a small typed pipeline with higher-order functions:

1. Define a `User` type.
2. Create `filterWhere(predicate)` — a higher-order function that takes a predicate and returns a function that filters an array.
3. Create `select(selector)` — a higher-order function that takes a selector and returns a function that maps an array.
4. Create `compose(f, g)` — a higher-order function that combines two functions.
5. Create `withTiming(fn, label)` — a higher-order function that wraps any function, measures how long it takes, logs the duration, and returns the original result.
6. Use these to create `processUsers(users)`, which returns the emails of active users who are at least 18 years old.

---

# Step-by-step solution

## 1. Model the data

Start with a simple `User` type.

```ts
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  age: number;
  active: boolean;
};
```

## 2. Create `filterWhere`

`filterWhere` is higher-order because it **returns a function**. The returned function closes over `predicate`.

```ts
function filterWhere<T>(
  predicate: (item: T) => boolean
): (items: readonly T[]) => T[] {
  return (items) => items.filter(predicate);
}
```

Why this shape is useful: you can create reusable filters once and apply them many times.

```ts
const activeAdults = filterWhere<User>((user) => user.active && user.age >= 18);
```

## 3. Create `select`

Similarly, `select` returns a mapper function.

```ts
function select<T, U>(
  selector: (item: T) => U
): (items: readonly T[]) => U[] {
  return (items) => items.map(selector);
}
```

Usage:

```ts
const toEmails = select<User, string>((user) => user.email);
```

## 4. Create `compose`

`compose(f, g)` means: first apply `g`, then apply `f`.

```ts
function compose<A, B, C>(
  f: (input: B) => C,
  g: (input: A) => B
): (input: A) => C {
  return (input) => f(g(input));
}
```

This lets us build a pipeline without nested calls:

```ts
const processUsersCore = compose(toEmails, activeAdults);
```

Data flow:

```text
User[] -> activeAdults -> User[] -> toEmails -> string[]
```

## 5. Create `withTiming`

`withTiming` wraps a function and measures its execution time. It is generic over both arguments and return type.

```ts
function withTiming<Args extends unknown[], Result>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => Result,
  label: string
): (...args: Args) => Result {
  return (...args: Args) => {
    const start = Date.now();

    try {
      return fn(...args);
    } finally {
      const duration = Date.now() - start;
      console.log(`${label} took ${duration} ms`);
    }
  };
}
```

The `try/finally` ensures timing is logged even if the wrapped function throws.

## 6. Wire everything together

```ts
const users: User[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com", age: 30, active: true },
  { id: 2, name: "Bob", email: "bob@example.com", age: 16, active: true },
  { id: 3, name: "Cara", email: "cara@example.com", age: 25, active: false },
  { id: 4, name: "Dan", email: "dan@example.com", age: 40, active: true },
];

const activeAdults = filterWhere<User>((user) => user.active && user.age >= 18);
const toEmails = select<User, string>((user) => user.email);

const processUsers = withTiming(
  compose(toEmails, activeAdults),
  "processUsers"
);

console.log(processUsers(users));
```

Expected output:

```text
processUsers took 0 ms
[ 'ada@example.com', 'dan@example.com' ]
```

The exact timing may vary, but the returned emails should be Ada's and Dan's.

---

# Final complete solution

```ts
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  age: number;
  active: boolean;
};

function filterWhere<T>(
  predicate: (item: T) => boolean
): (items: readonly T[]) => T[] {
  return (items) => items.filter(predicate);
}

function select<T, U>(
  selector: (item: T) => U
): (items: readonly T[]) => U[] {
  return (items) => items.map(selector);
}

function compose<A, B, C>(
  f: (input: B) => C,
  g: (input: A) => B
): (input: A) => C {
  return (input) => f(g(input));
}

function withTiming<Args extends unknown[], Result>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => Result,
  label: string
): (...args: Args) => Result {
  return (...args: Args) => {
    const start = Date.now();

    try {
      return fn(...args);
    } finally {
      const duration = Date.now() - start;
      console.log(`${label} took ${duration} ms`);
    }
  };
}

const users: User[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com", age: 30, active: true },
  { id: 2, name: "Bob", email: "bob@example.com", age: 16, active: true },
  { id: 3, name: "Cara", email: "cara@example.com", age: 25, active: false },
  { id: 4, name: "Dan", email: "dan@example.com", age: 40, active: true },
];

const activeAdults = filterWhere<User>((user) => user.active && user.age >= 18);
const toEmails = select<User, string>((user) => user.email);

const processUsers = withTiming(
  compose(toEmails, activeAdults),
  "processUsers"
);

console.log(processUsers(users));
```

This exercise demonstrates the core idea of higher-order functions: functions can accept other functions, return new functions, and be composed into reusable, typed pipelines.

## Higher-Order Functions in TypeScript

A **higher-order function (HOF)** is a function that does at least one of the following:

1. **Takes one or more functions as arguments** (callbacks, predicates, transformers).
2. **Returns a function** (factories, currying, decorators, middleware).

In TypeScript, functions are first-class values, so HOFs are idiomatic. The main TypeScript-specific concerns are:

- **Function types**: `(x: T) => U`, `Predicate<T>`, etc.
- **Generics**: preserve input/output types through transformations.
- **Inference**: let TS infer `T` and `U` from arguments.
- **Overloads / variadic typing**: needed for `compose`/`pipe` to keep strong types across many steps.
- **`this` binding and arity**: when wrapping functions, preserve parameters and return type.

Common HOF patterns:

| Pattern | Example | Why it’s a HOF |
|---|---|---|
| Transformation | `map(arr, x => x * 2)` | Takes a transform function |
| Selection | `filter(arr, x => x > 0)` | Takes a predicate |
| Aggregation | `reduce(arr, (acc, x) => acc + x, 0)` | Takes a reducer |
| Composition | `compose(f, g)(x)` | Takes and returns functions |
| Currying | `curry(add)(1)(2)` | Returns a function |
| Memoization | `memoize(expensiveFn)` | Wraps and returns a function |
| Decorators | `withLogging(fn)` | Wraps behavior around a function |

---

## Implementation: Core Higher-Order Function Utilities

Below is a compact but production-style set of core HOFs. It includes array HOFs, function combinators, and a wrapper example. Comments explain the typing decisions.

```ts
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Type aliases                                                        */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/** A function taking one argument. */
type Unary<A, B> = (a: A) => B;

/** A boolean test over a value. */
type Predicate<T> = (value: T) => boolean;

/** A reducer combining an accumulator with the current item. */
type Reducer<T, U> = (acc: U, item: T, index: number) => U;

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Array higher-order functions                                        */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * map: transform each element using a callback.
 * Generic T (input) and U (output) let TS infer the result type.
 */
function map<T, U>(
  items: readonly T[],
  transform: (item: T, index: number) => U
): U[] {
  const result: U[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    result.push(transform(items[i], i));
  }
  return result;
}

/**
 * filter: keep elements satisfying a predicate.
 * The output type stays T because filtering does not change the element type.
 */
function filter<T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  predicate: Predicate<T>
): T[] {
  const result: T[] = [];
  for (const item of items) {
    if (predicate(item)) result.push(item);
  }
  return result;
}

/**
 * reduce: fold an array into a single value.
 * U is the accumulator type; initial defines it.
 */
function reduce<T, U>(
  items: readonly T[],
  reducer: Reducer<T, U>,
  initial: U
): U {
  let acc = initial;
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    acc = reducer(acc, items[i], i);
  }
  return acc;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Function combinators                                                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * compose: apply functions right-to-left.
 * f(g(h(x))) === compose(f, g, h)(x)
 *
 * Overloads give strong types for common arities; the implementation
 * uses `any` internally because perfect variadic inference is complex.
 */
function compose<A, B, C>(
  f: (b: B) => C,
  g: (a: A) => B
): (a: A) => C;

function compose<A, B, C, D>(
  f: (c: C) => D,
  g: (b: B) => C,
  h: (a: A) => B
): (a: A) => D;

function compose(...fns: Array<(x: any) => any>): (x: any) => any {
  return (x: any) => fns.reduceRight((value, fn) => fn(value), x);
}

/**
 * pipe: apply functions left-to-right.
 * pipe(f, g)(x) === g(f(x))
 */
function pipe<A, B>(f: (a: A) => B): (a: A) => B;

function pipe<A, B, C>(
  f: (a: A) => B,
  g: (b: B) => C
): (a: A) => C;

function pipe(...fns: Array<(x: any) => any>): (x: any) => any {
  return (x: any) => fns.reduce((value, fn) => fn(value), x);
}

/**
 * curry: convert a binary function into a chain of unary functions.
 * Useful for partial application and point-free style.
 */
function curry<A, B, C>(
  fn: (a: A, b: B) => C
): (a: A) => (b: B) => C {
  return (a: A) => (b: B) => fn(a, b);
}

/**
 * memoize: cache results by serialized arguments.
 * Preserves the original function's parameter and return types.
 */
function memoize<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: T): T {
  const cache = new Map<string, ReturnType<T>>();

  const memoized = (...args: Parameters<T>): ReturnType<T> => {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);

    if (cache.has(key)) {
      return cache.get(key)!;
    }

    const result = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };

  return memoized as T;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Function wrapper / decorator example                                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * withLogging: a HOF that wraps any function with before/after logs.
 * Preserves the wrapped function's signature via generics.
 */
function withLogging<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(
  name: string,
  fn: T
): T {
  const wrapped = (...args: Parameters<T>): ReturnType<T> => {
    console.log(`[${name}] called with`, args);
    const result = fn(...args);
    console.log(`[${name}] returned`, result);
    return result;
  };

  return wrapped as T;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Demo                                                                */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

const doubled = map(numbers, (n) => n * 2);
const evens = filter(numbers, (n) => n % 2 === 0);
const sum = reduce(numbers, (acc, n) => acc + n, 0);

console.log("doubled:", doubled); // [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
console.log("evens:", evens);     // [2, 4]
console.log("sum:", sum);         // 15

// compose: square then add 1
const square = (x: number) => x * x;
const addOne = (x: number) => x + 1;
const squareThenAddOne = compose(addOne, square);
console.log("compose(3):", squareThenAddOne(3)); // 10

// pipe: same logic, left-to-right
const addOneThenSquare = pipe(addOne, square);
console.log("pipe(3):", addOneThenSquare(3)); // 16

// curry
const add = (a: number, b: number) => a + b;
const add5 = curry(add)(5);
console.log("curry add5(7):", add5(7)); // 12

// memoize
const expensive = (n: number) => {
  console.log("computing...");
  return n * n;
};
const memoizedSquare = memoize(expensive);
console.log(memoizedSquare(4)); // computing... 16
console.log(memoizedSquare(4)); // 16 (cached)

// withLogging
const divide = withLogging("divide", (a: number, b: number) => a / b);
console.log("divide(10, 2):", divide(10, 2));
```

---

## Interview Talking Points

- **Why HOFs matter**: they let you abstract *control flow* (map/filter/reduce) and *behavior* (logging, retry, caching) separately from business logic.
- **TypeScript challenge**: preserving types through variadic composition is hard; overloads or helper types like `Pipe`/`Compose` are common in real libraries.
- **Purity**: HOFs shine when the wrapped/passed functions are pure; side effects should be explicit.
- **Performance**: memoization needs a good cache key; `JSON.stringify` is simple but not ideal for objects/functions.
- **`this` safety**: when wrapping methods, bind `this` or use arrow functions to avoid losing context.

If you want, I can extend this into a **typed `pipe`/`compose` with unlimited arity using recursive conditional types**, or show **middleware-style HOFs** (e.g., Express/Koa style).

Below are common edge cases when writing/using higher-order functions (HOFs) in TypeScript, grouped by runtime behavior and type-system behavior. Each includes a small example and the recommended handling.

---

## 1. Losing `this` when a method is passed as a callback

Methods are not bound to their instance by default.

```ts
class Counter {
  n = 0;
  inc() {
    this.n++;
    return this.n;
  }
}

const c = new Counter();
const bad = c.inc; // runtime: `this` is undefined in strict mode
// bad(); // TypeError

// HOF that expects a no-arg function
function twice(fn: () => number) {
  return fn() + fn();
}

// twice(c.inc); // type error: c.inc is (this: Counter) => number
```

**Handling:** use an arrow function or `.bind`.

```ts
twice(() => c.inc());   // arrow captures `c`
twice(c.inc.bind(c));   // explicit bind
```

---

## 2. A generic wrapper can silently erase `this`

If your wrapper accepts `(...args) => R`, it may drop a `this` parameter.

```ts
function logged<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: F): F {
  // Returning a plain function loses the original `this` type unless handled.
  return function (...args: any[]) {
    console.log(args);
    return fn.apply(this, args);
  } as F;
}

class Box {
  value = 10;
  get() { return this.value; }
}

const b = new Box();
const wrapped = logged(b.get);
// wrapped(); // runtime `this` is undefined
```

**Handling:** carry `ThisParameterType<F>` through the wrapper.

```ts
function logged<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(
  fn: F
): (this: ThisParameterType<F>, ...args: Parameters<F>) => ReturnType<F> {
  return function (this: ThisParameterType<F>, ...args: Parameters<F>) {
    console.log(args);
    return fn.apply(this, args) as ReturnType<F>;
  };
}

const wrapped2 = logged(b.get);
wrapped2.call(b); // type-safe and runtime-correct
```

---

## 3. Overloads collapse when wrapped by a generic HOF

TypeScript cannot preserve multiple call signatures through a single generic type parameter.

```ts
function parse(x: string): number;
function parse(x: number): string;
function parse(x: any): any {
  return typeof x === "string" ? x.length : String(x);
}

function wrap<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: F): F {
  return ((...args: any[]) => fn(...args)) as F;
}

const wrappedParse = wrap(parse);
// wrappedParse("a") is typed as `any`/last signature, not `number`
```

Also:

```ts
type P = Parameters<typeof parse>;
// P is [x: any] (last overload), not [x: string] | [x: number]
```

**Handling:** declare overloads on the wrapper itself, or avoid generic wrapping for overloaded functions.

```ts
function wrapParse(x: string): number;
function wrapParse(x: number): string;
function wrapParse(x: any): any {
  return parse(x);
}
```

---

## 4. Optional and rest parameters must be preserved

A wrapper should not turn optional parameters into required ones.

```ts
function original(a: number, b?: string, ...rest: boolean[]) {
  return [a, b, ...rest];
}

// Bad: Args inferred as any[] loses optionality
function badWrap(fn: (...args: any[]) => any) {
  return (...args: any[]) => fn(...args);
}

// Good: preserve tuple shape
function goodWrap<Args extends any[], R>(
  fn: (...args: Args) => R
): (...args: Args) => R {
  return (...args: Args) => fn(...args);
}

const w = goodWrap(original);
w(1);          // OK
w(1, "x");     // OK
w(1, "x", true, false); // OK
```

**Edge:** default parameters are represented as optional in the type, so they are preserved similarly. But if inference falls back to `any[]`, optionality is lost; add explicit type arguments when needed.

---

## 5. `this` parameters in callbacks

Callbacks often depend on a specific `this`. HOFs should allow and propagate it.

```ts
type ClickHandler = (this: HTMLButtonElement, ev: MouseEvent) => void;

function on(el: HTMLButtonElement, handler: ClickHandler) {
  el.addEventListener("click", handler); // addEventListener binds `this` to element
}

// Wrapper that preserves `this`
function debounce<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(
  fn: F,
  ms: number
): (this: ThisParameterType<F>, ...args: Parameters<F>) => void {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  return function (this: ThisParameterType<F>, ...args: Parameters<F>) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), ms);
  };
}
```

---

## 6. Type predicates are lost unless you add overloads

A generic HOF returning `T[]` won't narrow results when the callback is a type guard.

```ts
// Bad: no narrowing
function myFilterBad<T>(arr: readonly T[], pred: (x: T) => boolean): T[] {
  return arr.filter(pred);
}

// Good: overload for type predicates
function myFilter<T, S extends T>(
  arr: readonly T[],
  pred: (x: T) => x is S
): S[];

function myFilter<T>(
  arr: readonly T[],
  pred: (x: T) => boolean
): T[];

function myFilter(arr: readonly any[], pred: (x: any) => boolean): any[] {
  return arr.filter(pred);
}

const mixed: (string | number)[] = ["a", 1, "b"];
const strings = myFilter(mixed, (x): x is string => typeof x === "string");
// strings: string[]
```

---

## 7. `void` return types allow ignored returns

`(x: T) => void` accepts functions returning anything. This is usually desirable for event handlers, but dangerous if the HOF actually needs the returned value.

```ts
function run(cb: () => void) {
  cb();
}

run(() => 42); // allowed; 42 is ignored

// If you need the value, type it explicitly:
function runAndUse(cb: () => number) {
  return cb() * 2;
}
```

**Edge in wrappers:** if your wrapper collects callback results, do not type the callback as returning `void`.

---

## 8. Strict function types and variance

Under `strictFunctionTypes`, callback parameters are checked contravariantly.

```ts
class Animal { name = "animal"; }
class Dog extends Animal { breed = "dog"; }

type AnimalHandler = (a: Animal) => void;
type DogHandler = (d: Dog) => void;

const dogHandler: DogHandler = (d) => console.log(d.breed);

// const h: AnimalHandler = dogHandler;
// Error: DogHandler is not assignable to AnimalHandler
// A handler for Dog cannot safely accept any Animal.
```

**Handling:** if your HOF accepts a handler for a broad type, consumers must provide a handler accepting that broad type. If you want to allow narrower handlers, use generics or overloads rather than forcing one callback type.

---

## 9. Async HOFs: sync vs async callbacks

A wrapper typed only for async functions cannot safely accept sync functions unless you normalize with `Promise.resolve`.

```ts
// Too narrow: only async callbacks
function withRetry<F extends (...args: any[]) => Promise<any>>(
  fn: F,
  retries = 3
) {
  return async (...args: Parameters<F>) => {
    for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
      try {
        return await fn(...args);
      } catch (e) {
        if (i === retries - 1) throw e;
      }
    }
  };
}

// More flexible: accept sync or async
function withRetry2<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(
  fn: F,
  retries = 3
): (...args: Parameters<F>) => Promise<ReturnType<F>> {
  return async (...args: Parameters<F>) => {
    for (let i = 0; i < retries; i++) {
      try {
        return await Promise.resolve(fn(...args));
      } catch (e) {
        if (i === retries - 1) throw e;
      }
    }
    throw new Error("unreachable");
  };
}
```

**Edge cases:** errors are `unknown` under `useUnknownInCatchVariables`; rethrow or wrap them carefully. Also avoid swallowing stack traces.

---

## 10. Memoization keying and identity

Naive memoization breaks on object arguments, circular structures, `NaN`, and async results.

```ts
function memoize<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: F) {
  const cache = new Map<string, ReturnType<F>>();
  return (...args: Parameters<F>): ReturnType<F> => {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args); // breaks on functions, circular, undefined
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key)!;
    const result = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}
```

**Handling (examples):**

```ts
// Single object argument: use WeakMap to avoid leaks
function memoizeUnary<T extends object, R>(fn: (x: T) => R) {
  const cache = new WeakMap<T, R>();
  return (x: T): R => {
    if (cache.has(x)) return cache.get(x)!;
    const r = fn(x);
    cache.set(x, r);
    return r;
  };
}

// Handle NaN and -0 in primitive keys
function stableKey(x: unknown): string {
  if (typeof x === "number") {
    if (Number.isNaN(x)) return "NaN";
    if (Object.is(x, -0)) return "-0";
  }
  return JSON.stringify(x);
}

// Async memoization should cache the promise, not the resolved value
function memoizeAsync<F extends (...args: any[]) => Promise<any>>(fn: F) {
  const cache = new Map<string, ReturnType<F>>();
  return (...args: Parameters<F>): ReturnType<F> => {
    const key = stableKey(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key)!;
    const p = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, p as any);
    return p as any;
  };
}
```

---

## 11. Currying and partial application are hard to type precisely

TypeScript cannot easily infer curried signatures with optional trailing arguments.

```ts
// Simple, fully typed currying for fixed arity
function curry2<A, B, R>(fn: (a: A, b: B) => R) {
  return (a: A) => (b: B) => fn(a, b);
}

// Variadic/partial application usually needs overloads or helper types
function partial<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(
  fn: F,
  ...preset: Partial<Parameters<F>>
): (...rest: any[]) => ReturnType<F> {
  return (...rest: any[]) => fn(...preset, ...rest);
}
```

**Handling:** for production, use well-typed utility libraries (e.g., lodash/fp, Remeda, or a custom implementation with explicit overloads). Avoid “magic” variadic currying unless you provide overloads for common arities.

---

## 12. `bind` typing edge cases

Modern TS types `bind` well, but it can fail with overloads, generics, or `this`-typed functions.

```ts
function f(this: { x: number }, y: number, z?: string) {
  return this.x + y + (z?.length ?? 0);
}

const g = f.bind({ x: 1 });      // (y: number, z?: string) => number
const h = f.bind({ x: 1 }, 2);  // (z?: string) => number
const i = f.bind({ x: 1 }, 2, "a"); // () => number

// Edge: binding a generic function often loses type params
function identity<T>(x: T): T { return x; }
const bound = identity.bind(null); // type becomes (x: unknown) => unknown in some cases
```

**Handling:** prefer arrow wrappers for generic or overloaded functions instead of `bind`.

```ts
const safeIdentity = <T>(x: T) => identity(x);
```

---

## 13. Composition pipelines need tuple typing

`compose(f, g)` requires the output of `g` to match input of `f`. Typing this for arbitrary length is nontrivial.

```ts
// Simple two-function pipe
function pipe<A, B, C>(
  g: (a: A) => B,
  f: (b: B) => C
): (a: A) => C {
  return (a) => f(g(a));
}

// Variadic pipe requires recursive conditional types or overloaded signatures
type Pipe = {
  <A, B>(g: (a: A) => B): (a: A) => B;
  <A, B, C>(g: (a: A) => B, f1: (b: B) => C): (a: A) => C;
  <A, B, C, D>(
    g: (a: A) => B,
    f1: (b: B) => C,
    f2: (c: C) => D
  ): (a: A) => D;
  // ...add as needed
};
```

**Handling:** provide overloads for common arities or use a library with robust tuple inference.

---

## 14. Nullish or optional callbacks

Accepting optional callbacks makes parameter types awkward because `Parameters<T>` of `undefined` is invalid.

```ts
// Bad: T may be undefined
function safeCallBad<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(
  fn?: T,
  ...args: Parameters<T>
) {
  return fn?.(...args);
}

// Better: validate at runtime and type narrowly
function safeCall(
  fn: ((...args: any[]) => any) | undefined,
  ...args: any[]
) {
  return fn?.(...args);
}

// Or use overloads for known callback shapes
function onClick(handler?: (ev: MouseEvent) => void) {
  return (ev: MouseEvent) => handler?.(ev);
}
```

---

## 15. HOFs returning HOFs and inference limits

When a HOF returns another generic function, TypeScript may fail to infer inner type parameters.

```ts
function makeMapper<T>() {
  return <U>(arr: T[], fn: (x: T) => U): U[] => arr.map(fn);
}

const mapNumbers = makeMapper<number>();
const out = mapNumbers([1, 2, 3], (x) => x.toFixed(2));
// out: string[]
```

If inference fails, add explicit type arguments at the call site:

```ts
const m = makeMapper<number>()<string>([1, 2, 3], (x) => x.toFixed(2));
```

---

## 16. Reentrancy and mutation in HOFs

HOFs that cache, debounce, or memoize can misbehave if the callback mutates shared state or calls the wrapper recursively.

```ts
function forEachReverse<T>(arr: T[], cb: (x: T, i: number) => void) {
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    cb(arr[i], i); // if cb mutates arr, indices can become invalid
  }
}
```

**Handling:** document mutation expectations, clone inputs when needed, and avoid relying on stable indices during callback execution.

---

## Practical checklist for writing a robust HOF

- Preserve `Parameters<F>`, `ReturnType<F>`, and `ThisParameterType<F>`.
- Decide whether the callback is sync, async, or “sync-or-async,” and normalize with `Promise.resolve`.
- Add overloads for type predicates and overloaded source functions.
- Use `unknown` for caught errors and narrow before use.
- Avoid `JSON.stringify` as a general memoization key; handle objects, `NaN`, `-0`, and circular references.
- Prefer arrow functions over `bind` for generic/overloaded functions.
- Test with `strictFunctionTypes`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, and `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`.

If you want, I can expand any one of these into a complete, production-ready utility (e.g., a fully typed `pipe`, `memoize`, or `debounce`) with tests.

# Immutability in TypeScript — Complete Guide with Runnable Example

TypeScript immutability exists at **two layers**:

1. **Compile-time** — `readonly`, `Readonly<T>`, `as const`, `readonly` arrays/tuples. Catches mutations during type-checking.
2. **Runtime** — `Object.freeze`, `Object.seal`, or libraries like **Immer**. Actually prevents mutation in JS.

Below is a single, runnable file that demonstrates both layers, plus deep-readonly utility types, functional updates, and common pitfalls.

---

## `immutability.ts`

```ts
// ============================================================================
// 1. COMPILE-TIME IMMUTABILITY (type-level)
// ============================================================================

// ---- 1.1 readonly properties ----
interface User {
  readonly id: number;
  name: string;
  address: {
    city: string;
  };
}

const u: User = { id: 1, name: "Ada", address: { city: "London" } };
// u.id = 2;            // ❌ compile error: cannot assign to 'id'
u.name = "Grace";        // ✅ allowed (not readonly)
u.address.city = "Paris"; // ✅ allowed (nested not readonly)  ← shallow only!

// ---- 1.2 Readonly<T> utility (shallow) ----
type ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;
const ru: ReadonlyUser = { id: 2, name: "Bob", address: { city: "Berlin" } };
// ru.name = "X";         // ❌ compile error
ru.address.city = "Munich"; // ✅ still allowed (shallow)

// ---- 1.3 DeepReadonly utility type ----
type DeepReadonly<T> = T extends (infer U)[]
  ? ReadonlyArray<DeepReadonly<U>>
  : T extends object
  ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
  : T;

type DeepUser = DeepReadonly<User>;
const du: DeepUser = { id: 3, name: "Linus", address: { city: "Helsinki" } };
// du.address.city = "Tampere"; // ❌ compile error (deep)

// ---- 1.4 Readonly arrays & tuples ----
const arr: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3];
// arr.push(4);            // ❌
// arr[0] = 9;             // ❌

const tuple: readonly [string, number] = ["x", 1];
// tuple[0] = "y";        // ❌

// ReadonlyArray<T> is the long form
const arr2: ReadonlyArray<number> = [4, 5, 6];

// ---- 1.5 const assertions (literal types + readonly) ----
const config = {
  endpoint: "https://api.example.com",
  retries: 3,
  methods: ["GET", "POST"] as const,
} as const;

// config.retries = 5;          // ❌
// config.methods.push("DELETE"); // ❌
type Methods = (typeof config)["methods"][number]; // "GET" | "POST"

// ---- 1.6 readonly in classes ----
class Point {
  constructor(
    public readonly x: number,
    public readonly y: number
  ) {}
  // move returns a new instance instead of mutating
  move(dx: number, dy: number): Point {
    return new Point(this.x + dx, this.y + dy);
  }
}

const p = new Point(1, 2);
const p2 = p.move(3, 4);
console.log(p, p2); // original untouched

// ---- 1.7 ReadonlyMap / ReadonlySet ----
const m: ReadonlyMap<string, number> = new Map([["a", 1]]);
// m.set("b", 2);  // ❌
const s: ReadonlySet<number> = new Set([1, 2]);
// s.add(3);       // ❌

// ============================================================================
// 2. RUNTIME IMMUTABILITY
// ============================================================================

// ---- 2.1 Object.freeze (shallow) ----
const frozen = Object.freeze({ a: 1, b: { c: 2 } });
// frozen.a = 2;        // ❌ (TS + runtime in strict mode)
frozen.b.c = 3;        // ✅ runtime allows (shallow freeze) — pitfall!

// ---- 2.2 Deep freeze helper ----
function deepFreeze<T>(obj: T): Readonly<T> {
  Object.keys(obj as object).forEach((key) => {
    const val = (obj as any)[key];
    if (val && typeof val === "object") deepFreeze(val);
  });
  return Object.freeze(obj) as Readonly<T>;
}

const deepFrozen = deepFreeze({ a: 1, b: { c: 2 } });
// deepFrozen.b.c = 3; // ❌ runtime throws in strict mode

// ---- 2.3 Object.seal (no add/remove, but values mutable) ----
const sealed = Object.seal({ x: 10 });
sealed.x = 20;          // ✅ allowed
// sealed.y = 30;       // ❌ (no new props)

// ============================================================================
// 3. FUNCTIONAL UPDATE PATTERNS (immutable data flow)
// ============================================================================

interface Todo {
  id: number;
  text: string;
  done: boolean;
}

const todos: readonly Todo[] = [
  { id: 1, text: "learn TS", done: false },
  { id: 2, text: "ship it", done: false },
];

// Add
const withNew = [...todos, { id: 3, text: "test", done: false }];

// Toggle (map returns new array)
