## Key takeaways

| Goal | Tool |
|------|------|
| Prevent accidental mutation in your own code | `readonly`, `Readonly<T>` |
| Deep compile-time immutability | Custom `DeepReadonly<T>` |
| Literal + readonly constants | `as const` |
| Prevent mutation at runtime (shallow) | `Object.freeze` |
| Prevent mutation at runtime (deep) | `deepFreeze` helper or Immer |
| Ergonomic nested updates | Immer's `produce` |
| Large collections / perf | Immutable.js, Immer patches |
| Type-safe state flow | Reducer returning new state + spread |

**Golden rule:** TypeScript's `readonly` is **compile-time only** and **shallow**. For real immutability guarantees (especially across module boundaries or untrusted code), combine `DeepReadonly<T>` for types with `Object.freeze`/Immer for runtime.

# Immutability in TypeScript

Immutability means a value, once created, cannot be changed. Instead of mutating, you create a new value from the old one. TypeScript supports immutability at several levels: lexical bindings, object shapes, and generic types.

## 1. Core idea

Mutating shared state makes programs hard to reason about: changes in one place can affect unrelated code, debugging becomes painful, and concurrency / React state bugs appear. Immutable values are “safe to share” because no one can accidentally change them.

```ts
// Mutable: dangerous sharing
const user = { name: "Ada", age: 30 };
const copy = user;
copy.age = 31; // also mutates `user`
console.log(user.age); // 31

// Immutable-ish: create new object
const user2 = { name: "Ada", age: 30 };
const user3 = { ...user2, age: 31 };
console.log(user2.age); // 30
console.log(user3.age); // 31
```

## 2. Syntax

### `const` for bindings

`const` prevents reassignment of the variable, but not mutation of the object it points to.

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.push(4);        // allowed (mutates contents)
// arr = [4, 5];    // error: cannot reassign

const frozen = Object.freeze([1, 2, 3]);
// frozen.push(4);  // type/runtime error-ish
```

### `readonly` for properties

```ts
interface Point { readonly x: number; readonly y: number; }
type ReadonlyPoint = { readonly x: number; readonly y: number };

const p: Point = { x: 1, y: 2 };
// p.x = 3; // error

class Vector {
  constructor(public readonly x: number, public readonly y: number) {}
}
```

### `Readonly<T>` utility type

```ts
type User = { name: string; settings: { theme: "light" | "dark" } };
type ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;

const u: ReadonlyUser = { name: "Ada", settings: { theme: "light" } };
// u.name = "Bob";          // error
// u.settings.theme = "dark"; // NOT error — Readonly is shallow!
```

### `ReadonlyArray<T>` / `readonly T[]`

```ts
const nums: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3];
// nums.push(4);   // error
// nums[0] = 9;    // error

function sum(xs: readonly number[]): number {
  return xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}
```

### Deep readonly (custom)

TypeScript has no built-in deep readonly, but you can define one:

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> = T extends (infer U)[]
  ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[]
  : T extends object
  ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
  : T;

type DeepUser = DeepReadonly<User>;
```

Also modern TS (5.0+) supports `const` type parameters and `as const`:

```ts
const config = { env: "prod", retries: 3 } as const;
// config.env = "dev"; // error; type is { readonly env: "prod"; readonly retries: 3 }
```

## 3. Common immutable update patterns

```ts
// Arrays
const add = (xs: readonly number[], v: number) => [...xs, v];
const removeAt = <T>(xs: readonly T[], i: number) =>
  xs.filter((_, idx) => idx !== i);
const updateAt = <T>(xs: readonly T[], i: number, v: T) =>
  xs.map((x, idx) => (idx === i ? v : x));

// Objects
const rename = (u: User, name: string) => ({ ...u, name });

// Nested updates (verbose — see Immer below)
const setTheme = (u: User, theme: "light" | "dark") => ({
  ...u,
  settings: { ...u.settings, theme },
});
```

## 4. Typical use cases

### React state

React relies on immutability to decide when to re-render. Mutating state in place breaks change detection.

```tsx
const [items, setItems] = useState<string[]>([]);
// bad: items.push("x"); setItems(items);
// good:
setItems(prev => [...prev, "x"]);
```

### Redux / state management

Reducers must be pure and return new state rather than mutate.

```ts
function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "INC": return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };
    default: return state;
  }
}
```

### Functional pipelines / data transforms

Immutability makes `map/filter/reduce` chains predictable and side-effect free.

### Shared constants / config

```ts
export const DEFAULTS = {
  timeout: 5_000,
  retries: 3,
} as const;
```

### Domain models / value objects

Making entities immutable prevents accidental invariant violations; updates return new instances.

## 5. Deep immutability helpers

For large nested updates, use Immer:

```ts
import { produce } from "immer";

const next = produce(state, draft => {
  draft.user.settings.theme = "dark"; // looks mutable, stays immutable
});
```

This gives ergonomic “mutable” syntax while preserving immutability and structural sharing.

## 6. Pitfalls and limits

- `readonly` is compile-time only; JavaScript can still mutate at runtime. Use `Object.freeze` for runtime protection (shallow) when needed.
- `Readonly<T>` is shallow. Nested objects still need deep readonly or discipline.
- Performance: naive deep cloning is costly; prefer structural sharing (spread, Immer).
- APIs expecting mutable arrays: cast carefully with `as` only when you control the callee, e.g. `readonlyArr as number[]` is unsafe if the callee mutates.

## 7. Practical checklist

- Prefer `const` for bindings.
- Mark public properties / params as `readonly` when they shouldn’t change.
- Use `as const` for literal configs.
- Treat arrays/objects as immutable in state code; use spread/functional methods.
- Reach for Immer for deeply nested updates.
- Add `DeepReadonly` aliases for domain models if you want stricter types.

If you want, I can show a small immutable “todo list” module or a React hook example next.

## Mistake: mutating the input object/array in place

```ts
interface User {
  name: string;
  tags: string[];
}

function addTag(user: User, tag: string): User {
  // ❌ Mutates the caller's object and its array
  user.name = user.name.toUpperCase();
  user.tags.push(tag);
  return user;
}

const alice: User = { name: "alice", tags: ["admin"] };
const result = addTag(alice, "beta");

console.log(alice);   // { name: "ALICE", tags: ["admin", "beta"] }  <- surprise!
console.log(result === alice); // true  <- same reference
```

### What's wrong

- `user.name = ...` and `user.tags.push(...)` modify the *original* argument object, not a copy.
- The returned value is the **same reference** as the input (`result === alice`). Callers that compare by reference (e.g., React `useMemo`/`useEffect` deps, Redux selectors, `===` checks) cannot tell anything changed.
- The spread `...user` would also be shallow-only, but here we didn't even do that — we mutated directly.
- Shared mutation is a bug magnet: any other code holding `alice` sees the change unexpectedly, and state-management libraries (Immer, Redux, Zustand) lose the ability to detect changes.

### Corrected version

```ts
interface User {
  name: string;
  tags: string[];
}

function addTag(user: User, tag: string): User {
  // ✅ Produce a new object and a new array; leave the input untouched
  return {
    ...user,
    name: user.name.toUpperCase(),
    tags: [...user.tags, tag],
  };
}

const alice: User = { name: "alice", tags: ["admin"] };
const result = addTag(alice, "beta");

console.log(alice);            // { name: "alice", tags: ["admin"] }  (unchanged)
console.log(result);           // { name: "ALICE", tags: ["admin", "beta"] }
console.log(result === alice); // false  (new reference)
```

### Why this is right

1. **No input mutation** — `alice` stays as the caller gave it. This makes the function pure and predictable.
2. **New reference** — `{ ...user, ... }` creates a fresh object; `[...user.tags, tag]` creates a fresh array. Reference equality now correctly reports "changed."
3. **Shallow copy is enough here** because `name` and `tags` are the only fields, and primitives/arrays are handled explicitly. For deeply nested data you'd recurse or use a library (Immer, `structuredClone`).
4. **Order of operations is safe** — we read from `user` while building the new object, never writing back.

### Extra tip: enforce it at the type level

```ts
function addTag(user: Readonly<User>, tag: string): User {
  return {
    ...user,
    name: user.name.toUpperCase(),
    tags: [...user.tags, tag],
  };
}
```

`Readonly<User>` makes `user.name = ...` and `user.tags.push(...)` compile-time errors, catching the mistake before it ships. (Note: `Readonly` is shallow; for deep immutability use `as const` or a library like `immer`.)

### Bonus: the "update one item in an array" variant

```ts
// ❌ Mistake
function rename(users: User[], id: number, name: string) {
  users[id].name = name; // mutates
  return users;
}

// ✅ Correct
function rename(users: readonly User[], id: number, name: string): User[] {
  return users.map((u, i) =>
    i === id ? { ...u, name } : u
  );
}
```

The pattern is always the same: **never write to the input; return a new structure.**

## Common immutability pitfalls in TypeScript

Below are the most common pitfalls, grouped from “compile-time only” issues to runtime mutation leaks. Each has a short explanation and runnable TypeScript code.

---

### 1. `readonly` is shallow

`readonly` only protects the top level. Nested objects remain mutable.

```ts
type User = {
  readonly name: string;
  address: { city: string };
};

const u: User = { name: "Ada", address: { city: "London" } };

u.name = "Grace";        // ❌ compile error
u.address.city = "Paris"; // ✅ allowed — nested mutation leaks
```

**Fix:** use a recursive `DeepReadonly` type (see pitfall 6).

---

### 2. `const` does not mean “immutable value”

`const` prevents reassignment of the binding, not mutation of the value.

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.push(4);        // ✅ allowed
arr[0] = 99;        // ✅ allowed
// arr = [9, 9, 9]; // ❌ compile error
```

---

### 3. Spread copies are shallow

Spreading creates a new top-level object, but nested references are shared.

```ts
const original = { nested: { x: 1 } };
const copy = { ...original };

copy.nested.x = 2;
console.log(original.nested.x); // 2 — original mutated
```

**Fix:** deep-clone when needed (`structuredClone`, `lodash.cloneDeep`, or immutable update helpers).

---

### 4. `Object.freeze` is shallow

`Object.freeze` only freezes the top level.

```ts
const obj = Object.freeze({ nested: { x: 1 } });

obj.nested.x = 2; // ✅ allowed at runtime
console.log(obj.nested.x); // 2
```

**Fix:** recursively freeze nested objects, or use a library.

```ts
function deepFreeze<T>(value: T): Readonly<T> {
  if (value && typeof value === "object") {
    Object.freeze(value);
    for (const key of Object.keys(value as object)) {
      deepFreeze((value as any)[key]);
    }
  }
  return value as Readonly<T>;
}
```

---

### 5. Readonly types are compile-time only

TypeScript readonly annotations disappear at runtime. Casts can bypass them.

```ts
const r: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3];

(r as number[]).push(4); // compiles, mutates at runtime
console.log(r);          // [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

**Fix:** combine readonly types with runtime freezing or immutable data structures.

---

### 6. `Readonly<T>` is shallow

`Readonly<T>` only makes direct properties readonly.

```ts
type Person = { name: string; tags: string[] };
type R = Readonly<Person>;

const p: R = { name: "Ada", tags: ["x"] };
p.tags.push("y"); // ✅ allowed — nested array mutable
```

**Fix:** define a recursive deep-readonly type.

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (infer U)[]
    ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[]
    : T extends object
      ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
      : T;

type DeepPerson = DeepReadonly<Person>;
const dp: DeepPerson = { name: "Ada", tags: ["x"] };
// dp.tags.push("y"); // ❌ compile error
```

> Caveat: recursive types can struggle with functions, `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, or circular structures. Add special cases as needed.

---

### 7. `as const` creates readonly tuples, which may not fit mutable APIs

`as const` infers narrow readonly types. Passing them to APIs expecting mutable arrays can fail.

```ts
const tuple = [1, 2, 3] as const; // readonly [1, 2, 3]

function takeMutable(arr: number[]): void {}
// takeMutable(tuple); // ❌ readonly tuple not assignable to number[]

takeMutable([...tuple]); // ✅ spread into a mutable copy
```

---

### 8. Readonly arrays lack mutating methods

`readonly T[]` has no `push`, `pop`, `splice`, etc. Code that assumes mutation must be rewritten.

```ts
const r: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3];

// r.push(4);     // ❌ Property 'push' does not exist
const next = [...r, 4]; // ✅ non-mutating
```

---

### 9. Class `readonly` fields still reference mutable objects

`readonly` on a class property prevents reassignment, not deep mutation.

```ts
class Box {
  readonly items: string[] = [];
}

const b = new Box();
b.items.push("mutated"); // ✅ allowed
// b.items = ["x"];      // ❌ compile error
```

**Fix:** freeze in constructor or use `DeepReadonly`.

```ts
class Box {
  readonly items: readonly string[];
  constructor(items: string[]) {
    this.items = Object.freeze([...items]);
  }
}
```

---

### 10. `ReadonlyMap` / `ReadonlySet` are also shallow and type-level only

They prevent mutating methods at the type level, but the underlying collection can still be mutated through another reference or cast.

```ts
const m: ReadonlyMap<string, { x: number }> = new Map([["a", { x: 1 }]]);

// m.set("b", { x: 2 }); // ❌ compile error
const inner = m.get("a")!;
inner.x = 99;            // ✅ nested value mutated

(m as Map<string, { x: number }>).set("b", { x: 2 }); // runtime mutation
```

---

### 11. `Object.assign` mutates its target

A common accidental mutation: passing an existing object as the first argument.

```ts
const base = { a: 1 };
const out = Object.assign(base, { b: 2 });

console.log(base); // { a: 1, b: 2 } — base mutated
console.log(out === base); // true
```

**Fix:** use an empty target.

```ts
const out = Object.assign({}, base, { b: 2 });
```

---

### 12. State updates in UI frameworks often require new references

Frameworks like React compare references. Mutating nested state and “setting” the same reference won’t trigger updates.

```ts
type State = { user: { name: string } };

function badUpdate(prev: State): State {
  prev.user.name = "Bob"; // mutates previous state
  return prev;            // same reference → no re-render
}

function goodUpdate(prev: State): State {
  return { ...prev, user: { ...prev.user, name: "Bob" } };
}
```

---

### 13. Type assertions can silently remove readonly

`as`, `as any`, or non-null assertions can strip readonly guarantees.

```ts
type Config = { readonly apiKey: string };
const cfg: Config = { apiKey: "secret" };

const mutable = cfg as { apiKey: string };
mutable.apiKey = "leaked"; // compiles
```

**Fix:** avoid casts that widen mutability; prefer `satisfies` or precise types.

---

### 14. Deep-clone helpers can break special objects

`structuredClone` and JSON cloning do not preserve functions, `Date` (JSON turns into string), `Map`, `Set`, prototypes, or circular refs in some cases.

```ts
const original = { created: new Date(), fn: () => 1 };
const cloned = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));

console.log(typeof cloned.created); // "string"
console.log(typeof cloned.fn);      // "undefined"
```

**Fix:** use `structuredClone` where supported, or domain-specific clone functions.

---

### 15. Immutable updates can become O(n) or O(size) accidentally

Naively copying large arrays/objects on every update can cause performance issues.

```ts
// Every toggle copies the whole array
function toggle(items: readonly string[], id: string): readonly string[] {
  return items.map((x) => (x === id ? `!${x}` : x));
}
```

**Fix:** for large/complex state, consider persistent data structures (Immer, Immutable.js) or normalized state.

---

### Recommended practical checklist

- Treat `readonly` / `Readonly<T>` as **shallow** by default.
- Use `const` for bindings, but never assume the value is immutable.
- Prefer non-mutating array/object methods: `map`, `filter`, `reduce`, spread.
- Freeze at runtime only when boundaries require it; remember it’s shallow.
- Use Immer or a recursive deep-readonly type for nested state.
- Avoid type assertions that reintroduce mutability.
- Test immutability assumptions with runtime checks when crossing trust boundaries.

## Exercise

You have this mutable TypeScript code:

```ts
interface Post {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  tags: string[];
}

function addTag(post: Post, tag: string): Post {
  post.tags.push(tag); // mutates the original post
  return post;
}
```

**Task:** Refactor `addTag` so that:

1. The original `post` is never mutated.
2. A new `Post` is returned.
3. TypeScript helps prevent accidental mutation through readonly types.
4. Adding an already-existing tag does not create a duplicate.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Make the interface readonly

Use `readonly` for properties and `readonly string[]` for the tags array. This makes TypeScript reject direct mutation such as `post.tags.push(...)`.

```ts
interface Post {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly tags: readonly string[];
}
```

### Step 2: Return a new object instead of mutating

Instead of changing `post`, create a shallow copy with the spread operator:

```ts
return {
  ...post,
  tags: [...post.tags, tag],
};
```

`...post` copies the top-level properties, and `[...post.tags, tag]` creates a new array.

### Step 3: Avoid duplicate tags

Check whether the tag already exists before adding it. If it exists, return the original post unchanged.

```ts
if (post.tags.includes(tag)) {
  return post;
}
```

### Step 4: Optional runtime protection

TypeScript readonly types are compile-time only. If you also want runtime protection, freeze the returned object:

```ts
return Object.freeze({
  ...post,
  tags: Object.freeze([...post.tags, tag]),
});
```

This is useful in libraries or shared state, but may be unnecessary in normal application code.

---

## Final solution

```ts
interface Post {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly tags: readonly string[];
}

function addTag(post: Post, tag: string): Post {
  if (post.tags.includes(tag)) {
    return post;
  }

  return {
    ...post,
    tags: [...post.tags, tag],
  };
}

// Example usage
const original: Post = {
  id: 1,
  title: "Immutability in TypeScript",
  tags: ["typescript", "functional"],
};

const updated = addTag(original, "immutability");

console.log(original.tags); // ["typescript", "functional"]
console.log(updated.tags);  // ["typescript", "functional", "immutability"]
```

### Why this is better

- **No mutation:** `original` remains unchanged.
- **Type safety:** `readonly` prevents accidental `push`, `pop`, or property assignment.
- **Predictable updates:** Each update creates a new value, making state changes easier to track.
- **No duplicates:** Re-adding the same tag returns the same logical result.

## Immutability in TypeScript

Immutability means: **once a value is created, it never changes**. Updates don’t mutate the original; they return a **new** value. In TypeScript this has two layers:

1. **Compile-time (types)**: `readonly`, `Readonly<T>`, `as const`, `satisfies`. These catch accidental mutation during development but disappear at runtime.
2. **Runtime (values)**: JavaScript objects are mutable by default, so we use `Object.freeze` (shallow) or deep-freeze, plus **persistent data structures** that share unchanged parts (structural sharing) instead of copying everything.

Key pitfalls:
- `const` freezes the *binding*, not the object.
- `readonly` is erased at runtime.
- `Object.freeze` is shallow.
- Array methods like `push`, `splice`, `sort` mutate in place.

---

## Core implementation

Below is a small, self-contained “core” of immutability in TS: type-level deep readonly, runtime deep freeze, and immutable update helpers.

```ts
// =========================
// 1) TYPE-LEVEL IMMUTABILITY
// =========================

/**
 * Recursively marks every property/array element as readonly.
 * Functions are left as-is (calling them isn't "mutation" of data).
 */
export type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (...args: any[]) => any
    ? T
    : T extends (infer E)[]
    ? ReadonlyArray<DeepReadonly<E>>
    : T extends object
    ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
    : T;

// Example usage:
// const cfg: DeepReadonly<{ db: { host: string; ports: number[] } }> = { ... };

// =========================
// 2) RUNTIME DEEP FREEZE
// =========================

/**
 * Recursively freezes an object/array (and Map/Set entries).
 * Returns the same reference, now frozen. Safe to call twice.
 */
export function deepFreeze<T>(value: T): Readonly<T> {
  // Primitives / null are already immutable.
  if (value === null || typeof value !== "object") {
    return value as Readonly<T>;
  }

  // Already frozen? Skip to avoid redundant work.
  if (Object.isFrozen(value)) {
    return value as Readonly<T>;
  }

  // Freeze this level first.
  Object.freeze(value);

  // Freeze nested plain objects / arrays.
  for (const key of Object.keys(value as object)) {
    deepFreeze((value as Record<string, unknown>)[key]);
  }

  // Extra: handle Map and Set (Object.keys won't list their entries).
  if (value instanceof Map) {
    for (const [k, v] of value) {
      deepFreeze(k);
      deepFreeze(v);
    }
  }
  if (value instanceof Set) {
    for (const v of value) {
      deepFreeze(v);
    }
  }

  return value as Readonly<T>;
}

// =========================
// 3) IMMUTABLE UPDATE HELPERS
// =========================

/**
 * Returns a NEW object with one property replaced.
 * The original is never touched.
 */
export function setProp<T extends object, K extends keyof T>(
  obj: T,
  key: K,
  value: T[K]
): T {
  // Shallow copy + override. Structural sharing for all other keys.
  return { ...obj, [key]: value } as T;
}

/**
 * Returns a NEW array with one index replaced.
 * (No mutation of the source array.)
 */
export function setIndex<T>(arr: readonly T[], index: number, value: T): T[] {
  if (index < 0 || index >= arr.length) {
    throw new RangeError(`Index ${index} out of bounds`);
  }
  // Copy only the prefix; share the suffix via slice.
  return [...arr.slice(0, index), value, ...arr.slice(index + 1)];
}

/**
 * Immutable "push": returns a new array with item appended.
 */
export function append<T>(arr: readonly T[], item: T): T[] {
  return [...arr, item];
}

/**
 * Immutable nested update by path.
 * Example: updatePath(user, ["address", "zip"], 90210)
 */
export function updatePath<T extends object>(
  obj: T,
  path: (string | number)[],
  value: unknown
): T {
  if (path.length === 0) return obj;

  const [head, ...rest] = path;

  if (Array.isArray(obj)) {
    const idx = Number(head);
    const next = rest.length === 0 ? value : updatePath(obj[idx] as any, rest, value);
    return setIndex(obj as any, idx, next) as T;
  }

  const current = (obj as Record<string, unknown>)[head as string];
  const next =
    rest.length === 0 ? value : updatePath(current as any, rest, value);

  return setProp(obj, head as keyof T, next as any);
}

// =========================
// 4) USAGE DEMO
// =========================

interface User {
  name: string;
  tags: string[];
  address: { city: string; zip: number };
}

const user: User = {
  name: "Ada",
  tags: ["ts", "fp"],
  address: { city: "London", zip: 10001 },
};

// Type-level: DeepReadonly<User> prevents mutation at compile time.
const frozenUser = deepFreeze(user);

// Runtime: these would throw in strict mode (or silently fail otherwise).
// frozenUser.name = "Bob";            // ❌ TS error + runtime freeze
// frozenUser.tags.push("x");          // ❌ TS error + runtime freeze

// Immutable updates produce new values; original unchanged.
const renamed = setProp(frozenUser, "name", "Grace");
const tagged = append(frozenUser.tags, "immutable");
const moved = updatePath(frozenUser, ["address", "zip"], 90210);

console.log("original zip:", frozenUser.address.zip); // 10001 (unchanged)
console.log("new zip:     ", moved.address.zip);     // 90210
```

### Why this is the “core”
- **Types** give developer-time safety (`DeepReadonly`).
- **deepFreeze** gives runtime safety (catches bugs in non-TS code, JSON, third-party libs).
- **Helpers** (`setProp`, `setIndex`, `append`, `updatePath`) implement the *functional update* pattern with **structural sharing**—only the changed path is copied; the rest of the tree is shared.

For large apps, you’d replace the helpers with a library like **Immer** (proxy-based mutable draft → immutable result) or **Immutable.js** (persistent collections), but the principles above are exactly what those libraries build on.

Below are the main edge cases when using immutability in TypeScript, grouped by where they occur (type system vs runtime), with examples.

---

## 1. `readonly` is shallow

`readonly` only prevents reassignment of the immediate property. Nested objects remain mutable.

```ts
type User = {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly address: { city: string; zip: number };
};

const u: User = {
  name: "Ada",
  address: { city: "NYC", zip: 10001 },
};

// Error: cannot assign to 'name'
// u.name = "Grace";

// Allowed: nested object is still mutable
u.address.city = "Boston";
u.address.zip = 2108;
```

**Fix:** use a recursive `DeepReadonly<T>` type (see section 4).

---

## 2. `const` variables vs `readonly` properties

`const` prevents rebinding a variable, not mutation of its value.

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.push(4);        // allowed
arr[0] = 99;        // allowed
// arr = [4, 5, 6]; // error

type Box = { readonly value: number };
const b: Box = { value: 1 };
// b.value = 2;      // error
```

For true immutability you usually need **both**: a `const`/`readonly` binding or property **and** runtime freezing.

---

## 3. Compile-time readonly does not protect runtime

TypeScript types are erased. A `readonly` annotation can be bypassed with casts.

```ts
type Point = { readonly x: number; readonly y: number };

const p = { x: 1, y: 2 } as Point;

// Type error, but runtime allows it if bypassed
(p as any).x = 999;
(p as unknown as { x: number }).x = 999;

console.log(p.x); // 999
```

**Fix at boundaries:** freeze data when it enters untrusted code.

```ts
function makePoint(x: number, y: number): Point {
  return Object.freeze({ x, y });
}
```

---

## 4. Deep immutability types: edge cases

A common `DeepReadonly` implementation:

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (infer U)[] ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[] :
  T extends readonly (infer U)[] ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[] :
  T extends Map<infer K, infer V> ? ReadonlyMap<DeepReadonly<K>, DeepReadonly<V>> :
  T extends Set<infer U> ? ReadonlySet<DeepReadonly<U>> :
  T extends Function ? T :
  T extends Date | RegExp | Promise<any> ? T :
  T extends object ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> } :
  T;
```

Important edge cases:

### 4.1 Tuples become arrays

```ts
type T = DeepReadonly<[number, string, boolean]>;
// Often becomes: readonly (number | string | boolean)[]
// Tuple length/positions may be lost depending on implementation.
```

For literals, prefer `as const`:

```ts
const t = [1, "a", true] as const;
// type: readonly [1, "a", true]
```

### 4.2 Optional properties

A homomorphic mapped type usually preserves optionality:

```ts
type A = { readonly x?: number };
type B = DeepReadonly<A>;
// x?: ... remains optional in most implementations
```

But if you rebuild objects manually, be careful not to drop `?`.

### 4.3 Unions and distribution

Conditional types distribute over unions. This can produce strange results:

```ts
type X = DeepReadonly<string | { a: number }>;
// string | { readonly a: number }
```

Usually fine, but if you want to avoid distribution, wrap it:

```ts
type DeepReadonlyNoDist<T> =
  [T] extends [infer U] ? /* ... */ : never;
```

### 4.4 Functions, Dates, Maps, Sets

Do not recursively convert functions or class instances into readonly property bags.

```ts
type Bad = DeepReadonly<Date>;
// Without a special case, Date becomes { readonly getTime: ...; ... }
```

That is why the implementation above returns `Date`, `RegExp`, `Function`, and `Promise` as-is.

### 4.5 ReadonlyMap / ReadonlySet are not frozen

```ts
const m: ReadonlyMap<string, number> = new Map([["a", 1]]);
// Type-level read-only map, but runtime Map methods still exist if you cast
(m as Map<string, number>).set("a", 2);
```

---

## 5. `Object.freeze` is shallow and type-level only partially

```ts
const obj = Object.freeze({
  nested: { value: 1 },
});

// Type: Readonly<{ nested: { value: number } }>
// nested is NOT readonly at type level in many lib definitions

obj.nested.value = 2; // allowed by TS in some versions, always allowed at runtime
```

### 5.1 Deep freeze implementation

A robust-ish deep freeze must handle objects, arrays, circular references, symbols, and special objects.

```ts
function deepFreeze<T>(value: T, seen = new WeakSet<object>()): T {
  if (value === null || typeof value !== "object") return value;

  const obj = value as object;
  if (seen.has(obj)) return value; // circular reference
  seen.add(obj);

  Object.freeze(obj);

  if (Array.isArray(obj)) {
    for (const item of obj) deepFreeze(item, seen);
    return value;
  }

  if (obj instanceof Map) {
    for (const [k, v] of obj) {
      deepFreeze(k, seen);
      deepFreeze(v, seen);
    }
    // Note: Object.freeze does not prevent Map.prototype.set at runtime.
    return value;
  }

  if (obj instanceof Set) {
    for (const v of obj) deepFreeze(v, seen);
    return value;
  }

  if (obj instanceof Date || obj instanceof RegExp) {
    // Object.freeze does not stop setDate/setTime; clone if true immutability is needed.
    return value;
  }

  // Includes symbol keys
  for (const key of Reflect.ownKeys(obj)) {
    deepFreeze((obj as any)[key], seen);
  }

  return value;
}
```

Edge cases:
- Freezing `Date` does not prevent `date.setDate(...)` because the internal slot is still mutable.
- Freezing `Map`/`Set` does not prevent `.set()`/`.add()`.
- Functions can be frozen, but freezing may break methods that rely on internal state.
- Non-extensible/sealed objects may throw in strict mode when assigning; deep freeze should not assume mutability.

### 5.2 Deep clone + freeze

For true runtime immutability, clone first, then freeze:

```ts
function immutableClone<T>(value: T): DeepReadonly<T> {
  // structuredClone does not clone functions, DOM nodes, errors, etc.
  const clone = structuredClone(value);
  return deepFreeze(clone) as DeepReadonly<T>;
}
```

---

## 6. Arrays and tuples

### 6.1 Runtime arrays are always mutable

```ts
const arr: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3];

// Type errors:
// arr.push(4);
// arr[0] = 9;

// Runtime escape hatch:
(arr as number[]).push(4); // mutates original
```

### 6.2 Readonly arrays are covariant

Mutable arrays are invariant; readonly arrays are covariant. This is a major reason to use readonly arrays in generic code.

```ts
interface Animal { name: string }
interface Dog extends Animal { bark(): void }

const dogs: Dog[] = [];
// const animals: Animal[] = dogs; // Error: mutable arrays are invariant

const roDogs: readonly Dog[] = dogs;
const roAnimals: readonly Animal[] = roDogs; // OK: readonly arrays are covariant
```

### 6.3 Mutating methods are excluded from readonly arrays

```ts
const arr = [3, 1, 2] as const;

// arr.sort();   // type error
// arr.reverse(); // type error
// arr.push(4);  // type error

// Non-mutating alternatives:
const sorted = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);
const reversed = arr.toReversed(); // ES2023
const withNew = arr.toSpliced(1, 1, 99); // ES2023
```

But runtime still allows mutation through casts.

### 6.4 Tuple methods and length

```ts
const t: readonly [number, number] = [1, 2];

// t[0] = 3; // error
// t.push(3); // error

const mutable = t as [number, number, ...number[]];
mutable.push(3); // mutates original tuple
```

---

## 7. Special objects

### 7.1 `Date` is internally mutable

```ts
const d = new Date("2024-01-01");
Object.freeze(d);

d.setFullYear(2025); // still works in many engines
```

Treat dates as immutable by cloning:

```ts
function addDays(date: Date, days: number): Date {
  const copy = new Date(date);
  copy.setDate(copy.getDate() + days);
  return copy;
}
```

Or store timestamps/ISO strings instead of `Date`.

### 7.2 `Map` and `Set`

`Object.freeze` does not prevent `.set()` or `.add()`:

```ts
const m = Object.freeze(new Map([["a", 1]]));
m.set("a", 2); // works
```

Options:
- Use `ReadonlyMap` / `ReadonlySet` for type-level safety.
- Replace entries by creating new collections.
- Use Immer or a proxy if runtime enforcement is needed.

```ts
function setImmutable<K, V>(m: ReadonlyMap<K, V>, key: K, value: V): ReadonlyMap<K, V> {
  const next = new Map(m);
  next.set(key, value);
  return next;
}
```

### 7.3 Classes and instances

`readonly` class fields can only be assigned in the constructor, but instances can still be mutated through methods.

```ts
class Counter {
  readonly value: number = 0;

  increment() {
    (this as any).value++;
  }
}
```

Deep freezing an instance can break methods that assign to `this`. If you need immutable domain objects, prefer plain data + pure functions.

```ts
type CounterState = { readonly value: number };
function increment(s: CounterState): CounterState {
  return { value: s.value + 1 };
}
```

### 7.4 Functions

Freezing a function object freezes its own properties, but not closures or prototype methods in a useful way. Avoid treating functions as immutable data containers.

---

## 8. Prototypes, descriptors, and symbols

### 8.1 `Object.freeze` does not freeze the prototype

```ts
const obj = Object.freeze({ a: 1 });
const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(obj);

// Prototype may still be mutable
proto.b = 2;
```

Freezing prototypes is usually wrong/dangerous; instead avoid prototype-based mutation in immutable code.

### 8.2 Accessor properties

Frozen objects with getters/setters can still mutate through setters.

```ts
const obj = Object.freeze({
  _x: 1,
  get x() { return this._x; },
  set x(v: number) { this._x = v; }, // freeze makes it non-configurable,
});                                    // but setter may still be callable

obj.x = 2; // may still work depending on strictness/implementation
```

### 8.3 Non-enumerable and symbol properties

Spread and `Object.keys` miss non-enumerable and symbol-keyed properties. Deep freeze should use `Reflect.ownKeys`.

```ts
const hidden = Symbol("hidden");

const obj = {
  a: 1,
  [hidden]: { b: 2 },
};

Object.defineProperty(obj, "secret", {
  value: { c: 3 },
  enumerable: false,
});

// Object.keys misses "secret" and symbol key
// Reflect.ownKeys captures all own keys
```

---

## 9. References, aliasing, and escape hatches

Immutability fails when the same object is reachable through multiple references and one path is mutable.

```ts
type User = { readonly name: string };
const original = { name: "Ada" } as User;

const alias: any = original;
alias.name = "Grace";

console.log(original.name); // Grace
```

### Common escape hatches

```ts
// 1. any
const x = obj as any;

// 2. unknown cast
const y = obj as unknown as Mutable<T>;

// 3. Non-null / indexed access hacks
obj["name" as any] = "Grace";

// 4. Delete/add properties
delete (obj as any).name;
```

At runtime, only proxies or actual frozen objects can resist these.

---

## 10. Proxy-based runtime immutability

A proxy can throw on mutation, but nested values must also be proxied.

```ts
function immutable<T extends object>(target: T, cache = new WeakMap()): T {
  if (cache.has(target)) return cache.get(target)!;

  const proxy = new Proxy(target, {
    set() {
      throw new Error("Cannot mutate immutable object");
    },
    deleteProperty() {
      throw new Error("Cannot delete property");
    },
    get(target, prop, receiver) {
      const value = Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);
      if (value && typeof value === "object") {
        return immutable(value, cache);
      }
      return value;
    },
  });

  cache.set(target, proxy);
  return proxy;
}
```

Edge cases:
- Returns a new proxy on every `get`, which can break `===` identity unless cached.
- Array methods like `push` internally call `set`, so they throw.
- `this` binding can be wrong for methods.
- Proxies add runtime overhead.
- Not all objects are proxyable cleanly (e.g. some host objects).

---

## 11. Assignability and generics

### 11.1 Functions expecting mutable arrays reject readonly arrays

```ts
function append(arr: string[], value: string) {
  arr.push(value);
}

const ro: readonly string[] = ["a"];
// append(ro, "b"); // Error
```

Fix: accept readonly input and return a new array.

```ts
function append(arr: readonly string[], value: string): readonly string[] {
  return [...arr, value];
}
```

### 11.2 `Readonly<T>` is shallow in generics too

```ts
function update<T extends object>(obj: Readonly<T>, patch: Partial<T>): T {
  return { ...obj, ...patch } as T;
}
```

This only shallow-copies. Nested updates require deep clone/spread or Immer.

---

## 12. React / state management edge cases

### 12.1 Mutating nested state does not trigger updates

```ts
type State = {
  readonly user: { readonly name: string };
};

function badUpdate(prev: State): State {
  (prev.user as any).name = "Grace"; // mutation
  return prev; // same reference, React may skip render
}
```

Correct:

```ts
function goodUpdate(prev: State): State {
  return {
    ...prev,
    user: { ...prev.user, name: "Grace" },
  };
}
```

### 12.2 Shallow spread is not enough

```ts
const next = { ...state };
next.nested.value = 2; // still mutates original nested object
```

Use deep spread or Immer.

### 12.3 Immer with readonly types

Immer drafts are mutable, but readonly types can make TypeScript complain.

```ts
import { produce } from "immer";

type State = { readonly items: readonly string[] };

const next = produce(state, draft => {
  // draft is mutable at runtime, but may be typed as readonly
  draft.items.push("x"); // type error in some setups
});
```

Often you need `WritableDraft` or cast the draft:

```ts
import { produce, WritableDraft } from "immer";

const next = produce(state, (draft: WritableDraft<State>) => {
  draft.items.push("x");
});
```

---

## 13. JSON, serialization, and cloning

### 13.1 `structuredClone` does not clone everything

```ts
const obj = {
  date: new Date(),
  fn: () => 1,
  el: document?.body,
};

const clone = structuredClone(obj);
// clone.fn is undefined
// clone.el may throw or be null depending on environment
```

### 13.2 Frozen objects serialize fine, but revivers may mutate

```ts
const frozen = Object.freeze({ a: { b: 1 } });
const json = JSON.stringify(frozen);
const revived = JSON.parse(json); // mutable again
```

If you need immutability after parsing, freeze again.

---

## 14. Practical recommended pattern

For robust immutability in TypeScript:

```ts
// 1. Type-level deep readonly
type DeepReadonly<T> = /* ... as above ... */;

// 2. Runtime deep freeze after cloning
function toImmutable<T>(value: T): DeepReadonly<T> {
  const clone = structuredClone(value);
  return deepFreeze(clone) as DeepReadonly<T>;
}

// 3. Pure updates
function updateName(user: DeepReadonly<User>, name: string): DeepReadonly<User> {
  return {
    ...user,
    name,
  } as DeepReadonly<User>;
}

// 4. Function signatures accept readonly inputs
function total(items: readonly number[]): number {
  return items.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}
```

### Rule of thumb

- Use `readonly` / `Readonly<T>` for **local, compile-time guarantees**.
- Use `as const` for **literal immutable values**.
- Use `Object.freeze` / deep freeze for **runtime boundaries**.
- Use deep readonly types for **nested data**.
- Use pure functions / spread / Immer for **updates**.
- Remember that casts, `any`, prototypes, and special objects (`Date`, `Map`, `Set`) can all break immutability.

Below is a complete, dependency-free TypeScript implementation of three persistent/immutable collections:

- `ImmutableList<T>` — persistent cons list (structural sharing)
- `ImmutableMap<K, V>` — persistent hash trie (path copying)
- `ImmutableSet<T>` — implemented on top of `ImmutableMap`

The example includes a runnable test harness with assertions.

## Design notes

- **Immutability**: every update returns a new collection; old values are never mutated.
- **Structural sharing**: unchanged branches/lists are reused.
- **Persistence**: the hash map uses a 32-way trie (`32 = 2^5`), giving roughly `O(log₃₂ n)` get/set/delete.
- **Keys**: primitives use value equality; objects/functions/symbols use identity by default. Custom `hash` and `equals` can be supplied.
- **Stored `undefined` values**: `has()` correctly distinguishes “missing key” from “stored `undefined`”.

## `immutable.ts`

```ts
// immutable.ts
// Run with: npx ts-node immutable.ts
// or: npx tsc immutable.ts --target es2020 --module commonjs && node immutable.js

/* =========================
   Shared helpers
   ========================= */

function stringHash(value: string): number {
  let hash = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < value.length; i++) {
    hash = (Math.imul(hash, 31) + value.charCodeAt(i)) | 0;
  }
  return hash;
}

const objectIds = new WeakMap<object, number>();
let nextObjectId = 1;

function defaultHash<K>(key: K): number {
  switch (typeof key) {
    case 'string':
      return stringHash(key as string);
    case 'number': {
      const n = key as number;
      if (Number.isNaN(n)) return 0x7fc00000 | 0;
      return Math.imul((n | 0) ^ 0x9e3779b9, 2246822519) | 0;
    }
    case 'boolean':
      return (key as boolean) ? 1231 : 1237;
    case 'bigint':
      return stringHash(String(key));
    case 'symbol':
      return stringHash(String(key));
    case 'undefined':
      return 1;
    case 'function':
    case 'object': {
      if (key === null) return 0;
      const obj = key as object;
      let id = objectIds.get(obj);
      if (id === undefined) {
        id = nextObjectId++;
        objectIds.set(obj, id);
      }
      return id | 0;
    }
    default:
      return stringHash(String(key));
  }
}

function defaultEquals<K>(a: K, b: K): boolean {
  return Object.is(a, b);
}

/* =========================
   ImmutableList
   ========================= */

interface ListNode<T> {
  readonly value: T;
  readonly tail: ListNode<T> | null;
}

export class ImmutableList<T> {
  private constructor(
    private readonly node: ListNode<T> | null,
    private readonly cachedLength: number,
  ) {}

  static empty<T>(): ImmutableList<T> {
    return new ImmutableList<T>(null, 0);
  }

  static fromArray<T>(items: readonly T[]): ImmutableList<T> {
    let list = ImmutableList.empty<T>();
    for (const item of items) list = list.cons(item);
    return list.reverse();
  }

  get isEmpty(): boolean {
    return this.node === null;
  }

  get length(): number {
    return this.cachedLength;
  }

  get head(): T {
    if (!this.node) throw new Error('head of an empty list');
    return this.node.value;
  }

  get tail(): ImmutableList<T> {
    if (!this.node) throw new Error('tail of an empty list');
    return new ImmutableList<T>(this.node.tail, this.cachedLength - 1);
  }

  cons(value: T): ImmutableList<T> {
    return new ImmutableList<T>(
      { value, tail: this.node },
      this.cachedLength + 1,
    );
  }

  prepend(value: T): ImmutableList<T> {
    return this.cons(value);
  }

  append(value: T): ImmutableList<T> {
    if (this.isEmpty) return this.cons(value);

    const newTail = this.tail.append(value);
    return new ImmutableList<T>(
      { value: this.head, tail: newTail.node },
      this.cachedLength + 1,
    );
  }

  concat(other: ImmutableList<T>): ImmutableList<T> {
    if (this.isEmpty) return other;
    if (other.isEmpty) return this;

    let result = other;
    const items = this.toArray();
    for (let i = items.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      result = result.cons(items[i]);
    }
    return result;
  }

  reverse(): ImmutableList<T> {
    let acc = ImmutableList.empty<T>();
    for (const item of this) acc = acc.cons(item);
    return acc;
  }

  map<U>(fn: (value: T) => U): ImmutableList<U> {
    const out: U[] = [];
    for (const item of this) out.push(fn(item));
    return ImmutableList.fromArray(out);
  }

  filter(predicate: (value: T) => boolean): ImmutableList<T> {
    const out: T[] = [];
    for (const item of this) {
      if (predicate(item)) out.push(item);
    }
    return ImmutableList.fromArray(out);
  }

  reduce<U>(fn: (accumulator: U, value: T) => U, initial: U): U {
    let acc = initial;
    for (const item of this) acc = fn(acc, item);
    return acc;
  }

  nth(index: number): T | undefined {
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.cachedLength) return undefined;
    let current = this.node;
    for (let i = 0; i < index && current; i++) {
      current = current.tail;
    }
    return current?.value;
  }

  set(index: number, value: T): ImmutableList<T> {
    if (!Number.isInteger(index) || index < 0 || index >= this.cachedLength) {
      throw new RangeError(`Index ${index} is out of bounds`);
    }
    const items = this.toArray();
    items[index] = value;
    return ImmutableList.fromArray(items);
  }

  toArray(): T[] {
    const out: T[] = [];
    for (const item of this) out.push(item);
    return out;
  }

  forEach(callback: (value: T, index: number, list: ImmutableList<T>) => void): void {
    let index = 0;
    for (const item of this) callback(item, index++, this);
  }

  [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<T> {
    let current: ListNode<T> | null = this.node;
    return {
      next(): IteratorResult<T> {
        if (current === null) return { done: true, value: undefined };
        const value = current.value;
        current = current.tail;
        return { done: false, value };
      },
    };
  }
}

/* =========================
   ImmutableMap (persistent hash trie)
   ========================= */

export interface MapOptions<K> {
  hash?: (key: K) => number;
  equals?: (a: K, b: K) => boolean;
}

type TrieEntry<K, V> = {
  readonly hash: number;
  readonly key: K;
  readonly value: V;
};

type TrieNode<K, V> =
  | { readonly kind: 'leaf'; readonly hash: number; readonly key: K; readonly value: V }
  | { readonly kind: 'collision'; readonly entries: readonly TrieEntry<K, V>[] }
  | { readonly kind: 'branch'; readonly children: readonly (TrieNode<K, V> | undefined)[] };

const BRANCHING = 32;
const MASK = 31;

function idx(hash: number, shift: number): number {
  return (hash >>> shift) & MASK;
}

function emptyBranch<K, V>(): TrieNode<K, V> & { kind: 'branch' } {
  return {
    kind: 'branch',
    children: new Array<TrieNode<K, V> | undefined>(BRANCHING),
  };
}

function setNode<K, V>(
  node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined,
  hash: number,
  key: K,
  value: V,
  shift: number,
  equals: (a: K, b: K) => boolean,
): { node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined; added: boolean } {
  if (!node) {
    return { node: { kind: 'leaf', hash, key, value }, added: true };
  }

  if (node.kind === 'leaf') {
    if (node.hash === hash && equals(node.key, key)) {
      if (Object.is(node.value, value)) return { node, added: false };
      return { node: { kind: 'leaf', hash, key, value }, added: false };
    }

    // Same hash, different key: store both in a collision bucket.
    if (node.hash === hash) {
      return {
        node: {
          kind: 'collision',
          entries: [
            { hash: node.hash, key: node.key, value: node.value },
            { hash, key, value },
          ],
        },
        added: true,
      };
    }

    // Different hash. At maximum depth, mix into a collision bucket.
    if (shift >= 35) {
      return {
        node: {
          kind: 'collision',
          entries: [
            { hash: node.hash, key: node.key, value: node.value },
            { hash, key, value },
          ],
        },
        added: true,
      };
    }

    const existingIndex = idx(node.hash, shift);
    const newIndex = idx(hash, shift);

    if (existingIndex !== newIndex) {
      const children = new Array<TrieNode<K, V> | undefined>(BRANCHING);
      children[existingIndex] = node;
      children[newIndex] = { kind: 'leaf', hash, key, value };
      return { node: { kind: 'branch', children }, added: true };
    }

    // Same slot at this level: recurse one level deeper.
    let child: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined;
    const first = setNode(child, node.hash, node.key, node.value, shift + 5, equals);
    child = first.node;
    const second = setNode(child, hash, key, value, shift + 5, equals);
    child = second.node;

    const children = new Array<TrieNode<K, V> | undefined>(BRANCHING);
    children[existingIndex] = child;
    return { node: { kind: 'branch', children }, added: second.added };
  }

  if (node.kind === 'collision') {
    const index = node.entries.findIndex(
      (entry) => entry.hash === hash && equals(entry.key, key),
    );

    if (index >= 0) {
      if (Object.is(node.entries[index].value, value)) {
        return { node, added: false };
      }
      const entries = node.entries.slice();
      entries[index] = { hash, key, value };
      return { node: { kind: 'collision', entries }, added: false };
    }

    return {
      node: {
        kind: 'collision',
        entries: [...node.entries, { hash, key, value }],
      },
      added: true,
    };
  }

  // Branch node.
  const branch = node;
  const branchIndex = idx(hash, shift);
  const child = branch.children[branchIndex];
  const result = setNode(child, hash, key, value, shift + 5, equals);

  if (result.node === child && !result.added) {
    return { node: branch, added: false };
  }

  const children = branch.children.slice() as (TrieNode<K, V> | undefined)[];
  children[branchIndex] = result.node;
  return { node: { kind: 'branch', children }, added: result.added };
}

function deleteNode<K, V>(
  node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined,
  hash: number,
  key: K,
  shift: number,
  equals: (a: K, b: K) => boolean,
): { node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined; removed: boolean } {
  if (!node) return { node: undefined, removed: false };

  if (node.kind === 'leaf') {
    if (node.hash === hash && equals(node.key, key)) {
      return { node: undefined, removed: true };
    }
    return { node, removed: false };
  }

  if (node.kind === 'collision') {
    const index = node.entries.findIndex(
      (entry) => entry.hash === hash && equals(entry.key, key),
    );
    if (index < 0) return { node, removed: false };

    const entries = node.entries.filter((_, i) => i !== index);
    if (entries.length === 0) {
      return { node: undefined, removed: true };
    }
    if (entries.length === 1) {
      const only = entries[0];
      return {
        node: { kind: 'leaf', hash: only.hash, key: only.key, value: only.value },
        removed: true,
      };
    }
    return { node: { kind: 'collision', entries }, removed: true };
  }

  const branchIndex = idx(hash, shift);
  const child = node.children[branchIndex];
  const result = deleteNode(child, hash, key, shift + 5, equals);
  if (!result.removed) return { node, removed: false };

  const children = node.children.slice() as (TrieNode<K, V> | undefined)[];
  children[branchIndex] = result.node;

  if (children.every((childNode) => childNode === undefined)) {
    return { node: undefined, removed: true };
  }

  return { node: { kind: 'branch', children }, removed: true };
}

function getNodeValue<K, V>(
  node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined,
  hash: number,
  key: K,
  shift: number,
  equals: (a: K, b: K) => boolean,
): V | undefined {
  if (!node) return undefined;

  if (node.kind === 'leaf') {
    return node.hash === hash && equals(node.key, key) ? node.value : undefined;
  }

  if (node.kind === 'collision') {
    const entry = node.entries.find(
      (item) => item.hash === hash && equals(item.key, key),
    );
    return entry?.value;
  }

  return getNodeValue(node.children[idx(hash, shift)], hash, key, shift + 5, equals);
}

function hasNode<K, V>(
  node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined,
  hash: number,
  key: K,
  shift: number,
  equals: (a: K, b: K) => boolean,
): boolean {
  if (!node) return false;

  if (node.kind === 'leaf') {
    return node.hash === hash && equals(node.key, key);
  }

  if (node.kind === 'collision') {
    return node.entries.some(
      (entry) => entry.hash === hash && equals(entry.key, key),
    );
  }

  return hasNode(node.children[idx(hash, shift)], hash, key, shift + 5, equals);
}

function forEachNode<K, V>(
  node: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined,
  callback: (key: K, value: V) => void,
): void {
  if (!node) return;

  if (node.kind === 'leaf') {
    callback(node.key, node.value);
    return;
  }

  if (node.kind === 'collision') {
    for (const entry of node.entries) callback(entry.key, entry.value);
    return;
  }

  for (const child of node.children) forEachNode(child, callback);
}

export class ImmutableMap<K, V> {
  private constructor(
    private readonly root: TrieNode<K, V> | undefined,
    public readonly size: number,
    private readonly hashFn: (key: K) => number,
    private readonly equalsFn: (a: K, b: K) => boolean,
  ) {}

  static empty<K, V>(options?: MapOptions<K>): ImmutableMap<K, V> {
    const hash = options?.hash ?? defaultHash<K>;
    const equals = options?.equals ?? defaultEquals<K>;
    return new ImmutableMap<K, V>(undefined, 0, hash, equals);
  }

  static fromEntries<K, V>(
    entries: Iterable<[K, V]>,
    options?: MapOptions<K>,
  ): ImmutableMap<K, V> {
    let map = ImmutableMap.empty<K, V>(options);
    for (const [key, value] of entries) map = map.set(key, value);
    return map;
  }

  get(key: K): V | undefined {
    return getNodeValue(this.root, this.hashFn(key), key, 0, this.equalsFn);
  }

  has(key: K): boolean {
    return hasNode(this.root, this.hashFn(key), key, 0, this.equalsFn);
  }

  set(key: K, value: V): ImmutableMap<K, V> {
    const result = setNode(
      this.root,
      this.hashFn(key),
      key,
      value,
      0,
      this.equalsFn,
    );

    // If nothing was added and the root is identical, no observable change occurred.
    if (result.node === this.root && !result.added) {
      return this;
    }

    const newSize = result.added ? this.size + 1 : this.size;
    return new ImmutableMap<K, V>(result.node, newSize, this.hashFn, this.equalsFn);
  }

  delete(key: K): ImmutableMap<K, V> {
    const result = deleteNode(
      this.root,
      this.hashFn(key),
      key,
      0,
      this.equalsFn,
    );

    if (!result.removed) return this;
    return new ImmutableMap<K, V>(result.node, this.size - 1, this.hashFn, this.equalsFn);
  }

  keys(): K[] {
    const out: K[] = [];
    forEachNode(this.root, (key) => out.push(key));
    return out;
  }

  values(): V[] {
    const out: V[] = [];
    forEachNode(this.root, (_, value) => out.push(value));
    return out;
  }

  entries(): [K, V][] {
    const out: [K, V][] = [];
    forEachNode(this.root, (key, value) => out.push([key, value]));
    return out;
  }

  forEach(
    callback: (value: V, key: K, map: ImmutableMap<K, V>) => void,
    thisArg?: unknown,
  ): void {
    for (const [key, value] of this.entries()) {
      callback.call(thisArg, value, key, this);
    }
  }

  merge(other: ImmutableMap<K, V> | Iterable<[K, V]>): ImmutableMap<K, V> {
    let result = this;
    const entries =
      other instanceof ImmutableMap ? other.entries() : Array.from(other);

    for (const [key, value] of entries) result = result.set(key, value);
    return result;
  }

  mapValues<U>(fn: (value: V, key: K) => U): ImmutableMap<K, U> {
    let result = ImmutableMap.empty<K, U>({
      hash: this.hashFn,
      equals: this.equalsFn,
    });

    for (const [key, value] of this.entries()) {
      result = result.set(key, fn(value, key));
    }
    return result;
  }

  filter(predicate: (value: V, key: K) => boolean): ImmutableMap<K, V> {
    let result = ImmutableMap.empty<K, V>({
      hash: this.hashFn,
      equals: this.equalsFn,
    });

    for (const [key, value] of this.entries()) {
      if (predicate(value, key)) result = result.set(key, value);
    }
    return result;
  }

  [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<[K, V]> {
    return this.entries()[Symbol.iterator]();
  }
}

/* =========================
   ImmutableSet
   ========================= */

export class ImmutableSet<T> {
  private constructor(
    private readonly map: ImmutableMap<T, true>,
    private readonly options?: MapOptions<T>,
  ) {}

  static empty<T>(options?: MapOptions<T>): ImmutableSet<T> {
    return new ImmutableSet<T>(ImmutableMap.empty<T, true>(options), options);
  }

  static from<T>(items: Iterable<T>, options?: MapOptions<T>): ImmutableSet<T> {
    let set = ImmutableSet.empty<T>(options);
    for (const item of items) set = set.add(item);
    return set;
  }

  get size(): number {
    return this.map.size;
  }

  has(value: T): boolean {
    return this.map.has(value);
  }

  add(value: T): ImmutableSet<T> {
    return new ImmutableSet<T>(this.map.set(value, true), this.options);
  }

  delete(value: T): ImmutableSet<T> {
    return new ImmutableSet<T>(this.map.delete(value), this.options);
  }

  toArray(): T[] {
    return this.map.keys();
  }

  union(other: ImmutableSet<T> | Iterable<T>): ImmutableSet<T> {
    let result = this;
    const items =
      other instanceof ImmutableSet ? other.toArray() : Array.from(other);

    for (const item of items) result = result.add(item);
    return result;
  }

  intersection(other: ImmutableSet<T> | Iterable<T>): ImmutableSet<T> {
    const otherSet =
      other instanceof ImmutableSet
        ? other
        : ImmutableSet.from(other as Iterable<T>, this.options);

    let result = ImmutableSet.empty<T>(this.options);
    for (const item of this.toArray()) {
      if (otherSet.has(item)) result = result.add(item);
    }
    return result;
  }

  difference(other: ImmutableSet<T> | Iterable<T>): ImmutableSet<T> {
    const otherSet =
      other instanceof ImmutableSet
        ? other
        : ImmutableSet.from(other as Iterable<T>, this.options);

    let result = ImmutableSet.empty<T>(this.options);
    for (const item of this.toArray()) {
      if (!otherSet.has(item)) result = result.add(item);
    }
    return result;
  }

  forEach(
    callback: (value: T, value2: T, set: ImmutableSet<T>) => void,
    thisArg?: unknown,
  ): void {
    for (const value of this.toArray()) {
      callback.call(thisArg, value, value, this);
    }
  }

  [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<T> {
    return this.toArray()[Symbol.iterator]();
  }
}

/* =========================
   Runnable example / checks
   ========================= */

function assert(condition: boolean, message: string): void {
  if (!condition) throw new Error(`Assertion failed: ${message}`);
}

function main(): void {
  // ---- ImmutableList ----
  const list = ImmutableList.fromArray([1, 2, 3]);
  const list2 = list.cons(0);
  const list3 = list.append(4);

  assert(list.toArray().join(',') === '1,2,3', 'original list unchanged');
  assert(list2.toArray().join(',') === '0,1,2,3', 'cons works');
  assert(list3.toArray().join(',') === '1,2,3,4', 'append works');

  const mapped = list.map((x) => x * 10);
  assert(mapped.toArray().join(',') === '10,20,30', 'map works');

  const filtered = list.filter((x) => x > 1);
  assert(filtered.toArray().join(',') === '2,3', 'filter works');

  assert(list.nth(1) === 2, 'nth works');
  assert(list.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) === 6, 'reduce works');

  const updatedList = list.set(1, 99);
  assert(updatedList.toArray().join(',') === '1,99,3', 'list set works');
  assert(list.toArray().join(',') === '1,2,3', 'list set is immutable');

  // ---- ImmutableMap ----
  const map = ImmutableMap.empty<string, number>()
    .set('a', 1)
    .set('b', 2);

  const map2 = map.set('a', 10);
  const map3 = map.set('c', 3);

  assert(map.get('a') === 1, 'original map unchanged');
  assert(map2.get('a') === 10, 'updated map has new value');
  assert(map2.size === 2, 'size unchanged on overwrite');
  assert(map3.size === 3, 'size grows on new key');
  assert(!map.has('c'), 'original map lacks new key');

  // Stored undefined values are handled correctly.
  const mapU = ImmutableMap.empty<string, number | undefined>().set('x', undefined);
  assert(mapU.has('x'), 'has() detects stored undefined');
  assert(mapU.get('x') === undefined, 'get() returns undefined');

  const deleted = map.delete('b');
  assert(deleted.size === 1, 'delete reduces size');
  assert(!deleted.has('b'), 'deleted key is gone');
  assert(map.has('b'), 'delete does not mutate original');

  // Structural sharing: unchanged subtrees are reused.
  assert(map.get('a') === 1 && map2.get('b') === 2, 'structural sharing preserves values');

  // ---- ImmutableSet ----
  const set = ImmutableSet.from([1, 2, 3]);
  const set2 = set.add(4).delete(1);

  assert(set.has(1), 'original set unchanged');
  assert(!set2.has(1), 'delete works');
  assert(set2.has(4), 'add works');
  assert(set2.size === 3, 'set size correct');

  const union = set.union(ImmutableSet.from([3, 4, 5]));
  assert(union.toArray().sort().join(',') === '1,2,3,4,5', 'union works');

  const intersection = set.intersection([2, 3, 9]);
  assert(intersection.toArray().sort().join(',') === '2,3', 'intersection works');

  const difference = set.difference([2]);
  assert(difference.toArray().sort().join(',') === '1,3', 'difference works');

  // ---- Custom key equality ----
  interface Point {
    readonly x: number;
    readonly y: number;
  }

  const pointMap = ImmutableMap.empty<Point, string>({
    hash: (p) => (p.x * 31 + p.y) | 0,
    equals: (a, b) => a.x === b.x && a.y === b.y,
  })
    .set({ x: 1, y: 2 }, 'A')
    .set({ x: 1, y: 2 }, 'B');

  assert(pointMap.size === 1, 'custom equals collapses equal keys');
  assert(pointMap.get({ x: 1, y: 2 }) === 'B', 'custom key lookup works');

  console.log('All immutable structure checks passed.');
  console.log('List:', list.toArray());
  console.log('Map entries:', map.entries());
  console.log('Set:', set.toArray());
}

main();
```

## Complexity summary

| Operation | `ImmutableList` | `ImmutableMap` | `ImmutableSet` |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| `cons` / `prepend` | `O(1)` | — | — |
| `append` | `O(n)` | — | — |
| `get` / `has` | `O(n)` | `O(log₃₂ n)` | `O(log₃₂ n)` |
| `set` / `add` | `O(n)` for `set(index)` | `O(log₃₂ n)` | `O(log₃₂ n)` |
| `delete` | `O(n)` for list rebuild | `O(log₃₂ n)` | `O(log₃₂ n)` |
| iteration | `O(n)` | `O(n)` | `O(n)` |

For production systems, libraries such as Immer, Immutable.js, or Mori provide more optimizations, but the above demonstrates the core persistent-data-structure techniques in plain TypeScript.

# Immutable Data Structures in TypeScript

## The idea

Immutability means **never changing an existing value in place**—instead you produce a *new* value that represents the updated state. The old value stays intact.

Why this matters in TS/JS:

- **Predictability**: no hidden side effects from shared references.
- **Debugging/time-travel**: old states remain valid (great for Redux DevTools, undo/redo).
- **React/Redux**: change detection becomes a simple reference comparison (`===`).
- **Concurrency safety**: no races on shared mutable state.

TypeScript gives *compile-time* immutability guarantees (`readonly`), while runtime libraries (or `Object.freeze`) enforce them at execution time.

---

## Syntax

### 1. `readonly` properties

```ts
type User = {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly name: string;
};

const u: User = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };
// u.name = "Bob";  // ❌ Compile error
```

### 2. `Readonly<T>` utility type

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string; tags: string[] };

const u: Readonly<User> = { id: 1, name: "Ada", tags: ["admin"] };
// u.name = "x";     // ❌
// u.tags.push("x"); // ✅ (shallow—see below)
```

### 3. Readonly arrays & tuples

```ts
const nums: readonly number[] = [1, 2, 3];
// nums.push(4);        // ❌
// nums[0] = 9;         // ❌

const pair: readonly [string, number] = ["age", 30];
```

`ReadonlyArray<T>` is the same thing spelled differently.

### 4. Deep readonly (recursive)

TS has no built-in deep readonly, but it’s a one-liner:

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] extends object
    ? DeepReadonly<T[K]>
    : T[K];
};

type State = DeepReadonly<{ user: { name: string; tags: string[] } }>;
```

### 5. `as const` — literal, deeply readonly values

```ts
const CONFIG = {
  apiUrl: "https://api.example.com",
  retries: 3,
} as const;

// CONFIG.apiUrl is "https://api.example.com" (literal), not string
// CONFIG.retries = 5;  // ❌
```

Great for action types, enums-as-objects, and config.

### 6. Runtime enforcement: `Object.freeze`

```ts
const state = Object.freeze({ count: 0 });
// state.count = 1;  // silently fails in non-strict, throws in strict mode
```

`freeze` is shallow; use a recursive freeze helper or a library for deep immutability at runtime.

---

## Idiomatic update patterns

### Update an object (spread)

```ts
const user = { id: 1, name: "Ada", age: 30 };

const renamed = { ...user, name: "Bob" };   // new object
// user is unchanged
```

### Update an array

```ts
const items = [1, 2, 3];

const added    = [...items, 4];
const removed  = items.filter(x => x !== 2);
const replaced = items.map(x => (x === 2 ? 20 : x));
```

### Nested updates (the painful part)

```ts
type State = { user: { profile: { name: string } } };

const s: State = { user: { profile: { name: "Ada" } } };

const updated = {
  ...s,
  user: { ...s.user, profile: { ...s.user.profile, name: "Bob" } },
};
```

This verbosity is why **Immer** is popular:

```ts
import { produce } from "immer";

const updated = produce(s, draft => {
  draft.user.profile.name = "Bob"; // looks mutable, stays immutable
});
```

---

## Typical use cases

| Use case | Why immutability helps |
|---|---|
| **Redux / Zustand / RTK** | Reducers must return new state; enables time-travel & devtools |
| **React state (`useState`, `useReducer`)** | Triggers re-render only when reference changes |
| **Memoization (`useMemo`, `React.memo`)** | Shallow prop comparison works correctly |
| **Undo/redo, history** | Keep an array of past states |
| **Pure functions / FP** | No hidden side effects; easy to test |
| **Caching / memoized selectors (Reselect)** | Cache keys are stable references |
| **Concurrent rendering (React 18)** | Safe to interrupt/replay renders |

---

## Pitfalls to know

1. **Shallow only.** `Readonly<T>` and `Object.freeze` protect the top level; nested objects/arrays are still mutable. Use `DeepReadonly` + Immer/freeze helpers.
2. **`readonly` is erasable at runtime.** It’s a TS compile-time hint; a cast (`as any`) or JS interop can still mutate. Don’t trust it for security.
3. **`readonly` arrays still expose mutating methods via index signatures** in older TS; prefer `readonly T[]`.
4. **Performance:** spreading large structures is O(n). For big state, use structural-sharing libraries (Immer, Immutable.js) or normalize state (Redux Toolkit).

---

## Minimal “production” pattern

```ts
// state.ts
export type Todo = { readonly id: string; readonly text: string; readonly done: boolean };
export type State = { readonly todos: readonly Todo[] };

// reducer.ts
export function addTodo(s: State, text: string): State {
  return {
    ...s,
    todos: [...s.todos, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), text, done: false }],
  };
}
```

Compile-time safety + predictable updates + trivial undo (just keep previous `State` references).

## Mistaken version

```ts
type State = {
  user: { name: string; tags: string[] };
};

function addTag(state: State, tag: string): State {
  // ❌ Mutates the original object and nested array in place
  state.user.tags.push(tag);
  state.user.name = state.user.name.toUpperCase();
  return state;
}

const initial: State = { user: { name: "ada", tags: ["ts"] } };
const next = addTag(initial, "fp");

console.log(initial.user.tags); // ["ts", "fp"]  ← original was mutated
console.log(next === initial);  // true          ← same reference
```

## Corrected version

```ts
type State = {
  user: { name: string; tags: string[] };
};

function addTag(state: State, tag: string): State {
  // ✅ Create new objects/arrays; leave the input untouched
  return {
    ...state,
    user: {
      ...state.user,
      name: state.user.name.toUpperCase(),
      tags: [...state.user.tags, tag],
    },
  };
}

const initial: State = { user: { name: "ada", tags: ["ts"] } };
const next = addTag(initial, "fp");

console.log(initial.user.tags); // ["ts"]        ← original unchanged
console.log(next.user.tags);    // ["ts", "fp"]  ← new array
console.log(next === initial);  // false         ← new top-level reference
```

## What was wrong and why

1. **`const` is not immutability**  
   `const` only prevents reassigning the variable binding. The object it points to is still fully mutable unless you enforce immutability by convention or with `readonly`/`Object.freeze`.

2. **Mutation breaks change detection**  
   Libraries and frameworks such as React, Redux, Immer-based stores, and memoized selectors rely on **reference equality** to know whether data changed. If you mutate and return the same object, `next === initial` is `true`, so downstream optimizations may skip re-rendering or fail to detect the update.

3. **Shared references cause spooky action at a distance**  
   `state.user.tags.push(tag)` modifies the array that `initial` still references. Any other code holding `initial` now sees the new tag unexpectedly, leading to bugs that are hard to trace.

4. **`readonly` is shallow**  
   Even `readonly State` only protects the top-level property assignments; nested arrays/objects can still be mutated unless you use deep readonly types or runtime freezing. The real fix is to **produce new values** at every level that changes.

5. **Spread creates shallow copies**  
   `[...state.user.tags, tag]` creates a new array, and `{ ...state.user, ... }` creates a new user object. Unchanged nested parts can be shared safely (structural sharing), which is efficient and preserves referential equality for untouched subtrees.

### Practical tips

- Treat inputs to pure functions as immutable by default.
- Prefer `readonly` in function parameters: `function addTag(state: Readonly<State>, tag: string)`.
- For deep immutability at runtime, use `Object.freeze` (shallow) or a library such as Immer when updates are complex.
- In React/Redux, never mutate state; always return a new state object.

If you want, I can also show a version using **Immer** (`produce`) that keeps the convenient mutable-looking syntax while preserving immutability.

Below are the most common pitfalls when working with “immutable” data in TypeScript, grouped by category. For each, the wrong pattern is shown first, then the safer pattern.

---

## 1. `Readonly<T>` is shallow

`Readonly<T>` only makes top-level properties readonly. Nested objects remain mutable.

```ts
type User = {
  name: string;
  address: { city: string; zip: string };
};

const user: Readonly<User> = {
  name: "Ada",
  address: { city: "London", zip: "SW1" },
};

user.name = "Grace";          // ❌ compile error
user.address.city = "Paris";  // ✅ compiles and mutates original!
```

**Fix:** use a recursive `DeepReadonly`, or freeze/clone deeply.

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (infer U)[]
    ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[]
    : T extends ReadonlyArray<infer U>
      ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[]
      : T extends (...args: any[]) => any
        ? T
        : T extends object
          ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
          : T;

const user2: DeepReadonly<User> = user;
user2.address.city = "Paris"; // ❌ compile error
```

---

## 2. `readonly` arrays still allow element mutation

```ts
type Item = { value: number };
const items: readonly Item[] = [{ value: 1 }];

items.push({ value: 2 }); // ❌ compile error
items[0].value = 99;     // ✅ compiles and mutates
```

**Fix:** make the element type readonly too.

```ts
const items2: readonly DeepReadonly<Item>[] = [{ value: 1 }];
items2[0].value = 99; // ❌ compile error
```

---

## 3. Spread copies are shallow

```ts
const next = {
  ...user,
  address: user.address, // same nested reference
};

next.address.city = "Paris";
console.log(user.address.city); // "Paris" — original mutated
```

**Fix:** spread at every nested level that changes.

```ts
const next2 = {
  ...user,
  address: { ...user.address, city: "Paris" },
};
```

For deep/unknown shapes, use a real deep clone (with caveats, see #12).

---

## 4. In-place array methods mutate the original

```ts
const nums = [3, 1, 2];
const sorted = nums.sort(); // mutates nums

console.log(nums);   // [1, 2, 3]
console.log(sorted); // [1, 2, 3] (same array)
```

Other foot-guns: `reverse`, `splice`, `sort`, `fill`, `copyWithin`.

**Fix:** copy first.

```ts
const sorted2 = [...nums].sort((a, b) => a - b);
// or
const sorted3 = nums.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);
```

---

## 5. `Object.freeze` is shallow and only runtime

```ts
const frozen = Object.freeze({ nested: { x: 1 } });

frozen.nested.x = 2; // ✅ runtime succeeds (TS may or may not complain)
console.log(frozen.nested.x); // 2
```

TypeScript types `Object.freeze(obj)` as `Readonly<T>`, so nested mutation may still type-check.

**Fix:** deep freeze recursively, or prefer immutable update patterns.

```ts
function deepFreeze<T>(obj: T): Readonly<T> {
  Object.freeze(obj);
  if (obj && typeof obj === "object") {
    for (const key of Object.keys(obj)) {
      deepFreeze((obj as any)[key]);
    }
  }
  return obj;
}
```

---

## 6. `const` does not mean immutable

```ts
const obj = { x: 1 };
obj.x = 2; // ✅ allowed
```

`const` only prevents rebinding the variable, not mutating the object.

**Fix:** combine `const` with `Readonly`/`as const` and freezing if runtime safety matters.

```ts
const obj2 = { x: 1 } as const;
obj2.x = 2; // ❌ compile error
```

---

## 7. `as const` creates readonly tuples/objects that may not fit mutable APIs

```ts
function takeMutable(arr: number[]) {
  arr.push(99);
}

const arr = [1, 2, 3] as const;
takeMutable(arr); // ❌ readonly [1,2,3] not assignable to number[]
```

**Fix:** copy into a mutable array when an API requires mutation.

```ts
takeMutable([...arr]);
```

---

## 8. Type assertions can lie about immutability

```ts
const x = { a: 1 } as const;

// Bypasses the type system; may mutate at runtime if not frozen
(x as { a: number }).a = 2;
```

**Fix:** avoid casts that remove readonly; if mutation is truly needed, clone instead.

```ts
const y = { ...x, a: 2 }; // new object
```

---

## 9. Readonly types are compile-time only

A `readonly` array is often just a normal mutable array at runtime.

```ts
function sortInPlace(arr: number[]) {
  arr.sort();
}

const r: readonly number[] = [3, 1, 2];
sortInPlace(r as number[]); // compiles due to cast; mutates original at runtime
```

**Fix:** never cast readonly to mutable. If a library needs mutation, pass a copy.

```ts
sortInPlace([...r]);
```

---

## 10. Immer: drafts must stay inside the producer

```ts
import { produce } from "immer";

const state = { count: 0, nested: { value: 1 } };

// ✅ correct
const next = produce(state, (draft) => {
  draft.count++;
  draft.nested.value = 2;
});

// ❌ wrong: draft escapes producer scope
let escaped: any;
const bad = produce(state, (draft) => {
  escaped = draft;
});
escaped.count = 999; // mutates outside producer; can corrupt state
```

Also avoid returning a value and mutating the same draft in confusing ways:

```ts
// Prefer one style:
const a = produce(state, (d) => { d.count++; });                 // mutate draft
const b = produce(state, () => ({ count: state.count + 1 }));   // return new value
```

---

## 11. Immutable.js: conversion and reference pitfalls

```ts
import { Map } from "immutable";

const m = Map({ a: 1 });
const next = m.set("a", 2);

console.log(m.get("a"));    // 1 (original unchanged)
console.log(next.get("a")); // 2

// Pitfall: toJS() deep-clones every time
const plain = next.toJS(); // expensive for large structures

// Pitfall: nested updates need path helpers
const m2 = Map({ user: Map({ name: "Ada" }) });
const m3 = m2.setIn(["user", "name"], "Grace");
```

Also, Immutable.js collections are not plain objects; passing them to APIs expecting POJOs causes bugs. Convert at boundaries.

---

## 12. Deep cloning is not free and loses types

```ts
const clone = structuredClone(original); // modern, handles Map/Set/Date
```

Pitfalls:

- `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` drops `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `undefined`, functions, and class instances.
- Cloning large graphs is expensive.
- Cloning breaks reference identity, which can hurt memoization.

**Fix:** prefer structural updates (spread/Immer) over full clones.

---

## 13. React state mutation breaks change detection

```ts
// ❌ mutates existing state
state.items.push(newItem);
setState(state); // often no re-render

// ❌ nested mutation
state.user.name = "Grace";
setState({ ...state }); // new top-level object, but nested mutation already happened
```

**Fix:** create new references along the changed path.

```ts
setState((prev) => ({
  ...prev,
  items: [...prev.items, newItem],
  user: { ...prev.user, name: "Grace" },
}));
```

---

## 14. New object identity can cause unnecessary re-renders

Immutability creates new references even when values are equal.

```ts
function Component({ user }: { user: { name: string } }) {
  // ❌ new object every render
  const style = { color: "red", name: user.name };
  return <Child style={style} />;
}
```

**Fix:** memoize stable references.

```ts
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: "red", name: user.name }), [user.name]);
```

---

## 15. DeepReadonly + functions/Dates/Maps need special handling

A naive recursive readonly type can break callable objects or built-ins.

```ts
type BadDeepReadonly<T> = T extends object
  ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: BadDeepReadonly<T[K]> }
  : T;

// Functions become non-callable-looking types; Date becomes { readonly ... }
type T1 = BadDeepReadonly<() => number>; // often okay-ish but fragile
type T2 = BadDeepReadonly<Date>;         // becomes readonly property bag
```

**Fix:** stop recursion at functions and known built-ins.

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (...args: any[]) => any
    ? T
    : T extends Date | RegExp | Map<any, any> | Set<any>
      ? T
      : T extends (infer U)[]
        ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[]
        : T extends ReadonlyArray<infer U>
          ? readonly DeepReadonly<U>[]
          : T extends object
            ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
            : T;
```

---

## 16. Readonly index signatures and mapped types

```ts
type Dict = { [key: string]: number };

const d: Readonly<Dict> = { a: 1 };
d.b = 2; // ❌ compile error (good)
// But Readonly<Dict> is still just { readonly [key: string]: number }
```

Pitfall: `Readonly<T>` on index signatures does not make values deeply readonly, and adding keys may still be allowed depending on the exact type. Be explicit.

```ts
type ImmutableDict = { readonly [key: string]: DeepReadonly<number> };
```

---

## Quick checklist

- Treat `readonly` as a **compile-time hint**, not runtime protection.
- Remember readonly is **shallow**.
- Copy arrays before `sort`, `reverse`, `splice`.
- Spread is shallow; spread nested changed paths.
- Prefer Immer/structural updates over deep cloning.
- Don’t mutate React/Redux state in place.
- Be careful with `as const`, casts, and mutable APIs.
- Memoize derived objects to avoid identity churn.

## Exercise

Create a small **immutable document model** in TypeScript.

Requirements:
- A `Document` has `id`, `title`, `tags`, `sections`, and `metadata`.
- Nested objects/arrays must be read-only at the type level.
- Provide pure operations:
  - `createDocument`
  - `addTag`
  - `removeSection`
  - `updateSectionBody`
- No function may mutate its input.
- At runtime, returned objects should also be frozen deeply enough to catch accidental mutation during development.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Define deeply readonly types

TypeScript’s `readonly` is shallow, so nested arrays need explicit `readonly` annotations. A reusable `DeepReadonly<T>` helper makes this easier to maintain.

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (infer U)[]
    ? ReadonlyArray<DeepReadonly<U>>
    : T extends object
      ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
      : T;
```

### 2. Model the domain

```ts
type Section = {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly body: string;
};

type Document = {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly tags: readonly string[];
  readonly sections: readonly Section[];
  readonly metadata: Readonly<{
    readonly createdAt: number;
    readonly updatedAt: number;
  }>;
};
```

### 3. Add runtime deep freezing

Type-level readonly only protects compile-time code. `Object.freeze` protects runtime mutation. Recursively freezing gives a useful development-time safety net.

```ts
function deepFreeze<T>(value: T): DeepReadonly<T> {
  if (value && typeof value === "object") {
    Object.freeze(value);

    for (const key of Object.keys(value)) {
      deepFreeze((value as Record<string, unknown>)[key]);
    }
  }
  return value as DeepReadonly<T>;
}
```

### 4. Implement pure transformations

Each function returns a new `Document` and leaves the original untouched. Spread syntax creates shallow copies, while unchanged nested values are shared structurally.

```ts
function createDocument(input: {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  tags?: readonly string[];
  sections?: readonly Section[];
}): Document {
  const now = Date.now();

  return deepFreeze({
    id: input.id,
    title: input.title,
    tags: [...(input.tags ?? [])],
    sections: [...(input.sections ?? [])],
    metadata: {
      createdAt: now,
      updatedAt: now,
    },
  });
}

function addTag(document: Document, tag: string): Document {
  const normalized = tag.trim();
  if (!normalized) {
    throw new Error("Tag cannot be empty");
  }

  if (document.tags.includes(normalized)) {
    return document;
  }

  return deepFreeze({
    ...document,
    tags: [...document.tags, normalized],
    metadata: {
      ...document.metadata,
      updatedAt: Date.now(),
    },
  });
}

function removeSection(document: Document, sectionId: string): Document {
  const sections = document.sections.filter(
    (section) => section.id !== sectionId
  );

  if (sections.length === document.sections.length) {
    return document;
  }

  return deepFreeze({
    ...document,
    sections,
    metadata: {
      ...document.metadata,
      updatedAt: Date.now(),
    },
  });
}

function updateSectionBody(
  document: Document,
  sectionId: string,
  body: string
): Document {
  let changed = false;

  const sections = document.sections.map((section) => {
    if (section.id !== sectionId) {
      return section;
    }

    changed = true;
    return {
      ...section,
      body,
    };
  });

  if (!changed) {
    return document;
  }

  return deepFreeze({
    ...document,
    sections,
    metadata: {
      ...document.metadata,
      updatedAt: Date.now(),
    },
  });
}
```

### 5. Verify immutability

```ts
const original = createDocument({
  id: "doc-1",
  title: "Immutable TypeScript",
  tags: [],
  sections: [
    { id: "s-1", title: "Intro", body: "Hello" },
  ],
});

const updated = addTag(original, "typescript");

console.assert(original !== updated);
console.assert(original.tags.length === 0);
console.assert(updated.tags.length === 1);
console.assert(original.sections === updated.sections); // structural sharing

// Runtime protection:
// updated.tags.push("mutate"); // throws in strict frozen object runtime
```

---

## Final solution

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> =
  T extends (infer U)[]
    ? ReadonlyArray<DeepReadonly<U>>
    : T extends object
      ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
      : T;

type Section = {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly body: string;
};

type Document = {
