## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Understand the iterator protocol

An object is iterable when it has a method keyed by `Symbol.iterator` that returns an `Iterator<T>`.  
An iterator has a `next()` method returning `{ value: T; done: boolean }`.

```ts
interface IteratorResult<T> {
  value: T;
  done: boolean;
}
```

### 2. Define the public types

```ts
export interface RangeOptions {
  start: number;
  end: number;
  step?: number;
}

export class Range implements Iterable<number> {
  constructor(
    public readonly start: number,
    public readonly end: number,
    public readonly step: number = 1
  ) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(start) || !Number.isInteger(end)) {
      throw new TypeError("start and end must be integers");
    }
    if (!Number.isInteger(step) || step <= 0) {
      throw new TypeError("step must be a positive integer");
    }
    if (start > end) {
      throw new RangeError("start must be <= end for a forward range");
    }
  }
  // ...
}
```

### 3. Implement `[Symbol.iterator]`

We keep iteration state inside the returned iterator object, not the `Range` instance, so the same `Range` can be iterated multiple times.

```ts
[Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<number> {
  let current = this.start;
  const { end, step } = this;

  return {
    next(): IteratorResult<number> {
      if (current > end) {
        return { value: undefined as unknown as number, done: true };
      }
      const value = current;
      current += step;
      return { value, done: false };
    },
  };
}
```

### 4. Add `reverse()`

Return a new `Range` with swapped bounds and the same step. Because `Range` is forward-only by construction, `reverse()` creates a descending view by flipping start/end and using a descending step internally. To keep the class simple, implement reverse as a separate descending iterator:

```ts
reverse(): Iterable<number> {
  const { start, end, step } = this;
  let current = end - ((end - start) % step);
  // ensure we land exactly on a valid grid point <= end
  if (current < start) current += step;
  if (current > end) current -= step;

  return {
    [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<number> {
      let c = current;
      return {
        next(): IteratorResult<number> {
          if (c < start) {
            return { value: undefined as unknown as number, done: true };
          }
          const value = c;
          c -= step;
          return { value, done: false };
        },
      };
    },
  };
}
```

### 5. Usage: sum of even numbers without an array

```ts
const range = new Range(0, 1000, 2); // only evens
let sum = 0;
for (const n of range) {
  sum += n;
  if (sum > 100_000) break; // early termination works
}
console.log(sum);
```

Because the iterator is lazy, `break` stops production immediately.

### 6. Generator shorthand (idiomatic alternative)

For production code, a generator is usually clearer:

```ts
*[Symbol.iterator](): Generator<number> {
  for (let i = this.start; i <= this.end; i += this.step) {
    yield i;
  }
}
```

The manual version above is shown to make the protocol explicit.

