### Key points to remember (interview checklist)
| Topic | Detail |
|---|---|
| Protocol | `next()` → `{ value, done }`; object needs `[Symbol.iterator]` to be iterable. |
| Type | `Iterator<T>`, `Iterable<T>`, `IterableIterator<T>` (iterator that is also iterable). |
| State | Iterators are **single-use & mutable**; store position internally. |
| Early exit | `for...of` / `break` calls `.return()` if present — implement it to release resources. |
| Generators | `function*` auto-implements both protocols; prefer for most cases. |
| Async | `AsyncIterable` + `for await...of` for promises; `AsyncGenerator`. |
| Common bug | Returning a fresh iterator each call vs. reusing one — reusing breaks re-iteration. |

If you want, I can extend this with a **custom linked-list iterator**, an **infinite sequence with `take()`**, or **iterator helpers** (`Iterator.prototype.map/filter` from TC39 stage 3).

Here are common edge cases when working with iterators in TypeScript, with practical examples.

## 1. Iterators are single-use and stateful

An iterator is not a collection. Once consumed, it is exhausted.

```ts
function* numbers() {
  yield 1;
  yield 2;
  yield 3;
}

const it = numbers();

console.log([...it]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...it]); // []
```

If you need to iterate multiple times, store the original iterable and call `[Symbol.iterator]()` again.

```ts
const values = [1, 2, 3];
const first = [...values];
const second = [...values]; // safe
```

## 2. Partial consumption affects later use

Destructuring, spread, `Array.from`, and `for...of` all consume the iterator.

```ts
const it = new Set([1, 2, 3, 4])[Symbol.iterator]();

const [a, b] = it;
console.log(a, b);      // 1 2
console.log([...it]);    // [3, 4]
```

## 3. Early termination calls `return()`

`break`, `return`, or an exception in `for...of` should trigger cleanup. Custom iterators must implement `return()`.

```ts
function* resourceGenerator() {
  const handle = { id: 42 };
  try {
    yield handle;
    yield "never reached if broken";
  } finally {
    console.log("released", handle.id);
  }
}

for (const x of resourceGenerator()) {
  console.log(x);
  break; // triggers finally
}
```

For a manual iterator, call `return()` yourself:

```ts
class RangeIterator implements Iterator<number> {
  private i = 0;

  constructor(private end: number) {}

  next(): IteratorResult<number> {
    if (this.i < this.end) {
      return { value: this.i++, done: false };
    }
    return { value: undefined, done: true };
  }

  return(value?: unknown): IteratorResult<number> {
    this.i = this.end; // stop future next() calls
    return { value, done: true };
  }
}
```

If `return()` is missing, cleanup may not happen on early exit.

## 4. `throw()` and `next(value)` are easy to mishandle

Generators can receive values and exceptions.

```ts
function* echo(): Generator<number | undefined, number, number> {
  const x = yield;
  if (x < 0) throw new Error("negative input");
  return x * 2;
}

const g = echo();
g.next();        // start
console.log(g.next(21)); // { value: 42, done: true }

const g2 = echo();
g2.next();
console.log(g2.throw(new Error("bad"))); // propagates or is caught if generator catches it
```

A common mistake is calling `next(value)` before the generator has yielded. The first `next()` starts execution; later `next(value)` resumes it.

## 5. `done: true` does not guarantee a meaningful `value`

Runtime iterators may omit `value` when done. TypeScript's `TReturn` type can make this look safer than it is.

```ts
const it = [1, 2][Symbol.iterator]();

console.log(it.next()); // { value: 1, done: false }
console.log(it.next()); // { value: 2, done: false }
console.log(it.next()); // { value: undefined, done: true }
```

Always check `done` before using `value`.

```ts
let r = it.next();
while (!r.done) {
  console.log(r.value);
  r = it.next();
}
```

## 6. `Iterable` and `Iterator` are not interchangeable in TypeScript

`Iterable<T>` can be iterated. A plain `Iterator<T>` cannot, unless it also implements `Symbol.iterator`.

```ts
function sum(iterable: Iterable<number>): number {
  let total = 0;
  for (const n of iterable) total += n;
  return total;
}

class Counter implements Iterator<number> {
  private i = 0;
  next(): IteratorResult<number> {
    return this.i < 3
      ? { value: this.i++, done: false }
      : { value: undefined, done: true };
  }
}

const c = new Counter();
// sum(c); // Error: Counter is not Iterable

class IterableCounter implements IterableIterator<number> {
  private i = 0;
  next(): IteratorResult<number> {
    return this.i < 3
      ? { value: this.i++, done: false }
      : { value: undefined, done: true };
  }
  [Symbol.iterator](): IterableIterator<number> {
    return this;
  }
}

sum(new IterableCounter()); // OK
```

## 7. Detached iterator methods lose `this`

```ts
const it = new Set([1, 2, 3])[Symbol.iterator]();
const next = it.next;

// next(); // TypeError: Method next called on incompatible receiver

const boundNext = it.next.bind(it);
console.log(boundNext()); // { value: 1, done: false }
```

## 8. Mutating a collection while iterating can skip or duplicate items

### Arrays

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];

for (const x of arr) {
  if (x === 2) arr.splice(1, 1); // mutating during iteration
  console.log(x);
}
// May skip elements because indices shift
```

### Sets and Maps

```ts
const set = new Set([1, 2, 3]);

for (const x of set) {
  set.delete(x);
  set.add(x + 10);
  console.log(x);
}
// Newly added values may or may not be visited depending on position
```

Prefer collecting changes first, then applying them.

```ts
const toDelete = [...set].filter(x => x < 2);
for (const x of toDelete) set.delete(x);
```

## 9. Sparse arrays and holes

`for...of` yields `undefined` for holes, while methods like `forEach` skip them.

```ts
const sparse = [1, , 3];

for (const x of sparse) {
  console.log(x); // 1, undefined, 3
}

sparse.forEach(x => console.log(x)); // 1, 3
```

Use explicit checks if holes matter:

```ts
for (let i = 0; i < sparse.length; i++) {
  if (!(i in sparse)) continue;
  console.log(sparse[i]);
}
```

## 10. Strings iterate by code points, not UTF-16 units

```ts
const text = "a😀b";

console.log(text.length); // 4
console.log([...text]);   // ["a", "😀", "b"]
console.log([...text].length); // 3

for (const ch of text) {
  console.log(ch); // a, 😀, b
}
```

Use code-point iteration for user-visible characters, but remember that grapheme clusters, such as flags or combining marks, can still be multiple code points.

## 11. Async iterators have separate protocols and cleanup rules

```ts
async function* pages(signal?: AbortSignal) {
  let page = 0;
  while (true) {
    if (signal?.aborted) throw new Error("Aborted");
    const data = await fetch(`/api?page=${page++}`, { signal }).then(r => r.json());
    if (!data.length) return;
    for (const row of data) yield row;
  }
}

const controller = new AbortController();

for await (const row of pages(controller.signal)) {
  if (shouldStop(row)) {
    controller.abort();
    break;
  }
  console.log(row);
}
```

Edge cases:

- `for await...of` can also consume sync iterables, awaiting each value.
- Manual async iteration must handle `Promise<IteratorResult<T>>`.
- Cancellation must be implemented explicitly; iterators do not auto-cancel.

```ts
const it = pages()[Symbol.asyncIterator]();
const r = await it.next();
console.log(r);
await it.return?.(); // cleanup if stopping early
```

## 12. Generator `yield*` captures the inner return value

`for...of` ignores a generator's final return value, but `yield*` exposes it.

```ts
function* inner() {
  yield 1;
  return "inner done";
}

function* outer() {
  const result = yield* inner();
  console.log(result); // "inner done"
  yield 2;
}

console.log([...outer()]); // [1, 2]
```

## 13. Generators cannot be re-entered while running

Calling `next()` on the same generator instance from within its own execution throws.

```ts
const g = (function* () {
  // This attempts to advance the same generator while it is running.
  yield (g.next() as any).value;
})();

try {
  g.next();
} catch (e) {
  console.error(e); // TypeError: Generator is already running
}
```

## 14. TypeScript target and `downlevelIteration`

With older targets, spread on non-array iterables may require `downlevelIteration`.

```ts
const set = new Set([1, 2, 3]);

// With target "ES5" and no downlevelIteration:
// const arr = [...set]; // compile error

const arr = Array.from(set); // safer across targets
```

If you truly need spread syntax for `Set`, `Map`, generators, etc. on older targets, enable:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES5",
    "downlevelIteration": true
  }
}
```

## 15. Iterator helpers are lazy and chainable, but support varies

Recent runtimes and TypeScript versions support iterator helpers. They are lazy and can exhaust the source.

```ts
const source = [1, 2, 3, 4].values();

const result = source
  .map(n => n * 2)
  .filter(n => n > 4);

console.log([...result]); // [6, 8]
console.log([...source]);  // [] because source was consumed
```

Edge cases:

- Errors propagate lazily when iteration happens, not when chaining.
- Early termination calls `return()` on upstream iterators.
- Availability depends on runtime and TypeScript/lib version.

## 16. Objects are not iterable by default

Plain objects use `for...in` or `Object.keys/values/entries`, not `for...of`.

```ts
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };

// for (const x of obj) {} // Error

for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
  console.log(k, v);
}
```

`Map` and `Set` are iterable; `WeakMap` and `WeakSet` are not.

```ts
// for (const x of new WeakSet()) {} // Error: not iterable
```

## Practical checklist

- Check `done` before reading `value`.
- Treat iterators as one-shot resources.
- Implement `return()` and `throw()` for custom iterators.
- Avoid mutating collections during iteration.
- Bind iterator methods when extracting them.
- Use `Array.from` or target-appropriate helpers for compatibility.
- Clean up manually when not using `for...of`/`for await...of`.
- Prefer `IterableIterator<T>` when an iterator must also be iterable.

In TypeScript, “lambda functions” are **arrow functions**. They are first-class values, support lexical `this`, implicit returns, closures, and higher-order programming. Below is a complete, runnable example.

## `lambdas.ts`

```ts
// lambdas.ts
// Run with: npx ts-node lambdas.ts

type NumberFn = (n: number) => number;

// 1. Basic lambda with explicit types and explicit return
const add = (a: number, b: number): number => {
  return a + b;
};

// 2. Implicit return
const multiply = (a: number, b: number): number => a * b;

// 3. Single parameter: parentheses optional
const square: NumberFn = n => n * n;

// 4. No parameters
const now = (): string => new Date().toISOString();

// 5. String template lambda
const greet = (name: string): string => `Hello, ${name}!`;

// 6. Generic lambda
const identity = <T>(value: T): T => value;

// 7. Closure / factory
const makeAdder = (x: number): ((y: number) => number) => (y: number) => x + y;

// 8. Higher-order usage with array methods
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const doubled = numbers.map((n: number): number => n * 2);
const evens = numbers.filter((n: number): boolean => n % 2 === 0);

// 9. Reduce with a lambda
const product = numbers.reduce(
  (acc: number, n: number): number => acc * n,
  1
);

// 10. Lexical `this` example
class Counter {
  private count = 0;

  // Arrow function keeps `this` bound to the Counter instance
  increment = (): void => {
    this.count++;
  };

  getCount = (): number => this.count;
}

// 11. Async lambda
const fetchNumber = async (url: string): Promise<number> => {
  // Dummy async operation; replace with real fetch in browser/Node fetch environments
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10));
  return 42;
};

// 12. Immediately invoked lambda expression (IIFE)
const area = ((width: number, height: number): number => width * height)(4, 5);

// 13. Destructuring parameters
const pointDistance = ({ x, y }: { x: number; y: number }): number =>
  Math.sqrt(x * x + y * y);

// Demonstration
function main(): void {
  console.log("add(2, 3) =", add(2, 3));
  console.log("multiply(4, 5) =", multiply(4, 5));
  console.log("square(6) =", square(6));
  console.log("now() =", now());
  console.log(greet("TypeScript"));
  console.log("identity(123) =", identity(123));
  console.log("identity('abc') =", identity("abc"));

  const addTen = makeAdder(10);
  console.log("addTen(5) =", addTen(5));

  console.log("doubled =", doubled);
  console.log("evens =", evens);
  console.log("product =", product);

  const counter = new Counter();
  counter.increment();
  counter.increment();
  console.log("counter count =", counter.getCount());

  fetchNumber("https://example.com").then(value => {
    console.log("async lambda result =", value);
  });

  console.log("area =", area);
  console.log("pointDistance({x:3, y:4}) =", pointDistance({ x: 3, y: 4 }));
}

main();
```

