## Why the corrected version is better

### Clear responsibilities

- **Model** owns domain state and validation.
- **View** owns DOM structure, rendering, and user-event capture.
- **Controller** coordinates the flow: receive input from the view, update the model, persist through a repository, then ask the view to render.
- **Repository** owns persistence details.

### Easier testing

- `ProfileModel` can be tested without a browser or DOM.
- `ProfileView` can be tested by injecting a container element.
- `ProfileController` can be tested with fake model/view/repository implementations.
- Storage can be swapped from `localStorage` to an API or database without changing controller logic.

### Lower coupling

- The model no longer knows anything about HTML.
- The view no longer depends on model-produced markup.
- The controller no longer queries specific DOM IDs or calls `alert()`.
- Persistence is hidden behind `ProfileRepository`.

### Safer rendering

Using `escapeHtml()` prevents user-controlled values from being injected directly into `innerHTML`, which reduces accidental XSS risk when rendering form values.

This structure keeps MVC meaningful: the model protects domain rules, the view presents data, and the controller orchestrates rather than absorbing every responsibility.

Below are the most common MVC pitfalls in TypeScript, with “bad” and “better” examples. The examples are framework-agnostic (you can map them to Angular, React-MVC hybrids, or vanilla TS).

---

### 1. Fat controller / anemic model

**Pitfall:** business rules end up in the controller, and the model becomes a dumb data bag.

```ts
// BAD: controller decides prices, discounts, validation
class OrderController {
  constructor(private view: OrderView) {}

  submit(items: any[]) {
    const total = items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0);
    const discount = total > 100 ? 0.1 : 0;
    if (items.length === 0) throw new Error("empty");
    this.view.showTotal(total * (1 - discount));
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: model owns invariants, controller orchestrates
class Order {
  constructor(private lines: OrderLine[]) {}

  total(): number {
    return this.lines.reduce((s, l) => s + l.subtotal(), 0);
  }

  discount(): number {
    return this.total() > 100 ? 0.1 : 0;
  }

  payable(): number {
    return this.total() * (1 - this.discount());
  }

  static validate(lines: OrderLine[]): void {
    if (lines.length === 0) throw new Error("empty order");
  }
}

class OrderController {
  constructor(private view: OrderView) {}

  submit(items: OrderLine[]) {
    Order.validate(items);
    const order = new Order(items);
    this.view.showTotal(order.payable());
  }
}
```

---

### 2. Direct DOM manipulation in the controller

**Pitfall:** controller knows about HTML, making it untestable and view-swappable only with pain.

```ts
// BAD
class UserController {
  save(name: string) {
    document.getElementById("status")!.textContent = "Saving...";
    fetch(`/users`, { method: "POST", body: name })
      .then(() => {
        document.getElementById("status")!.textContent = "Saved";
      });
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: view abstraction; controller never touches DOM
interface UserView {
  showSaving(): void;
  showSaved(): void;
  showError(e: Error): void;
}

class UserController {
  constructor(private view: UserView, private api: UserApi) {}

  async save(name: string) {
    this.view.showSaving();
    try {
      await this.api.create(name);
      this.view.showSaved();
    } catch (e) {
      this.view.showError(e as Error);
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 3. Tight coupling to concrete classes

**Pitfall:** controller/model instantiate concrete views or services, blocking testing and substitution.

```ts
// BAD
class ProductController {
  private view = new HtmlProductView(); // hard-coded
  private db = new PostgresProductRepository(); // hard-coded

  load(id: string) {
    const p = this.db.find(id);
    this.view.render(p);
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: depend on interfaces, inject dependencies
interface ProductView { render(p: Product): void; }
interface ProductRepository { find(id: string): Promise<Product>; }

class ProductController {
  constructor(
    private view: ProductView,
    private repo: ProductRepository
  ) {}

  async load(id: string) {
    const p = await this.repo.find(id);
    this.view.render(p);
  }
}
```

---

### 4. Mutable shared state in the model

**Pitfall:** views/models share mutable arrays/objects; updates are lost or produce inconsistent renders.

```ts
// BAD: mutating shared array
class TodoModel {
  items: Todo[] = [];

  add(t: Todo) {
    this.items.push(t); // mutation, no notification
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: immutable update + typed event emitter
type TodoListener = (items: readonly Todo[]) => void;

class TodoModel {
  private items: Todo[] = [];
  private listeners = new Set<TodoListener>();

  subscribe(fn: TodoListener): () => void {
    this.listeners.add(fn);
    return () => this.listeners.delete(fn);
  }

  add(t: Todo): void {
    this.items = [...this.items, t]; // immutable
    this.emit();
  }

  private emit() {
    const snapshot = Object.freeze([...this.items]);
    this.listeners.forEach(l => l(snapshot));
  }
}
```

---

### 5. Memory leaks from listeners

**Pitfall:** views subscribe to models but never unsubscribe; DOM nodes/controllers stay alive.

```ts
// BAD
class ChartView {
  constructor(model: SalesModel) {
    model.on("change", () => this.render(model.data));
    // no cleanup -> leak on unmount
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: explicit dispose; use AbortController-style or returned unsubscribe
class ChartView {
  private unsubs: (() => void)[] = [];

  constructor(private model: SalesModel) {
    this.unsubs.push(
      model.subscribe(data => this.render(data))
    );
  }

  dispose() {
    this.unsubs.forEach(u => u());
    this.unsubs = [];
  }

  private render(data: SalesData) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### 6. `any` and weak typing in events/data

**Pitfall:** untyped payloads make controller/model boundaries unsafe and refactor errors invisible.

```ts
// BAD
class EventBus {
  emit(event: string, payload: any) {}
  on(event: string, cb: (p: any) => void) {}
}

controller.on("userLoaded", (u: any) => view.render(u.name.toUpperCase()));
// runtime crash if payload shape changes
```

```ts
// BETTER: typed event map with discriminated unions
type AppEvents = {
  userLoaded: { id: string; name: string };
  userSaved: { id: string };
};

class TypedBus<E extends Record<string, unknown>> {
  private handlers = new Map<keyof E, Set<(p: any) => void>>();

  on<K extends keyof E>(ev: K, cb: (p: E[K]) => void): () => void {
    const set = this.handlers.get(ev) ?? new Set();
    set.add(cb as any);
    this.handlers.set(ev, set);
    return () => set.delete(cb as any);
  }

  emit<K extends keyof E>(ev: K, payload: E[K]): void {
    this.handlers.get(ev)?.forEach(cb => cb(payload));
  }
}

const bus = new TypedBus<AppEvents>();
```

---

### 7. Ignoring async boundaries and race conditions

**Pitfall:** controller triggers model update, then reads stale state; views render out of order.

```ts
// BAD
class SearchController {
  constructor(private model: SearchModel, private view: SearchView) {}

  query(q: string) {
    this.model.search(q); // fire-and-forget
    this.view.show(this.model.results); // may use old results
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: await, guard stale requests, single source of truth
class SearchController {
  private latest = 0;

  constructor(private model: SearchModel, private view: SearchView) {}

  async query(q: string) {
    const token = ++this.latest;
    this.view.showLoading();
    try {
      const results = await this.model.search(q);
      if (token !== this.latest) return; // stale response
      this.view.showResults(results);
    } catch (e) {
      if (token === this.latest) this.view.showError(e as Error);
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 8. Circular dependencies

**Pitfall:** Model imports View or Controller imports Model which imports Controller; bundler/runtime errors, hidden coupling.

```ts
// BAD: model knows view
import { ProductView } from "./view";

class ProductModel {
  constructor(private view: ProductView) {}
  update(p: Product) {
    this.view.render(p); // cycle waiting to happen
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: model emits; controller mediates view update
// model.ts
class ProductModel {
  private listeners = new Set<(p: Product) => void>();
  subscribe(cb: (p: Product) => void) { this.listeners.add(cb); return () => this.listeners.delete(cb); }
  set(p: Product) { this.listeners.forEach(cb => cb(p)); }
}

// controller.ts
class ProductController {
  constructor(model: ProductModel, view: ProductView) {
    model.subscribe(p => view.render(p));
  }
}
```

---

### 9. Validation only in the view

**Pitfall:** UI validation is bypassed by API calls, tests, or model-to-model flows; invariants broken.

```ts
// BAD: only input handler validates
input.addEventListener("change", e => {
  if (Number(e.target.value) < 0) alert("bad");
});
```

```ts
// BETTER: model enforces invariants; view reflects errors
class Price {
  constructor(private value: number) {
    if (!Number.isFinite(value) || value < 0) {
      throw new RangeError("price must be non-negative finite number");
    }
  }
  get amount() { return this.value; }
}

class ProductModel {
  setPrice(raw: number) {
    try {
      this.price = new Price(raw);
      this.emit("ok");
    } catch (e) {
      this.emit("error", e as Error);
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 10. Global singletons / service locators

**Pitfall:** hidden dependencies make tests order-dependent and components non-reusable.

```ts
// BAD
class OrderController {
  save() {
    GlobalStore.instance.orders.push(...); // hidden global
  }
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: explicit constructor injection
interface OrderRepository { save(o: Order): Promise<void>; }

class OrderController {
  constructor(
    private view: OrderView,
    private repo: OrderRepository
  ) {}

  async save(o: Order) {
    await this.repo.save(o);
    this.view.showSaved();
  }
}
```

---

### 11. Not using strict TypeScript settings

**Pitfall:** `noStrict`, implicit `any`, and non-null assertions hide MVC boundary bugs.

```json
// tsconfig.json (recommended)
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true
  }
}
```

```ts
// BAD
function renderUser(u: any) {
  document.getElementById("name")!.innerText = u.name; // trusts runtime
}

// BETTER
function renderUser(u: User, root: HTMLElement) {
  const el = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("#name");
  if (!el) throw new Error("name element missing");
  el.textContent = u.name;
}
```

---

### 12. Mixing “view model” with domain model

**Pitfall:** formatting, localization, and UI state leak into domain entities; reuse across views breaks.

```ts
// BAD: domain entity carries UI concern
class User {
  name = "";
  displayName = ""; // derived for one screen
  isSelected = false; // view state in domain
}
```

```ts
// BETTER: separate domain model from view model
interface User { id: string; name: string; }

interface UserRowVm {
  id: string;
  displayName: string; // "Dr. Smith"
  selected: boolean;
}

function toVm(u: User, selected: boolean): UserRowVm {
  return { id: u.id, displayName: `Dr. ${u.name}`, selected };
}
```

---

### Practical checklist

- Keep controllers thin: parse input, call model/service, update view.
- Keep models pure-ish: invariants, state transitions, notifications.
- Let views own rendering and DOM; expose semantic methods, not HTML.
- Cross boundaries through interfaces + constructor injection.
- Use immutable snapshots and typed events.
- Always clean up subscriptions.
- Handle async staleness and errors explicitly.
- Enable `strict` mode and avoid `any` at boundaries.

If you want, I can adapt these pitfalls to a specific stack (e.g., Angular, React with MVC-ish layering, or a custom vanilla TS app).

## Exercise

Build a small **Book Library** app using MVC in TypeScript.

Requirements:
- **Model**: stores books, supports `add`, `remove`, `list`, and `searchByAuthor`.
- **View**: prints results/messages to the console.
- **Controller**: receives user intentions, updates the model, and asks the view to render.
- Use TypeScript interfaces and classes, and keep each layer independent.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Define the shared data type

Start with a `Book` interface so the model, view, and controller agree on the shape of data.

### 2. Implement the Model

The model owns state and business rules. It should not know about the console or UI.

### 3. Implement the View

The view only renders. It does not mutate data or make decisions.

### 4. Implement the Controller

The controller is the traffic cop: it validates input, calls model methods, and triggers view updates.

### 5. Wire everything in a demo

Create instances and simulate user actions.

---

## Final solution

```ts
// book-library.ts

// -----------------------------
// 1. Shared types
// -----------------------------
export interface Book {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  author: string;
  year?: number;
}

export type NewBook = Omit<Book, "id">;

// -----------------------------
// 2. Model
// -----------------------------
export class BookModel {
  private books: Book[] = [];
  private nextId = 1;

  add(book: NewBook): Book {
    const created: Book = {
      id: String(this.nextId++),
      ...book,
    };
    this.books.push(created);
    return created;
  }

  remove(id: string): boolean {
    const index = this.books.findIndex((book) => book.id === id);
    if (index === -1) return false;
    this.books.splice(index, 1);
    return true;
  }

  all(): ReadonlyArray<Book> {
    return [...this.books];
  }

  searchByAuthor(author: string): Book[] {
    const needle = author.toLowerCase();
    return this.books.filter((book) =>
      book.author.toLowerCase().includes(needle)
    );
  }

  findById(id: string): Book | undefined {
    return this.books.find((book) => book.id === id);
  }
}

// -----------------------------
// 3. View
// -----------------------------
export class BookView {
  showBooks(books: ReadonlyArray<Book>): void {
    if (books.length === 0) {
      console.log("No books found.");
      return;
    }

    console.log("\nLibrary:");
    for (const book of books) {
      const year = book.year ? ` (${book.year})` : "";
      console.log(`- [${book.id}] ${book.title} — ${book.author}${year}`);
    }
  }

  showMessage(message: string): void {
    console.log(message);
  }
}

// -----------------------------
// 4. Controller
// -----------------------------
export class BookController {
  constructor(
    private readonly model: BookModel,
    private readonly view: BookView
  ) {}

  addBook(input: NewBook): void {
    if (!input.title.trim() || !input.author.trim()) {
      this.view.showMessage("Title and author are required.");
      return;
    }

    const book = this.model.add(input);
    this.view.showMessage(`Added: ${book.title} by ${book.author}.`);
  }

  listBooks(): void {
    this.view.showBooks(this.model.all());
  }

  removeBook(id: string): void {
    const removed = this.model.remove(id);
    if (removed) {
      this.view.showMessage(`Removed book ${id}.`);
    } else {
      this.view.showMessage(`Book ${id} not found.`);
    }
  }

  searchByAuthor(author: string): void {
    const results = this.model.searchByAuthor(author);
    this.view.showBooks(results);
  }
}

// -----------------------------
// 5. Demo / wiring
// -----------------------------
function main(): void {
  const model = new BookModel();
  const view = new BookView();
  const controller = new BookController(model, view);

  controller.addBook({ title: "The Hobbit", author: "J.R.R. Tolkien", year: 1937 });
  controller.addBook({ title: "1984", author: "George Orwell", year: 1949 });
  controller.addBook({ title: "Animal Farm", author: "George Orwell", year: 1945 });

  controller.listBooks();

  console.log("\nSearch author: Orwell");
  controller.searchByAuthor("Orwell");

  console.log("\nRemove book 2");
  controller.removeBook("2");

  controller.listBooks();
}

main();
```

### How to run

```bash
npx ts-node book-library.ts
```

