## 5. Shared memory example (optional, advanced)

By default, `postMessage` clones data. For very large buffers, use `SharedArrayBuffer` and `Atomics`.

`src/shared-worker.ts`

```ts
import { parentPort, workerData } from 'worker_threads';

const { sab } = workerData as { sab: SharedArrayBuffer };
const view = new Int32Array(sab);

for (let i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
  Atomics.add(view, 0, 1);
}

parentPort?.postMessage('done');
```

`src/shared-main.ts` (you can adapt `main.ts` to call this instead)

```ts
import { Worker } from 'worker_threads';
import path from 'path';

const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(4);
const view = new Int32Array(sab);
Atomics.store(view, 0, 0);

const worker = new Worker(path.join(__dirname, 'shared-worker.js'), {
  workerData: { sab },
});

// Main thread also increments
for (let i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
  Atomics.add(view, 0, 1);
}

worker.on('message', () => {
  console.log('Final shared counter:', Atomics.load(view, 0));
  // Expected: 2_000_000
});
```

Notes:

- In Node.js, `SharedArrayBuffer` works without special headers.
- In browsers, `SharedArrayBuffer` requires cross-origin isolation (`COOP`/`COEP` headers).
- Always use `Atomics` for reads/writes to shared memory.

