## 5. Patterns and best practices

### Pool workers instead of spawning per task
```ts
import Piscina from 'piscina';
const pool = new Piscina({ filename: './worker.js' });
const result = await pool.run({ n: 40 });
```
(Piscina is a popular Node worker pool.)

### Typed messages
```ts
type InMsg = { type: 'compute'; payload: number[] };
type OutMsg = { type: 'result'; value: number };

// In worker:
parentPort?.on('message', (m: InMsg) => { ... });
```

### Terminate when done
```ts
worker.terminate(); // browser
// or worker.unref() in Node to allow process exit
```

### Error handling
Workers can crash independently; always attach `error` / `messageerror` handlers.

---

## 6. Concurrency vs. parallelism (important distinction)

```ts
// Concurrency (single thread, async) — good for I/O
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetch('/a'), fetch('/b')]);

// Parallelism (multiple threads) — good for CPU
const [r1, r2] = await Promise.all([
  pool.run(task1),
  pool.run(task2),
]);
```

