## Why this design works

| Concern | How it's handled |
|---|---|
| Coupling | Subject only knows the `Observer` interface, not concrete displays. |
| Open/Closed | New display types (e.g., `WebDashboardDisplay`) can be added without touching `WeatherStation`. |
| Subscription lifecycle | `attach`/`detach` make it explicit; `Set` prevents duplicate observers. |
| Noise control | `setTemperature` skips notification when value is unchanged. |

## Common pitfalls to avoid

1. **Memory leaks** — If observers hold references to the subject (or vice versa) and never detach, GC can't collect them. In long-lived apps, prefer weak references or an explicit unsubscribe (e.g., returning an unsubscribe function like RxJS).
2. **Re-entrancy** — An observer calling `setTemperature` inside `update` can cause infinite recursion. Guard against it or defer notifications.
3. **Ordering guarantees** — Observers are notified in insertion order here, but don't rely on it for correctness; design observers to be independent.
4. **Error isolation** — One throwing observer can break the notification loop. Wrap `observer.update(...)` in `try/catch` in production.

## Final consolidated solution

```typescript
// observer.ts
export interface Observer { update(temperature: number): void; }
export interface Subject { attach(o: Observer): void; detach(o: Observer): void; notify(): void; }

// weather-station.ts
export class WeatherStation implements Subject {
  private observers = new Set<Observer>();
  private temperature = 0;

  attach(o: Observer) { this.observers.add(o); }
  detach(o: Observer) { this.observers.delete(o); }
  notify() { for (const o of this.observers) { try { o.update(this.temperature); } catch (e) { console.error(e); } } }
  setTemperature(v: number) { if (v !== this.temperature) { this.temperature = v; this.notify(); } }
  getTemperature() { return this.temperature; }
}

// displays.ts
export class ConsoleDisplay implements Observer {
  update(t: number) { console.log(`Console: ${t}°C`); }
}
export class AlertDisplay implements Observer {
  constructor(private threshold: number) {}
  update(t: number) {
    if (t > this.threshold) console.warn(`ALERT ${t}°C > ${this.threshold}`);
    else console.log(`Alert: OK ${t}°C`);
  }
}

// main.ts
const station = new WeatherStation();
const consoleD = new ConsoleDisplay();
const alertD = new AlertDisplay(30);
