### Reading the results

| Artifact | How to consume |
|---|---|
| Console `[time]` / `[measure]` | Quick sanity numbers; `measure` entries also appear in DevTools Performance panel when run with `--inspect`. |
| `heavy-workload.cpuprofile` | Drag into Chrome DevTools → **Performance** → "Load profile", or open with `speedscope` (`npx speedscope heavy-workload.cpuprofile`) for a flame graph. |
| `*.heapsnapshot` | Chrome DevTools → **Memory** → "Load" → compare "before"/"after" to find retained objects / leaks. |
| Event-loop lag lines | Indicate synchronous blocking (e.g., `fib(40)` will print large lag). Fix with chunking, worker threads, or moving CPU work to `worker_threads`. |

### Built-in Node profilers (no code changes)

```bash
# V8 sampling profiler -> text report
node --prof dist/profile.js
node --prof-process isolate-*.log > profile.txt

# Attach Chrome DevTools for live CPU/heap profiling
node --inspect dist/profile.js
# open chrome://inspect

# Memory: heap snapshots on demand via SIGUSR2 (Node >= 20 with --heapsnapshot-signal)
node --heapsnapshot-signal=SIGUSR2 dist/profile.js
kill -USR2 <pid>
```

### Practical tips

- **Don't profile in dev mode only.** Run with `NODE_ENV=production` and realistic data sizes; JIT warm-up changes numbers (run hot loops a few times before measuring).
- **Async vs sync:** `time()`/`measured()` here detect Promises and time the full resolution. For micro-benchmarks prefer `process.hrtime.bigint()` or repeated runs.
- **Worker threads:** Move CPU-bound `fib`/sort work to `worker_threads` and profile the worker separately with its own inspector session.
- **Sampling interval trade-off:** Smaller `samplingIntervalUs` (e.g., 10 µs) gives finer detail but larger files and overhead; 100 µs is a good default.
- **Avoid observer overhead in hot paths:** `PerformanceObserver` callbacks add cost; disable or gate behind an env flag in production.
- **Memory leaks:** Compare two heap snapshots; sort by "Retained size" and look for growing `(array)` / closure groups.

This gives you a complete, runnable baseline covering timing, marks, CPU flames, heap snapshots, event-loop health, and memory — all in idiomatic TypeScript.

Performance profiling in TypeScript happens at **two layers**: the TypeScript compiler/type-checker (compile time) and the emitted JavaScript runtime. TypeScript itself adds no new profiling syntax; you use standard JavaScript/Node/browser APIs plus compiler flags. Source maps are what let profilers point back from compiled JS to your `.ts` files.

## The idea

- **Runtime profiling** asks: which functions, I/O, or allocations make the running app slow or memory-heavy?
- **Compile-time profiling** asks: which types, files, or project references make `tsc` slow?
- TypeScript is erased at runtime, so runtime profiles are really JavaScript profiles; source maps make them readable as TypeScript.
- Good profiling is iterative: measure → identify bottleneck → change → measure again.

## Common syntax and tools

### 1. Quick timers in code

```ts
function expensive(n: number): number {
  const start = performance.now();
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) total += i;
  console.log(`expensive took ${(performance.now() - start).toFixed(2)}ms`);
  return total;
}
```

### 2. Named marks/measures (better for DevTools)

```ts
performance.mark("work:start");

doWork();

performance.mark("work:end");
performance.measure("work", "work:start", "work:end");

const [entry] = performance.getEntriesByName("work");
console.log(entry.duration);
```

### 3. Node.js CPU/heap profiling

```bash
# CPU profile readable in Chrome DevTools
node --cpu-prof dist/main.js

# Heap snapshot/profile
node --heap-prof dist/main.js

# Interactive debugging/profiling
node --inspect dist/main.js
```

Then open `chrome://inspect` or load the generated `.cpuprofile`/`.heapprofile` in DevTools.

### 4. Browser profiling

Use Chrome DevTools **Performance** and **Memory** tabs. Enable source maps so stack traces point to `.ts`:

```json
// tsconfig.json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "sourceMap": true,
    "declarationMap": true
  }
}
```

### 5. TypeScript compiler profiling

```bash
# High-level timing and project stats
npx tsc --noEmit --extendedDiagnostics

# Detailed compiler trace for Chrome tracing/perfetto
npx tsc --generateTrace ./trace
```

Open `./trace/trace.json` in `chrome://tracing` or Perfetto. This is especially useful for slow type-checking, complex generics, or large monorepos.

### 6. Node runtime hooks for more control

```ts
import { performance, PerformanceObserver } from "node:perf_hooks";

const obs = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
  for (const entry of list.getEntries()) {
    console.log(entry.name, entry.duration.toFixed(2), "ms");
  }
});
obs.observe({ entryTypes: ["measure"] });

performance.mark("a");
heavyTask();
performance.mark("b");
performance.measure("heavyTask", "a", "b");
```

## Typical use cases

### Runtime bottlenecks

```ts
async function loadUsers(ids: string[]) {
  performance.mark("load:start");

  // Bad: sequential awaits
  // const users = [];
  // for (const id of ids) users.push(await fetchUser(id));

  // Better: bounded concurrency
  const users = await mapWithConcurrency(ids, fetchUser, 8);

  performance.mark("load:end");
  performance.measure("loadUsers", "load:start", "load:end");
  return users;
}
```

Profile to decide whether the win comes from concurrency, caching, or reducing payload size.

### Memory leaks / growth

```ts
const cache = new Map<string, LargeObject>();

function get(key: string) {
  if (!cache.has(key)) {
    cache.set(key, createLargeObject(key)); // check whether this grows unbounded
  }
  return cache.get(key)!;
}
```

Use heap snapshots over time in DevTools or `--heap-prof` to find retained objects.

### Slow TypeScript compilation

Common causes: deep recursive types, excessive conditional types, huge unions, or poor project references. Use:

```bash
npx tsc --noEmit --extendedDiagnostics
npx tsc --generateTrace ./trace
```

Then inspect which files or type operations dominate.

### Bundle/startup profiling

```bash
# Example: profile Node startup/import cost
node --cpu-prof --cpu-prof-dir=. dist/index.js
```

Combine with bundler analysis (e.g. webpack/rollup/vite bundle visualizers) to reduce initial load.

## Practical workflow

1. Reproduce with a realistic workload.
2. Capture a profile rather than guessing.
3. Optimize the hottest path first.
4. Re-run the same profile to confirm improvement.
5. Keep source maps enabled so TypeScript-level stacks remain readable.

In short: TypeScript profiling is mostly **JavaScript profiling plus TypeScript compiler tracing**, with source maps bridging the gap between emitted JS and your original `.ts` code.

A common mistake is to time a function once, on a cold run, using `Date.now()`, and to log or otherwise use values in a way that leaves the benchmark vulnerable to JIT warmup, GC, clock resolution, and dead-code elimination.

### Mistake

```ts
const data = Array.from({ length: 1_000_000 }, (_, i) => i);

function sumArray(arr: readonly number[]): number {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    total += arr[i];
  }
  return total;
}

const start = Date.now();
const answer = sumArray(data);
console.log("answer:", answer, "elapsed ms:", Date.now() - start);
```

### Corrected version

```ts
const data = Array.from({ length: 1_000_000 }, (_, i) => i);
const EXPECTED = ((1_000_000 - 1) * 1_000_000) / 2;

function sumArray(arr: readonly number[]): number {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    total += arr[i];
  }
  return total;
}

// Warm up the JIT so the measured run is not dominated by compilation.
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (sumArray(data) !== EXPECTED) {
    throw new Error("warmup result mismatch");
  }
}

const RUNS = 30;
const samples: number[] = [];

for (let i = 0; i < RUNS; i++) {
  const start = performance.now();
  const result = sumArray(data);
  const elapsed = performance.now() - start;

  // Use the result so the optimizer cannot remove the computation.
  if (result !== EXPECTED) {
    throw new Error(`unexpected result: ${result}`);
  }

  samples.push(elapsed);
}

// Sort and trim a few slow outliers, then use the median.
samples.sort((a, b) => a - b);
const trimmed = samples.slice(
  Math.floor(RUNS * 0.2),
  Math.ceil(RUNS * 0.8),
);
const median = trimmed[Math.floor(trimmed.length / 2)];

console.log(`median elapsed ms: ${median.toFixed(3)}`);
```

### What was wrong and why

1. **Cold measurement**  
   The original code measures the first call. In V8/Node or browser engines, that call may include parsing/JIT compilation, cache warming, and first-time property lookup costs. The result is not representative of steady-state performance.

2. **`Date.now()` is the wrong clock**  
   `Date.now()` has coarse resolution and is not monotonic. For short benchmarks it can return the same value twice or jump due to system clock changes. `performance.now()` is monotonic and high-resolution.

3. **Single sample**  
   One measurement is easily distorted by GC, background tasks, timer jitter, or CPU scheduling. Multiple samples plus a median or trimmed mean are much more stable.

4. **No result validation / possible dead-code elimination**  
   If the benchmark result is not used, an optimizer may reduce or remove the work. Validating against a known expected value both proves correctness and keeps the computation alive.

5. **No outlier handling**  
   A single slow run caused by GC can dominate the average. Sorting and trimming slow samples, then taking the median, reduces that noise.

6. **Logging inside or around the timed path**  
   `console.log` is relatively expensive and can distort measurements if placed inside the hot loop. Here logging happens only after all timed runs are complete.

For deeper profiling, wall-clock benchmarking is still only a starting point. Use browser/Node profiling tools, flame charts, allocation timelines, and CPU profiles to identify where time is actually spent.

Below are common performance-profiling pitfalls in TypeScript/JavaScript code, with practical examples and fixes.

---

### 1) Measuring cold code (no JIT warm-up)

The engine optimizes hot functions after they’ve run a few times. A single cold run measures interpretation + compilation, not steady-state performance.

**Pitfall**
```ts
function work(n: number): number {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) s += i * i;
  return s;
}

const t0 = performance.now();
work(1_000_000);
console.log("cold:", performance.now() - t0, "ms");
```

**Better**
```ts
// Warm up so V8/TurboFan can optimize
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) work(1_000_000);

const t1 = performance.now();
const r = work(1_000_000);
console.log("warm:", performance.now() - t1, "ms", r);
```

---

### 2) Using the wrong timer (low resolution / wrong environment)

`Date.now()` is millisecond-resolution and can be coarse. In browsers, `performance.now()` may be quantized. In Node, use `process.hrtime.bigint()` for nanosecond precision.

**Pitfall**
```ts
const start = Date.now();
doWork();
console.log(Date.now() - start); // coarse, skewed by system clock
```

**Better**
```ts
// Node.js
const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
doWork();
const end = process.hrtime.bigint();
console.log(Number(end - start) / 1e6, "ms");

// Browser
const s = performance.now();
doWork();
console.log(performance.now() - s, "ms");
```

---

### 3) Microbenchmarks that get optimized away

If the result is unused, the engine may eliminate the loop or hoist invariants, making the benchmark meaningless.

**Pitfall**
```ts
function add(a: number, b: number) { return a + b; }

const t = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {
  add(i, i + 1); // result discarded
}
console.log(performance.now() - t);
```

**Better**
```ts
let acc = 0;
const t = performance.now();
for (let i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {
  acc += add(i, i + 1);
}
console.log("result:", acc, "time:", performance.now() - t);
```

---

### 4) Ignoring GC pauses and allocations

A single run can be dominated by garbage collection. Measure allocations, warm up, and (in Node) force GC between runs.

**Pitfall**
```ts
const t = performance.now();
const big = new Array(1_000_000).fill(0).map((_, i) => ({ i }));
console.log(performance.now() - t);
// GC may happen later and is not attributed
```

**Better (Node, run with `--expose-gc`)**
```ts
const gc = (global as any).gc as (() => void) | undefined;

gc?.();
const t = performance.now();
const big = new Array(1_000_000).fill(0).map((_, i) => ({ i }));
console.log("alloc time:", performance.now() - t);
gc?.(); // observe cleanup cost separately if needed
```

---

### 5) Profiling dev builds instead of production builds

TypeScript itself is erased at runtime, but dev tooling (ts-node, source maps, unminified code, `NODE_ENV !== "production"`) changes performance characteristics.

**Pitfall**
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "bench": "ts-node src/bench.ts"
  }
}
```

**Better**
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc -p tsconfig.prod.json",
    "bench": "NODE_ENV=production node dist/bench.js"
  }
}
```

Also avoid measuring while source-map support is active if you care about raw runtime cost.

---

### 6) Incorrect async measurement

Floating promises or not awaiting inside the timed region produces misleading numbers.

**Pitfall**
```ts
console.time("fetch");
fetch("/api/data"); // not awaited
console.timeEnd("fetch"); // measures only scheduling
```

**Better**
```ts
console.time("fetch");
const res = await fetch("/api/data");
await res.json();
console.timeEnd("fetch");
```

For many tasks, measure throughput rather than one-off latency:
```ts
const N = 100;
const t = performance.now();
await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: N }, () => fetch("/api/data")));
console.log("avg ms/request:", (performance.now() - t) / N);
```

---

### 7) Trusting source-mapped profiles blindly

DevTools can map profiles back to `.ts`, but the executed JavaScript may differ (enums, decorators, downleveling, bundler transforms). Always verify the generated JS when a hotspot looks suspicious.

**Pitfall (tsconfig)**
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES5",
    "module": "CommonJS"
  }
}
```
This can introduce helpers (`__awaiter`, `__decorate`) that show up in profiles.

**Better**
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler"
  }
}
```
Then inspect `dist/*.js` for the actual hot path.

---

### 8) TypeScript-specific runtime constructs

Type annotations are erased, but `enum`, decorators, and metadata can add real runtime cost. Profiling “TypeScript” often actually profiles these constructs.

**Pitfall**
```ts
enum Status { Pending, Running, Done }

function isDone(s: Status) {
  return s === Status.Done; // enum object lookup
}
```

**Better**
```ts
const enum Status { Pending, Running, Done } // inlined at compile time

function isDone(s: Status) {
  return s === Status.Done; // becomes a constant comparison
}
```

Decorators add wrapping overhead:
```ts
function log(_target: any, key: string, desc: PropertyDescriptor) {
  const orig = desc.value;
  desc.value = function (...args: any[]) {
    console.log(key, args);
    return orig.apply(this, args);
  };
}

class Service {
  @log
  compute(x: number) { return x * 2; }
}
```
If `compute` is hot, remove or make the decorator conditional in production builds.

---

### 9) Not isolating state between runs

Global caches, memoized results, or mutated shared objects make later runs artificially fast.

**Pitfall**
```ts
const cache = new Map<number, number>();
function fib(n: number): number {
  if (cache.has(n)) return cache.get(n)!;
  const r = n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
  cache.set(n, r);
  return r;
}

// First run populates cache; second run is trivial
```

**Better**
```ts
function makeFib() {
  const cache = new Map<number, number>();
  return function fib(n: number): number {
    if (cache.has(n)) return cache.get(n)!;
    const r = n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    cache.set(n, r);
    return r;
  };
}

const fib1 = makeFib();
const fib2 = makeFib(); // fresh cache
```

---

### 10) Ignoring profiler overhead and sampling bias

CPU profilers (V8 `--prof`, Chrome DevTools) add overhead and may miss very short functions. Use tracing for wall-clock latency and sampling for CPU hotspots.

**Commands**
```bash
# Node CPU profile
node --cpu-prof --cpu-prof-dir=. dist/bench.js

# Node trace (V8)
node --trace-opt --trace-deopt dist/bench.js
```

In code, avoid measuring the profiler’s own work:
```ts
// Bad: console.log inside hot loop
for (let i = 0; i < 1e7; i++) {
  console.log(i); // I/O dominates profile
}

// Better: aggregate, then log once
let sum = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < 1e7; i++) sum += i;
console.log(sum);
```

---

### 11) Memory profiling without baselines / snapshots

Heap snapshots are large and retainers can be surprising. Always take a baseline, perform the action, then take a second snapshot and diff.

**Example**
```ts
import { writeHeapSnapshot } from "node:v8";

writeHeapSnapshot("baseline.heapsnapshot");

// Perform suspected leaky operation
const holders: any[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) holders.push({ data: new Array(1000).fill(i) });

writeHeapSnapshot("after.heapsnapshot");
```
Then compare in Chrome DevTools (`Memory` → `Load` snapshot → diff).

Common TS pitfall: closures capturing large scopes:
```ts
function makeHandler(bigData: number[]) {
  return () => bigData.length; // retains bigData
}
```
Fix by extracting only what’s needed:
```ts
function makeHandler(len: number) {
  return () => len;
}
```

---

### 12) Comparing algorithms with unrepresentative data

Profiling on tiny arrays or uniform data hides real-world costs (branch prediction, cache misses, sorting almost-sorted input).

**Pitfall**
```ts
const data = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => i);
benchmarkSort(data);
```

**Better**
```ts
function randomData(n: number) {
  return Array.from({ length: n }, () => Math.random() * n | 0);
}
const sizes = [1_000, 100_000, 1_000_000];
for (const n of sizes) {
  const data = randomData(n);
  const t = performance.now();
  data.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  console.log(n, performance.now() - t);
}
```

---

### Practical checklist

- Warm up before timing.  
- Use `process.hrtime.bigint()` (Node) or `performance.now()` (browser).  
- Keep results alive to avoid dead-code elimination.  
- Run in production mode with realistic `target`/`module`.  
- Await async work inside the measured region.  
- Force GC and isolate state between runs.  
- Inspect generated JS for TS constructs like enums/decorators.  
- Use CPU profiles for hotspots, heap snapshots for memory, and traces for latency.  
- Benchmark with representative data sizes and distributions.

## Exercise

You are given two arrays:

- `users`: `{ id, name, score }`
- `lookups`: `{ userId, region }`

Task: produce an enriched array containing the matching user plus `region`.

The starter implementation uses `users.find(...)` inside a loop over `lookups`. That is **O(users × lookups)**. Your exercise is to:

1. Measure the baseline with a small TypeScript benchmark harness.
2. Identify the hot spot.
3. Refactor to an indexed lookup.
4. Re-measure and verify the results are identical.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Create the intentionally slow baseline

The bottleneck is repeated linear scans:

```ts
const user = users.find(u => u.id === lookup.userId);
```

For 20,000 users and 20,000 lookups, this can do hundreds of millions of comparisons.

### Step 2: Add a reusable benchmark harness

Use `performance.now()` from `node:perf_hooks`. Run warm-up iterations first, then collect several samples and report median, min, and max.

### Step 3: Profile with Node/V8

You can run the compiled script with:

```bash
node --prof dist/profile.js
node --prof-process isolate-*.log > profile.txt
```

Or use Chrome DevTools:

```bash
node --inspect-brk dist/profile.js
```

Then open `chrome://inspect`, record a CPU profile, and look for time spent in `Array.prototype.find` or the callback.

### Step 4: Optimize

Build a `Map<number, User>` once:

```ts
const byId = new Map(users.map(user => [user.id, user]));
```

Then each lookup becomes average **O(1)**.

### Step 5: Verify correctness

Compare result length and fields. The optimized version must produce the same output in the same order.

---

## Final TypeScript solution

Save as `src/profile-exercise.ts`:

```ts
import { performance } from 'node:perf_hooks';

interface User {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly score: number;
}

interface Lookup {
  readonly userId: number;
  readonly region: string;
}

interface EnrichedUser extends User {
  readonly region: string;
}

interface BenchmarkResult<T> {
  readonly label: string;
  readonly iterations: number;
  readonly durationsMs: number[];
  readonly medianMs: number;
  readonly minMs: number;
  readonly maxMs: number;
  readonly lastValue: T;
}

function makeData(
  userCount = 20_000,
  lookupCount = 20_000,
): { users: User[]; lookups: Lookup[] } {
  const users: User[] = Array.from({ length: userCount }, (_, index) => ({
    id: index + 1,
    name: `user-${index + 1}`,
    score: Math.random() * 100,
  }));

  const lookups: Lookup[] = Array.from({ length: lookupCount }, (_, index) => ({
    userId: 1 + ((index * 7919) % userCount),
    region: index % 5 === 0 ? 'EU' : 'US',
  }));

  return { users, lookups };
}

function enrichNaive(users: readonly User[], lookups: readonly Lookup[]): EnrichedUser[] {
  const result: EnrichedUser[] = [];

  for (const lookup of lookups) {
    const user = users.find(candidate => candidate.id === lookup.userId);
    if (user !== undefined) {
      result.push({ ...user, region: lookup.region });
    }
  }

  return result;
}

function enrichIndexed(users: readonly User[], lookups: readonly Lookup[]): EnrichedUser[] {
  const byId = new Map(users.map(user => [user.id, user] as const));
  const result: EnrichedUser[] = [];

  for (const lookup of lookups) {
    const user = byId.get(lookup.userId);
    if (user !== undefined) {
      result.push({ ...user, region: lookup.region });
    }
  }

  return result;
}

function median(values: readonly number[]): number {
  const sorted = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const middle = Math.floor(sorted.length / 2);

  if (sorted.length % 2 === 0) {
    return (sorted[middle - 1] + sorted[middle]) / 2;
  }

  return sorted[middle];
}

function benchmark<T>(
  label: string,
  task: () => T,
  iterations = 7,
  warmupIterations = 2,
): BenchmarkResult<T> {
  // Warm up so V8 can compile and optimize the function before measuring.
  for (let i = 0; i < warmupIterations; i++) {
    task();
  }

  const durationsMs: number[] = [];
  let lastValue!: T;

  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
    const start = performance.now();
    lastValue = task();
    const end = performance.now();
    durationsMs.push(end - start);
  }

  return {
    label,
    iterations,
    durationsMs,
    medianMs: median(durationsMs),
    minMs: Math.min(...durationsMs),
    maxMs: Math.max(...durationsMs),
    lastValue,
  };
}

function resultsEqual(a: readonly EnrichedUser[], b: readonly EnrichedUser[]): boolean {
  if (a.length !== b.length) {
    return false;
  }

  return a.every((item, index) => {
    const other = b[index];
    return (
      item.id === other.id &&
      item.name === other.name &&
      item.score === other.score &&
      item.region === other.region
    );
  });
}

function main(): void {
  const { users, lookups } = makeData();

  console.log(`Users: ${users.length.toLocaleString()}`);
  console.log(`Lookups: ${lookups.length.toLocaleString()}`);
  console.log('');

  const naive = benchmark('naive: Array.find in loop', () =>
    enrichNaive(users, lookups),
  );

  const indexed = benchmark('indexed: Map.get in loop', () =>
    enrichIndexed(users, lookups),
  );

  console.table([
    {
      strategy: naive.label,
      medianMs: naive.medianMs.toFixed(2),
      minMs: naive.minMs.toFixed(2),
      maxMs: naive.maxMs.toFixed(2),
    },
    {
      strategy: indexed.label,
      medianMs: indexed.medianMs.toFixed(2),
      minMs: indexed.minMs.toFixed(2),
      maxMs: indexed.maxMs.toFixed(2),
    },
  ]);

  const speedup = naive.medianMs / indexed.medianMs;
  console.log(`Median speedup: ${speedup.toFixed(1)}x`);
  console.log(`Results identical: ${resultsEqual(naive.lastValue, indexed.lastValue)}`);
}

main();
```

### Run it

Compile and run:

```bash
npx tsc src/profile-exercise.ts \
  --target es2022 \
  --module commonjs \
  --moduleResolution node \
  --esModuleInterop \
  --strict \
  --outDir dist

node dist/profile-exercise.js
```

Or, if you prefer not to compile manually:

```bash
npx tsx src/profile-exercise.ts
```

### Expected result

On a typical modern machine with 20,000 users and 20,000 lookups, you should see something like:

```text
Users: 20,000
Lookups: 20,000

┌─────────┬────────────────────────────┬──────────┬────────┬────────┐
│ (index) │ strategy                   │ medianMs │ minMs  │ maxMs  │
├─────────┼────────────────────────────┼──────────┼────────┼────────┤
│ 0       │ naive: Array.find in loop  │ 250.00   │ 240.00 │ 270.00 │
│ 1       │ indexed: Map.get in loop   │ 3.00     │ 2.80   │ 3.40   │
└─────────┴────────────────────────────┴──────────┴────────┴────────┘

Median speedup: 83.3x
Results identical: true
```

The exact numbers will vary, but the indexed version should be dramatically faster.

### Key takeaways

- **Profile before optimizing.** The `find` inside the loop is the measurable hotspot.
- **Choose the right data structure.** A `Map` turns repeated linear search into constant-time lookup.
- **Keep optimizations behavior-preserving.** The `resultsEqual` check proves the refactor did not change output.
- **Use warm-up iterations.** V8 JIT compilation can distort the first few measurements.
- **Report median, not just one sample.** This reduces noise from GC pauses or OS scheduling.

## Performance profiling in TypeScript — interview-style answer

### 1. What profiling means here
Performance profiling is the practice of **measuring where time and resources go** in a running program. In TypeScript/JavaScript, profiling usually focuses on:

- **CPU time** – how long functions take
- **Call frequency** – how often hot paths run
- **Latency distribution** – min / max / average / p99
- **Memory** – heap growth, retained objects, GC pressure
- **Async work** – promises, timers, I/O, event-loop stalls
- **Render/UI work** – in browsers, layout/paint (outside core JS)

TypeScript itself adds **no runtime overhead** for types; profiling is really JavaScript runtime profiling plus build/tooling concerns (transpilation, bundling, source maps).

### 2. Common approaches
| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|---|---|---|
| **Instrumentation** (`console.time`, wrappers) | Exact, deterministic | Overhead, code changes |
| **Sampling profiler** (Node `--prof`, Chrome DevTools) | Low overhead, call stacks | Statistical, less exact |
| **Performance API** (`performance.now`, `performance.mark`) | High-res, browser/Node standard | Manual aggregation |
| **Memory snapshots** | Finds leaks/retention | Expensive, pauses |
| **Event-loop monitoring** | Detects blocking | Indirect |

### 3. Key pitfalls
- **JIT warm-up**: first runs are slower; benchmark after warm-up.
- **GC noise**: force/avoid GC between measurements.
- **Async boundaries**: `await` splits execution; measure wall time vs CPU time.
- **Reentrancy**: same function can be active multiple times (recursion/async).
- **Overhead**: profiler itself must be cheap or disabled in production.
- **Source maps**: profile generated JS, map back to TS.

### 4. Core implementation
Below is a small, production-style **instrumentation profiler**. It supports sync + async functions, reentrant/nested timers, error tracking, stats aggregation, memory deltas, and reporting.

```ts
// profiler.ts

/**
 * High-resolution time source.
 * Works in browsers and Node.js.
 */
function nowMs(): number {
  if (typeof performance !== "undefined" && typeof performance.now === "function") {
    return performance.now();
  }
  // Node.js fallback (very rare nowadays, but safe)
  const hr = (globalThis as any).process?.hrtime?.();
  if (Array.isArray(hr)) return hr[0] * 1000 + hr[1] / 1e6;
  return Date.now();
}

/**
 * Best-effort heap usage in bytes.
 * Returns undefined when unavailable.
 */
function heapUsedBytes(): number | undefined {
  // Node.js
  if (typeof process !== "undefined" && typeof process.memoryUsage === "function") {
    return process.memoryUsage().heapUsed;
  }
  // Chrome (non-standard, but commonly available)
  const perfMemory = (globalThis as any).performance?.memory;
  if (perfMemory && typeof perfMemory.usedJSHeapSize === "number") {
    return perfMemory.usedJSHeapSize;
  }
  return undefined;
}

export interface TimingStats {
  name: string;
  calls: number;
  totalTimeMs: number;
  minMs: number;
  maxMs: number;
  avgMs: number;
  errors: number;
  /** Largest observed heap delta for this label (bytes), if memory tracking enabled. */
  maxHeapDeltaBytes?: number;
}

interface ActiveTimer {
  name: string;
  startMs: number;
  startHeap?: number;
}

interface MutableStats extends TimingStats {
  // internal accumulator; avg is derived
}

/**
 * A lightweight profiler for TS/JS code.
 *
 * Usage:
 *   const p = new Profiler();
 *   p.start("work");
 *   doWork();
 *   p.end("work");
 *
 *   // or wrap:
 *   const timed = p.wrap("work", doWork);
 *   timed();
 */
export class Profiler {
  private readonly stats = new Map<string, MutableStats>();
  private readonly stack: ActiveTimer[] = [];
  private enabled = true;

  constructor(enabled = true) {
    this.enabled = enabled;
  }

  /** Enable/disable collection without removing wrappers. */
  setEnabled(enabled: boolean): void {
    this.enabled = enabled;
  }

  /** Begin a named timer. Supports nesting and reentrancy. */
  start(name: string): void {
    if (!this.enabled) return;
    this.stack.push({
      name,
      startMs: nowMs(),
      startHeap: heapUsedBytes(),
    });
  }

  /**
   * End a named timer.
   * If `name` is provided, ends the most recent matching timer.
   * If omitted, ends the top of the stack.
   */
  end(name?: string, hadError = false): number | undefined {
    if (!this.enabled) return undefined;

    // Find target timer.
    let index = this.stack.length - 1;
    if (name) {
      for (let i = this.stack.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (this.stack[i].name === name) {
          index = i;
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    if (index < 0 || index >= this.stack.length) return undefined;

    const timer = this.stack.splice(index, 1)[0];
    const duration = nowMs() - timer.startMs;

    // Update aggregate stats.
    let rec = this.stats.get(timer.name);
    if (!rec) {
      rec = {
        name: timer.name,
        calls: 0,
        totalTimeMs: 0,
        minMs: duration,
        maxMs: duration,
        avgMs: 0,
        errors: 0,
      };
      this.stats.set(timer.name, rec);
    }

    rec.calls += 1;
    rec.totalTimeMs += duration;
    rec.minMs = Math.min(rec.minMs, duration);
    rec.maxMs = Math.max(rec.maxMs, duration);
    rec.avgMs = rec.totalTimeMs / rec.calls;
    if (hadError) rec.errors += 1;

    // Track heap delta if available.
    const endHeap = heapUsedBytes();
    if (timer.startHeap !== undefined && endHeap !== undefined) {
      const delta = endHeap - timer.startHeap;
      if (rec.maxHeapDeltaBytes === undefined || delta > rec.maxHeapDeltaBytes) {
        rec.maxHeapDeltaBytes = delta;
      }
    }

    return duration;
  }

  /** Convenience: measure an already-created duration label around a sync callback. */
  time<T>(name: string, fn: () => T): T {
    this.start(name);
    try {
      const result = fn();
      this.end(name);
      return result;
    } catch (e) {
      this.end(name, true);
      throw e;
    }
  }

  /**
   * Wrap a function so every call is profiled.
   * Preserves `this`, arguments, and supports sync/async results.
   */
  wrap<F extends (...args: any[]) => any>(name: string, fn: F): F {
    const self = this;
    const wrapped = function (this: any, ...args: any[]) {
      if (!self.enabled) return fn.apply(this, args);

      self.start(name);
      try {
        const result = fn.apply(this, args);

        // Async path: end when promise settles.
        if (result && typeof result.then === "function") {
          return result.then(
            (value: unknown) => {
              self.end(name);
              return value;
            },
            (error: unknown) => {
              self.end(name, true);
              throw error;
            }
          );
        }

        self.end(name);
        return result;
      } catch (error) {
        self.end(name, true);
        throw error;
      }
    };

    return wrapped as F;
  }

  /** Get stats for one label or all labels. */
  getStats(name?: string): TimingStats | TimingStats[] {
    if (name) {
      const rec = this.stats.get(name);
      if (!rec) throw new Error(`Unknown profile label: ${name}`);
      return { ...rec };
    }
    return Array.from(this.stats.values())
      .map((r) => ({ ...r }))
      .sort((a, b) => b.totalTimeMs - a.totalTimeMs);
  }

  /** Remove all collected stats (keeps wrappers working). */
  reset(): void {
    this.stats.clear();
    this.stack.length = 0;
  }

  /**
   * Human-readable report.
   * Sorts by total time descending.
   */
  report(): string {
    const rows = this.getStats() as TimingStats[];
    if (rows.length === 0) return "No profiling data.";

    const lines = [
      "name | calls | total(ms) | avg(ms) | min(ms) | max(ms) | errors",
      "---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:",
    ];

    for (const r of rows) {
      lines.push(
        `${r.name} | ${r.calls} | ${r.totalTimeMs.toFixed(3)} | ${r.avgMs.toFixed(3)} | ` +
          `${r.minMs.toFixed(3)} | ${r.maxMs.toFixed(3)} | ${r.errors}`
      );
    }

    return lines.join("\n");
  }
}

/* ----------------------------- Demo usage ----------------------------- */

function expensive(n: number): number {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += Math.sqrt(i);
  return sum;
}

async function fetchLikeWork(ms: number): Promise<string> {
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
  return "done";
}

// Example: run once after warm-up in real benchmarks.
const profiler = new Profiler();

const timedExpensive = profiler.wrap("expensive", expensive);
timedExpensive(1_000_000);

profiler.start("manual");
expensive(500_000);
profiler.end("manual");

const timedFetch = profiler.wrap("fetchLikeWork", fetchLikeWork);
await timedFetch(10);

console.log(profiler.report());
console.log("manual stats:", profiler.getStats("manual"));
```

### 5. How you’d extend this in production
- **Sampling**: periodically capture stacks (`Error().stack`) instead of wrapping every call.
- **Percentiles**: store a reservoir of durations for p50/p95/p99.
- **Async context**: use `AsyncLocalStorage` in Node to correlate timers across awaits.
- **Flame graphs**: export Chrome/Node trace format (`.cpuprofile`).
- **Threshold alerts**: warn if `avgMs` or `maxMs` exceeds budget.
- **Disable by env**: `new Profiler(process.env.PROFILE === "1")`.

### 6. Interview talking points
- Instrumentation gives **exact** numbers but costs CPU; sampling gives **approximate** stacks cheaply.
- Always **warm up** before measuring JIT-compiled JS.
- Separate **wall time** from **CPU time**; async I/O inflates wall time.
- TypeScript types don’t run, so profile the emitted JS and use source maps.
- For memory, look for **retained size**, not just `heapUsed`.

This implementation keeps the core concerns separate: timing, aggregation, async handling, and reporting—clean enough to drop into a service or test suite.

# Performance Profiling in TypeScript: Edge Cases & Patterns

Below are the edge cases that routinely bite teams when profiling TypeScript (Node, browser, or bundlers), with idiomatic TS examples.

---

## 1. Clock choice & resolution (monotonicity, throttling, GC timing)

**Problem:** `Date.now()` can go backwards or jump; `performance.now()` may be throttled in background tabs / some sandboxes; `process.hrtime` exists only in Node.

**Edge cases:**
- Cross-platform code that assumes one timer.
- Measuring GC pauses with userland timers (GC can freeze timers).
- BigInt ticks overflow if mishandled.

```ts
// timer.ts
export type Clock = () => number; // ms

export function makeClock(): Clock {
  // Node: monotonic, high-res
  if (typeof process !== "undefined" && typeof process.hrtime?.bigint === "function") {
    const t0 = process.hrtime.bigint();
    return () => Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - t0) / 1e6;
  }
  // Browser / Workers: performance.now (may be throttled)
  if (typeof performance !== "undefined" && typeof performance.now === "function") {
    const t0 = performance.now();
    return () => performance.now() - t0;
  }
  // Fallback (never for perf-critical code)
  const t0 = Date.now();
  return () => Date.now() - t0;
}

// Prefer BigInt for intervals to avoid float drift in long runs
export function measureNs(fn: () => void): bigint {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  fn();
  return process.hrtime.bigint() - start;
}
```

**Rule:** Never mix `Date.now` and `performance.now` in one measurement; calibrate clocks per runtime.

---

## 2. JIT warm-up & cold-start effects

**Problem:** First calls trigger compile + optimization; later calls run optimized. Measuring cold path gives misleading “slow”.

```ts
// warmup.ts
interface BenchmarkResult { medianMs: number; samples: number[] }

export async function benchmark(
  fn: () => void | Promise<void>,
  opts = { warmup: 1000, samples: 100 }
): Promise<BenchmarkResult> {
  // Warm-up (synchronously; let V8 tier up)
  for (let i = 0; i < opts.warmup; i++) {
    const r = fn();
    if (r && typeof (r as any).then === "function") await r;
  }

  // Optional: deopt guard? Force no GC during sampling (Node)
  const samples: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < opts.samples; i++) {
    const t0 = performance.now();
    const r = fn();
    if (r && typeof (r as any).then === "function") await r;
    samples.push(performance.now() - t0);
  }

  samples.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  return { medianMs: samples[Math.floor(samples.length / 2)], samples };
}
```

**Edge:** Async warmup must `await` each iteration; otherwise you measure scheduler, not function.

---

## 3. GC noise & forced collection (Node)

**Problem:** GC pauses masquerade as function cost.

```ts
// node-gc.ts
declare const global: { gc?: () => void };

export async function withGcControl<T>(fn: () => T | Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  if (typeof global.gc !== "function") {
    throw new Error("Run node with --expose-gc");
  }
  global.gc(); // major before
  const result = await fn();
  global.gc(); // major after (optional)
  return result;
}

// Usage:
// node --expose-gc --import tsx ./bench.ts
```

**Browser:** You cannot force GC; profile allocations instead (`PerformanceMemory`/heap snapshots) and run long-enough samples.

---

## 4. Async / event-loop boundaries

**Problem:** `await` yields; measuring wall time includes unrelated work. `PerformanceObserver` entries may arrive late or be dropped.

```ts
// async-profile.ts
export function measureAsync<T>(label: string, fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  const start = performance.now();
  return fn().finally(() => {
    // Measures until promise settles (includes microtasks)
    performance.measure(label, { start, end: performance.now() });
  });
}

// Better: track causal intervals with marks
export async function profilePipeline<T>(stage: string, work: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  const mark = `${stage}:start`;
  performance.mark(mark);
  try {
    return await work();
  } finally {
    performance.measure(stage, mark);
  }
}
```

**Edge cases for `PerformanceObserver`:** buffering, dropped entries when main thread busy, and `entry.duration` resolution limits.

```ts
const obs = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
  for (const e of list.getEntries()) {
    // e may be a PerformanceMeasure with detail
    console.log(e.name, e.duration.toFixed(3));
  }
});
obs.observe({ entryTypes: ["measure", "mark"], buffered: true });
```

---

## 5. Source maps: TS ↔ compiled JS attribution

**Problem:** Profilers attach to emitted JS; stack frames point at `.js`, not `.ts`, unless source maps enabled.

**Node:**
```bash
node --enable-source-maps --prof dist/index.js
node --prof-process isolate-*.log > profile.txt
```

**TS config:**
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "sourceMap": true,
    "inlineSourceMap": false,
    "declarationMap": true
  }
}
```

**Edge case:** V8 `--prof` + `--enable-source-maps` can inflate overhead and still show inlined frames; use Chrome DevTools “CPU profile” for TS source-map-aware view when possible.

---

## 6. Sampling profiler blind spots (recursion, native, async)

**Problem:** Sampling profilers miss very short functions, recursive collapse issues, and async continuations.

```ts
// Intentionally allocate to create measurable work; tiny helpers get inlined
function hotPath(n: number): number {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) s += i * i; // keep arithmetic
  return s;
}

// Avoid “function never appears” by ensuring call not inlined:
// use (globalThis as any).__keep = hotPath; or export + dynamic import
export const keepRef = hotPath;
```

**Edge:** Async stack traces are truncated by default; run Node with `--async-stack-traces` or use `Error.captureStackTrace` sparingly (expensive).

---

## 7. V8 deoptimizations / hidden-class churn

**Problem:** Polymorphic calls cause megamorphic call sites; profiling shows “unknown” or slow paths.

```ts
// bad: multiple shapes
function sum(a: any) { return a.x + a.y; }
sum({ x: 1, y: 2 });
sum({ x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 }); // different hidden class

// good: stable shape
interface Point { x: number; y: number }
function sumP(p: Point) { return p.x + p.y; }
```

**Inspect deopts:**
```bash
node --trace-deopt --trace-opt dist/index.js 2>&1 | grep -i deopt
```

---

## 8. Memory profiling: retainers, not just heap size

**Problem:** Comparing `process.memoryUsage().heapUsed` ignores external memory, typed arrays, and detached contexts.

```ts
// memory-snapshot.ts
export function snapshot(label: string): void {
  if (!global.gc) return;
  global.gc();
  const m = process.memoryUsage();
  console.log(label, {
    rss: m.rss,
    heapUsed: m.heapUsed,
    heapTotal: m.heapTotal,
    external: m.external,
    arrayBuffers: m.arrayBuffers,
  });
}

// For leak detection, use heap snapshots via v8:
import { writeHeapSnapshot } from "v8";
writeHeapSnapshot(`./heap-${Date.now()}.heapsnapshot`);
```

**Edge:** `heapUsed` can drop after GC even if logical allocation grows; always force GC in Node before comparing. Browser heap snapshots capture more (closures, DOM). Use `WeakRef`/`FinalizationRegistry` carefully—they can mislead profiling by delaying collection.

---

## 9. Profiler overhead (observer effect)

**Problem:** Instrumentation (marks/measure, console.time) can become the bottleneck and change behavior.

```ts
// sampling-gate.ts
const PROFILING = process.env.PROFILE === "1";

export function measureIfEnabled<T>(label: string, fn: () => T): T {
  if (!PROFILING) return fn();
  const t0 = performance.now();
  try {
    return fn();
  } finally {
    const d = performance.now() - t0;
    if (d > 1) console.log(`${label}: ${d.toFixed(2)}ms`);
  }
}
```

**Best practice:** put high-frequency paths behind a compile-time flag (`if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production')`) or strip via bundler.

---

## 10. Bundler / tree-shaking removing profiled work

**Problem:** Pure functions can be dropped; side-effect-free modules disappear; profiling code isn’t reached.

```ts
// side-effects.ts
export function expensive(n: number): number {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) s += Math.sqrt(i);
  return s;
}

// Prevent DCE in bundlers
if (process.env.KEEP_BENCH === "1") {
  (globalThis as any).__benchKeep = expensive;
}

// package.json
// "sideEffects": ["./src/bench.ts"]  (or false carefully)
```

**Edge:** Dynamic `import()` can change chunking; profile with the same bundler config as production (minification changes inlining).

---

## 11. Worker threads & serialization

**Problem:** Wall time includes `postMessage` structuring clone; CPU profile per-thread only.

```ts
// worker-main.ts
import { Worker } from "node:worker_threads";

const w = new Worker(new URL("./worker.ts", import.meta.url));
const t0 = performance.now();
w.postMessage({ payload: bigData });

w.on("message", (res) => {
  console.log("roundtrip ms:", performance.now() - t0);
  // Per-thread CPU profile: run node --prof on worker entrypoint
});
```

**Edge:** `SharedArrayBuffer` access needs Atomics; profiling atomic contention separately.

---

## 12. Background-tab / timer throttling (browser)

**Problem:** `setTimeout`/`requestAnimationFrame` throttled; `performance.now` still advances but tasks queue.

```ts
// browser.ts
export function profileInWorker(code: () => void): void {
  // Move heavy perf harness to a Web Worker to avoid tab throttling
  const blob = new Blob([`(${code.toString()})()`], { type: "text/javascript" });
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const worker = new Worker(url);
  worker.onmessage = (e) => console.log("worker result", e.data);
}
```

---

## 13. Decorators / metadata overhead (TS-specific)

**Problem:** Legacy/experimental decorators wrap methods; profiling may attribute cost to wrapper or skip original.

```ts
function Profile(target: any, key: string, desc: PropertyDescriptor) {
  const orig = desc.value;
  desc.value = function (...args: any[]) {
    const t0 = performance.now();
    const r = orig.apply(this, args);
    console.log(`${key} took ${(performance.now() - t0).toFixed(2)}ms`);
    return r;
  };
  return desc;
}

class Service {
  @Profile
  compute(n: number) { /* ... */ }
}
```

**Edge:** Decorator-based profiling changes call shape (extra function), can disable inlining; use only in dev.

---

## 14. Micro-benchmark pitfalls (tinybench / vitest bench)

**Problem:** Dead-code elimination, loop hoisting, and constant folding.

```ts
// vite bench example
import { bench, describe } from "vitest";

describe("sum", () => {
  bench("loop", () => {
    let s = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < 1e6; i++) s += i;
    // consume result to prevent DCE
    globalThis.__benchResult = s;
  });
});
```

**Edge:** Return/consume result; disable minification for bench builds.

---

## 15. Recursive profiling & stack depth

**Problem:** `Error.stackTraceLimit` truncates; recursive functions appear collapsed.

```ts
Error.stackTraceLimit = 100; // dev only
```

**Edge:** Deep recursion may stack-overflow in profiler’s wrapper; prefer iterative algorithms for perf harnesses.

---

## 16. Cross-realm & WeakMap-based caching

**Problem:** Profiling caches may retain keys; use `WeakMap` to avoid leak, but WeakMap not enumerable (harder to inspect).

```ts
const cache = new WeakMap<object, number>();

function measureCached(obj: object, compute: () => number): number {
  if (cache.has(obj)) return cache.get(obj)!;
  const v = compute();
  cache.set(obj, v);
  return v;
}
```

---

## 17. AsyncLocalStorage / context overhead

**Problem:** `AsyncLocalStorage` adds per-async-op cost; profiling with it enabled distorts real app perf.

```ts
import { AsyncLocalStorage } from "node:async_hooks";
const als = new AsyncLocalStorage<{ traceId: string }>();

// Measure with and without als.run to isolate overhead
```

**Edge:** Don’t leave tracing ALS enabled in hot paths in production.

---

## Checklist (practical)

| Edge case | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Clock drift/throttle | Per-runtime clock factory; BigInt in Node |
| Cold JIT | Warmup + discard first samples |
| GC pauses | `--expose-gc`, force GC in Node; long samples in browser |
| Async attribution | Marks across `await`; track promise lifetime |
| Source maps | `--enable-source-maps`, DevTools CPU profile |
| Inlining/deopts | `--trace-deopt`, stable hidden classes |
| Memory false drops | Force GC, heap snapshots, include external/arrayBuffers |
| Observer overhead | Compile-time flags; sampled logging |
| DCE in benches | Consume results; sideEffects config |
| Workers | Per-thread profiles; measure serialization separately |

If you want, I can tailor this to a specific runtime (Node 20+, Chrome, Bun/Deno) or show a full `tinybench` + `clinic.js` workflow.

TypeScript/JavaScript does not have raw pointers or references in the C/C++ sense. Objects are already accessed by reference, while primitives are copied by value. To teach and simulate pointer/reference semantics, we can build two abstractions:

1. **References (mutable cells)** — a `Ref<T>` box that can be shared and mutated through aliases.
2. **Pointers** — an addressable heap simulation with allocation, freeing, dereferencing, pointer arithmetic, null pointers, and use-after-free detection.

Below is one complete, runnable file. Save it as `pointers-and-references.ts`.

```ts
// pointers-and-references.ts
// Run with: npx ts-node pointers-and-references.ts
// Or: tsc pointers-and-references.ts --target es2020 --module commonjs --strict && node pointers-and-references.js

type Address = number;

export const NULLPTR: Address = 0;

const FREED = Symbol("freed");

export class MemoryError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "MemoryError";
  }
}

/**
 * Simulated heap. Address 0 is reserved as the null pointer.
 * Each allocation gets a base address; multi-element allocations
 * occupy contiguous slots, enabling pointer arithmetic.
 */
export class Heap {
  // Slot 0 is intentionally unused so that 0 can represent null.
  private slots: unknown[] = [null];

  // base address -> number of slots allocated for that allocation
  private ranges = new Map<Address, number>();

  // addresses that have been freed
  private freed = new Set<Address>();

  allocate<T>(value: T): Address {
    return this.allocateRange([value]);
  }

  allocateArray<T>(values: readonly T[]): Address {
    if (values.length === 0) {
      // Allocate a one-slot placeholder so callers still receive
      // a valid, non-null base address for an empty array.
      return this.allocateRange([]);
    }
    return this.allocateRange(values);
  }

  private allocateRange<T>(values: readonly T[]): Address {
    const base = this.slots.length;
    for (const value of values) {
      this.slots.push(value);
    }
    // Even an empty allocation occupies one sentinel slot.
    if (values.length === 0) {
      this.slots.push(undefined);
    }
    this.ranges.set(base, Math.max(values.length, 1));
    return base;
  }

  free(baseAddress: Address): void {
    const length = this.ranges.get(baseAddress);
    if (length === undefined) {
      throw new MemoryError(
        `free() expects an allocation base address; ${baseAddress} is not one`
      );
    }

    for (let i = 0; i < length; i++) {
      const addr = baseAddress + i;
      this.slots[addr] = FREED;
      this.freed.add(addr);
    }
    this.ranges.delete(baseAddress);
  }

  read<T>(address: Address): T {
    this.ensureUsable(address);
    return this.slots[address] as T;
  }

  write<T>(address: Address, value: T): void {
    this.ensureUsable(address);
    this.slots[address] = value;
  }

  offset(address: Address, n: number): Address {
    if (address === NULLPTR) {
      throw new MemoryError("pointer arithmetic on null pointer");
    }
    return address + n;
  }

  isNull(address: Address): boolean {
    return address === NULLPTR;
  }

  private ensureUsable(address: Address): void {
    if (address === NULLPTR) {
      throw new MemoryError("null pointer dereference");
    }
    if (address <= 0 || address >= this.slots.length) {
      throw new MemoryError(`invalid address: ${address}`);
    }
    if (this.freed.has(address)) {
      throw new MemoryError(`use after free: ${address}`);
    }
  }
}

/**
 * A mutable reference cell. Sharing the same Ref instance
 * gives multiple variables a shared view of one value.
 */
export class Ref<T> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}

  deref(): T {
    return this.value;
  }

  assign(value: T): void {
    this.value = value;
  }

  update(fn: (current: T) => T): void {
    this.value = fn(this.value);
  }
}

export function ref<T>(value: T): Ref<T> {
  return new Ref(value);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Demonstration and assertions
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

function assert(condition: boolean, message: string): void {
  if (!condition) {
    throw new Error(`Assertion failed: ${message}`);
  }
}

function increment(r: Ref<number>): void {
  // Mutating the shared cell is visible to all aliases.
  r.value += 1;
}

function main(): void {
  // ---------------- References ----------------
  const counter = ref(0);
  const alias = counter;

  alias.value = 41;
  assert(counter.value === 41, "alias observes mutation");

  increment(counter);
  assert(alias.value === 42, "function mutation visible through alias");

  counter.update((x) => x * 2);
  assert(counter.value === 84 && alias.value === 84, "update() mutates shared cell");

  // ---------------- Pointers / heap ----------------
  const heap = new Heap();

  const p = heap.allocate(7);
  assert(heap.read<number>(p) === 7, "read allocated value");
  heap.write(p, 9);
  assert(heap.read<number>(p) === 9, "write allocated value");

  const arrBase = heap.allocateArray<number>([10, 20, 30]);
  const p2 = heap.offset(arrBase, 2);
  assert(heap.read<number>(p2) === 30, "pointer arithmetic reaches third element");

  heap.write(heap.offset(arrBase, 1), 25);
  assert(heap.read<number>(heap.offset(arrBase, 1)) === 25, "write through offset pointer");

  // Distinct allocations have distinct addresses.
  const q = heap.allocate("hello");
  assert(p !== q, "allocations have distinct addresses");
  assert(heap.read<string>(q) === "hello", "string allocation");

  // Null pointer handling.
  let nullDerefThrew = false;
  try {
    heap.read(NULLPTR);
  } catch (error) {
    nullDerefThrew = error instanceof MemoryError;
  }
  assert(nullDerefThrew, "null pointer dereference throws MemoryError");

  // Use-after-free detection.
  const r = heap.allocate(123);
  heap.free(r);
  let useAfterFreeThrew = false;
  try {
    heap.read(r);
  } catch (error) {
    useAfterFreeThrew = error instanceof MemoryError;
  }
  assert(useAfterFreeThrew, "use after free throws MemoryError");

  // Freeing an interior/non-base pointer is rejected.
  let badFreeThrew = false;
  try {
    heap.free(heap.offset(arrBase, 1));
  } catch (error) {
    badFreeThrew = error instanceof MemoryError;
  }
  assert(badFreeThrew, "free() rejects non-base address");

  // Freeing the array base frees the whole contiguous range.
  heap.free(arrBase);
  let arrayFreedThrew = false;
  try {
    heap.read(heap.offset(arrBase, 0));
  } catch (error) {
    arrayFreedThrew = error instanceof MemoryError;
  }
  assert(arrayFreedThrew, "array base free frees all slots");

  console.log("All assertions passed: references and simulated pointers work.");
}

main();
```

## How it works

### References (`Ref<T>`)

- `Ref<T>` wraps a value in a mutable object.
- Because objects are passed by reference in JavaScript/TypeScript, two variables holding the same `Ref` instance see the same underlying `.value`.
- `assign()` and `update()` mutate the shared cell, modeling pass-by-reference behavior for primitive-like data.

### Pointers (`Heap`)

- The heap stores values in an array called `slots`.
- Address `0` is reserved as `NULLPTR`.
- `allocate()` returns the index of a new slot, simulating a pointer/address.
- `allocateArray()` allocates contiguous slots and returns the base address.
- `offset(address, n)` performs pointer arithmetic.
- `read()` and `write()` simulate dereferencing and assignment through a pointer.
- `free()` releases an allocation; reading or writing it afterward throws `MemoryError`.
- Freeing a non-base address is rejected, and freeing an array base frees its whole range.

### Running it

With `ts-node`:

```bash
npx ts-node pointers-and-references.ts
```

Or compile first:

```bash
tsc pointers-and-references.ts --target es2020 --module commonjs --strict
node pointers-and-references.js
```

Expected output:

```text
All assertions passed: references and simulated pointers work.
```

## Important limitations

This is a simulation, not true systems-level memory management:

- There is no actual hardware memory, address space, or stack/heap distinction.
- JavaScript’s garbage collector still owns the underlying objects.
- Type safety is enforced at compile time by TypeScript, but the heap itself stores `unknown` values and casts at read time.
- Pointer arithmetic is unchecked except for null and freed-slot cases; out-of-range reads are caught by bounds checks.

For real TypeScript programs, prefer ordinary object references, mutable state objects, or libraries designed for immutable updates rather than hand-rolled pointer systems.

## Typical mistake: confusing “copying” with “aliasing” a reference

In TypeScript/JavaScript there are no explicit pointers, but objects and arrays are manipulated **by reference**. A very common bug is assigning one variable to another and thinking you got an independent copy.

### ❌ Mistaken version

```ts
interface User {
  name: string;
  tags: string[];
}

const original: User = {
  name: "Alice",
  tags: ["admin", "editor"],
};

// Looks like we "copied" original into draft.
const draft = original;

// Edit the draft's tags...
draft.tags.push("viewer");
draft.name = "Alice (draft)";

console.log(original.name); // "Alice (draft)"  ← unexpected
console.log(original.tags); // ["admin", "editor", "viewer"]  ← unexpected
```

### ✅ Corrected version

```ts
interface User {
  name: string;
  tags: string[];
}

const original: User = {
  name: "Alice",
  tags: ["admin", "editor"],
};

// Create a real, independent shallow copy.
const draft: User = {
  ...original,
  tags: [...original.tags], // deep-copy the nested array
};

draft.tags.push("viewer");
draft.name = "Alice (draft)";

console.log(original.name); // "Alice"
console.log(original.tags); // ["admin", "editor"]
console.log(draft.name);    // "Alice (draft)"
console.log(draft.tags);    // ["admin", "editor", "viewer"]
```

### What was wrong

1. **`const draft = original;` does not clone anything.**  
   Both variables point to the *same object in memory*. Changing `draft` therefore changes `original` as well.

2. **Top-level spread alone is not enough.**  
   `{ ...original }` copies enumerable own properties, but nested values (like `tags`) are still shared references. That is why we also spread `tags` into a new array.

3. **`const` only prevents reassignment of the binding, not mutation of the object.**  
   `original` can still have its `.name` and `.tags` mutated even though it is declared with `const`.

### Practical takeaways

- Use spread syntax for shallow copies: `{ ...obj }`, `[...arr]`.
- For nested structures, copy each level you intend to mutate, or use a deep-clone utility (`structuredClone`, Lodash `cloneDeep`, etc.).
- If an object should never change, model it with `readonly` and enforce immutability at the API boundary:

```ts
interface ReadonlyUser {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly tags: readonly string[];
}
```

- Be especially careful with shared state in React/Redux: mutating a prop or store value directly can skip change detection and cause stale renders.

This pattern—aliasing a reference instead of cloning—is the TypeScript/JavaScript equivalent of the classic “forgot to copy the pointer” bug you see in C/C++.

TypeScript (and JavaScript) has **no raw pointers**, but it does have **object references**. Most “pointer bugs” are really **shared-reference / aliasing / mutation** bugs. Below are the common pitfalls, with code.

---

## 1. Mutating a shared object through a parameter

Functions receive a reference to the same object, so mutations leak back to the caller.

```ts
function addTag(user: { tags: string[] }, tag: string) {
  user.tags.push(tag); // mutates caller's array
}

const u = { tags: ["a"] };
addTag(u, "b");
console.log(u.tags); // ["a", "b"]  (surprise side effect)
```

**Fix:** treat inputs as immutable, or explicitly clone.

```ts
function withTag(user: { tags: string[] }, tag: string) {
  return { ...user, tags: [...user.tags, tag] };
}
```

---

## 2. Shallow copies still share nested references

Spread / `Object.assign` copy only the top level.

```ts
const original = { profile: { name: "Ada" } };
const copy = { ...original };

copy.profile.name = "Grace";
console.log(original.profile.name); // "Grace"  (nested still shared)
```

**Fix:** deep clone when needed (`structuredClone` in modern runtimes).

```ts
const deep = structuredClone(original);
deep.profile.name = "Grace";
console.log(original.profile.name); // "Ada"
```

---

## 3. `const` does **not** mean immutable

`const` only prevents reassigning the binding, not mutating properties.

```ts
const user = { name: "Ada" };
user.name = "Grace"; // allowed
// user = { name: "X" }; // error: cannot reassign
```

**Fix:** use `Readonly` / `as const` / `Object.freeze` (shallow) for intent.

---

## 4. Reference equality vs value equality

`===` on objects compares identity, not contents.

```ts
const a = { x: 1 };
const b = { x: 1 };
console.log(a === b); // false
console.log(a === a); // true
```

**Fix:** compare by value explicitly (e.g., `JSON.stringify` for simple cases, or a deep-equal library).

---

## 5. Closures capturing mutable variables (the `var` loop bug)

```ts
const funcs: (() => number)[] = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  funcs.push(() => i);
}
console.log(funcs.map(f => f())); // [3, 3, 3]
```

`let` is block-scoped per iteration, so it fixes this; but capturing mutable outer state still causes surprises.

```ts
const funcs2: (() => number)[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  funcs2.push(() => i);
}
console.log(funcs2.map(f => f())); // [0, 1, 2]
```

---

## 6. Losing `this` when passing methods as callbacks

A method reference detached from its object loses `this`.

```ts
class Counter {
  count = 0;
  inc() { this.count++; }
}

const c = new Counter();
const fn = c.inc;
fn(); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (strict mode)
```

**Fix:** bind or use arrow functions.

```ts
const bound = c.inc.bind(c);
bound(); // ok

// or define as arrow property
class Counter2 {
  count = 0;
  inc = () => { this.count++; };
}
```

---

## 7. In-place array mutation

`sort`, `reverse`, `splice`, `push`, etc. mutate the original array.

```ts
const nums = [3, 1, 2];
const sorted = nums.sort(); // mutates nums
console.log(nums);   // [1, 2, 3]
console.log(sorted); // [1, 2, 3] (same reference)
```

**Fix:** copy first.

```ts
const sorted2 = [...nums].sort();
```

---

## 8. Non-null assertions hiding `null`/`undefined`

`!` tells the compiler “trust me,” but runtime may disagree.

```ts
const el = document.getElementById("missing")!; // type says HTMLElement
el.textContent = "hi"; // runtime: Cannot set property of null
```

**Fix:** check at runtime.

```ts
const el = document.getElementById("missing");
if (el) el.textContent = "hi";
```

---

## 9. `Readonly<T>` is shallow

```ts
type User = Readonly<{ profile: { name: string } }>;
const u: User = { profile: { name: "Ada" } };

u.profile = { name: "X" }; // error (top-level readonly)
u.profile.name = "Grace";  // allowed (nested mutable)
```

**Fix:** recursive readonly types or immutable data structures.

---

## 10. `Object.freeze` is shallow

```ts
const o = Object.freeze({ nested: { x: 1 } });
o.nested.x = 2; // allowed (nested not frozen)
```

---

## 11. `Map`/`Set` keys are compared by reference

Two structurally equal objects are different keys.

```ts
const m = new Map<object, string>();
const key = { id: 1 };
m.set(key, "value");

console.log(m.get({ id: 1 })); // undefined (different reference)
console.log(m.get(key));       // "value"
```

**Fix:** use a primitive key (id string/number) or a stable canonical key.

---

## 12. Destructuring creates aliases to nested objects

Destructuring a property gives a reference to the same nested object.

```ts
const obj = { a: { b: 1 } };
const { a } = obj;
a.b = 2;
console.log(obj.a.b); // 2
```

This is sometimes desired, sometimes a bug. Be explicit about cloning.

---

## 13. `JSON` round-tripping loses types and special values

```ts
const data = { d: new Date(), u: undefined, f: () => 1 };
const copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(data));

console.log(copy.d instanceof Date); // false (string)
console.log(copy.u);                 // undefined property dropped
console.log(typeof copy.f);          // "undefined"
```

**Fix:** use `structuredClone` (for cloneable types) or a serializer that preserves types.

---

## 14. Mutating during iteration

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3, 4];
for (const x of arr) {
  if (x % 2 === 0) arr.splice(arr.indexOf(x), 1);
}
console.log(arr); // [1, 3, 4] (skipped element)
```

**Fix:** iterate over a copy or build a new array.

```ts
const result = arr.filter(x => x % 2 !== 0);
```

---

## 15. Shared default parameter objects (when you cache them)

Default params are evaluated per call, so this is usually safe—but caching a default object is not.

```ts
const DEFAULTS = { items: [] as number[] };

function f(opts = DEFAULTS) {
  opts.items.push(1); // mutates shared DEFAULTS forever
}
f(); f();
console.log(DEFAULTS.items); // [1, 1]
```

**Fix:** create defaults inside the function or clone.

---

## 16. Prototype mutation affects all instances

```ts
function Person(this: any, name: string) { this.name = name; }
const a = new (Person as any)("Ada");
const b = new (Person as any)("Grace");

(Person as any).prototype.greet = function () { return `hi ${this.name}`; };
console.log(a.greet(), b.greet()); // both see new method
```

Avoid mutating prototypes of built-ins / shared classes in application code.

---

## Practical defaults to avoid these

- Prefer **immutable updates**: spread + new arrays/objects.
- Use `structuredClone` for deep copies (where supported).
- Type with `readonly` and `ReadonlyArray<T>`.
- Bind callbacks or use arrow functions.
- Avoid non-null `!` unless you immediately guard.
- Don’t mutate function arguments; return new values.
- Compare objects by value, not `===`, when contents matter.

If you want, I can tailor this to a specific codebase pattern (e.g., React state updates, Redux, or class-based services).

## Practical exercise: “Shared reference bug” in TypeScript

TypeScript/JavaScript has **no raw pointers**, but objects and arrays are accessed through **references**. Assigning an object to another variable copies the *reference*, not the object, so mutations leak across variables. Primitives (`string`, `number`, `boolean`, etc.) are copied by value.

### Exercise

Given:

```ts
type Address = { city: string; zip: string };
type User = { id: number; name: string; address: Address; tags: string[] };

const original: User = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Alice",
  address: { city: "NYC", zip: "10001" },
  tags: ["admin", "beta"],
};
```

Do the following:

1. **Bug hunt:** explain why this mutates `original`:
   ```ts
   const draft = original;
   draft.address.city = "LA";
   draft.tags.push("new");
   ```
2. **Task A:** create `draft` so editing `draft.address.city` and pushing to `draft.tags` does **not** change `original`.
3. **Task B:** implement `sameReference(a, b)` that is `true` only when `a` and `b` point to the **exact same object** (reference equality), not just equal contents.
4. **Task C:** implement `cloneUser(u)` that returns a **deep copy**.
5. **Task D:** implement `updateCity(user, city)` that returns a **new** `User` with updated city and leaves `user` unchanged (immutable update).

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Understand reference vs. value

```ts
const a = { x: 1 };
const b = a;        // b holds a COPY of the reference, not a copy of {x:1}
b.x = 99;
console.log(a.x);   // 99  ← same object
```

For primitives there is no sharing:

```ts
let n = 5;
let m = n;          // m gets its own value
m = 10;
console.log(n);     // 5
```

So the bug in Task 0 is: `draft = original` makes two names for one object graph. Any nested mutation (`draft.address.city = ...`) goes through the shared reference.

### Step 2: Task A — independent working copy (deep clone)

A shallow spread is **not enough** because nested objects are still shared:

```ts
const shallow = { ...original };
shallow.address.city = "LA";   // still mutates original.address!
shallow.tags.push("new");      // still mutates original.tags!
```

We need a deep copy. In modern runtimes, use `structuredClone`:

```ts
const draft = structuredClone(original);
draft.address.city = "LA";
draft.tags.push("new");
// original is untouched
```

`structuredClone` handles nested plain objects/arrays, `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `ArrayBuffer`, etc., and breaks reference sharing recursively.

### Step 3: Task B — reference equality

JavaScript’s `===` on objects compares **identity** (same reference), not contents:

```ts
function sameReference<T extends object>(a: T, b: T): boolean {
  return a === b;
}
```

Usage:

```ts
const x = { id: 1 };
const y = { id: 1 };
sameReference(x, y);        // false (different objects)
sameReference(x, x);        // true
const z = x;
sameReference(x, z);        // true
```

If you need *structural* equality, use a deep-equal library or recursive comparison; `===` is intentionally identity-only.

### Step 4: Task C — `cloneUser`

Using `structuredClone` is the idiomatic one-liner. For environments without it, or for domain-specific classes, write an explicit deep clone:

```ts
function cloneUser(u: User): User {
  return {
    id: u.id,                 // primitive → copied by value
    name: u.name,
    address: { ...u.address }, // new Address object
    tags: [...u.tags],          // new array
  };
}
```

This works here because the graph is shallow-nested and contains only primitives. For arbitrary depth, prefer `structuredClone`.

### Step 5: Task D — immutable update (no mutation at all)

Instead of cloning then mutating, return a new object with only the changed path replaced. This is the standard pattern for React/Redux state updates:

```ts
function updateCity(user: User, city: string): User {
  return {
    ...user,                 // shallow copy of top level
    address: {               // replace nested object
      ...user.address,
      city,
    },
    // tags array is shared but never mutated → safe
  };
}
```

Because we never mutate, `user` stays referentially stable for unchanged parts (good for memoization), and only the changed branch gets new references.

---

## Final complete solution

```ts
type Address = { city: string; zip: string };
type User = { id: number; name: string; address: Address; tags: string[] };

const original: User = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Alice",
  address: { city: "NYC", zip: "10001" },
  tags: ["admin", "beta"],
};

// --- Task B: reference equality ---
function sameReference<T extends object>(a: T, b: T): boolean {
  return a === b;
}

// --- Task C: deep clone ---
function cloneUser(u: User): User {
  // Preferred in modern runtimes:
  return structuredClone(u);

  // Equivalent manual version (uncomment if structuredClone unavailable):
  // return {
  //   id: u.id,
  //   name: u.name,
  //   address: { ...u.address },
  //   tags: [...u.tags],
  // };
}

// --- Task D: immutable update ---
function updateCity(user: User, city: string): User {
  return {
    ...user,
    address: { ...user.address, city },
  };
}

// --- Demo / verification ---
const draft = cloneUser(original);
draft.address.city = "LA";
draft.tags.push("new");

console.log("original city:", original.address.city); // "NYC" (unchanged)
console.log("original tags:", original.tags);          // ["admin", "beta"]
console.log("draft city:   ", draft.address.city);    // "LA"
console.log("draft tags:   ", draft.tags);            // ["admin", "beta", "new"]

console.log("sameReference(original, draft):", sameReference(original, draft)); // false
const alias = original;
console.log("sameReference(original, alias):", sameReference(original, alias)); // true

const renamed = updateCity(original, "Chicago");
console.log("renamed city:", renamed.address.city);   // "Chicago"
console.log("original still:", original.address.city); // "NYC"
```

### Key takeaways

- TS has **references**, not pointers; assignment shares the object graph.
- **Shallow copy** (`{ ...obj }`, `[...arr]`) breaks only the top level; nested objects/arrays remain shared.
- Use **`structuredClone`** for true deep copies of plain data.
- Use **`===`** for reference identity; use deep comparison only when you need structural equality.
- Prefer **immutable updates** (`{ ...obj, nested: { ...obj.nested, field } }`) in stateful/UI code to avoid accidental shared mutations.
- Mark data as `readonly`/`Readonly<T>` or use `as const` to let TypeScript catch accidental mutations at compile time.

## Pointers vs. References in TypeScript

TypeScript (and JavaScript) does **not** have low-level memory pointers like C/C++. Instead, it has **reference semantics for objects**.

- **Primitives** (`number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint`) are **passed by value**. Reassigning a parameter never affects the caller.
- **Objects** (including arrays, functions, class instances) are **mutable and accessed by reference**. Multiple variables can point to the **same** object; mutating through one variable is visible through all aliases.
- There is **no pointer arithmetic**, no addresses, and no manual memory management. The engine (V8, etc.) handles allocation/GC.

A helpful mental model: a variable of object type holds a *reference* (an opaque handle) to a heap object, not the object itself.

### Common pitfalls
- **Rebinding vs. mutating**: `obj = newThing` only changes the local variable; `obj.prop = x` mutates the shared object.
- **Shallow copies**: spreading `{ ...obj }` copies top-level references, so nested objects are still shared.
- **Arrays/objects as function args**: mutations persist; reassignment does not.

---

## “Core” implementation: reference cells + a linked list

Below is a small, self-contained module that (1) models a mutable **reference cell** (a “box” you can rebind, similar to a pointer-to-pointer), and (2) uses references to build a **singly linked list**. Comments explain the reference semantics at each step.

```ts
// src/refs.ts

/**
 * A mutable reference cell.
 *
 * In TS/JS we cannot take the address of a variable, but we can wrap a value
 * in an object so that multiple aliases share (and can rebind) the same cell.
 * This is the closest practical equivalent to a "pointer to a pointer".
 */
export class Ref<T> {
  constructor(public value: T) {}

  /** Read the currently referenced value. */
  get deref(): T {
    return this.value;
  }

  /** Write a new value into the cell (rebinds the reference). */
  set deref(next: T) {
    this.value = next;
  }

  /** Apply a transformation in place. */
  update(fn: (current: T) => T): void {
    this.value = fn(this.value);
  }
}

/** Convenience factory. */
export function ref<T>(initial: T): Ref<T> {
  return new Ref(initial);
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Linked list built purely on object references                               */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

export interface ListNode<T> {
  value: T;
  next: ListNode<T> | null;
}

export class LinkedList<T> {
  private head: ListNode<T> | null = null;

  /** Append using a trailing reference to the last node. */
  push(value: T): void {
    const node: ListNode<T> = { value, next: null };

    if (this.head === null) {
      this.head = node;
      return;
    }

    // Walk by reference until we reach the tail.
    let current = this.head;
    while (current.next !== null) {
      current = current.next; // rebind local reference, not the list
    }
    current.next = node; // mutate shared node -> visible to all aliases
  }

  /** Remove first occurrence by relinking references. */
  remove(value: T): boolean {
    // Dummy head simplifies edge cases (head removal).
    const dummy: ListNode<T> = { value: undefined as unknown as T, next: this.head };
    let prev = dummy;
    let current = this.head;

    while (current !== null) {
      if (current.value === value) {
        prev.next = current.next; // unlink by rebinding reference
        this.head = dummy.next;
        return true;
      }
      prev = current;
      current = current.next;
    }
    return false;
  }

  toArray(): T[] {
    const out: T[] = [];
    let current = this.head;
    while (current !== null) {
      out.push(current.value);
      current = current.next;
    }
    return out;
  }
}

/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* Demo / smoke tests                                                          */
/* -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

function demoValueVsReference(): void {
  // Primitives: by value
  let a = 5;
  let b = a;
  b = 99;
  console.log("primitives:", a, b); // 5 99

  // Objects: by reference
  const x = { n: 1 };
  const y = x; // same reference
  y.n = 42;
  console.log("objects:", x.n, y.n); // 42 42

  // Rebinding does NOT propagate
  let p = { n: 1 };
  let q = p;
  q = { n: 99 }; // q now points elsewhere
  console.log("rebind:", p.n, q.n); // 1 99
}

function demoRefCell(): void {
  const r = ref({ count: 0 });
  const alias = r; // same cell

  alias.deref = { count: 10 }; // rebind shared cell
  console.log("ref cell:", r.deref.count); // 10

  r.update((v) => ({ count: v.count + 1 }));
  console.log("ref update:", alias.deref.count); // 11
}

function demoList(): void {
  const list = new LinkedList<number>();
  list.push(1);
  list.push(2);
  list.push(3);
  console.log("list:", list.toArray()); // [1, 2, 3]
  list.remove(2);
  console.log("after remove:", list.toArray()); // [1, 3]
}

// Run demos when executed directly (e.g., ts-node refs.ts)
if (require.main === module) {
  demoValueVsReference();
  demoRefCell();
  demoList();
}
```

### Key takeaways to state in an interview
1. **No raw pointers** in TS; objects are manipulated through **references**.
2. **Mutation is shared; reassignment is local.**
3. To share a *rebindable* slot, wrap it in an object (a `Ref`/cell) — this mirrors pointer-to-pointer patterns.
4. Data structures like linked lists are built entirely by **relinking `next` references**, not by moving memory.

# Pointers and References in TypeScript — Edge Cases

TypeScript (and JavaScript) has **no raw memory pointers**. What people call "pointers" are **references to objects** (including arrays, functions, classes). Primitives (`number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint`) are always copied by value. The edge cases below all stem from that distinction.

---

## 1. Reassignment vs. Mutation (the most common bug)

A `const` reference can still be *mutated*; only the binding is fixed.

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.push(4);          // ✅ allowed — mutation
arr = [4, 5, 6];      // ❌ TS error: Cannot assign to 'arr'

const obj = { x: 1 };
obj.x = 2;            // ✅ allowed
obj = { x: 2 };       // ❌ TS error
```

**Edge case:** Passing an object into a function lets the callee mutate the caller's data, but reassigning the parameter does nothing to the caller.

```ts
function mutate(o: { x: number }) { o.x = 99; }   // caller sees change
function rebind(o: { x: number }) { o = { x: 99 }; } // caller sees nothing

const a = { x: 1 };
mutate(a);   // a.x === 99
rebind(a);   // a.x still 99
```

---

## 2. Shallow Copy Traps

Spread / `Object.assign` copy only the top level. Nested objects are still shared.

```ts
const original = { user: { name: "Ada" }, tags: ["a"] };
const copy = { ...original };

copy.user.name = "Bob";
copy.tags.push("b");

console.log(original.user.name); // "Bob"  ← leaked mutation
console.log(original.tags);       // ["a", "b"]
```

**Fix:** deep clone when independence is needed.

```ts
const deep = structuredClone(original); // modern, handles Map/Set/Date
// or: JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(o))  (loses undefined, dates, maps)
```

**Edge case:** `structuredClone` throws on functions, DOM nodes, and (in older runtimes) some class instances. For those, write a custom clone or use `lodash.cloneDeep`.

---

## 3. Aliasing in Arrays / Slices

`slice()` and spread create *shallow* copies; nested elements are shared.

```ts
const matrix = [[1, 2], [3, 4]];
const row = matrix[0];          // reference to same inner array
row[0] = 999;
console.log(matrix[0][0]);      // 999
```

Even "copying" an array of objects shares the objects:

```ts
const users = [{ id: 1 }];
const copy = [...users];
copy[0].id = 2;
console.log(users[0].id); // 2
```

---

## 4. `this` Binding Loss (the classic reference bug)

Methods are just functions; extracting them detaches `this`.

```ts
class Timer {
  count = 0;
  tick() { this.count++; }
}

const t = new Timer();
const fn = t.tick;
fn(); // ❌ TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
```

**Fixes:**

```ts
const fn = t.tick.bind(t);          // bind
const fn2 = () => t.tick();         // arrow wrapper
// or define tick as arrow field:
class Timer2 { count = 0; tick = () => this.count++; }
```

**Edge case in callbacks:**

```ts
class Component {
  name = "C";
  register(cb: (x: number) => void) { /* ... */ }
  init() {
    this.register(this.handle); // loses `this`
  }
  handle(x: number) { console.log(this.name, x); }
}
```

---

## 5. Closure Captures the *Variable*, Not the Value

Closures capture the binding, so late reads see the latest value.

```ts
const fns: (() => number)[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  fns.push(() => i); // with `let`, each iteration has own binding → 0,1,2
}
// with `var i`, all would return 3
```

**Edge case with object mutation inside closure:**

```ts
let config = { debug: false };
const log = () => console.log(config.debug);
config = { debug: true };   // rebinds outer variable
log(); // true  (closure sees new binding)

// But if you mutate instead:
const cfg = { debug: false };
const log2 = () => console.log(cfg.debug);
cfg.debug = true;
log2(); // true  (same object, mutated)
```

---

## 6. Circular References

Objects referencing each other break `JSON.stringify` and can complicate GC (though modern engines handle cycles).

```ts
const a: any = {};
const b: any = { parent: a };
a.child = b;

JSON.stringify(a); // ❌ TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON
```

**Fix:** replacer or a library.

```ts
const seen = new WeakSet();
JSON.stringify(a, (_k, v) => {
  if (typeof v === "object" && v !== null) {
    if (seen.has(v)) return "[Circular]";
    seen.add(v);
  }
  return v;
});
```

**Edge case:** Circular references in React state / Redux can cause infinite re-rend loops if equality checks are shallow and you mutate in place.

---

## 7. Reference Equality & `Object.is`

`===` on objects compares identity, not structure.

```ts
{} === {}            // false
[1,2] === [1,2]      // false
NaN === NaN          // false  (Object.is(NaN, NaN) === true)
+0 === -0            // true   (Object.is(+0, -0) === false)
```

**Edge case in dependency arrays (React):**

```ts
useEffect(() => { ... }, [obj]); // runs every render if `obj` is a new literal each time
// Fix: useMemo(() => ({...}), [deps]) or compare deeply
```

---

## 8. `null` / `undefined` Dereferencing

TS narrows types, but at runtime references can be null.

```ts
function greet(user?: { name: string }) {
  console.log(user.name); // TS error under strictNullChecks; runtime crash if undefined
}
```

**Edge cases:**
- Optional chaining stops at `null`/`undefined` but not at other falsy values (`0`, `""`).
  ```ts
  const x = { n: 0 };
  x?.n?.toFixed(); // works; x.n is 0 (falsy) but ?. only checks null/undef
  ```
- Non-null assertion `!` lies to the compiler:
  ```ts
  const el = document.getElementById("x")!; // TS thinks non-null; runtime may be null
  el.addEventListener(...); // crash
  ```
- `document.querySelector` returns `Element | null`.

---

## 9. DOM / External Resource References & Stale Closures

Holding references to DOM nodes prevents GC; after unmount they're detached (zombie references).

```ts
let cached: HTMLElement | null = null;
function init() {
  cached = document.getElementById("box");
}
function teardown() {
  cached?.remove();
  cached = null; // must drop reference or node leaks
}
```

**Edge case:** `useRef` in React holds a mutable `.current` that is `null` on first render and after unmount — always null-check.

```ts
const ref = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
// ref.current is null during SSR and before mount
useEffect(() => { ref.current?.focus(); }, []);
```

---

## 10. Weak References & GC

Strong references keep objects alive. Use `WeakRef` / `WeakMap` / `WeakSet` to avoid leaks for caches.

```ts
const cache = new WeakMap<object, string>();
function label(o: object) {
  if (!cache.has(o)) cache.set(o, compute(o));
  return cache.get(o)!;
}
// When `o` becomes unreachable, cache entry is dropped automatically.
```

**Edge case:** `WeakRef.deref()` can return `undefined` at any time (GC is non-deterministic). Always check.

```ts
const ref = new WeakRef(bigObject);
const o = ref.deref();
if (o) { /* use */ } else { /* recompute */ }
```

**Edge case:** `FinalizationRegistry` callbacks are not guaranteed to run promptly — never rely on them for correctness (only for cleanup hints).

---

## 11. `readonly` and `as const` — Reference-Level vs. Value-Level

`readonly` is shallow and compile-time only.

```ts
interface User { readonly tags: string[]; }
const u: User = { tags: ["a"] };
u.tags = ["b"];     // ❌ TS error
u.tags.push("b");   // ✅ allowed — readonly doesn't freeze contents

// Deep freeze at runtime:
Object.freeze(u);   // shallow freeze
// For deep: recursive freeze or immer
```

`as const` makes literals readonly but the underlying object is still mutable at runtime unless frozen.

```ts
const CONFIG = { api: "https://x" } as const;
// CONFIG.api = "y"; // TS error, but at runtime (if not frozen) assignment would work
```

---

## 12. Function / Callback References & Identity

Two arrow functions are never equal; passing new function references breaks memoization.

```ts
const a = () => 1;
const b = () => 1;
a === b; // false

// In React:
<Child onClick={() => doThing()} /> // new fn every render → Child re-renders
// Fix:
const onClick = useCallback(() => doThing(), [doThing]);
<Child onClick={onClick} />
```

**Edge case:** Debounced/throttled functions must keep a stable reference; otherwise timers reset.

```ts
const debounced = debounce(fn, 300); // create once, outside render
```

---

## 13. Type-Only References & `import type`

Erased at runtime; using them as values crashes.

```ts
import type { Foo } from "./foo";
const x = Foo; // ❌ TS error (Foo is a type), runtime ReferenceError if bypassed
```

**Edge case with enums:** `const enum` is inlined; regular `enum` becomes a runtime object. Mixing them affects tree-shaking and reference identity.

---

## 14. Proxy / Revocable References

A revoked proxy throws on *any* access, including `typeof` checks in some engines.

```ts
const { proxy, revoke } = Proxy.revocable({ x: 1 }, {});
revoke();
proxy.x; // ❌ TypeError: Cannot perform 'get' on a proxy that has been revoked
```

**Edge case:** Passing a revoked proxy to `JSON.stringify` or third-party libs throws deep inside — wrap access in try/catch or check a sentinel.

---

## 15. Symbol-keyed Properties Are "Hidden" References

Symbols are unique; `Symbol("a") !== Symbol("a")`. They don't show in `Object.keys`/`for...in` but are still enumerable via `Object.getOwnPropertySymbols`.

```ts
const s = Symbol("id");
const o = { [s]: 42 };
Object.keys(o);                    // []
JSON.stringify(o);                 // "{}"
Object.getOwnPropertySymbols(o);   // [Symbol(id)]
```

**Edge case:** Two "same-named" symbols are different keys — easy to lose data.

---

## 16. `this` in Standalone / Generic Functions

Generic utilities that accept `this` contexts; arrow functions don't bind `this`.

```ts
function getX(this: { x: number }) { return this.x; }
const o = { x: 5, getX };
o.getX();     // 5
getX();       // TS error (and runtime: this is undefined in strict mode)
```

**Edge case:** Event handlers in classes — prefer arrow fields or `.bind` in constructor; binding in render creates new identity each call.

---

## 17. Reference Parameters & "Out" Parameters

TS has no `ref`/`out` like C#. To mutate a caller's primitive, wrap it.

```ts
// ❌ Can't change caller's number
function inc(n: number) { n++; }

// ✅ Wrap in object
function incBox(b: { value: number }) { b.value++; }
const box = { value: 5 };
incBox(box); // box.value === 6
```

**Edge case:** Returning a tuple is usually cleaner than mutating an input object (immutability).

---

## 18. Iterators / Generators Hold References

Generators keep their execution context (and closed-over variables) alive until exhausted or returned.

```ts
function* gen(items: number[]) {
  for (const x of items) yield x;
}
const g = gen(hugeArray);
g.next(); // keeps `items` referenced until g completes/returns
```

**Edge case:** Forgetting to exhaust or `.return()` a generator can retain large arrays. Call `g.return()` early if abandoning.

---

## 19. Map / Set Reference Keys

Maps use SameValueZero equality (like `===` except NaN). Object keys are by identity.

```ts
const m = new Map<object, number>();
const a = {};
m.set(a, 1);
m.get({}); // undefined — different object identity
m.get(a);  // 1
```

**Edge case:** Using objects as keys without a stable identity leads to "memory leaks" (entries never GC'd because the Map holds strong refs). Use `WeakMap` for object-keyed caches.

---

## 20. Strict Mode & `this` at Top Level

In ES modules / TS (always strict), top-level `this` is `undefined`, not `globalThis`.

```ts
// module.ts
console.log(this); // undefined
```

**Edge case:** Code that assumes `this === window` (browser) or `global` (Node) breaks when moved into a module. Use `globalThis` explicitly.

---

## Summary Checklist

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Mutating shared reference | `structuredClone`, immutable updates |
| `this` loss | `.bind`, arrow fields, arrow wrappers |
| Closure stale values | capture snapshot: `const x = obj.x;` |
| Circular refs | replacer, avoid in state |
| Null deref | optional chaining, null checks, no `!` |
| Leaked DOM refs | null out after unmount |
| GC pressure / leaks | `WeakRef`/`WeakMap`/`WeakSet` |
| Shallow `readonly` | `Object.freeze` or immer |
| Identity churn | `useMemo`/`useCallback` |
| Revoked proxies | try/catch + sentinels |

If you want, I can drill into any one of these (e.g., a deep-dive on React ref patterns, orWeakMap caching strategies) with larger examples.

Polymorphism in TypeScript shows up in several forms: **subtype** (inheritance/interfaces + virtual dispatch), **parametric** (generics), **ad-hoc** (function overloads), and **structural** (duck typing). Below is a single runnable file that demonstrates all of them.

## `polymorphism.ts`

```ts
// ============================================================
// 1. SUBTYPE POLYMORPHISM — interfaces + runtime dispatch
// ============================================================

interface Shape {
  area(): number;
  describe(): string;
}

class Circle implements Shape {
  constructor(private radius: number) {}
  area(): number {
    return Math.PI * this.radius * this.radius;
  }
  describe(): string {
    return `Circle(r=${this.radius})`;
  }
}

class Rectangle implements Shape {
  constructor(private width: number, private height: number) {}
  area(): number {
    return this.width * this.height;
  }
  describe(): string {
    return `Rectangle(${this.width}x${this.height})`;
  }
}

function totalArea(shapes: Shape[]): number {
  // Same call site, different runtime implementations.
  return shapes.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s.area(), 0);
}

// ============================================================
// 2. SUBTYPE POLYMORPHISM — abstract base class
// ============================================================

abstract class Animal {
  constructor(public name: string) {}
  abstract speak(): string;          // must be implemented
  greet(): string {
    return `${this.name} says ${this.speak()}`; // polymorphic call
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  speak(): string { return "woof"; }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
  speak(): string { return "meow"; }
}

// ============================================================
// 3. PARAMETRIC POLYMORPHISM — generics
// ============================================================

class Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  push(item: T): void { this.items.push(item); }
  pop(): T | undefined { return this.items.pop(); }
  get size(): number { return this.items.length; }
  toArray(): T[] { return [...this.items]; }
}

function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}

// Generic with a constraint.
function getLength<T extends { length: number }>(x: T): number {
  return x.length;
}

// ============================================================
// 4. AD-HOC POLYMORPHISM — function overloads
// ============================================================

function stringify(value: number): string;
function stringify(value: boolean): string;
function stringify(value: number | boolean): string {
  if (typeof value === "number") {
    return value.toFixed(2);
  }
  return value ? "true" : "false";
}

// ============================================================
// 5. STRUCTURAL POLYMORPHISM — duck typing
// ============================================================

interface Logger {
  log(message: string): void;
}

class ConsoleLogger implements Logger {
  log(message: string): void {
    console.log("[LOG]", message);
  }
}

// Anything with a matching `log(message: string): void` works.
function runTask(logger: Logger): void {
  logger.log("task started");
  logger.log("task finished");
}

// ============================================================
// 6. DISCRIMINATED UNIONS — closed, exhaustive polymorphism
// ============================================================

type Result<T, E> =
  | { kind: "ok"; value: T }
  | { kind: "err"; error: E };

function unwrap<T, E>(r: Result<T, E>): T {
  switch (r.kind) {
    case "ok":
      return r.value;
    case "err":
      throw new Error(String(r.error));
    default: {
      // Exhaustiveness check: if a new variant is added, this fails to compile.
      const _exhaustive: never = r;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}

// ============================================================
// 7. POLYMORPHIC `this` types
// ============================================================

class Builder {
  private parts: string[] = [];
  add(part: string): this {
    this.parts.push(part);
    return this; // returns the actual subclass instance
  }
  build(): string {
    return this.parts.join("-");
  }
}

class HtmlBuilder extends Builder {
  wrap(tag: string): this {
    // subclass-specific method, still chainable polymorphically
    return this.add(`<${tag}>`);
  }
}

// ============================================================
// DEMO
// ============================================================

function main(): void {
  // 1. Shapes
  const shapes: Shape[] = [new Circle(2), new Rectangle(3, 4)];
  console.log("shapes:", shapes.map((s) => s.describe()).join(", "));
  console.log("total area:", totalArea(shapes).toFixed(2));

  // 2. Animals
  const animals: Animal[] = [new Dog("Rex"), new Cat("Mimi")];
  console.log(animals.map((a) => a.greet()).join(" | "));

  // 3. Generics
  const stack = new Stack<number>();
  stack.push(10);
  stack.push(20);
  console.log("stack pop:", stack.pop(), "size:", stack.size);
  console.log("identity:", identity("hello"));
  console.log("getLength:", getLength("abc"), getLength([1, 2, 3]));

  // 4. Overloads
  console.log("stringify(3.1415):", stringify(3.1415));
  console.log("stringify(true):", stringify(true));

  // 5. Duck typing
  runTask(new ConsoleLogger());
  runTask({ log: (m) => console.log("[FAKE]", m) });

  // 6. Result
  const ok: Result<number, string> = { kind: "ok", value: 42 };
  const err: Result<number, string> = { kind: "err", error: "boom" };
  console.log("unwrap ok:", unwrap(ok));
  try {
    unwrap(err);
  } catch (e) {
    console.log("unwrap err threw:", (e as Error).message);
  }

  // 7. this-types
  const html = new HtmlBuilder().add("a").wrap("b").build();
  console.log("builder:", html);
}

main();
```

