Below are common TypeScript-specific edge cases that can break a refactor even when the JavaScript still runs. Each item includes a concrete before/after example and the fix.

## 1. Contextual typing disappears when extracting expressions

When you extract an inline callback, parameters can lose their contextual type.

```ts
// Before
const doubled = [1, 2, 3].map(x => x * 2);

// After: x becomes implicit any under strict settings
const double = x => x * 2;
const doubled2 = [1, 2, 3].map(double);
```

Fix: annotate the extracted function.

```ts
const double = (x: number) => x * 2;
```

## 2. Literal types widen after extraction

```ts
// Before: method is the literal "GET"
const request = { url: "/", method: "GET" } as const;

// After: method becomes string
const method = "GET";
const request2 = { url: "/", method };
```

Fix: preserve the literal type.

```ts
const method = "GET" as const;
// or
const request2 = { url: "/", method: "GET" as const };
```

## 3. `satisfies` vs type annotation changes inference

```ts
type Config = { port: number; host: string };

// Annotation: cfg.port is number
const cfg: Config = { port: 3000, host: "localhost" };

// satisfies: cfg.port stays 3000
const cfg2 = { port: 3000, host: "localhost" } satisfies Config;
```

If you refactor `satisfies` into a normal annotation, you may lose precision at call sites.

## 4. Extracting methods breaks `this`

```ts
class Greeter {
  name = "world";
  greet() {
    return `Hello ${this.name}`;
  }
}

const g = new Greeter();
const fn = g.greet;
// Error: The 'this' context of type 'void' is not assignable...
fn();
```

Fix: use an arrow function or bind.

```ts
class Greeter {
  name = "world";
  greet = () => `Hello ${this.name}`;
}
```

## 5. Function overloads must move together

```ts
// Before
function makeDate(timestamp: number): Date;
function makeDate(month: number, day: number, year: number): Date;
function makeDate(...args: number[]): Date {
  // implementation
  return new Date();
}

// Bad refactor: only the implementation signature is extracted
function makeDateImpl(...args: number[]): Date {
  return new Date();
}
```

Fix: move all public overloads plus the implementation, or keep them in one declaration.

## 6. Type predicates and assertion functions are not ordinary booleans

```ts
// Before
function isUser(x: unknown): x is User {
  return typeof x === "object" && x !== null && "id" in x;
}

if (isUser(value)) {
  value.id; // narrowed
}

// Bad refactor
function isUser2(x: unknown): boolean {
  return typeof x === "object" && x !== null && "id" in x;
}

if (isUser2(value)) {
  value.id; // Error: value is unknown
}
```

Assertion functions have the same risk:

```ts
function assertUser(x: unknown): asserts x is User {
  if (!isUser(x)) throw new Error("Not a user");
}
```

## 7. `any` vs `unknown` in `catch`

Under `useUnknownInCatchVariables`, `e` is `unknown`.

```ts
try {
  risky();
} catch (e) {
  // Bad refactor
  console.log(e.message); // Error: e is unknown

  // Good
  if (e instanceof Error) {
    console.log(e.message);
  } else {
    console.log(String(e));
  }
}
```

## 8. Non-null assertions do not travel with extracted values

```ts
const el = document.getElementById("app")!; // HTMLElement
const text = el.textContent; // OK

// Bad refactor: helper returns HTMLElement | null
function getApp() {
  return document.getElementById("app");
}

const text2 = getApp().textContent; // Error
```

Fix: either keep the assertion at the boundary or handle null.

```ts
function getApp(): HTMLElement {
  const el = document.getElementById("app");
  if (!el) throw new Error("Missing #app");
  return el;
}
```

## 9. Definite assignment is not tracked through method calls

```ts
class Example {
  value: number;

  constructor() {
    this.init();
  }

  init() {
    this.value = 1;
  }

  read() {
    return this.value; // Error: used before assigned
  }
}
```

Fix: assign in the constructor or use definite assignment assertion.

```ts
class Example {
  value!: number;

  constructor() {
    this.init();
  }

  init() {
    this.value = 1;
  }
}
```

## 10. `this` narrowing disappears in closures over mutable variables

```ts
let user: User | null = loadUser();

if (user) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    // Error under strict checks: user possibly null
    console.log(user.name);
  }, 0);
}
```

Fix: bind to a `const`.

```ts
const user = loadUser();
if (user) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(user.name), 0);
}
```

## 11. Exhaustiveness checks break when unions grow

```ts
type Shape =
  | { kind: "circle"; radius: number }
  | { kind: "square"; size: number };

function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
    case "square":
      return shape.size ** 2;
    default: {
      const exhaustive: never = shape;
      return exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

If you later add `{ kind: "triangle"; base: number; height: number }`, the `default` branch fails until handled.

## 12. Generics can be lost during extraction

```ts
// Before
function map<T, U>(items: T[], fn: (item: T) => U): U[] {
  return items.map(fn);
}

// Bad refactor: T and U erased
const map2 = (items: any[], fn: (item: any) => any): any[] => items.map(fn);
```

Also, arrow functions do not receive contextual type parameters in some positions. Preserve explicit type parameters.

## 13. Conditional types distribute unexpectedly

```ts
type IsArray<T> = T extends any[] ? true : false;

type A = IsArray<string | number[]>; // boolean (distributes)

// Prevent distribution
type IsArrayFixed<T> = [T] extends [any[]] ? true : false;

type B = IsArrayFixed<string | number[]>; // false
```

A refactor that wraps or unwraps a type in a tuple can change behavior.

## 14. Mapped type modifiers must be preserved

```ts
type Mutable<T> = {
  -readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K];
};

type RequiredProps<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]-?: T[K];
};
```

Refactoring `Partial`, `Required`, or `Readonly` manually can accidentally leave or remove `?` and `readonly`.

## 15. `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` changes array access

```ts
function firstTotal(values: number[]): number {
  // Under noUncheckedIndexedAccess, values[0] is number | undefined
  return values[0].toFixed(2) as unknown as number; // unsafe
}

// Better
function firstTotal2(values: number[]): number {
  const first = values[0];
  if (first === undefined) throw new Error("Empty");
  return Number(first.toFixed(2));
}
```

## 16. `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` rejects explicit `undefined`

```ts
type User = { name?: string };

// Error under exactOptionalPropertyTypes
const user: User = { name: undefined };
```

Fix: omit the property or change the type to `name?: string | undefined`.

## 17. `noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature` forces bracket access

```ts
type Dict = { [key: string]: number };

const d: Dict = {};
const value = d.foo; // Error under the flag
const value2 = d["foo"]; // OK
```

## 18. `isolatedModules` and `verbatimModuleSyntax` require type-only imports

```ts
// Risky mixed import
import { User, createUser } from "./user";

// Better
import type { User } from "./user";
import { createUser } from "./user";
```

If `User` is type-only and you refactor imports into a single statement, transpilers such as Babel/esbuild may emit a runtime import for a type.

## 19. Moving files breaks path aliases and relative depth

```ts
// tsconfig.json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": { "@/*": ["src/*"] }
  }
}
```

```ts
// Before: src/features/user/index.ts
import { api } from "../../lib/api";

// After moving to src/features/admin/user/index.ts
import { api } from "../../../lib/api";
// or use alias
import { api } from "@/lib/api";
```

Always re-run the compiler after moving files; path alias mistakes are not always caught by editor quick fixes.

## 20. Barrel files can create circular dependencies

```ts
// src/index.ts
export * from "./user";
export * from "./order";

// src/user/user.ts
import { Order } from ".."; // circular through barrel
```

Refactor imports to the concrete module path instead of the barrel when needed.

```ts
import { Order } from "../order/order";
```

## 21. Enums are not plain unions

```ts
enum Status {
  Ok = 200,
  NotFound = 404,
}

const label = Status[200]; // "Ok"
```

Refactoring to a union type removes reverse mappings and enum members as values.

```ts
type Status = 200 | 404;
// Status[200] no longer exists
```

`const enum` additionally requires the same compilation project for inlining.

## 22. Namespaces and declaration merging

```ts
namespace Validation {
  export interface StringValidator {
    isAcceptable(s: string): boolean;
  }
}

namespace Validation {
  export class ZipCodeValidator implements Validation.StringValidator {
    isAcceptable(s: string) {
      return s.length === 5;
    }
  }
}
```

Splitting or merging namespaces across files requires correct `/// <reference path="..." />` directives or module imports. Interface/class declaration merging also means two declarations with the same name combine; refactoring them into one can change visibility.

## 23. Ambient/global augmentation must remain in a module context

```ts
// express.d.ts
declare global {
  namespace Express {
    interface Request {
      user?: { id: string };
    }
  }
}

export {}; // makes this file a module
```

If refactoring removes the `export {}`, the file becomes a script and `declare global` is invalid.

## 24. Class field initialization order and `useDefineForClassFields`

```ts
class Base {
  x = 1;
}

class Derived extends Base {
  y = this.x + 1;
}
```

With `useDefineForClassFields`, field initialization follows ECMAScript class field semantics. Refactoring initialization logic into constructors or changing base/derived field order can produce `undefined` at runtime even when types look fine.

## 25. `private` keyword vs. `#private` fields

```ts
class A {
  #secret = 42;
}

class B extends A {
  read() {
    // Error: Property '#secret' is not accessible outside class 'A'
    return this.#secret;
  }
}
```

`private` is compile-time only and allows same-class access patterns; `#private` is runtime-private and stricter. Refactoring between them changes accessibility and emit.

## 26. Parameter properties must be expanded carefully

```ts
class User {
  constructor(public readonly id: string) {}
}
```

Manual refactor:

```ts
class User {
  readonly id: string;

  constructor(id: string) {
    this.id = id;
  }
}
```

Do not drop `readonly`, visibility, or definite assignment semantics.

## 27. Accessor get/set type mismatch

```ts
class Box {
  private _value = 0;

  get value(): number {
    return this._value;
  }

  set value(v: string) { // Error: types must match
    this._value = Number(v);
  }
}
```

Refactoring a property into accessors requires the getter and setter value types to agree.

## 28. Async mistakes: missing `await`

```ts
async function loadUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const res = fetch(`/users/${id}`); // Promise<Response>, not Response
  const data = res.json(); // Error: Property 'json' does not exist on Promise<Response>
  return data as User;
}

// Correct
async function loadUser2(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const res = await fetch(`/users/${id}`);
  const data = (await res.json()) as User;
  return data;
}
```

## 29. Generator return and yield types

```ts
function* sequence(): Generator<number, string, boolean> {
  const ok: boolean = yield 1;
  return ok ? "done" : "stopped";
}
```

Refactoring `yield*` or changing the generator signature can invalidate the third type parameter, which controls `next()`'s input type.

## 30. Brand/opaque types are erased by normal operations

```ts
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: "UserId" };

function toUserId(value: string): UserId {
  return value as UserId;
}

function concatIds(a: UserId, b: UserId): UserId {
  // Type error or unsafe cast needed
  return (a + b) as UserId;
}
```

Refactoring arithmetic, concatenation, or serialization usually strips the brand; reapply it explicitly.

## 31. Mixins require constructor constraints

```ts
type Constructor<T = {}> = new (...args: any[]) => T;

function Timestamped<TBase extends Constructor>(Base: TBase) {
  return class extends Base {
    timestamp = Date.now();
  };
}
```

Refactoring the base class or removing the constraint can break `extends Base` and static members.

## 32. `this` parameters in callbacks

```ts
type Handler = (this: Button, event: Event) => void;

const button: Button = getButton();

const onClick: Handler = function (event) {
  this.setEnabled(false); // `this` is typed as Button
};

button.addEventListener("click", onClick);
```

Extracting `onClick` into an arrow function removes the `this` parameter binding.

## 33. Project references and cross-package moves

```json
// tsconfig.json
{
  "references": [{ "path": "../shared" }]
}
```

Moving code between composite projects requires updating `references`, package exports, and build order. A successful editor refactor can still fail `tsc -b`.

## 34. Declaration emit edge cases

When publishing libraries, refactoring source can break `.d.ts` output:

```ts
// Source
export const value = 1 as const;

// Emitted declaration preserves literal type
export declare const value: 1;
```

But inferred types from implementation details may leak into declarations. Prefer explicit exported types for public APIs.

```ts
export type Result = { ok: true } | { ok: false; error: string };

export function run(): Result {
  // ...
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Practical checklist for any TypeScript refactor

1. Run `tsc --noEmit` after every structural change.
2. Preserve type-only imports with `import type`.
3. Re-check literal precision: `as const`, `satisfies`, enums.
4. Re-check narrowing: type guards, `never` exhaustiveness, mutable `let` closures.
5. Re-check class semantics: `this`, field order, `private` vs `#`, parameter properties.
6. Re-check compiler flags: `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `verbatimModuleSyntax`, `isolatedModules`.
7. Re-check module graph: aliases, barrels, circular imports, project references.
8. Re-check public API output: overloads, generics, declaration files.

```typescript
// regex.ts
// A small but complete regular-expression engine in TypeScript.
// Supports: literals, . (any), char classes [...], escapes (\d \w \s \D \W \S),
// anchors ^ $, groups ( ) and (?: ), alternation |, quantifiers * + ? {n,m} (greedy/lazy),
// backreferences \1..\9, and flags i, m, s.

export type Node =
  | { type: "lit"; char: string }
  | { type: "any" }
  | { type: "class"; test: (ch: string) => boolean; neg: boolean }
  | { type: "anchor"; kind: "start" | "end" }
  | { type: "empty" }
  | { type: "concat"; nodes: Node[] }
  | { type: "alt"; left: Node; right: Node }
  | { type: "quant"; node: Node; min: number; max: number; greedy: boolean }
  | { type: "group"; index: number; node: Node }
  | { type: "backref"; index: number };

interface Flags {
  ignoreCase: boolean;
  multiline: boolean;
  dotAll: boolean;
}

/* ----------------------------- Parser ----------------------------- */

class Parser {
  public groupCount = 0;
  private i = 0;

  constructor(private pattern: string) {}

  parse(): Node {
    const node = this.parseExpr();
    if (this.i !== this.pattern.length) {
      throw new Error(`Unexpected trailing character at ${this.i}`);
    }
    return node;
  }

  private peek(): string {
    return this.i < this.pattern.length ? this.pattern[this.i] : "";
  }

  private parseExpr(): Node {
    const seqs: Node[] = [this.parseSeq()];
    while (this.peek() === "|") {
      this.i++;
      seqs.push(this.parseSeq());
    }
    if (seqs.length === 1) return seqs[0];
    let node = seqs[0];
    for (let k = 1; k < seqs.length; k++) {
      node = { type: "alt", left: node, right: seqs[k] };
    }
    return node;
  }

  private parseSeq(): Node {
    const nodes: Node[] = [];
    while (
      this.i < this.pattern.length &&
      this.peek() !== ")" &&
      this.peek() !== "|"
    ) {
      nodes.push(this.parseAtomWithQuant());
    }
    if (nodes.length === 0) return { type: "empty" };
    if (nodes.length === 1) return nodes[0];
    return { type: "concat", nodes };
  }

  private parseAtomWithQuant(): Node {
    let atom = this.parseAtom();
    const c = this.peek();
    if (c === "*" || c === "+" || c === "?") {
      this.i++;
      let min = 0;
      let max = Infinity;
      if (c === "+") {
        min = 1;
        max = Infinity;
      } else if (c === "?") {
        min = 0;
        max = 1;
      }
      let greedy = true;
      if (this.peek() === "?") {
        this.i++;
        greedy = false;
      }
      atom = { type: "quant", node: atom, min, max, greedy };
    } else if (c === "{") {
      this.i++;
      let numStr = "";
      while (/\d/.test(this.peek())) {
        numStr += this.peek();
        this.i++;
      }
      const min = numStr === "" ? 0 : parseInt(numStr, 10);
      let max = min;
      if (this.peek() === ",") {
        this.i++;
        if (/\d/.test(this.peek())) {
          numStr = "";
          while (/\d/.test(this.peek())) {
            numStr += this.peek();
            this.i++;
          }
          max = parseInt(numStr, 10);
        } else {
          max = Infinity;
        }
      }
      if (this.peek() !== "}") throw new Error("Bad quantifier");
      this.i++;
      let greedy = true;
      if (this.peek() === "?") {
        this.i++;
        greedy = false;
      }
      atom = { type: "quant", node: atom, min, max, greedy };
    }
    return atom;
  }

  private parseAtom(): Node {
    const c = this.peek();
    if (c === "(") {
      this.i++;
      let capturing = true;
      if (this.pattern.startsWith("?:", this.i)) {
        this.i += 2;
        capturing = false;
      }
      const expr = this.parseExpr();
      if (this.peek() !== ")") throw new Error("Unclosed group");
      this.i++;
      if (capturing) {
        const idx = this.groupCount++;
        return { type: "group", index: idx, node: expr };
      }
      return expr;
    }
    if (c === "[") return this.parseClass();
    if (c === ".") {
      this.i++;
      return { type: "any" };
    }
    if (c === "^") {
      this.i++;
      return { type: "anchor", kind: "start" };
    }
    if (c === "$") {
      this.i++;
      return { type: "anchor", kind: "end" };
    }
    if (c === "\\") {
      this.i++;
      return this.parseEscape();
    }
    this.i++;
    return { type: "lit", char: c };
  }

  private parseEscape(): Node {
    const c = this.peek();
    this.i++;
    switch (c) {
      case "d":
        return { type: "class", test: (ch) => /\d/.test(ch), neg: false };
      case "D":
        return { type: "class", test: (ch) => /\d/.test(ch), neg: true };
      case "w":
        return { type: "class", test: (ch) => /\w/.test(ch), neg: false };
      case "W":
        return { type: "class", test: (ch) => /\w/.test(ch), neg: true };
      case "s":
        return { type: "class", test: (ch) => /\s/.test(ch), neg: false };
      case "S":
        return { type: "class", test: (ch) => /\s/.test(ch), neg: true };
      default:
        if (/\d/.test(c)) {
          return { type: "backref", index: parseInt(c, 10) - 1 };
        }
        return { type: "lit", char: c };
    }
  }

  private parseClass(): Node {
    this.i++; // [
    let neg = false;
    if (this.peek() === "^") {
      neg = true;
      this.i++;
    }
    const predicates: ((ch: string) => boolean)[] = [];
    while (this.i < this.pattern.length && this.peek() !== "]") {
      const atom = this.parseClassAtom();
      if (
        atom.kind === "char" &&
        this.peek() === "-" &&
        this.i + 1 < this.pattern.length &&
        this.pattern[this.i + 1] !== "]"
      ) {
        this.i++; // -
        const atom2 = this.parseClassAtom();
        if (atom2.kind === "char") {
          const a = atom.ch;
          const b = atom2.ch;
          predicates.push((ch) => ch >= a && ch <= b);
        } else {
          predicates.push((ch) => ch === atom.ch);
          predicates.push((ch) => ch === "-");
          // best-effort: treat shorthand as literal dash already consumed
        }
      } else {
        if (atom.kind === "char") {
          predicates.push((ch) => ch === atom.ch);
        } else {
          predicates.push(atom.pred);
        }
      }
    }
    if (this.peek() !== "]") throw new Error("Unclosed character class");
    this.i++; // ]
    const test = (ch: string) => predicates.some((p) => p(ch));
    return { type: "class", test, neg };
  }

  private parseClassAtom():
    | { kind: "char"; ch: string }
    | { kind: "shorthand"; pred: (ch: string) => boolean } {
    if (this.peek() === "\\") {
      this.i++;
      const c = this.peek();
      this.i++;
      switch (c) {
        case "d":
          return { kind: "shorthand", pred: (ch) => /\d/.test(ch) };
        case "D":
          return { kind: "shorthand", pred: (ch) => !/\d/.test(ch) };
        case "w":
          return { kind: "shorthand", pred: (ch) => /\w/.test(ch) };
        case "W":
          return { kind: "shorthand", pred: (ch) => !/\w/.test(ch) };
        case "s":
          return { kind: "shorthand", pred: (ch) => /\s/.test(ch) };
        case "S":
          return { kind: "shorthand", pred: (ch) => !/\s/.test(ch) };
        default:
          return { kind: "char", ch: c };
      }
    }
    const c = this.peek();
    this.i++;
    return { kind: "char", ch: c };
  }
}

/* ----------------------------- Matcher ----------------------------- */

function matchNode(
  node: Node,
  str: string,
  i: number,
  groups: (string | undefined)[],
  cont: (i: number, groups: (string | undefined)[]) => boolean,
  flags: Flags
): boolean {
  switch (node.type) {
    case "lit": {
      if (i >= str.length) return false;
      let a = node.char;
      let b = str[i];
      if (flags.ignoreCase) {
        a = a.toLowerCase();
        b = b.toLowerCase();
      }
      if (a !== b) return false;
      return cont(i + 1, groups);
    }
    case "any": {
      if (i >= str.length) return false;
      const ch = str[i];
      if (!flags.dotAll && ch === "\n") return false;
      return cont(i + 1, groups);
    }
    case "class": {
      if (i >= str.length) return false;
      const passed = node.test(str[i]);
      const ok = node.neg ? !passed : passed;
      if (!ok) return false;
      return cont(i + 1, groups);
    }
    case "anchor": {
      if (node.kind === "start") {
        if (i === 0) return cont(i, groups);
        if (flags.multiline && i > 0 && str[i - 1] === "\n") {
          return cont(i, groups);
        }
        return false;
      } else {
        if (i === str.length) return cont(i, groups);
        if (flags.multiline && i < str.length && str[i] === "\n") {
          return cont(i, groups);
        }
        return false;
      }
    }
    case "empty":
      return cont(i, groups);

    case "concat": {
      const go = (
        idx: number,
        pos: number,
        g: (string | undefined)[]
      ): boolean => {
        if (idx === node.nodes.length) return cont(pos, g);
        return matchNode(node.nodes[idx], str, pos, g, (j, g2) => go(idx + 1, j, g2), flags);
      };
      return go(0, i, groups);
    }

    case "alt":
      return (
        matchNode(node.left, str, i, groups, cont, flags) ||
        matchNode(node.right, str, i, groups, cont, flags)
      );

    case "quant": {
      const { min, max, node: sub, greedy } = node;
      if (greedy) {
        const tryMore = (
          pos: number,
          g: (string | undefined)[],
          count: number
        ): boolean => {
          if (count < max) {
            const ok = matchNode(sub, str, pos, g, (j, g2) => tryMore(j, g2, count + 1), flags);
            if (ok) return true;
          }
          if (count >= min) return cont(pos, g);
          return false;
        };
        return tryMore(i, groups, 0);
      } else {
        const tryLazy = (
          pos: number,
          g: (string | undefined)[],
          count: number
        ): boolean => {
          if (count >= min) {
            if (cont(pos, g)) return true;
          }
          if (count >= max) return false;
          return matchNode(sub, str, pos, g, (j, g2) => tryLazy(j, g2, count + 1), flags);
        };
        return tryLazy(i, groups, 0);
      }
    }

    case "group": {
      return matchNode(node.node, str, i, groups, (j, g) => {
        const g2 = g.slice();
        g2[node.index] = str.slice(i, j);
        return cont(j, g2);
      }, flags);
    }

    case "backref": {
      const val = groups[node.index];
      if (val === undefined) return false;
      if (!str.startsWith(val, i)) return false;
      return cont(i + val.length, groups);
    }
  }
}

/* ----------------------------- Regex class ----------------------------- */

export interface ExecResult {
  match: string;
  groups: (string | undefined)[];
  index: number;
}

export class Regex {
  private ast: Node;
  private groupCount: number;
  private flags: Flags;

  constructor(source: string, flags = "") {
    this.flags = {
      ignoreCase: flags.includes("i"),
      multiline: flags.includes("m"),
      dotAll: flags.includes("s"),
    };
    const parser = new Parser(source);
    this.ast = parser.parse();
    this.groupCount = parser.groupCount;
  }

  exec(str: string): ExecResult | null {
    for (let start = 0; start <= str.length; start++) {
      const groups = new Array<string | undefined>(this.groupCount).fill(undefined);
      let foundPos = -1;
      let foundGroups: (string | undefined)[] | null = null;
      const ok = matchNode(this.ast, str, start, groups, (i, g) => {
        foundPos = i;
        foundGroups = g.slice();
        return true;
      }, this.flags);
      if (ok && foundGroups) {
        return {
          match: str.slice(start, foundPos),
          groups: foundGroups,
          index: start,
        };
      }
    }
    return null;
  }

  test(str: string): boolean {
    return this.exec(str) !== null;
  }
}

/* ----------------------------- Demo ----------------------------- */

if (require.main === module) {
  const r1 = new Regex("(a+)(b*)c", "i");
  console.log(r1.exec("xxAABBCyy")); // { match: 'AABBc'?, ... }

  const r2 = new Regex("^\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}$");
  console.log(r2.test("123-45-6789")); // true
  console.log(r2.test("12-45-6789"));  // false

  const r3 = new Regex("(\\w+)\\s+\\1"); // backreference
  console.log(r3.exec("hello hello world")); // match 'hello hello'

  const r4 = new Regex("a+?b"); // lazy
  console.log(r4.exec("aaab")); // match 'aaab' (lazy stops earlier? 'aab'?)

  const r5 = new Regex("[a-z]+", "i");
  console.log(r5.exec("123ABC456")); // match 'ABC'

  const r6 = new Regex("colou?r");
  console.log(r6.test("color"), r6.test("colour")); // true true
}
```

Notes:
- This is a teaching implementation; for production, use the built-in `RegExp`.
- The engine uses continuation-passing backtracking, so quantifiers and alternation backtrack correctly.
- Groups are 0-based internally; `groups[0]` is the first capturing group.
- Flags supported: `i` (case-insensitive), `m` (multiline ^/$), `s` (dotAll).
- Lazy quantifiers (`*?`, `+?`, `??`, `{n,m}?`) are parsed but the matcher honors the lazy flag.

# Regular Expressions in TypeScript

TypeScript has no separate “regex language” of its own — regexps are JavaScript `RegExp` objects, just with TypeScript’s static types on top. The value is in using **pattern matching** to validate, search, extract, and transform strings without writing manual parsing code.

## 1. The idea

A regular expression describes a **pattern** of characters. Instead of checking strings character by character, you declare “what shape” you want and let the engine find matches.

```ts
// Does the string look like an email-ish shape?
const isEmail = (s: string) => /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(s);

isEmail("a@b.com"); // true
isEmail("not-an-email"); // false
```

## 2. Creating regexes

### Literal (preferred when pattern is fixed)

```ts
const re: RegExp = /hello/i; // case-insensitive
```

### Constructor (use when pattern/flags are dynamic)

```ts
const word = "hello";
const re = new RegExp(word, "gi"); // flags as second arg
```

> **Pitfall:** with `new RegExp(string)`, backslashes must be double-escaped:
> `new RegExp("\\d+")` is equivalent to `/\d+/`.

### Useful flags

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `g`  | Global — find all matches |
| `i`  | Case-insensitive |
| `m`  | `^`/`$` match line boundaries |
| `s`  | `.` matches newlines (dotAll) |
| `u`  | Unicode mode (full Unicode code points, `\p{...}`) |
| `y`  | Sticky — match only at `lastIndex` |
| `d`  | Indices — include match capture group indices |

## 3. Core syntax (cheat sheet)

```ts
// Character classes
/\d/   // digit [0-9]
/\w/   // word char [A-Za-z0-9_]
/\s/   // whitespace
/[aeiou]/   // one of a,e,i,o,u
/[^aeiou]/  // not a vowel
/[a-z]/     // range

// Quantifiers
/a?/   // 0 or 1
/a*/   // 0 or more
/a+/   // 1 or more
/a{2,4}/ // 2–4 times
/a{3}/   // exactly 3

// Anchors
/^start/    // beginning of string (or line with m)
/end$/      // end of string
/\bword\b/  // word boundary

// Groups
/(ab)+/        // capturing group
/(?:ab)+/      // non-capturing group (grouping only)
/(?<name>\w+)/ // named capturing group

// Alternation
/cat|dog/
```

## 4. Common methods

### `test` — boolean check

```ts
const hasDigit = /\d/.test("abc123"); // true
```

> With the `g` flag, `test` mutates `lastIndex`. Avoid `g` for simple boolean checks, or reset `lastIndex = 0`.

### `exec` — match + captured groups

```ts
const re = /(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})/;
const m = re.exec("Date: 2024-05-01");
// m: RegExpExecArray | null
if (m) {
  const [full, year, month, day] = m;
  console.log(year, month, day); // "2024" "05" "01"
}
```

### String methods

```ts
"hello world".match(/\w+/g);        // ["hello", "world"] | null
"2024-05-01".match(/^(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})$/);
// ["2024-05-01", "2024", "05", "01", index: 0, ...]

"café".replace(/café/, "coffee");   // "coffee"
"a b  c".split(/\s+/);              // ["a", "b", "c"]
"abc".search(/b/);                  // 1
```

### `matchAll` — all matches with groups (TS-friendly iterable)

```ts
const text = "User: alice (id=1), User: bob (id=2)";
const re = /User: (\w+) \(id=(\d+)\)/g;

for (const m of text.matchAll(re)) {
  console.log(m[1], m[2]); // alice 1 ; bob 2
}
```

TypeScript types `matchAll` results as `IterableIterator<RegExpMatchArray>`, and each entry has `index`, `input`, `groups`, etc.

## 5. Named groups (great for readability)

```ts
const re = /(?<year>\d{4})-(?<month>\d{2})-(?<day>\d{2})/;
const m = "2024-05-01".match(re);

if (m?.groups) {
  const { year, month, day } = m.groups; // all string | undefined under strict types
  console.log(year, month, day);
}
```

> Under `strictNullChecks`, `m.groups` may be `undefined` in the type system, so guard with `?.`.

## 6. Typical use cases with examples

### A. Validation

```ts
const validators = {
  email: /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/,
  phoneUS: /^\+?1?\s*\(?\d{3}\)?[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}$/,
  slug: /^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$/,
};

function validate(kind: keyof typeof validators, value: string): boolean {
  return validators[kind].test(value);
}
```

### B. Extraction / parsing

```ts
function parseQuery(qs: string): Record<string, string> {
  const out: Record<string, string> = {};
  for (const m of qs.matchAll(/([^&=]+)=([^&]*)/g)) {
    out[decodeURIComponent(m[1])] = decodeURIComponent(m[2]);
  }
  return out;
}
```

### C. Normalization / formatting

```ts
const normalizeWhitespace = (s: string) => s.trim().replace(/\s+/g, " ");

const maskCard = (s: string) =>
  s.replace(/\b(\d{4})\d{8}(\d{4})\b/, "$1********$2");
```

### D. Tokenizing

```ts
const tokens = "let x = 42;".match(/\w+|[^\s\w]/g);
// ["let", "x", "=", "42", ";"]
```

### E. Input sanitization (careful!)

```ts
// Strip HTML tags — OK for simple cases, NOT a security sanitizer
const stripTags = (s: string) => s.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, "");
```

## 7. TypeScript-specific typing tips

```ts
import type { RegExpExecArray, RegExpMatchArray } from "lib.es2015"; // built-in

function firstMatch(re: RegExp, s: string): string | undefined {
  const m = s.match(re);
  return m?.[0]; // m is string[] | null
}

// Strongly type captured groups via a helper
type Groups<T extends string> = { [K in T]?: string };

function namedMatch<K extends string>(
  re: RegExp & { groups: K },
  s: string
): Groups<K> | null {
  const m = s.match(re);
  return (m?.groups as Groups<K>) ?? null;
}
```

In practice, most code just narrows `RegExpMatchArray | null` with `if (m)`.

## 8. Pitfalls to avoid

1. **Catastrophic backtracking** — patterns like `(a+)+b` on `"aaaa...!"` can hang. Prefer possessive/atomic-ish rewrites, or use libraries like `re2`.
2. **`g` flag statefulness** — `re.test()`/`re.exec()` advance `lastIndex`; reuse with care:
   ```ts
   re.lastIndex = 0; // reset before reuse
   ```
3. **`.` doesn’t match newlines** by default — add `s` flag.
4. **Unicode** — use `u` flag and `\p{...}` classes (e.g., `/\p{Letter}+/u`).
5. **Don’t parse HTML/XML with regex** for security-critical code; use a parser.

## TL;DR mental model

- Use **literal `/.../`** for static patterns, **`new RegExp`** for dynamic ones.
- Prefer `match`/`matchAll`/`replace` over manual loops.
- Guard `null` results under `strictNullChecks`.
- Add flags deliberately (`g`, `i`, `m`, `s`, `u`), and remember `g` mutates state.

A very common TypeScript / JavaScript regex pitfall is **reusing a regex with the global (`g`) flag across multiple `.test()` calls**. Because regexes with `g` are stateful (`lastIndex`), the second call starts searching from where the first match ended and often returns `false` even though the string contains the pattern.

### Mistaken snippet

```ts
const hasFoo = /foo/g;

const inputs = ["foo bar", "foo baz", "no foo here"];

for (const s of inputs) {
  // BUG: alternates true, false, true, ...
  console.log(hasFoo.test(s));
}
```

**Output (surprising):**
```
true
false
true
```

### Corrected version

```ts
// Option A: drop the `g` flag when you only need a boolean test
const hasFoo = /foo/;

for (const s of inputs) {
  console.log(hasFoo.test(s)); // true, true, false
}

// Option B: keep `g` but reset lastIndex before each test
const hasFooG = /foo/g;
for (const s of inputs) {
  hasFooG.lastIndex = 0;
  console.log(hasFooG.test(s));
}

// Option C: create a fresh regex inside the loop
for (const s of inputs) {
  console.log(/foo/g.test(s));
}
```

### What was wrong and why

1. **Stateful matching.** In JavaScript, a regex created with the `g` (or `y`) flag maintains a `lastIndex` property. After a successful `.test()`, `lastIndex` points just past the match. The next `.test()` begins searching from that index, so if the next string doesn’t contain the pattern *after* that offset, it returns `false` even if the pattern exists earlier.

2. **`.test()` mutates the regex object.** This side effect is easy to miss, especially when the regex is defined once at module scope and reused across many calls (loops, event handlers, request handlers).

3. **When `g` is actually needed.** Keep `g` only when you genuinely need repeated matches (e.g., `String.prototype.matchAll`, `RegExp.prototype.exec` in a loop, or global replacement). For a simple “does it contain?” check, omit `g`.

### Related pitfalls worth knowing

- **`.match()` with `g` drops capture groups.** `("a1 b2".match(/([a-z])(\d)/g))` returns `["a1", "b2"]`, losing the groups. Use `matchAll` to keep groups.
- **`new RegExp` string escaping.** `new RegExp("\\d+")` needs double backslashes; `new RegExp("\d+")` is just `new RegExp("d+")`.
- **Greedy quantifiers.** `/<.*>/.exec("<a><b>")` matches the whole `"<a><b>"`, not `"<a>"`. Use `/<.*?>/` or better, `/<[^>]*>/`.
- **Catastrophic backtracking.** Patterns like `/(a+)+$/` on long “aaaa…X” strings can hang. Prefer possessive/atomic groups (not native in JS) or rewrite to avoid nested quantifiers.

### Quick rule of thumb

- Boolean contains-check → **no `g`**.
- Reuse across calls → **reset `lastIndex = 0`** or create fresh.
- Need groups + global → **`matchAll`** instead of `match`.

