## Notes / Design choices

| Algorithm | Requires sorted input | Typical complexity | Best for |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Linear search | No | `O(n)` | Small/unsorted data |
| Binary search | Yes | `O(log n)` | General sorted arrays |
| Jump search | Yes | `O(√n)` | When jumping is cheaper than binary midpoint access |
| Interpolation search | Yes, numeric & uniform | `O(log log n)` avg | Uniformly distributed numbers |
| Exponential search | Yes | `O(log n)` | Unknown/unbounded array size |
| Ternary search | Yes | `O(log₃ n)` | Sorted arrays; also used for unimodal functions |
| Fibonacci search | Yes | `O(log n)` | When division is expensive (uses only + and -) |

All functions return the **index** of the found value, or `-1` if not present. The generic comparators let you reuse the same algorithms for numbers, strings, objects, or any ordered type.

## Searching algorithms in TypeScript

Searching means: given a collection and a target, find whether (and where) the target occurs. In TypeScript the “syntax” is mostly plain JavaScript plus types, generics, and comparators.

### 1. Linear search (sequential scan)

**Idea:** check elements one by one until a match is found. Works on any array, sorted or not.

**Use cases:** small arrays, unsorted data, one-off lookups, predicates (e.g. “first user with role admin”).

```ts
function linearSearch<T>(
  arr: readonly T[],
  predicate: (item: T, index: number) => boolean
): number {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (predicate(arr[i], i)) return i;
  }
  return -1;
}

const users = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }, { id: 3 }];
const idx = linearSearch(users, u => u.id === 2); // 1
```

Complexity: **O(n)** time, **O(1)** space.

---

### 2. Binary search

**Idea:** repeatedly halve a **sorted** range by comparing the middle element to the target.

**Precondition:** the input must be sorted according to the same comparator.

**Use cases:** large sorted arrays, numeric/string IDs, “lower bound / upper bound” queries.

```ts
function binarySearch<T>(
  arr: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  compare: (a: T, b: T) => number
): number {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length - 1;

  while (lo <= hi) {
    // avoids overflow vs (lo+hi)/2
    const mid = lo + ((hi - lo) >> 1);
    const cmp = compare(arr[mid], target);

    if (cmp === 0) return mid;
    if (cmp < 0) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}

const nums = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11];
const i = binarySearch(nums, 7, (a, b) => a - b); // 3
```

Complexity: **O(log n)** time, **O(1)** space (iterative).

#### Recursive variant
```ts
function binarySearchRec<T>(
  arr: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  compare: (a: T, b: T) => number,
  lo = 0,
  hi = arr.length - 1
): number {
  if (lo > hi) return -1;
  const mid = lo + ((hi - lo) >> 1);
  const cmp = compare(arr[mid], target);
  if (cmp === 0) return mid;
  return cmp < 0
    ? binarySearchRec(arr, target, compare, mid + 1, hi)
    : binarySearchRec(arr, target, compare, lo, mid - 1);
}
```

#### Lower/upper bound (useful for duplicates)
```ts
function lowerBound<T>(arr: readonly T[], target: T, cmp: (a: T, b: T) => number): number {
  let lo = 0, hi = arr.length;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + ((hi - lo) >> 1);
    if (cmp(arr[mid], target) < 0) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid;
  }
  return lo; // first index with arr[i] >= target
}
```

---

### 3. Other array searching algorithms (brief)

| Algorithm | Idea | Complexity | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| Jump search | Jump by `√n`, then linear scan in block | O(√n) | Sorted, when binary search jump cost is high |
| Interpolation search | Estimate position from value range | O(log log n) avg, O(n) worst | Uniformly distributed sorted data |
| Exponential search | Double index until overshoot, then binary search | O(log n) | Unbounded / unknown length sorted arrays |
| Ternary search | Split into thirds | O(log₃ n) | Unimodal functions, not arrays |

Example: exponential search
```ts
function exponentialSearch(arr: readonly number[], target: number): number {
  if (arr.length === 0 || arr[0] > target) return -1; // assumes ascending
  let bound = 1;
  while (bound < arr.length && arr[bound] <= target) bound <<= 1;
  const lo = bound >> 1;
  const hi = Math.min(bound, arr.length) - 1;
  // binary search in [lo, hi]
  let l = lo, r = hi;
  while (l <= r) {
    const m = l + ((r - l) >> 1);
    if (arr[m] === target) return m;
    if (arr[m] < target) l = m + 1;
    else r = m - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

---

### 4. Hash-based lookup (Set / Map)

**Idea:** trade space for O(1) average lookup using a hash table.

**Use cases:** membership tests, deduplication, “have I seen this before”.

```ts
const seen = new Set<number>([1, 2, 3]);
if (seen.has(2)) { /* O(1) avg */ }

const byId = new Map<number, { name: string }>();
byId.set(42, { name: "Ada" });
const user = byId.get(42); // O(1) avg
```

---

### 5. Built-in array search methods

TypeScript inherits JS array methods; prefer them for clarity unless you need custom comparison or performance control.

```ts
const nums = [10, 20, 30, 20];

nums.indexOf(20);        // 1
nums.lastIndexOf(20);    // 3
nums.includes(30);       // true
nums.find(x => x > 15);  // 20
nums.findIndex(x => x > 15); // 1
```

For objects, `find` with a predicate is usually the right tool:
```ts
interface Product { id: number; name: string }
const products: Product[] = [{ id: 1, name: "A" }, { id: 2, name: "B" }];
const p = products.find(p => p.id === 2);
```

---

### 6. Graph / tree searches (BFS & DFS)

“Searching” also includes traversing structures.

**BFS** (shortest path in unweighted graphs):
```ts
function bfs(adj: Map<number, number[]>, start: number): number[] {
  const visited = new Set<number>([start]);
  const queue: number[] = [start];
  const order: number[] = [];
  while (queue.length) {
    const u = queue.shift()!;
    order.push(u);
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) {
        visited.add(v);
        queue.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return order;
}
```

**DFS** (deep exploration, topological-ish):
```ts
function dfs(adj: Map<number, number[]>, start: number): number[] {
  const visited = new Set<number>();
  const order: number[] = [];
  const stack: number[] = [start];
  while (stack.length) {
    const u = stack.pop()!;
    if (visited.has(u)) continue;
    visited.add(u);
    order.push(u);
    for (const v of adj.get(u) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(v)) stack.push(v);
    }
  }
  return order;
}
```

---

### 7. TypeScript-specific patterns

#### Generics with constraints
```ts
function searchById<T extends { id: number }>(items: readonly T[], id: number): T | undefined {
  return items.find(x => x.id === id);
}
```

#### Overloads for flexible APIs
```ts
function findItem(items: number[], target: number): number;
function findItem<T>(items: T[], predicate: (x: T) => boolean): T | undefined;
function findItem(items: any[], arg: any): any {
  if (typeof arg === "function") return items.find(arg);
  return items.indexOf(arg);
}
```

#### Type guards in search
```ts
type Result = { kind: "ok"; value: number } | { kind: "err"; msg: string };

function findOk(results: Result[]): Extract<Result, { kind: "ok" }> | undefined {
  return results.find((r): r is Extract<Result, { kind: "ok" }> => r.kind === "ok");
}
```

#### Comparator factories
```ts
const byName = (a: { name: string }, b: { name: string }) =>
  a.name.localeCompare(b.name);

const sorted = [...users].sort(byName);
const idx = binarySearch(sorted, { name: "Ada" }, byName);
```

---

### 8. Choosing the right approach

- **Unsorted, small, or predicate-based** → `find` / `findIndex` / linear search.
- **Sorted array, many queries** → binary search (or keep a `Map` for O(1)).
- **Membership only** → `Set`.
- **Key → value** → `Map`.
- **Graph connectivity / shortest path** → BFS/DFS (or Dijkstra for weighted).
- **Huge dataset, repeated lookups** → sort once + binary search, or build an index (`Map`).

### 9. Complexity cheat sheet

| Method | Time (avg) | Space | Requires sorted? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Linear search | O(n) | O(1) | No |
| Binary search | O(log n) | O(1) | Yes |
| Jump search | O(√n) | O(1) | Yes |
| Interpolation | O(log log n) | O(1) | Yes (uniform) |
| Set/Map `has`/`get` | O(1) | O(n) | No (hashed) |
| BFS/DFS | O(V+E) | O(V) | Graph structure |

### Practical tip
In real TypeScript code, **measure first**: V8 optimizes `Array.prototype.find` and `Map`/`Set` heavily. Implement custom binary search when profiling shows the built-in linear scan is a bottleneck on large sorted data; otherwise prefer readability and the standard library.

## Typical mistake: binary search with non-progressing bounds

A very common bug is updating `low` or `high` to `mid` instead of `mid + 1` / `mid - 1`. This can make the search loop forever.

### Mistaken version

```ts
function binarySearch(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let low = 0;
  let high = arr.length - 1;

  while (low <= high) {
    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);

    if (arr[mid] === target) {
      return mid;
    }

    if (arr[mid] < target) {
      low = mid;        // BUG: should be mid + 1
    } else {
      high = mid;       // BUG: should be mid - 1
    }
  }

  return -1;
}
```

### Corrected version

```ts
function binarySearch(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let low = 0;
  let high = arr.length - 1;

  while (low <= high) {
    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);

    if (arr[mid] === target) {
      return mid;
    }

    if (arr[mid] < target) {
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return -1;
}
```

### What was wrong

In binary search, once `arr[mid]` is known to be less than the target, every index from `low` through `mid` is also too small, assuming the array is sorted. Therefore the next search range should start at `mid + 1`, not `mid`.

Similarly, if `arr[mid]` is greater than the target, every index from `mid` through `high` is too large, so the next range should end at `mid - 1`, not `mid`.

With the buggy version, when only two adjacent elements remain, `mid` can equal `low`. If `arr[mid] < target`, setting `low = mid` does not move `low` forward, so the loop repeats with the exact same range forever.

Example failure:

```ts
const nums = [10, 20];
console.log(binarySearch(nums, 20)); // Infinite loop in the buggy version
```

Trace of the buggy version:

1. `low = 0`, `high = 1`
2. `mid = 0`
3. `arr[0] = 10 < 20`, so `low = 0`
4. State is unchanged: `low = 0`, `high = 1`
5. Repeat forever

Trace of the corrected version:

1. `low = 0`, `high = 1`
2. `mid = 0`
3. `arr[0] = 10 < 20`, so `low = 1`
4. `low = 1`, `high = 1`
5. `mid = 1`
6. `arr[1] = 20 === 20`, return `1`

### Important assumptions and edge cases

- The input array must be sorted in ascending order. Binary search is incorrect on unsorted data.
- Duplicate values are allowed; this implementation returns some matching index, not necessarily the first or last.
- An empty array works correctly: `high = -1`, so the loop never runs and `-1` is returned.
- For very large arrays in languages with fixed-width integers, `(low + high) / 2` can overflow; TypeScript/JavaScript numbers are floating-point and do not have the same integer-overflow issue, but a safer midpoint formula is still a good habit:

```ts
const mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);
```

This avoids relying on `low + high` and is the conventional form used in production code.

Below are common pitfalls when implementing or using searching algorithms in TypeScript, with “bad” vs “better” code for each.

---

### 1. Off-by-one errors in binary search

The most common bug: wrong bounds initialization or bound updates, causing infinite loops or missed elements.

**Bad**
```ts
function binarySearchBad(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;          // should usually be length - 1 for inclusive hi
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;
    if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid;   // should be mid + 1
    else hi = mid;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

**Better**
```ts
function binarySearch(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length - 1;

  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2); // avoids overflow/precision issues
    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;
    if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

---

### 2. Mutating the input array

Many “search after sort” implementations sort in place, surprising callers.

**Bad**
```ts
function containsSorted(arr: number[], target: number): boolean {
  arr.sort((a, b) => a - b); // mutates original!
  return binarySearch(arr, target) !== -1;
}
```

**Better**
```ts
function containsSorted(arr: readonly number[], target: number): boolean {
  const sorted = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  return binarySearch(sorted, target) !== -1;
}
```

---

### 3. Assuming unsorted data is sorted

Binary search on unsorted data silently returns wrong results.

**Bad**
```ts
const data = [5, 1, 9, 3];
const idx = binarySearch(data, 3); // wrong: data is not sorted
```

**Better**
```ts
function searchSortedOnly(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  // Enforce or document precondition; optionally validate in debug builds.
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) {
      throw new Error("binarySearch requires a sorted array");
    }
  }
  return binarySearch(arr, target);
}
```

---

### 4. Ignoring `undefined` from `Array.prototype.find`

`find` returns `T | undefined`. Accessing properties without narrowing is a classic TS pitfall.

**Bad**
```ts
interface User { id: number; name: string; }
const users: User[] = [{ id: 1, name: "Ada" }];

const user = users.find(u => u.id === 2);
console.log(user.name); // TS error in strict mode, runtime crash if ignored
```

**Better**
```ts
const user = users.find(u => u.id === 2);
if (!user) {
  throw new Error("User not found");
}
console.log(user.name);
```

---

### 5. Comparator returning a boolean instead of a number

`Array.prototype.sort` expects `-1 | 0 | 1` (or any number). A boolean comparator corrupts ordering, breaking subsequent searches.

**Bad**
```ts
const items = [{ v: 2 }, { v: 10 }, { v: 1 }];
items.sort((a, b) => a.v > b.v); // boolean, not number
```

**Better**
```ts
items.sort((a, b) => a.v - b.v);
```

For non-numeric keys:
```ts
items.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
```

---

### 6. Duplicate values and “first vs any” index

Binary search may return *any* matching index. If you need the first/last occurrence, use lower/upper bound logic.

**Bad**
```ts
// Returns some index of target, not necessarily the first.
const idx = binarySearch([1, 2, 2, 2, 3], 2);
```

**Better**
```ts
function lowerBound(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;

  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid;
  }
  return lo; // first index where arr[i] >= target
}

function upperBound(arr: number[], target: number): number {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;

  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] <= target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid;
  }
  return lo; // first index where arr[i] > target
}
```

---

### 7. Recursion depth / stack overflow

Recursive binary search can overflow for very large inputs or deep call stacks.

**Bad**
```ts
function recursiveBinarySearch(
  arr: number[],
  target: number,
  lo = 0,
  hi = arr.length - 1
): number {
  if (lo > hi) return -1;
  const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
  if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;
  if (arr[mid] < target) {
    return recursiveBinarySearch(arr, target, mid + 1, hi);
  }
  return recursiveBinarySearch(arr, target, lo, mid - 1);
}
```

**Better**
Use the iterative version from pitfall #1 for production code, especially with large datasets.

---

### 8. Floating-point comparison issues

Direct `===` on computed floats can fail due to precision.

**Bad**
```ts
const values = [0.1 + 0.2];
const found = values.indexOf(0.3); // -1
```

**Better**
```ts
function approxEqual(a: number, b: number, eps = 1e-9): boolean {
  return Math.abs(a - b) < eps;
}

function findApprox(arr: number[], target: number, eps = 1e-9): number {
  return arr.findIndex(x => Math.abs(x - target) < eps);
}
```

---

### 9. Regex search state bugs with the global flag

`RegExp.prototype.test` and `exec` mutate `lastIndex` when the `g` flag is set.

**Bad**
```ts
const re = /foo/g;
console.log(re.test("foo bar foo")); // true
console.log(re.test("foo bar foo")); // false (lastIndex advanced)
```

**Better**
```ts
const re = /foo/g;
re.lastIndex = 0;
const matches = [..."foo bar foo".matchAll(re)];
```

Or avoid global state:
```ts
function containsPattern(text: string, pattern: RegExp): boolean {
  // Create a fresh regex without global flag for a simple boolean check.
  const safe = new RegExp(pattern.source, pattern.flags.replace("g", ""));
  return safe.test(text);
}
```

---

### 10. Case sensitivity and locale issues in string search

`includes` and `indexOf` are case-sensitive; naive lowercasing can break for some locales.

**Bad**
```ts
const names = ["Éclair", "eclair"];
const has = names.some(n => n.toLowerCase().includes("eclair"));
```

**Better**
```ts
const has = names.some(n =>
  n.toLocaleLowerCase("en-US").includes("eclair")
);
```

For sorting/searching with locale rules, use `localeCompare`:
```ts
names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, "en-US"));
```

---

### 11. Using `for...in` to search arrays

`for...in` iterates enumerable property names, not necessarily array indices in order, and includes inherited properties.

**Bad**
```ts
for (const key in arr) {
  if (arr[key] === target) return Number(key);
}
```

**Better**
```ts
for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  if (arr[i] === target) return i;
}
// or
return arr.findIndex(x => x === target);
```

---

### 12. Not handling `null` / `undefined` elements

Arrays typed as `T[]` may still contain holes or nullable values at runtime (especially from JSON).

**Bad**
```ts
const ids: (number | null)[] = [1, null, 3];
const found = ids.find(x => x > 2); // TS: x is number | null, comparison error
```

**Better**
```ts
const found = ids.find((x): x is number => x !== null && x > 2);
```

---

### 13. Quadratic “search” via nested loops

Searching for pairs or subarrays with nested loops is often accidentally `O(n²)`.

**Bad**
```ts
function hasPairSum(arr: number[], sum: number): boolean {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
      if (arr[i] + arr[j] === sum) return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

**Better** (sort + two pointers, `O(n log n)`)
```ts
function hasPairSum(arr: number[], sum: number): boolean {
  const sorted = [...arr].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = sorted.length - 1;

  while (lo < hi) {
    const s = sorted[lo] + sorted[hi];
    if (s === sum) return true;
    if (s < sum) lo++;
    else hi--;
  }
  return false;
}
```

---

### 14. Inconsistent “not found” return values

Mixing `-1`, `undefined`, and `null` makes callers error-prone.

**Bad**
```ts
function findUser(id: number): User | -1 { ... } // -1 is not a User
```

**Better**
Pick one convention and document it:
```ts
function findUser(users: User[], id: number): User | undefined {
  return users.find(u => u.id === id);
}

// Or, if index is needed:
function findUserIndex(users: User[], id: number): number {
  return users.findIndex(u => u.id === id); // -1 means not found
}
```

---

### Quick checklist

- Validate or document sortedness before binary search.
- Never mutate caller data unless explicitly intended.
- Use inclusive/exclusive bounds consistently.
- Compute `mid` as `lo + floor((hi - lo) / 2)`.
- Narrow `undefined` from `find`.
- Return numbers from comparators.
- Handle duplicates with lower/upper bounds.
- Prefer iterative search for large inputs.
- Be careful with floats, regex global state, and locale-sensitive strings.
- Use `findIndex`/`find` instead of manual loops when clarity matters.

## Exercise

**Title:** First Occurrence Search in a Sorted Array with Duplicates  

**Task:** Write a TypeScript function `findFirstOccurrence(nums: number[], target: number): number` that returns the index of the **first** occurrence of `target` in a sorted (non-decreasing) array. If `target` is not present, return `-1`. The array may contain duplicates.

**Example:**
```ts
findFirstOccurrence([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4], 2) // 1
findFirstOccurrence([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4], 5) // -1
findFirstOccurrence([], 1) // -1
```

---

## Step-by-Step Solution

### 1. Understand the Problem

- The array is **sorted non-decreasingly**.
- We need the **leftmost** index where `nums[i] === target`.
- A plain linear scan works but is `O(n)`. Since the array is sorted, we can use **binary search** to achieve `O(log n)`.

### 2. Binary Search Strategy

Standard binary search finds *some* occurrence. To find the *first*, when we find `nums[mid] === target`, we don’t stop immediately. Instead, we record `mid` as a candidate and continue searching the **left half** (`high = mid - 1`) for an earlier occurrence.

Invariant:
- `low` and `high` define the current search window.
- `result` stores the best (smallest) index found so far.

### 3. Edge Cases

- Empty array → return `-1`.
- Target smaller than first or larger than last → binary search naturally returns `-1`.
- All elements equal to target → should return `0`.
- No duplicates → works like standard binary search.

### 4. TypeScript Implementation

```ts
function findFirstOccurrence(nums: number[], target: number): number {
  let low = 0;
  let high = nums.length - 1;
  let result = -1;

  while (low <= high) {
    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);

    if (nums[mid] === target) {
      result = mid;          // candidate found
      high = mid - 1;        // keep searching left for earlier occurrence
    } else if (nums[mid] < target) {
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

### 5. Complexity Analysis

- **Time:** `O(log n)` — each iteration halves the search space.
- **Space:** `O(1)` — only a few variables used.

### 6. Test Cases

```ts
console.log(findFirstOccurrence([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4], 2)); // 1
console.log(findFirstOccurrence([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4], 5)); // -1
console.log(findFirstOccurrence([], 1));                 // -1
console.log(findFirstOccurrence([2, 2, 2, 2], 2));       // 0
console.log(findFirstOccurrence([1, 3, 5, 7], 4));       // -1
console.log(findFirstOccurrence([1, 3, 5, 7], 1));       // 0
```

---

## Final Solution

```ts
/**
 * Returns the index of the first occurrence of target in a sorted array.
 * Returns -1 if target is not found.
 */
function findFirstOccurrence(nums: number[], target: number): number {
  let low = 0;
  let high = nums.length - 1;
  let result = -1;

  while (low <= high) {
    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);

    if (nums[mid] === target) {
      result = mid;
      high = mid - 1; // search left half for earlier occurrence
    } else if (nums[mid] < target) {
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return result;
}

export { findFirstOccurrence };
```

### Key Takeaway

When a sorted structure is available, prefer binary search variants that adjust the search boundary instead of stopping early—this pattern (record candidate + continue searching one side) generalizes to “first/last occurrence,” “lower/upper bound,” and “insertion position” problems.

## Searching Algorithms — TypeScript (Interview Style)

### 1. Problem & Intuition
**Searching** means: given a collection `data` and a `target`, find an index (or element) satisfying a predicate.  
The “best” algorithm depends on:

| Property | Implication |
|---|---|
| Sorted? | Enables binary/interpolation/jump search |
| Size | Small → linear may win due to cache |
| Access cost | Random access (array) vs sequential (linked list) |
| Duplicates | Need lower/upper bound semantics |

### 2. Common Algorithms

- **Linear Search** — scan left to right until match.  
  - Works on **unsorted** data.  
  - Time: `O(n)`, Space: `O(1)`.

- **Binary Search** — repeatedly halve a **sorted** range.  
  - Time: `O(log n)`, Space: `O(1)` iterative / `O(log n)` recursive.  
  - Core idea: compare middle element; discard half.

- **Jump Search** — jump by `√n`, then linear scan.  
  - Sorted array, `O(√n)`.

- **Interpolation Search** — estimate position by value distribution.  
  - Uniformly distributed sorted data, average `O(log log n)`.

- **Exponential Search** — find range by doubling, then binary search.  
  - Unbounded / unknown-size sorted data, `O(log n)`.

- **Ternary Search** — divide into thirds; useful for unimodal functions, not typical arrays.

### 3. “Core” Implementation: Binary Search (+ Bounds)

Below is a clean, production-ish TypeScript core: generic, comparator-driven, handles duplicates via `lowerBound`/`upperBound`.

```ts
// search.ts

/**
 * Linear search: works on unsorted data.
 * Returns first index where predicate is true, or -1.
 */
export function linearSearch<T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  eq: (a: T, b: T) => boolean = Object.is
): number {
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    if (eq(items[i], target)) return i;
  }
  return -1;
}

/**
 * Comparator convention: negative if a < b, 0 if equal, positive if a > b.
 */
export type Comparator<T> = (a: T, b: T) => number;

/**
 * Iterative binary search on a sorted array.
 * Returns index of target if found, otherwise -1.
 */
export function binarySearch<T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  cmp: Comparator<T> = (a, b) => (a as any) - (b as any)
): number {
  let left = 0;
  let right = items.length - 1;

  while (left <= right) {
    // Avoid (left + right) overflow for very large indices.
    const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);
    const order = cmp(items[mid], target);

    if (order === 0) return mid;          // found
    if (order < 0) left = mid + 1;        // target is to the right
    else right = mid - 1;                 // target is to the left
  }

  return -1;
}

/**
 * Lower bound: first index i such that items[i] >= target (by cmp).
 * Useful for insertion point and duplicate handling.
 */
export function lowerBound<T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  cmp: Comparator<T> = (a, b) => (a as any) - (b as any)
): number {
  let left = 0;
  let right = items.length; // note: half-open [left, right)

  while (left < right) {
    const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);
    if (cmp(items[mid], target) < 0) {
      left = mid + 1; // too small, move right
    } else {
      right = mid;    // candidate (>= target)
    }
  }
  return left; // may be items.length
}

/**
 * Upper bound: first index i such that items[i] > target.
 */
export function upperBound<T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  cmp: Comparator<T> = (a, b) => (a as any) - (b as any)
): number {
  let left = 0;
  let right = items.length;

  while (left < right) {
    const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);
    if (cmp(items[mid], target) <= 0) {
      left = mid + 1; // <= target, move right
    } else {
      right = mid;
    }
  }
  return left;
}

/**
 * Count occurrences of target in a sorted array.
 */
export function countOccurrences<T>(
  items: readonly T[],
  target: T,
  cmp: Comparator<T> = (a, b) => (a as any) - (b as any)
): number {
  return upperBound(items, target, cmp) - lowerBound(items, target, cmp);
}
```

### 4. Example Usage

```ts
const nums = [1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 5, 9];

console.log(binarySearch(nums, 5));           // 5
console.log(binarySearch(nums, 7));           // -1
console.log(lowerBound(nums, 2));             // 1
console.log(upperBound(nums, 2));             // 4
console.log(countOccurrences(nums, 2));       // 3

// Custom comparator for objects
const users = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ada" },
  { id: 3, name: "Grace" },
  { id: 5, name: "Linus" },
];
const byId = (a: { id: number }, b: { id: number }) => a.id - b.id;
console.log(binarySearch(users, { id: 3, name: "Grace" }, byId)); // 1
```

### 5. Interview Discussion Points

- **Preconditions**: Binary search requires sorted data; if unsorted, sort first (`O(n log n)`) only if many queries.
- **Duplicates**: Decide exact-match vs first/last occurrence; use bounds.
- **Stability/Comparator**: Default numeric subtraction fails for non-numbers; always allow custom `cmp`.
- **Edge cases**: empty array, single element, target smaller/larger than all, all duplicates.
- **Recursion vs iteration**: iterative avoids stack overflow and is usually preferred in production.
- ** interviewer trick**: ask for “insertion position” → `lowerBound`, not `binarySearch`.

### 6. Complexity Summary

| Algorithm | Sorted? | Time (avg) | Space |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Linear | No | `O(n)` | `O(1)` |
| Binary | Yes | `O(log n)` | `O(1)` |
| Jump | Yes | `O(√n)` | `O(1)` |
| Interpolation | Yes, uniform | `O(log log n)` | `O(1)` |
| Exponential | Yes, unbounded | `O(log n)` | `O(1)` |

