## Design notes

- **Storage**: A `Map<T, T>` gives O(1) average `has`/`add`/`delete` and preserves insertion order, matching native `Set` behavior.
- **Equality**: Relies on `Map`'s SameValueZero keys, so `NaN` is treated as equal to itself and objects are compared by reference. This matches the native `Set`/`Map` contract.
- **Generics**: The class is fully generic (`SetImpl<T>`); operations return `SetImpl<T>` so chaining stays type-safe.
- **Iterables**: Every operation accepts `Iterable<T>`, not just `SetImpl`, so you can pass arrays, native `Set`s, generators, etc.
- **Immutability of operations**: Algebra methods return new sets and never mutate their inputs, which makes them safe to use in functional pipelines.
- **Complexity**: `union`/`intersection`/etc. are O(n + m) where n, m are the input sizes (assuming O(1) membership).

If you want a truly "from scratch" version that doesn't delegate to `Map`, you can replace `store` with an array and linear scans — but you'd lose O(1) membership. The `Map`-backed approach above is what production-quality libraries (and the engine's own `Set`) effectively do.

## What a `Set` is

A `Set` is a built-in collection of **unique values**.  
Key properties:

- **No duplicates** — adding the same value twice has no effect.
- **Insertion order** — iteration follows the order in which values were first added.
- **Fast membership tests** — `has(value)` is typically O(1).
- **Any value type** — primitives and objects (objects compared by reference).

In TypeScript, a `Set` is generic: `Set<T>`.

---

## Basic syntax

### Creating a set

```ts
// Empty set with explicit type
const ids = new Set<number>();

// From an iterable (array, string, another Set, etc.)
const tags = new Set<string>(["ts", "js", "ts"]); // {"ts", "js"}

// Inferred type
const letters = new Set(["a", "b", "c"]); // Set<string>
```

### Core operations

```ts
const s = new Set<number>();

s.add(1);
s.add(2);
s.add(1); // ignored

console.log(s.size);   // 2
console.log(s.has(2)); // true
console.log(s.has(3)); // false

s.delete(1);           // true
s.delete(99);          // false
s.clear();             // removes everything
```

---

## Iteration

Sets are iterable, so they work with `for...of`, spread, and destructuring.

```ts
const colors = new Set(["red", "green", "blue"]);

for (const color of colors) {
  console.log(color);
}

colors.forEach((value, valueAgain, set) => {
  // value === valueAgain (Set has no keys distinct from values)
  console.log(value);
});

// Destructure first two
const [first, second] = colors;
```

Useful iterators:

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);

console.log([...s.keys()]);   // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...s.values()]); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log([...s.entries()]); // [[1,1],[2,2],[3,3]]
```

---

## Conversion to and from arrays

Deduplication is the most common reason to use `Set`.

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3];

// Array -> Set -> Array
const unique = [...new Set(arr)]; // [1, 2, 3]

// Or:
const unique2 = Array.from(new Set(arr));
```

Filtering with membership:

```ts
const allowed = new Set(["admin", "editor"]);

function canAccess(role: string): boolean {
  return allowed.has(role);
}
```

---

## TypeScript typing

### Explicit element types

```ts
const userIds = new Set<number>();
const names = new Set<string>();

// Object sets require a precise type
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

const users = new Set<User>();
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Ada" });
```

### Readonly sets

For APIs that should not mutate the set:

```ts
function printTags(tags: ReadonlySet<string>): void {
  for (const tag of tags) console.log(tag);
}

const tags = new Set(["ts", "js"]);
printTags(tags); // OK: Set<string> is assignable to ReadonlySet<string>
```

### Union and literal types

```ts
type Status = "pending" | "done" | "failed";
const finished = new Set<Status>(["done"]);
```

---

## Common use cases

### 1. Remove duplicates

```ts
function unique<T>(items: T[]): T[] {
  return [...new Set(items)];
}
```

### 2. Fast “seen” tracking

```ts
function hasDuplicateChars(s: string): boolean {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  for (const ch of s) {
    if (seen.has(ch)) return true;
    seen.add(ch);
  }
  return false;
}
```

### 3. Graph traversal / visited nodes

```ts
type Node = { id: number; neighbors: Node[] };

function dfs(start: Node): number[] {
  const visited = new Set<Node>();
  const result: number[] = [];

  function visit(node: Node) {
    if (visited.has(node)) return;
    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node.id);
    for (const n of node.neighbors) visit(n);
  }

  visit(start);
  return result;
}
```

### 4. Tag/permission sets

```ts
const permissions = new Set(["read", "write"]);

if (permissions.has("delete")) {
  // ...
}
```

### 5. Set operations (union, intersection, difference)

Modern TypeScript/JavaScript supports these directly in newer runtimes via `Set.prototype.union`, etc. For broad compatibility, implement manually:

```ts
function union<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  return new Set([...a, ...b]);
}

function intersection<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  return new Set([...a].filter(x => b.has(x)));
}

function difference<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  return new Set([...a].filter(x => !b.has(x)));
}
```

---

## Important pitfalls

### Object equality is by reference

```ts
const s = new Set<{ id: number }>();

s.add({ id: 1 });
s.add({ id: 1 });

console.log(s.size); // 2, not 1
```

If you want value-based uniqueness, use a primitive key (e.g., `id`) or a `Map`.

### `NaN` is treated as equal to itself

```ts
const s = new Set<number>();
s.add(NaN);
s.add(NaN);
console.log(s.size); // 1
```

### `-0` and `+0` are considered the same

```ts
const s = new Set<number>();
s.add(-0);
s.add(+0);
console.log(s.size); // 1
```

---

## `WeakSet`

A `WeakSet` holds objects weakly: it does not prevent garbage collection and is not iterable.

```ts
const seen = new WeakSet<object>();

function process(obj: object) {
  if (seen.has(obj)) return;
  seen.add(obj);
  // ...
}
```

Use `WeakSet` when you only need “has this object been seen?” and do not want to keep objects alive.

---

## Summary

- Use `Set<T>` for **unique values** and **fast membership checks**.
- Prefer it over arrays when duplicates are invalid or lookups are frequent.
- Convert with `[...new Set(arr)]` for deduplication.
- Remember object identity rules; use primitive keys or `Map` for value-based lookups.
- Use `WeakSet` for non-enumerable, garbage-collectable object tracking.

## Mistake: treating a `Set` like a “value-equal” collection

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

const users = new Set<User>();

// Add a user object
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });

// Later, check whether that "same" user is in the set
if (users.has({ id: 1, name: "Alice" })) {
  console.log("Alice is in the set");
} else {
  console.log("Alice is NOT in the set"); // <-- this runs
}
```

### Corrected version

**Option A — keep the original reference**

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

const alice: User = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };

const users = new Set<User>();
users.add(alice);

if (users.has(alice)) {
  console.log("Alice is in the set"); // now runs
}
```

**Option B — key by a stable primitive (often better)**

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

const users = new Map<number, User>();

users.set(1, { id: 1, name: "Alice" });

if (users.has(1)) {
  console.log("Alice is in the map");
}
```

---

## What was wrong and why

### 1. `Set` uses identity, not deep equality

A JavaScript/TypeScript `Set` decides whether two values are “the same” using **SameValueZero** equality:

- For primitives: `1 === 1`, `"a" === "a"`, `NaN === NaN` (special case: `NaN` is considered equal to itself in a `Set`).
- For objects: **reference identity**. Two different object literals are never equal, even if their properties are identical.

So in the mistaken code:

```ts
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Alice" }); // object A
users.has({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });  // object B, a brand-new reference
```

Object A and object B are different references, therefore `has` returns `false`.

This is one of the most common `Set` mistakes: assuming that “looks the same” means “is the same” in a `Set`.

### 2. Why Option A fixes it

If you store the exact same reference you later check for, identity matches:

```ts
const alice = { id: 1, name: "Alice" };
users.add(alice);
users.has(alice); // true
```

The object is not copied or normalized; the `Set` just remembers the reference.

### 3. Why Option B is often the better design

If your real question is “is there a user with id 1?”, then a `Set<User>` is the wrong tool. A `Map<number, User>` (or `Map<string, User>`) expresses that intent directly and avoids identity pitfalls entirely.

This also makes related operations simpler:

```ts
users.get(1);        // fetch by id
users.delete(1);     // remove by id
users.set(1, updatedAlice); // replace by id
```

### 4. Other common `Set` pitfalls worth remembering

- **`size`, not `length`**  
  ```ts
  const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
  s.length; // undefined
  s.size;   // 3
  ```

- **No indexing**  
  ```ts
  s[0]; // undefined
  // Use: [...s][0] or s.values().next().value
  ```

- **No array methods directly**  
  ```ts
  s.map(x => x * 2); // Error: map does not exist on Set
  // Use: [...s].map(x => x * 2)
  ```

- **`delete` returns a boolean, not the removed value**  
  ```ts
  const removed = s.delete(2); // true/false, not 2
  ```

- **`NaN` equality is special**  
  ```ts
  const s = new Set<number>();
  s.add(NaN);
  s.has(NaN); // true  (unlike ===, where NaN === NaN is false)
  ```

- **`+0` and `-0` are considered the same**  
  ```ts
  const s = new Set<number>();
  s.add(+0);
  s.add(-0);
  s.size; // 1
  ```

### Summary

The core issue in the original snippet is **reference identity vs. value equality**. `Set` does not compare object contents; it compares references. If you need value-based membership, either keep and reuse the same reference, or use a `Map` keyed by a stable primitive identifier.

# Common pitfalls when working with `Set` in TypeScript

Below are the most common traps, each with a short explanation and runnable code.

---

## 1. Object “uniqueness” is by reference, not by value

`Set` uses **SameValueZero** equality. For objects that means reference identity, so two distinct objects with identical contents are both kept.

```ts
const s = new Set<{ id: number }>();
s.add({ id: 1 });
s.add({ id: 1 });

console.log(s.size); // 2, not 1

// Fix: use a stable key, or a Map keyed by the identity you care about
const byId = new Map<number, { id: number }>();
for (const o of [{ id: 1 }, { id: 1 }]) byId.set(o.id, o);
console.log(byId.size); // 1
```

---

## 2. There is no `.map`, `.filter`, `.reduce`, or `.sort` on `Set`

You must convert to an array first. (ES2024 added `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, etc., but not higher-order helpers.)

```ts
const s = new Set([3, 1, 2]);

// ❌ s.map(...)  // does not exist
const sorted = [...s].sort((a, b) => a - b); // [1, 2, 3]
const evens  = [...s].filter((n) => n % 2 === 0); // [2]

// Note: sorting does NOT mutate the set; build a new one if needed
const sortedSet = new Set(sorted);
```

---

## 3. `forEach` passes `(value, value, set)` — value twice

This surprises people coming from arrays/Maps and can cause bugs when the callback assumes `(value, index)`.

```ts
const s = new Set(["a", "b"]);
s.forEach((v, v2, set) => {
  console.log(v, v2, set === s); // a a true
});

// If you need an index, use an array:
[...s].forEach((v, i) => console.log(i, v));
```

---

## 4. `JSON.stringify` turns a `Set` into `{}`

Sets are not JSON-serializable by default.

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
console.log(JSON.stringify(s)); // "{}"

// Fix: convert explicitly
const json = JSON.stringify([...s]); // "[1,2,3]"

// And when parsing, rebuild the set
const restored = new Set(JSON.parse(json) as number[]);
```

---

## 5. Mutating a set while iterating can skip elements

The iterator is live. Deleting the current item is usually fine, but adding items during iteration may or may not be visited, and arbitrary deletions can cause skips.

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3, 4]);

for (const x of s) {
  if (x === 2) s.delete(3); // may skip 3 or 4 depending on engine/order
  s.add(x + 10);            // newly added may or may not be visited
}
console.log([...s]);

// Safer: iterate over a snapshot
for (const x of [...s]) {
  if (x % 2 === 0) s.delete(x);
}
```

---

## 6. Return values differ: `add` chains, `delete` is boolean, `clear` is void

```ts
const s = new Set<number>();

s.add(1).add(2);      // chainable, returns the Set
console.log(s.delete(1)); // true (boolean, NOT the set)
console.log(s.delete(99)); // false

// ❌ s.clear().add(3)  // clear() returns void
s.clear();
s.add(3);
```

---

## 7. `NaN`, `+0`, and `-0` equality rules

`Set` treats `NaN` as equal to `NaN` (unlike `===`), and `+0`/`-0` as equal. This is usually desirable but can surprise.

```ts
const s = new Set<number>();
s.add(NaN);
s.add(NaN);
s.add(0);
s.add(-0);

console.log(s.size); // 2  (NaN once, 0/-0 once)
console.log(s.has(NaN)); // true
```

---

## 8. Insertion order is preserved, and re-adding moves an item to the end

A `Set` is not a sorted structure. Deleting and re-adding changes order.

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
s.delete(2);
s.add(2);
console.log([...s]); // [1, 3, 2] — not [1, 2, 3]

// If you need sorted output, sort explicitly (see pitfall 2).
```

---

## 9. Type invariance: `Set<Derived>` is not assignable to `Set<Base>`

Because `Set` is mutable, TypeScript treats it as invariant.

```ts
class Animal {}
class Dog extends Animal {}

const dogs = new Set<Dog>();

// ❌ Type 'Set<Dog>' is not assignable to type 'Set<Animal>'
const animals: Set<Animal> = dogs;

// Fix: widen at the boundary, or use ReadonlySet (TS 4.9+) when available
const ro: ReadonlySet<Animal> = dogs; // OK if types align; often use ReadonlySet<Animal>
```

---

## 10. `new Set()` with no arguments has a weak/inferred type

Depending on version, `new Set()` may infer `Set<any>`/`Set<unknown>` or `Set<never>`, leading to either lost type safety or “argument of type X is not assignable to never”.

```ts
// Ambiguous:
const s1 = new Set();          // often Set<any> or Set<unknown>
s1.add(1);                     // may be allowed with `any`, unsafe

// Better: always provide the type parameter
const s2 = new Set<number>();
s2.add(1);
// s2.add("x"); // ❌ compile error
```

---

## 11. Membership checks use reference identity — `new` objects never match

```ts
const s = new Set([{ id: 1 }]);

console.log(s.has({ id: 1 })); // false — different object

// Fix: keep the reference, or key by an id
const obj = { id: 1 };
const s2 = new Set([obj]);
console.log(s2.has(obj)); // true
```

---

## 12. `in` operator does not work on sets

`"x" in set` checks property names, not membership.

```ts
const s = new Set(["x"]);
console.log("x" in s);   // false (usually)
console.log(s.has("x")); // true
```

---

## 13. `WeakSet` is very limited compared to `Set`

`WeakSet` only holds objects, is not iterable, has no `size`, and cannot be cleared explicitly.

```ts
const ws = new WeakSet<object>();
const o = { a: 1 };
ws.add(o);

console.log(ws.has(o)); // true
// ws.size;      // ❌ does not exist
// for (const x of ws) {} // ❌ not iterable
// ws.clear();   // ❌ does not exist (removed/never added)
```

Use `WeakSet` only for attaching “marks” to objects you don’t want to keep alive.

---

## 14. ES2024 set methods may not exist in your target

Methods like `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, `symmetricDifference`, `isSubsetOf`, `isSupersetOf`, `isDisjointFrom` are relatively new. Calling them on older lib/targets fails at runtime/compile time.

```ts
const a = new Set([1, 2]);
const b = new Set([2, 3]);

// Modern (ES2024+): a.union(b), a.intersection(b), a.difference(b)
// Portable fallback:
const union = new Set([...a, ...b]);                 // {1,2,3}
const inter = new Set([...a].filter((x) => b.has(x))); // {2}
const diff  = new Set([...a].filter((x) => !b.has(x))); // {1}
```

---

## 15. Spreading a `Set` into a function loses the “set-ness”

Rest parameters and spreads work, but the result is an array; and a `Set` is not a valid single argument where an array is expected unless converted.

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);

Math.max(...s); // OK, spreads to numbers

// ❌ someFnExpectingArray(s)  // type error
someFnExpectingArray([...s]);    // ✅
```

---

### Quick checklist

- Treat objects in sets as **references**; key by an id for value equality.
- Convert with `[...set]` / `Array.from(set)` before array methods.
- Remember `forEach`’s double value and live iteration.
- Provide explicit type args: `new Set<T>()`.
- Don’t rely on ES2024 set methods without checking `target`/`lib`.
- Serialize sets manually; `JSON.stringify` gives `{}`.

If you’d like, I can also show a small utility module (e.g., `toSortedSet`, `setEquals`, `valueSet`) that papers over several of these.

## Exercise

You have two blog posts with tag lists (possibly containing duplicates).  
Using TypeScript `Set`, do the following:

1. **Deduplicate** the tags of each post.
2. Find tags **common to both** posts.
3. Find tags that appear **only in the first** post.
4. Find tags that appear **only in the second** post.
5. Find **all unique tags** across both posts.

Input:

```ts
const post1Tags = ["typescript", "javascript", "web", "typescript", "node"];
const post2Tags = ["react", "typescript", "web", "css", "react"];
```

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Deduplicate each post with `new Set(...)`

A `Set` stores unique values. Passing an array removes duplicates automatically.

```ts
const set1 = new Set(post1Tags);
const set2 = new Set(post2Tags);

// set1: Set { "typescript", "javascript", "web", "node" }
// set2: Set { "react", "typescript", "web", "css" }
```

### Step 2: Common tags (intersection)

Iterate over the smaller set and keep values that exist in the other set.

```ts
function intersection<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  const result = new Set<T>();
  for (const value of a) {
    if (b.has(value)) result.add(value);
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Step 3: Tags only in the first post (difference `set1 - set2`)

```ts
function difference<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  const result = new Set<T>();
  for (const value of a) {
    if (!b.has(value)) result.add(value);
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Step 4: Tags only in the second post (difference `set2 - set1`)

Reuse the same `difference` helper with arguments swapped.

### Step 5: All unique tags across both (union)

```ts
function union<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  return new Set<T>([...a, ...b]);
}
```

### Convert back to arrays when needed

Sets are iterable, so `[...set]` or `Array.from(set)` gives a plain array.

---

## Final solution

```ts
// ---------- helpers ----------
function toSet<T>(values: readonly T[]): Set<T> {
  return new Set(values);
}

function intersection<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  const out = new Set<T>();
  for (const v of a) if (b.has(v)) out.add(v);
  return out;
}

function difference<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  const out = new Set<T>();
  for (const v of a) if (!b.has(v)) out.add(v);
  return out;
}

function union<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): Set<T> {
  return new Set<T>([...a, ...b]);
}

// ---------- data ----------
const post1Tags = ["typescript", "javascript", "web", "typescript", "node"];
const post2Tags = ["react", "typescript", "web", "css", "react"];

// ---------- steps ----------
const set1 = toSet(post1Tags);
const set2 = toSet(post2Tags);

const common = intersection(set1, set2);          // Set { "typescript", "web" }
const onlyPost1 = difference(set1, set2);        // Set { "javascript", "node" }
const onlyPost2 = difference(set2, set1);        // Set { "react", "css" }
const allUnique = union(set1, set2);             // Set { "typescript", "javascript", "web", "node", "react", "css" }

// ---------- output ----------
console.log("Deduped post1:", [...set1]);
console.log("Deduped post2:", [...set2]);
console.log("Common:", [...common]);
console.log("Only post1:", [...onlyPost1]);
console.log("Only post2:", [...onlyPost2]);
console.log("All unique:", [...allUnique]);
console.log("Total unique count:", allUnique.size);
```

### Key takeaways

- `new Set(array)` removes duplicates in **O(n)**.
- `Set.prototype.has` is **O(1)** on average, making intersection/difference efficient.
- Spread (`[...set]`) converts a `Set` back to an array.
- Generic helpers (`<T>`) keep the code reusable for numbers, objects by reference, etc.

If you want, I can extend this with a `Set`-based permission checker (e.g., allowed users = active users minus banned users) as a follow-up exercise.

## Sets in TypeScript

A `Set<T>` is a collection of **unique values** of type `T`. In TypeScript/JavaScript:

- **Uniqueness** is based on **SameValueZero** equality:
  - `NaN === NaN` is considered equal inside a `Set`
  - `+0` and `-0` are considered equal
- **Insertion order** is preserved during iteration.
- It can hold any value: primitives, objects, functions, etc.
- Object equality is **reference equality**, not deep equality.

Common built-in operations:

```ts
const set = new Set<number>();

set.add(1);          // Set { 1 }
set.add(1);          // still Set { 1 }
set.has(1);          // true
set.delete(1);       // true
set.size;            // 0
set.clear();
```

Time complexity for the built-in `Set` is roughly:

| Operation | Average | Worst-ish notes |
|---|---:|---|
| `add(value)` | O(1) | hash table |
| `has(value)` | O(1) | hash table |
| `delete(value)` | O(1) | hash table |
| iteration | O(n) | preserves insertion order |

Use a `Set` when you need deduplication, fast membership checks, or a collection where each item appears at most once. Use `Map` when you need key/value pairs. Use `WeakSet` when you want object-only membership without preventing garbage collection.

---

## Core implementation

Below is a clean, educational implementation of a `Set`-like class. It uses a `Map` internally as the hash table. In a real engine, `Set` is implemented with a hash table too; using `Map` keeps the focus on **Set semantics** rather than reimplementing hashing.

```ts
/**
 * A minimal but complete Set-like collection.
 *
 * Design notes:
 * - Internally backed by Map<T, true>.
 * - Map uses SameValueZero equality, matching built-in Set behavior.
 * - Most operations delegate to Map, giving average O(1) add/has/delete.
 */
class MySet<T> implements Iterable<T> {
  // The boolean value is only a placeholder; keys are the actual set members.
  private readonly store = new Map<T, true>();

  /**
   * Number of unique values currently stored.
   */
  get size(): number {
    return this.store.size;
  }

  /**
   * Adds a value if it is not already present.
   * Returns `this` to allow chaining, like the built-in Set.
   */
  add(value: T): this {
    this.store.set(value, true);
    return this;
  }

  /**
   * Returns true if the value exists in the set.
   */
  has(value: T): boolean {
    return this.store.has(value);
  }

  /**
   * Removes a value.
   * Returns true if it existed and was removed, false otherwise.
   */
  delete(value: T): boolean {
    return this.store.delete(value);
  }

  /**
   * Removes all values.
   */
  clear(): void {
    this.store.clear();
  }

  /**
   * Calls callback once for each value, in insertion order.
   *
   * Signature mirrors built-in Set.forEach:
   * (value, valueAgain, set) => void
   */
  forEach(
    callback: (value: T, valueAgain: T, set: this) => void,
    thisArg?: unknown
  ): void {
    for (const value of this.store.keys()) {
      callback.call(thisArg, value, value, this);
    }
  }

  /**
   * Returns an iterator over values in insertion order.
   */
  *values(): IterableIterator<T> {
    yield* this.store.keys();
  }

  /**
   * Alias for values(), matching built-in Set.
   */
  keys(): IterableIterator<T> {
    return this.values();
  }

  /**
   * Returns [value, value] pairs, matching built-in Set.entries().
   */
  *entries(): IterableIterator<[T, T]> {
    for (const value of this.store.keys()) {
      yield [value, value];
    }
  }

  /**
   * Makes the set iterable with for...of.
   */
  [Symbol.iterator](): IterableIterator<T> {
    return this.values();
  }

  /**
   * Useful debugging representation.
   */
  toString(): string {
    return `MySet(${this.size}) { ${[...this].join(", ")} }`;
  }
}

export { MySet };
```

---

## Usage example

```ts
const numbers = new MySet<number>();

numbers.add(1);
numbers.add(2);
numbers.add(1); // ignored

console.log(numbers.has(2)); // true
console.log(numbers.size);   // 2

for (const value of numbers) {
  console.log(value); // 1, then 2
}

numbers.forEach((value) => {
  console.log("forEach:", value);
});

numbers.delete(1);
console.log(numbers.toString()); // MySet(1) { 2 }
```

---

## Interview notes

If asked to implement a Set **without** using `Map`, the simplest educational version is array-backed:

```ts
class ArraySet<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  has(value: T): boolean {
    return this.items.includes(value);
  }

  add(value: T): this {
    if (!this.has(value)) {
      this.items.push(value);
    }
    return this;
  }

  delete(value: T): boolean {
    const index = this.items.indexOf(value);
    if (index === -1) return false;
    this.items.splice(index, 1);
    return true;
  }

  get size(): number {
    return this.items.length;
  }

  [Symbol.iterator](): IterableIterator<T> {
    return this.items[Symbol.iterator]();
  }
}
```

That version is easy to understand but has **O(n)** `has`, `add`, and `delete`. The `Map`-backed version above is the better practical core implementation because it gives average **O(1)** membership operations while preserving insertion order.

# Edge Cases When Using `Set` in TypeScript

Below are the most important edge cases, grouped by category, with runnable TypeScript examples.

---

## 1. Type Inference of Empty Sets

An empty `Set` literal often infers to `Set<any>` (or `Set<unknown>` depending on config), which defeats strict typing.

```ts
// ❌ Inferred as Set<any> — loses type safety
const s1 = new Set();
s1.add(42);        // no error
s1.add("hello");   // no error

// ✅ Annotate explicitly
const s2 = new Set<string>();
// s2.add(42);     // Error: Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to 'string'

// ✅ Or seed with a typed array
const s3 = new Set<string>([]);
```

**Tip:** With `noImplicitAny`, `new Set()` may still infer `any`; always annotate or seed.

---

## 2. Equality Semantics: `SameValueZero`

`Set` uses `SameValueZero` equality, **not** `===` and **not** deep equality.

```ts
const s = new Set<number>();

// NaN equals NaN inside a Set (unlike ===)
s.add(NaN);
s.add(NaN);
console.log(s.size); // 1

// +0 and -0 are considered the same
s.add(0);
s.add(-0);
console.log(s.size); // 2 (NaN + one zero)

// Objects compared by reference
const a = { id: 1 };
const b = { id: 1 };
const o = new Set<object>();
o.add(a);
o.add(b);
console.log(o.size); // 2 — distinct references
```

**Edge:** Two structurally identical objects are NOT deduplicated.

---

## 3. No Deep Equality — Value Objects Need Canonical Keys

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

const users = new Set<User>();
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });
users.add({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });
console.log(users.size); // 2 — not deduplicated!

// ✅ Use a Map keyed by id, or store canonical keys
const byId = new Map<number, User>();
byId.set(1, { id: 1, name: "Alice" });

// ✅ Or normalize to a primitive key set
const ids = new Set<number>();
ids.add(1);
ids.add(1);
console.log(ids.size); // 1
```

---

## 4. `has()` Does Not Narrow Types (Usually)

Even when an element's type is a union, `Set.has` generally returns `boolean`, not a type predicate. (Some newer TS versions add narrowing for certain collections, but don't rely on it.)

```ts
type Status = "pending" | "done" | "failed";
const allowed = new Set<Status>(["pending", "done"]);

function check(x: string): x is Status {
  return allowed.has(x as Status); // manual guard
}

let v: string = "done";
if (allowed.has(v as Status)) {
  // v is still `string` here in most TS versions
  const narrowed: Status = v as Status;
}
```

**Pattern:** Write a small type guard instead of trusting `has()`.

---

## 5. Mutating a Set While Iterating

### a) `forEach` skips newly added elements in some engines? Actually spec: elements added during iteration may be visited.

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);

s.forEach((v) => {
  if (v === 2) s.add(4); // 4 may or may not be visited depending on position
  console.log(v);
});
// Typically prints 1,2,3,4 (4 visited because added before iterator reaches end)
```

### b) Deleting during `for...of` is safe and well-defined

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3, 4]);
for (const v of s) {
  if (v % 2 === 0) s.delete(v); // safe
}
console.log([...s]); // [1, 3]
```

### c) Safest: snapshot first

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
for (const v of [...s]) {
  s.delete(v);
  s.add(v * 10);
}
console.log([...s]); // [10, 20, 30]
```

**Rule of thumb:** If logic is non-trivial, iterate over `[...set]`.

---

## 6. Insertion Order & Re-insertion Moves to End

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
s.delete(2);
s.add(2);
console.log([...s]); // [1, 3, 2] — 2 moved to end
```

This matters for pagination, LRU caches, and deterministic output.

---

## 7. Constructor Pitfalls

```ts
// ❌ null/undefined throw TypeError
try { new Set(null as any); } catch (e) { console.log("throws"); }

// ❌ Non-iterable throws
try { new Set(42 as any); } catch (e) { console.log("throws"); }

// ✅ Dedup on construction
const s = new Set([1, 1, 2, 2, 3]);
console.log(s.size); // 3
```

---

## 8. Serialization: `JSON.stringify` Loses Set Data

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
console.log(JSON.stringify(s)); // "{}"  — Set is not enumerable like arrays

// ✅ Convert first
const arr = [...s];
console.log(JSON.stringify(arr)); // "[1,2,3]"

// ✅ Reviver on parse
const parsed = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(arr), (_k, v) =>
  Array.isArray(v) && v.every((x) => typeof x === "number") ? new Set(v) : v
);
```

**Edge:** Nested Sets inside objects also serialize as `{}`.

---

## 9. Spread / `Array.from` Downlevel Target

```ts
// Requires "downlevelIteration": true or target >= ES2015 in tsconfig
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const arr1 = [...s];            // needs iterable support
const arr2 = Array.from(s);     // works broadly
const arr3 = Array.from(s, (x) => x * 2); // map during conversion
```

If you target ES5 without downlevel iteration, `[...s]` is a compile error.

---

## 10. `WeakSet` vs `Set` for Objects

Use `WeakSet` when you only need object membership and want GC to reclaim objects.

```ts
class Token { constructor(public id: number) {} }

const strong = new Set<Token>();
const weak = new WeakSet<Token>();

let t = new Token(1);
strong.add(t);
weak.add(t);

t = null as any; // object still retained by `strong` (memory leak risk)
// `weak` allows GC; but you can't iterate, size, or clear a WeakSet

// WeakSet API is minimal:
// weak.has(obj), weak.add(obj), weak.delete(obj)
```

**Edge:** `WeakSet` only accepts objects; primitives throw.

```ts
const w = new WeakSet<any>();
try { w.add(42 as any); } catch (e) { console.log("throws: primitive not allowed"); }
```

---

## 11. Readonly Sets & Immutability

```ts
type Config = { allowed: ReadonlySet<string> };

const cfg: Config = { allowed: new Set(["a", "b"]) };

// cfg.allowed.add("c"); // Error: Property 'add' does not exist on ReadonlySet

// Defensive copy when exposing
function getTags(): ReadonlySet<string> {
  return new Set(internalTags); // copy, not leak
}
```

**Edge:** `ReadonlySet<T>` still holds the same underlying references; immutability is shallow.

---

## 12. Comparing Two Sets (No Built-in Equality)

```ts
function setsEqual<T>(a: Set<T>, b: Set<T>): boolean {
  if (a.size !== b.size) return false;
  for (const v of a) if (!b.has(v)) return false;
  return true;
}

const x = new Set([1, 2]);
const y = new Set([2, 1]);
console.log(setsEqual(x, y)); // true (order-independent)
```

**Edge:** For object sets, `has` uses reference equality, so equality is reference-based.

---

## 13. Subset / Superset Checks

```ts
function isSubset<T>(sub: Set<T>, sup: Set<T>): boolean {
  if (sub.size > sup.size) return false;
  for (const v of sub) if (!sup.has(v)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

---

## 14. Union / Intersection / Difference (Manual)

```ts
const a = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const b = new Set([2, 3, 4]);

const union = new Set([...a, ...b]);                 // {1,2,3,4}
const intersection = new Set([...a].filter((x) => b.has(x))); // {2,3}
const difference = new Set([...a].filter((x) => !b.has(x)));  // {1}
```

**Edge:** For large sets, prefer iterating the smaller set for intersection/difference.

---

## 15. `delete()` Return Value & Idempotency

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2]);
console.log(s.delete(1)); // true
console.log(s.delete(1)); // false (already removed)
console.log(s.delete(999)); // false
s.clear();
console.log(s.size); // 0
```

Use the boolean to avoid redundant work or to detect presence.

---

## 16. Type Narrowing with Discriminated Unions

```ts
type Event = { type: "click"; x: number } | { type: "key"; key: string };

const handled = new Set<Event["type"]>(["click"]);

function dispatch(e: Event) {
  if (handled.has(e.type)) {
    // e is still the full union; narrow manually
    if (e.type === "click") console.log(e.x);
  }
}
```

---

## 17. Using a `Set` as a `Map` Key (Identity Semantics)

```ts
const cache = new Map<Set<string>, number>();
const key = new Set(["a", "b"]);
cache.set(key, 42);
console.log(cache.get(new Set(["a", "b"]))); // undefined — different reference
console.log(cache.get(key)); // 42
```

**Edge:** Two structurally equal sets are different Map keys.

---

## 18. Async Population

`Set` has no async constructor; build values first.

```ts
async function loadIds(): Promise<Set<number>> {
  const res = await fetch("/ids");
  const data: number[] = await res.json();
  return new Set(data);
}

// Or dedupe async results
const ids = new Set(await Promise.all(tasks.map((t) => t())));
```

---

## 19. Subclassing `Set`

```ts
class TagSet extends Set<string> {
  addTag(tag: string) {
    const normalized = tag.trim().toLowerCase();
    if (normalized) super.add(normalized);
    return this;
  }
}

const t = new TagSet();
t.addTag("  Foo  ");
console.log([...t]); // ["foo"]
```

**Edge:** Subclasses must call `super()`; methods that create new sets (like a hypothetical `union`) may return base `Set`, not subclass, in native implementations.

---

## 20. Memory Leaks with Long-Lived Sets

```ts
const seen = new Set<object>();
function process(obj: object) {
  if (seen.has(obj)) return;
  seen.add(obj);
  // ... if `obj` is short-lived but `seen` lives forever, leak
}
```

**Mitigations:** use `WeakSet` when only membership is needed, or evict with a bounded cache (e.g., LRU).

---

## 21. `size` Is a Getter, Not a Method

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
console.log(s.size);      // 3  (property)
// console.log(s.size()); // Error: not a function
```

---

## 22. Iteration Protocols: `keys`, `values`, `entries`

```ts
const s = new Set(["a", "b"]);
for (const [k, v] of s.entries()) {
  // k === v for Sets
}
// keys() and values() are identical for Set
console.log([...s.keys()]);   // ["a","b"]
console.log([...s.values()]); // ["a","b"]
```

**Edge:** `entries()` yields `[value, value]` pairs, mirroring `Map`.

---

## 23. `instanceof` vs Cross-Realm Sets

```ts
// In iframes/workers, `instanceof Set` can be false for foreign Sets
function isSet(x: unknown): x is Set<unknown> {
  return Object.prototype.toString.call(x) === "[object Set]";
}
```

---

## 24. Filtering/Mapping Produces Arrays, Not Sets

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3, 4]);
// There is no Set.prototype.map/filter in standard JS
const evens = new Set([...s].filter((x) => x % 2 === 0));
const doubled = new Set([...s].map((x) => x * 2));
```

---

## 25. Exhaustiveness & `Set` of Literal Unions

```ts
type Role = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";
const allRoles = new Set<Role>(["admin", "editor", "viewer"]);

function assertExhaustive(r: Role) {
  if (!allRoles.has(r)) {
    const _exhaustive: never = r; // compile-time safety net
    throw new Error("Unknown role");
  }
}
```

---

## 26. Performance: Choosing `Set` vs `Array`

```ts
// Membership test
const arr = Array.from({ length: 100_000 }, (_, i) => i);
const set = new Set(arr);

console.time("array");
arr.includes(99_999); // O(n)
console.timeEnd("array");

console.time("set");
set.has(99_999);     // O(1)
console.timeEnd("set");
```

**Edge:** For tiny collections (< ~10 items), arrays can be faster due to cache; measure.

---

## 27. Deduping Arrays of Objects by a Key

```ts
const items = [
  { id: 1, v: "a" },
  { id: 1, v: "b" },
  { id: 2, v: "c" },
];

const seen = new Set<number>();
const unique = items.filter((it) => {
  if (seen.has(it.id)) return false;
  seen.add(it.id);
  return true;
});
// unique: [{id:1,v:"a"}, {id:2,v:"c"}]
```

---

## 28. Converting `Set` to `Map` or Object

```ts
const s = new Set(["a", "b"]);
const m = new Map([...s].map((v) => [v, true]));
const obj = Object.fromEntries([...s].map((v) => [v, true]));
```

---

## 29. Strict Mode & `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`

Not directly applicable to `Set` (no indexing), but converting to arrays:

```ts
const s = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const arr = [...s];
// arr[0] is `number` (not `number | undefined`) unless noUncheckedIndexedAccess
```

---

## 30. `Set` of `undefined` / `null`

```ts
const s = new Set([undefined, null, 0, ""]);
console.log(s.size); // 4 — all distinct under SameValueZero
s.add(undefined);
console.log(s.size); // 4 — deduped
```

---

## Summary Checklist

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| Empty set typing | Annotate `new Set<T>()` |
| Object equality | Use primitive keys or `Map` |
| Mutation while iterating | Snapshot with `[...set]` |
| Serialization | Convert to array first |
| Long-lived collections | Prefer `WeakSet` for objects |
| Comparing sets | Implement manually (size + has) |
| Deep equality | Not supported; normalize keys |
| Type narrowing via `has` | Write explicit type guards |
| Cross-realm checks | Use `toString` tag check |

These patterns cover the vast majority of real-world `Set` pitfalls in TypeScript.

Below is a self-contained TypeScript implementation of a small reactive system (“signals”) plus a timer class whose state is exposed as signals. It includes:

- `Signal<T>`: mutable reactive value
- `Computed<T>`: lazily recomputed derived value
- `Effect`: side effect that re-runs when dependencies change
- `batch()`: batches multiple updates
- `Timer`: `setTimeout`/`setInterval`-style timer with pause/resume/stop/reset and signals for status, remaining time, and tick count

Save as `signals-and-timers.ts` and run with:

```bash
npx ts-node signals-and-timers.ts
```

or:

```bash
npx tsc signals-and-timers.ts --target es2020 --module commonjs && node signals-and-timers.js
```

```ts
// signals-and-timers.ts

type TimerStatus = "idle" | "running" | "paused";

type ReactiveSource = { subscribers: Set<Subscriber> };

interface Subscriber {
  notify(): void;
  dependencies: Set<ReactiveSource>;
}

let activeSubscriber: Subscriber | null = null;

let batchDepth = 0;
const pendingSubscribers = new Set<Subscriber>();

function track(source: ReactiveSource, subscriber: Subscriber): void {
  source.subscribers.add(subscriber);
  subscriber.dependencies.add(source);
}

function batch(fn: () => void): void {
  batchDepth++;
  try {
    fn();
  } finally {
    batchDepth--;
    if (batchDepth === 0) flushPending();
  }
}

function flushPending(): void {
  while (pendingSubscribers.size > 0) {
    const current = [...pendingSubscribers];
    pendingSubscribers.clear();
    for (const sub of current) sub.notify();
  }
}

class Signal<T> implements ReactiveSource {
  subscribers = new Set<Subscriber>();
  private _value: T;

  constructor(
    initial: T,
    private equals: (a: T, b: T) => boolean = Object.is
  ) {
    this._value = initial;
  }

  get value(): T {
    if (activeSubscriber) track(this, activeSubscriber);
    return this._value;
  }

  set value(next: T) {
    if (this.equals(this._value, next)) return;
    this._value = next;

    if (batchDepth > 0) {
      for (const sub of this.subscribers) pendingSubscribers.add(sub);
    } else {
      for (const sub of [...this.subscribers]) sub.notify();
    }
  }

  update(fn: (previous: T) => T): void {
    this.value = fn(this._value);
  }
}

class Computed<T> implements ReactiveSource, Subscriber {
  subscribers = new Set<Subscriber>();
  dependencies = new Set<ReactiveSource>();
  private _value!: T;
  private dirty = true;

  constructor(private fn: () => T) {}

  get value(): T {
    if (this.dirty) this.evaluate();
    if (activeSubscriber) track(this, activeSubscriber);
    return this._value;
  }

  private evaluate(): void {
    // Remove stale dependencies before re-evaluating.
    for (const dep of this.dependencies) dep.subscribers.delete(this);
    this.dependencies.clear();

    const previous = activeSubscriber;
    activeSubscriber = this;

    try {
      this._value = this.fn();
      this.dirty = false;
    } finally {
      activeSubscriber = previous;
    }
  }

  notify(): void {
    if (this.dirty) return;
    this.dirty = true;

    if (batchDepth > 0) {
      for (const sub of this.subscribers) pendingSubscribers.add(sub);
    } else {
      for (const sub of [...this.subscribers]) sub.notify();
    }
  }
}

class Effect implements Subscriber {
  dependencies = new Set<ReactiveSource>();
  private stopped = false;
  private cleanup?: () => void;

  constructor(private fn: () => void | (() => void)) {
    this.run();
  }

  run(): void {
    if (this.stopped) return;

    if (this.cleanup) {
      this.cleanup();
      this.cleanup = undefined;
    }

    for (const dep of this.dependencies) dep.subscribers.delete(this);
    this.dependencies.clear();

    const previous = activeSubscriber;
    activeSubscriber = this;

    try {
      const result = this.fn();
      if (typeof result === "function") {
        this.cleanup = result as () => void;
      }
    } finally {
      activeSubscriber = previous;
    }
  }

  notify(): void {
    if (this.stopped) return;

    if (batchDepth > 0) {
      pendingSubscribers.add(this);
    } else {
      this.run();
    }
  }

  stop(): void {
    this.stopped = true;
    if (this.cleanup) this.cleanup();
    for (const dep of this.dependencies) dep.subscribers.delete(this);
    this.dependencies.clear();
  }
}

class Timer {
  readonly delayMs: number;
  readonly repeat: boolean;
  readonly onTick?: (timer: Timer) => void;

  status: Signal<TimerStatus>;
  remainingMs: Signal<number>;
  ticks: Signal<number>;

  private handle?: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;
  private startedAt = 0;

  constructor(options: {
    delayMs: number;
    repeat?: boolean;
    onTick?: (timer: Timer) => void;
  }) {
    this.delayMs = options.delayMs;
    this.repeat = options.repeat ?? false;
    this.onTick = options.onTick;

    this.status = new Signal<TimerStatus>("idle");
    this.remainingMs = new Signal<number>(this.delayMs);
    this.ticks = new Signal<number>(0);
  }

  get isRunning(): boolean {
    return this.status.value === "running";
  }

  start(): void {
    if (this.status.value === "running") return;

    if (this.status.value === "idle") {
      this.remainingMs.value = this.delayMs;
    }

    this.status.value = "running";
    this.schedule(this.remainingMs.value);
  }

  pause(): void {
    if (this.status.value !== "running" || !this.handle) return;

    clearTimeout(this.handle);
    this.handle = undefined;

    const elapsed = Date.now() - this.startedAt;
    this.remainingMs.value = Math.max(0, this.remainingMs.value - elapsed);
    this.status.value = "paused";
  }

  resume(): void {
    if (this.status.value === "paused") this.start();
  }

  stop(): void {
    if (this.handle) {
      clearTimeout(this.handle);
      this.handle = undefined;
    }
    this.status.value = "idle";
    this.remainingMs.value = this.delayMs;
  }

  reset(): void {
    this.stop();
    this.ticks.value = 0;
  }

  private schedule(ms: number): void {
    this.startedAt = Date.now();
    this.handle = setTimeout(() => {
      this.handle = undefined;

      if (this.status.value !== "running") return;

      this.ticks.value = this.ticks.value + 1;
      this.onTick?.(this);

      if (this.repeat && this.status.value === "running") {
        this.remainingMs.value = this.delayMs;
        this.schedule(this.delayMs);
      } else {
        this.status.value = "idle";
        this.remainingMs.value = this.delayMs;
      }
    }, ms);
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Runnable example
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

const count = new Signal(0);
const doubled = new Computed(() => count.value * 2);

// Logs whenever count or doubled changes.
const valueLogger = new Effect(() => {
  console.log(`[signal] count=${count.value}, doubled=${doubled.value}`);
});

const timer = new Timer({
  delayMs: 500,
  repeat: true,
  onTick: (t) => {
    count.update((n) => n + 1);
    console.log(`[timer] tick ${t.ticks.value}`);
  },
});

// Logs timer state changes.
const timerLogger = new Effect(() => {
  console.log(
    `[timer-state] status=${timer.status.value}, remaining=${timer.remainingMs.value}ms, ticks=${timer.ticks.value}`
  );
});

console.log("Starting repeating 500ms timer...");
timer.start();

// Stop after roughly 3 ticks.
setTimeout(() => {
  timer.stop();
  console.log("Timer stopped.");
  console.log("Final count:", count.value);
  console.log("Final ticks:", timer.ticks.value);

  valueLogger.stop();
  timerLogger.stop();
}, 1700);
```

### How it works

- Reading a signal or computed inside an `Effect` or `Computed` registers it as a dependency.
- When a signal changes, it notifies all subscribers.
- `Computed` is lazy: it only recomputes when read while dirty.
- `Effect` re-runs immediately when any dependency changes.
- `batch()` queues subscriber notifications until the batch finishes, avoiding redundant intermediate work.
- `Timer` exposes reactive state through signals, so effects can react to status, remaining time, and tick count. Internally it uses recursive `setTimeout`, which makes pause/resume straightforward.

# Signals and Timers in TypeScript

“Signals” and “timers” mean different things depending on context. In TypeScript/JavaScript there is **no single built-in “signal” primitive**; the word is used for (1) reactive state signals in frameworks, (2) cancellation signals (`AbortSignal`), and (3) OS process signals (Node). Timers are the standard `setTimeout`/`setInterval` family. Below is a practical tour.

---

## 1. Reactive Signals (Angular / Solid / Preact)

### Idea
A **signal** is a mutable value container that notifies subscribers when it changes. Unlike plain variables, reads are tracked automatically, so derived values and side effects re-run only when needed (fine-grained reactivity).

### Syntax (three common flavors)

**Angular 16+**
```ts
import { signal, computed, effect } from '@angular/core';

const count = signal(0);          // WritableSignal<number>
count.set(1);
count.update(v => v + 1);

const doubled = computed(() => count() * 2); // Signal<number>, read-only derived
effect(() => console.log('count =', count())); // side effect, auto-tracked
```

**SolidJS**
```ts
import { createSignal, createEffect } from 'solid-js';

const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);
setCount(1);
setCount(v => v + 1);
createEffect(() => console.log(count()));
```

**Preact Signals**
```ts
import { signal, computed, effect } from '@preact/signals';

const count = signal(0);
count.value = 1;                 // mutate via .value
const doubled = computed(() => count.value * 2);
effect(() => console.log(count.value));
```

### Typical use cases
- Component/UI state that many dependents read.
- Derived state without manual memoization.
- Replacing RxJS for simple synchronous reactive state.
- Form fields, toggles, counters, selected tab.

---

## 2. `AbortSignal` (standard Web API “signal”)

### Idea
An `AbortSignal` is a cancellation token. You pass it to `fetch`, streams, etc.; calling `abort()` cancels the operation. This is the most common “signal” in plain TypeScript browser/Node code.

```ts
const controller = new AbortController();

async function load(url: string) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  return res.json();
}

// Cancel after 5s
const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);

try {
  await load('/api/data');
  clearTimeout(timer);
} catch (e) {
  if ((e as Error).name === 'AbortError') console.log('cancelled');
}
```

You can also listen:
```ts
controller.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => console.log('aborted'));
```

### Use cases
- Cancelling fetches on unmount / route change.
- Timeouts for async work.
- Coordinating cancellation across multiple operations.

---

## 3. Node.js Process Signals (OS signals)

```ts
process.on('SIGINT', () => {
  console.log('Ctrl+C received');
  process.exit(0);
});

process.on('SIGTERM', () => {
  // graceful shutdown
});
```

Use cases: graceful shutdown, reloading config, cleanup. Mostly server-side.

---

## 4. Timers

### Core APIs
```ts
const t1: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> = setTimeout(() => {}, 1000);
const t2: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> = setInterval(() => {}, 1000);

clearTimeout(t1);
clearInterval(t2);
```

**Typing note:** In browsers `setTimeout` returns `number`; in Node it returns `Timeout`. `ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>` works in both. Prefer it over `NodeJS.Timeout`/`number` for portability.

### Other timers
```ts
// Animation loop (browser)
let rafId = requestAnimationFrame(function tick(now) {
  // now = DOMHighResTimeStamp
  rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
});
cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);

// High-res timing
const start = performance.now();
// ... work ...
console.log(performance.now() - start);
```

### Common patterns

**Debounce**
```ts
function debounce<T extends (...a: any[]) => void>(fn: T, ms: number) {
  let id: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  return (...args: Parameters<T>) => {
    if (id) clearTimeout(id);
    id = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
}
```

**Throttle**
```ts
function throttle<T extends (...a: any[]) => void>(fn: T, ms: number) {
  let last = 0;
  return (...args: Parameters<T>) => {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= ms) { last = now; fn(...args); }
  };
}
```

---

## 5. Combining Signals + Timers

### Countdown with a reactive signal (Angular-style)
```ts
import { signal, effect } from '@angular/core';

const remaining = signal(10);
const id = setInterval(() => {
  remaining.update(v => (v > 0 ? v - 1 : 0));
  if (remaining() === 0) clearInterval(id);
}, 1000);

effect(() => {
  if (remaining() === 0) console.log('done!');
});
```

### Auto-polling with cleanup + AbortSignal
```ts
function startPolling(url: string, intervalMs: number) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const { signal } = controller;

  const tick = async () => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
      const data = await res.json();
      console.log(data);
    } catch (e) {
      if ((e as Error).name !== 'AbortError') console.error(e);
    }
  };

  tick();
  const id = setInterval(tick, intervalMs);
  return () => { controller.abort(); clearInterval(id); }; // cleanup
}

const stop = startPolling('/api/status', 3000);
// later: stop();
```

### Debounced signal update (Solid-style)
```ts
import { createSignal } from 'solid-js';

const [query, setQuery] = createSignal('');
let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;

function onInput(value: string) {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(() => setQuery(value), 300);
}
```

---

## 6. Best Practices

- **Always clear timers** on unmount/cancellation to avoid leaks and “state update on unmounted component” errors.
- **Use `ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>`** for portable timer IDs.
- **Prefer `AbortSignal`** over manual flags for cancelling async work; it composes with `fetch`, streams, and many APIs.
- **Reactive signals** are synchronous and fine-grained; use them for UI state, not for async orchestration (use async/await or RxJS there).
- **Don’t mix `setInterval` polling with signals naively** without cleanup; wrap in a function returning a disposer.
- In Node, handle `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` for graceful shutdown and clear intervals there.

---

### Quick decision guide

| Need | Use |
|------|-----|
| Reactive UI state / derived values | Framework signals (Angular/Solid/Preact) |
| Cancel fetch/async work | `AbortController` + `AbortSignal` |
| Delay/repeat execution | `setTimeout` / `setInterval` |
| Animation loop | `requestAnimationFrame` |
| OS-level process control | `process.on('SIGINT', …)` |

If you tell me your framework (Angular, Solid, React, Node, etc.) and whether you mean reactive signals or cancellation, I can narrow this to a focused example.

Here is a small example using React state as the “signal-like” value, because stale closures plus missing timer cleanup are the classic combined bug. The same two fixes (functional update and cleanup) apply to Solid/Angular signals too.

### Mistake

```ts
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export function Ticker() {
  const [ticks, setTicks] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Bug 1: `ticks` is captured from the first render.
    //        Even after setTicks runs, this closure still sees 0,
    //        so the count effectively stays at 1.
    //
    // Bug 2: the interval is never cleared. In StrictMode/dev,
    //        or after unmount/remount, intervals accumulate and
    //        continue updating state for a component that may be gone.
    setInterval(() => {
      setTicks(ticks + 1);
    }, 1000);
  }, []);

  return <p>{ticks}</p>;
}
```

### Corrected version

```ts
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export function Ticker() {
  const [ticks, setTicks] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Use the functional updater so we never depend on a stale
    // captured value.
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setTicks((previous) => previous + 1);
    }, 1000);

    // Always return a cleanup function for timers created in an effect.
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);

  return <p>{ticks}</p>;
}
```

### What was wrong and why

1. **Stale closure over `ticks`**
   - The interval callback was created during the first render, when `ticks` was `0`.
   - JavaScript closures capture variables by reference to the scope at creation time. In React, each render creates a new `ticks` binding, but the old interval still references the old one.
   - Result: `setTicks(0 + 1)` runs every second, so the displayed value never progresses past `1`.
   - The functional form `setTicks(previous => previous + 1)` asks React for the latest state at update time, avoiding the stale value entirely.

2. **Missing cleanup / leaked timer**
   - `setInterval` keeps running until explicitly cleared.
   - If the component unmounts, the interval continues and may call `setTicks` on an unmounted component, causing warnings, wasted work, and possible memory retention.
   - In development with StrictMode, effects run twice, so two intervals can be created unless cleanup removes the first one.
   - Returning `() => clearInterval(id)` from `useEffect` ensures the timer is disposed exactly when the effect is torn down.

### Extra best practices

- Prefer `setInterval` only when you truly need repeated ticks. For many “update after delay” cases, recursive `setTimeout` plus cleanup is easier to reason about.
- If the timer depends on props or state, include those dependencies in the effect dependency array, or restructure the effect so it does not capture stale values.
- In Solid, the same cleanup idea is `onCleanup(() => clearInterval(id))`; in Angular, clear the timer in `ngOnDestroy` or use `DestroyRef`.
- If using an `AbortSignal` with a timer, clear the timeout in `finally` or when the signal aborts, otherwise a completed operation can still be aborted later by a dangling timeout.

# Common Pitfalls with Signals and Timers in TypeScript

Below are the most common pitfalls, grouped into **timers**, **signals (AbortSignal/AbortController)**, and **combined usage**, with bad/good code for each.

---

## Timers

### 1. Forgetting to clear timers → memory leaks / ghost callbacks
```ts
// ❌ Bad: interval runs forever, captures scope
setInterval(() => syncData(), 5000);

// ✅ Good: store handle, clear on teardown
const handle: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> = setInterval(() => syncData(), 5000);
// later
clearInterval(handle);
```

### 2. `setInterval` with async work → overlapping executions
```ts
// ❌ Bad: if fetch takes > 1s, calls pile up
setInterval(async () => {
  await fetchData();
}, 1000);

// ✅ Good: recursive setTimeout guarantees no overlap
const loop = async () => {
  await fetchData();
  setTimeout(loop, 1000);
};
loop();
```

### 3. `this` binding lost in timer callbacks
```ts
class Poller {
  count = 0;
  start() {
    // ❌ `this` is undefined (strict mode) or global
    setInterval(this.tick, 1000);
  }
  tick() { this.count++; }

  startFixed() {
    // ✅ arrow preserves `this`
    setInterval(() => this.tick(), 1000);
  }
}
```

### 4. Stale closures over loop variables
```ts
// ❌ Bad: all callbacks log 3 (var hoisting)
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

// ✅ Good: block scope
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```

### 5. Floating promises / unhandled rejections inside timers
```ts
// ❌ Bad: rejection becomes unhandled
setTimeout(async () => {
  await riskyOperation(); // may throw
}, 1000);

// ✅ Good: catch inside
setTimeout(() => {
  riskyOperation().catch(err => console.error('timer failed', err));
}, 1000);
```

### 6. Type confusion of timer handles (browser vs Node)
```ts
// Browser: number; Node: NodeJS.Timeout
// ❌ Bad: hard-coding `number` breaks in Node
let id: number;

// ✅ Good: derive the correct type
type TimerId = ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;
const id: TimerId = setTimeout(() => {}, 100);
clearTimeout(id);
```

### 7. Assuming `setTimeout(fn, 0)` is “immediate”
It is **not** immediate; it’s queued as a macrotask. If you need microtask timing:
```ts
// ❌ may run after other macrotasks
setTimeout(() => init(), 0);

// ✅ runs before next macrotask (but after current sync code)
queueMicrotask(() => init());
```

### 8. Not cleaning up in component lifecycles (React/Vue/Svelte)
```ts
// ❌ Bad: interval survives unmount
useEffect(() => {
  setInterval(tick, 1000);
}, []);

// ✅ Good: return cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

### 9. Background-tab throttling
Browsers throttle timers in background tabs (often ≥1s). Don’t rely on sub-second accuracy for background work; use the Page Visibility API or Web Workers if timing matters.

---

## Signals (AbortSignal / AbortController)

### 10. Reusing an aborted controller
```ts
const controller = new AbortController();
controller.abort();

// ❌ Bad: same signal is already aborted; fetch rejects immediately
fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });

// ✅ Good: create a new controller per operation
const c2 = new AbortController();
fetch(url, { signal: c2.signal });
```

### 11. Not checking `signal.aborted` before starting expensive work
```ts
// ❌ Bad: starts work even if already aborted
async function process(signal: AbortSignal) {
  const data = await fetchBigData();
  transform(data);
}

// ✅ Good: early exit
async function process(signal: AbortSignal) {
  if (signal.aborted) throw signal.reason;
  const data = await fetchBigData(signal); // propagate
  if (signal.aborted) throw signal.reason;
  transform(data);
}
```

### 12. Not propagating the signal to nested operations
```ts
// ❌ Bad: inner fetch ignores abort
async function load(signal: AbortSignal) {
  const res = await fetch('/api', { signal });
  const json = await res.json(); // no signal here, but okay for json()
  await fetch('/other'); // ❌ not aborted when parent aborts
}

// ✅ Good: pass signal down
await fetch('/other', { signal });
```

### 13. Abort listeners that are never removed → leak
```ts
const onAbort = () => cleanup();
signal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort);

// ❌ If abort never fires, listener stays
// ✅ Remove manually or use { once: true } when appropriate
signal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
// and/or
signal.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
```

### 14. Assuming abort cancels synchronous code
`abort()` only sets state and fires events; it does not interrupt currently running synchronous JavaScript.
```ts
controller.abort();
// This still completes:
for (let i = 0; i < 1e9; i++) {} // not cancelled
```
You must check `signal.aborted` at await points / loop iterations.

### 15. Mishandling `AbortError` (treating expected abort as failure)
```ts
try {
  await fetch(url, { signal });
} catch (e) {
  // ❌ Logs expected aborts as errors
  console.error(e);
  // ✅ Distinguish expected aborts
  if (e instanceof DOMException && e.name === 'AbortError') return;
  throw e;
}
```

### 16. Signal reason is `unknown` (typing)
```ts
// reason is `unknown` by spec
const r: unknown = signal.reason;
// ❌ Bad: assuming Error
console.log(signal.reason.message);

// ✅ Good: narrow
if (signal.reason instanceof Error) console.log(signal.reason.message);
```

### 17. Custom async APIs that ignore signals
If you write your own “wait” helper, it must honor abort:
```ts
// ✅ Robust delay that supports AbortSignal
function delay(ms: number, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
      return;
    }
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
      resolve();
    }, ms);

    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal!.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    };
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}
```
Note: when the timer fires first, we manually remove the abort listener to avoid leaks (`{ once: true }` only auto-removes on abort, not on resolve).

---

## Combined: Timers + Signals

### 18. Timeout without abort / abort without timeout
When racing a promise against a timer, clean up **both** sides:
```ts
async function fetchWithTimeout(url: string, ms: number): Promise<Response> {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(new Error('Timeout')), ms);
  try {
    return await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timer); // always clear
  }
}
```

### 19. Linking multiple signals (parent + child)
```ts
// ❌ Bad: only listens to one signal
function childWork(parent: AbortSignal) { ... }

// ✅ Good: combine so any abort propagates
function linkSignals(...signals: AbortSignal[]): AbortController {
  const c = new AbortController();
  const abort = (s: AbortSignal) => c.abort(s.reason);
  for (const s of signals) {
    if (s.aborted) { c.abort(s.reason); break; }
    s.addEventListener('abort', () => abort(s), { once: true });
  }
  return c;
}
```

### 20. Double cleanup / double reject
Ensure idempotent teardown when both timer and signal can fire:
```ts
function wait(ms: number, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
  let done = false;
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const finish = (fn: () => void) => {
      if (done) return;
      done = true;
      fn();
    };
    const timer = setTimeout(() => finish(resolve), ms);
    const onAbort = () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      finish(() => reject(signal!.reason));
    };
    if (signal?.aborted) onAbort();
    else signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}
```

---

## Quick checklist

- Store every timer handle and clear it on teardown.
- Prefer recursive `setTimeout` over `setInterval` for async work.
- Use `ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>` for portable typing.
- Always `try/catch` (or `.catch`) inside timer callbacks.
- Create a **new** `AbortController` per operation; never reuse after abort.
- Check `signal.aborted` at each `await`/loop boundary and propagate the signal downstream.
- Remove abort listeners (or use `{ once: true }`) and clear timers in `finally`.
- Narrow `signal.reason` (it’s `unknown`) and treat `AbortError` as expected, not a bug.

## Exercise

Build a **reactive countdown timer** in TypeScript:

- Keep the remaining time in a `Signal<number>`.
- Decrement it every second with `setInterval`.
- Whenever the signal changes, print the new value.
- When it reaches `0`, stop the interval and print `Done`.
- Return a small controller so the caller can `pause()` and `reset()`.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Minimal signal primitive

A signal stores a value and notifies subscribers when it changes. We also return an unsubscribe function to avoid leaks.

```ts
type Listener<T> = (value: T) => void;

class Signal<T> {
  private value: T;
  private listeners = new Set<Listener<T>>();

  constructor(initial: T) {
    this.value = initial;
  }

  get(): T {
    return this.value;
  }

  set(next: T): void {
    if (Object.is(this.value, next)) return; // no-op on identical value
    this.value = next;
    this.listeners.forEach((listener) => listener(next));
  }

  subscribe(listener: Listener<T>): () => void {
    listener(this.value); // emit current value immediately
    this.listeners.add(listener);
    return () => this.listeners.delete(listener);
  }
}
```

**Why `Object.is`?** It prevents unnecessary notifications when the same number is set twice, which matters for timers that might fire more than once at the boundary.

---

### 2. Countdown controller

The controller owns the interval id and exposes `pause()` / `reset()`.

```ts
interface TimerController {
  remaining: Signal<number>;
  pause(): void;
  resume(): void;
  reset(seconds?: number): void;
}

function createCountdown(initialSeconds: number): TimerController {
  const remaining = new Signal(initialSeconds);
  let intervalId: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;

  function tick(): void {
    const next = remaining.get() - 1;
    remaining.set(next);

    if (next <= 0) {
      if (intervalId !== null) {
        clearInterval(intervalId);
        intervalId = null;
      }
    }
  }

  function start(): void {
    if (intervalId !== null) return; // already running
    if (remaining.get() <= 0) return;  // nothing to count
    intervalId = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  }

  function pause(): void {
    if (intervalId !== null) {
      clearInterval(intervalId);
      intervalId = null;
    }
  }

  function reset(seconds = initialSeconds): void {
    pause();
    remaining.set(seconds);
  }

  function resume(): void {
    start();
  }

  // Kick off the timer immediately.
  start();

  return { remaining, pause, resume, reset };
}
```

**Notes on correctness**

- `intervalId` is guarded so `pause()` is idempotent.
- `reset()` defaults to the original duration but also accepts a new one.
- The timer stops itself when `remaining` reaches `0`, preventing a negative drift forever.

---

### 3. Reactive side effect

Subscribe to the signal so UI/console reacts automatically. The subscription should be cleaned up when done.

```ts
const timer = createCountdown(5);

const unsubscribe = timer.remaining.subscribe((value) => {
  console.log(`remaining: ${value}`);
  if (value === 0) {
    console.log("Done");
    unsubscribe(); // stop listening once finished
  }
});
```

Because `subscribe` emits the current value immediately, you will see `remaining: 5` right away, then `4`, `3`, ... `0`, `Done`.

---

### 4. Full runnable example

Save as `countdown.ts` and run with `npx ts-node countdown.ts` (or compile with `tsc`).

```ts
// countdown.ts
type Listener<T> = (value: T) => void;

class Signal<T> {
  private value: T;
  private listeners = new Set<Listener<T>>();

  constructor(initial: T) {
    this.value = initial;
  }

  get(): T {
    return this.value;
  }

  set(next: T): void {
    if (Object.is(this.value, next)) return;
    this.value = next;
    this.listeners.forEach((listener) => listener(next));
  }

  subscribe(listener: Listener<T>): () => void {
    listener(this.value);
    this.listeners.add(listener);
    return () => this.listeners.delete(listener);
  }
}

interface TimerController {
  remaining: Signal<number>;
  pause(): void;
  resume(): void;
  reset(seconds?: number): void;
}

function createCountdown(initialSeconds: number): TimerController {
  const remaining = new Signal(initialSeconds);
  let intervalId: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;

  function tick(): void {
    const next = remaining.get() - 1;
    remaining.set(next);
    if (next <= 0 && intervalId !== null) {
      clearInterval(intervalId);
      intervalId = null;
    }
  }

  function start(): void {
    if (intervalId !== null || remaining.get() <= 0) return;
    intervalId = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  }

  function pause(): void {
    if (intervalId !== null) {
      clearInterval(intervalId);
      intervalId = null;
    }
  }

  function reset(seconds = initialSeconds): void {
    pause();
    remaining.set(seconds);
  }

  function resume(): void {
    start();
  }

  start();
  return { remaining, pause, resume, reset };
}

// --- Demo ---
const timer = createCountdown(5);

const unsubscribe = timer.remaining.subscribe((value) => {
  console.log(`remaining: ${value}`);
  if (value === 0) {
    console.log("Done");
    unsubscribe();
  }
});

// Example of manual control (uncomment to test):
// setTimeout(() => timer.pause(), 2500);
// setTimeout(() => timer.resume(), 4000);
// setTimeout(() => timer.reset(3), 1000);
```

