## Recap

- **Signal** gives you a single source of truth plus automatic notifications.
- **Timer** mutates that source of truth; subscribers do not need to know about the interval.
- **Cleanup** (`clearInterval` + unsubscribe) prevents memory leaks and duplicate ticks.

This pattern scales directly to UI frameworks: replace `console.log` with rendering a component, and the same signal/timer logic stays unchanged.

## Signals and Timers in TypeScript (Node.js)

### Signals
Node.js exposes OS signals through `process`. The common ones:
- `SIGINT` — Ctrl+C in the terminal.
- `SIGTERM` — graceful termination request (e.g. `kill <pid>`, Docker stop, systemd).
- `SIGHUP` — terminal closed / reload config.
- `SIGUSR1`/`SIGUSR2` — user-defined.
- `SIGKILL` / `SIGSTOP` — **cannot** be caught or ignored (OS-level, immediate).

Handlers are registered with `process.on('SIGINT', cb)` and should do cleanup then `process.exit(code)`. A handler that never exits leaves the process alive.

### Timers
Node.js timers are macrotasks scheduled by libuv:
- `setTimeout(cb, ms)` — run once after ≥ ms.
- `setInterval(cb, ms)` — repeat.
- `setImmediate(cb)` — run in the "check" phase, after I/O.
- `process.nextTick(cb)` — microtask-ish, runs before the event loop continues (highest priority, can starve I/O if abused).

Order in one loop iteration (simplified): `nextTick` queue → microtasks (Promises) → timers phase (expired `setTimeout`/`setInterval`) → I/O callbacks → `setImmediate` (check phase) → close callbacks.

### Combining them: graceful shutdown
The "core" pattern: catch `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM`, run registered cleanup tasks each guarded by a timeout, then exit. This is what production services implement.

---

## Implementation

```ts
// graceful-shutdown.ts
// A small, dependency-free graceful-shutdown coordinator.
// Combines process signals (SIGINT/SIGTERM) with timers (setTimeout) to
// bound cleanup work and guarantee the process exits.

export type Signal = 'SIGINT' | 'SIGTERM' | 'SIGHUP';

export interface CleanupTask {
  /** Human-readable name, used in logs. */
  name: string;
  /** The work to do. May be sync or async. */
  run: () => void | Promise<void>;
  /** Hard cap for this task in ms. Default: 5000. */
  timeoutMs?: number;
}

export interface GracefulShutdownOptions {
  /** Default per-task timeout in ms. Default: 5000. */
  defaultTimeoutMs?: number;
  /** Total shutdown budget in ms. Default: 10000. */
  totalTimeoutMs?: number;
  /** Signals to listen to. Default: SIGINT, SIGTERM. */
  signals?: Signal[];
  /** Called right before process.exit. Useful for logging. */
  onExit?: (code: number) => void;
  /** Override process.exit for testing. */
  exit?: (code: number) => void;
}

export class GracefulShutdown {
  private readonly tasks = new Map<string, CleanupTask>();
  private readonly signals: Signal[];
  private readonly defaultTimeoutMs: number;
  private readonly totalTimeoutMs: number;
  private readonly exitFn: (code: number) => void;
  private readonly onExit?: (code: number) => void;

  private shuttingDown = false;
  private installed = false;

  constructor(opts: GracefulShutdownOptions = {}) {
    this.signals = opts.signals ?? ['SIGINT', 'SIGTERM'];
    this.defaultTimeoutMs = opts.defaultTimeoutMs ?? 5000;
    this.totalTimeoutMs = opts.totalTimeoutMs ?? 10000;
    this.exitFn = opts.exit ?? ((c) => process.exit(c));
    this.onExit = opts.onExit;
  }

  /** Register a cleanup task. Idempotent by name. */
  register(task: CleanupTask): this {
    if (this.shuttingDown) {
      // Too late to register; run it immediately-ish is out of scope.
      return this;
    }
    this.tasks.set(task.name, task);
    return this;
  }

  /** Remove a previously registered task. */
  unregister(name: string): this {
    this.tasks.delete(name);
    return this;
  }

  /** Attach signal listeners. Call once at startup. */
  install(): this {
    if (this.installed) return this;
    this.installed = true;
    for (const sig of this.signals) {
      process.on(sig, () => void this.handle(sig));
    }
    return this;
  }

  /** Detach signal listeners (useful in tests). */
  uninstall(): this {
    if (!this.installed) return this;
    this.installed = false;
    for (const sig of this.signals) {
      process.removeAllListeners(sig);
    }
    return this;
  }

  /** Trigger shutdown programmatically (e.g. from a health check). */
  async shutdown(code = 0): Promise<void> {
    await this.handle(null, code);
  }

  private async handle(signal: Signal | null, code = 0): Promise<void> {
    if (this.shuttingDown) return; // re-entrancy guard
    this.shuttingDown = true;

    if (signal) {
      // eslint-disable-next-line no-console
      console.log(`[shutdown] received ${signal}`);
    }

    // Total budget timer: if cleanup hangs, force exit.
    const forceTimer = setTimeout(() => {
      // eslint-disable-next-line no-console
      console.error(`[shutdown] exceeded total budget (${this.totalTimeoutMs}ms); forcing exit`);
      this.finish(1);
    }, this.totalTimeoutMs);
    // Don't keep the event loop alive solely for this timer.
    forceTimer.unref?.();

    try {
      // Run tasks in registration order, each bounded by its own timeout.
      for (const task of this.tasks.values()) {
        await this.runWithTimeout(task);
      }
    } finally {
      clearTimeout(forceTimer);
    }

    this.finish(code);
  }

  private async runWithTimeout(task: CleanupTask): Promise<void> {
    const ms = task.timeoutMs ?? this.defaultTimeoutMs;
    let timer: NodeJS.Timeout | undefined;

    const timeoutPromise = new Promise<never>((_, reject) => {
      timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`task "${task.name}" timed out after ${ms}ms`)), ms);
      timer.unref?.();
    });

    try {
      await Promise.race([Promise.resolve(task.run()), timeoutPromise]);
    } catch (err) {
      // eslint-disable-next-line no-console
      console.error(`[shutdown] task "${task.name}" failed:`, err);
    } finally {
      if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    }
  }

  private finish(code: number): void {
    this.onExit?.(code);
    this.exitFn(code);
  }
}

// ---------- Timer utilities ----------

/** Promise-based sleep. */
export function sleep(ms: number): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const t = setTimeout(resolve, ms);
    t.unref?.();
  });
}

/** Run `fn`, reject if it takes longer than `ms`. */
export async function withTimeout<T>(
  fn: () => T | Promise<T>,
  ms: number,
  label = 'operation',
): Promise<T> {
  let timer: NodeJS.Timeout | undefined;
  const timeout = new Promise<never>((_, reject) => {
    timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`${label} timed out after ${ms}ms`)), ms);
    timer.unref?.();
  });
  try {
    return await Promise.race([Promise.resolve(fn()), timeout]);
  } finally {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
  }
}

/** Retry with exponential backoff + jitter. */
export async function retry<T>(
  fn: () => T | Promise<T>,
  opts: { attempts?: number; baseMs?: number; maxMs?: number } = {},
): Promise<T> {
  const attempts = opts.attempts ?? 5;
  const baseMs = opts.baseMs ?? 100;
  const maxMs = opts.maxMs ?? 5000;

  let lastErr: unknown;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      lastErr = err;
      if (i === attempts - 1) break;
      const exp = Math.min(maxMs, baseMs * 2 ** i);
      const jitter = Math.random() * exp * 0.3;
      await sleep(exp + jitter);
    }
  }
  throw lastErr;
}
```

### Usage

```ts
// main.ts
import { GracefulShutdown } from './graceful-shutdown';

const shutdown = new GracefulShutdown({
  totalTimeoutMs: 10_000,
  onExit: (code) => console.log(`exiting with code ${code}`),
}).install();

// Register cleanup tasks in the order you want them to run.
shutdown.register({
  name: 'http-server',
  run: () => server.close(), // stops accepting new connections
  timeoutMs: 3000,
});

shutdown.register({
  name: 'db-pool',
  run: async () => {
    await db.end();
  },
  timeoutMs: 5000,
});

// Programmatic shutdown (e.g. from a /healthz failure or admin endpoint):
// await shutdown.shutdown(0);
```

### Minimal test (Vitest/Jest style)

```ts
import { GracefulShutdown } from './graceful-shutdown';

test('runs cleanup tasks then exits', async () => {
  const calls: string[] = [];
  const exit = jest.fn();
  const gs = new GracefulShutdown({ exit, signals: [] });

  gs.register({ name: 'a', run: () => { calls.push('a'); } });
  gs.register({ name: 'b', run: async () => { calls.push('b'); } });

  await gs.shutdown(0);
  expect(calls).toEqual(['a', 'b']);
  expect(exit).toHaveBeenCalledWith(0);
});

test('times out a hanging task but continues', async () => {
  const exit = jest.fn();
  const gs = new GracefulShutdown({ exit, signals: [], defaultTimeoutMs: 50 });
  gs.register({ name: 'hang', run: () => new Promise(() => {}) });
  gs.register({ name: 'after', run: () => {} });
  await gs.shutdown(0);
  expect(exit).toHaveBeenCalledWith(0);
});
```

### Key points to mention in an interview
1. **`SIGKILL` cannot be handled** — only `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT` allow graceful cleanup. Design for the case where even `SIGTERM` is followed by `SIGKILL`.
2. **Timers are macrotasks**; `process.nextTick` and Promise microtasks drain first. Don't `nextTick`-loop or you starve I/O.
3. **`unref()`** timers so a pending `setTimeout` doesn't keep a process alive unnecessarily (important for CLI tools and tests).
4. **Re-entrancy guard** (`shuttingDown`) prevents double cleanup when both `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` arrive.
5. **Bound everything with timeouts** — a hanging DB close must not block exit forever; the total budget + per-task timeout guarantees termination.
6. **Order matters**: stop accepting traffic first (HTTP server), then drain in-flight work, then close DB/queues, then exit.

### Bonus: modern bridge — `AbortSignal`
Modern APIs (fetch, streams, timers via `setTimeout`'s `{ signal }` option in Node 16+) use `AbortController`/`AbortSignal`. You can bridge signals to cleanup:

```ts
const ac = new AbortController();
process.on('SIGTERM', () => ac.abort());
// fetch(url, { signal: ac.signal })
// setTimeout(cb, ms, { signal: ac.signal })  // auto-cancelled on abort
```

# Edge cases when using signals and timers in TypeScript

“Signals and timers” covers three common families: **`AbortSignal` + timers**, **reactive signals (Solid/Angular/Preact) + timers**, and **OS/process signals + timers**. Below are the important edge cases for each, with idiomatic TypeScript examples.

---

## 1. Timer IDs are environment-dependent

```ts
// Browser: number
// Node.js: NodeJS.Timeout
const id: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> = setTimeout(() => {}, 100);
clearTimeout(id);
```

**Edge case:** mixing DOM `lib` and `@types/node` in one project causes type conflicts. Prefer a project split (browser vs node tsconfigs) or use `ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>`.

---

## 2. Invalid / extreme delay values

```ts
setTimeout(cb, -100);   // clamped to 0
setTimeout(cb, NaN);     // treated as 0 in practice
setTimeout(cb, 2 ** 31); // overflow: may fire immediately or be capped
```

**Edge case:** delays larger than ~24.8 days (`2^31 - 1` ms) overflow 32-bit timer internals. Split long waits into recursive timers or use a monotonic clock check.

---

## 3. `this` binding and stale closures

```ts
const obj = {
  count: 0,
  start() {
    // BUG: `this` is undefined/lost in the callback
    setTimeout(this.tick, 100);
    // also stale if `count` is captured by value instead of read lazily
  },
  tick() { this.count++; }
};

// Fix
setTimeout(() => obj.tick(), 100);
// or
setTimeout(obj.tick.bind(obj), 100);
```

**Edge case:** `setInterval` callbacks that read a closed-over *value* instead of a *binding* see stale data. Read mutable state from a container or use functional updates (React/Solid).

---

## 4. Exceptions inside timer callbacks

```ts
// Browser: logged, other timers keep running
// Node: uncaught exception -> process crashes by default
setTimeout(() => {
  try {
    risky();
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('timer failed', e);
  }
}, 100);
```

**Edge case:** always wrap timer bodies in `try/catch` for library code; in Node also consider `process.on('uncaughtException')` only as a last resort.

---

## 5. Memory leaks and cleanup

```ts
// Leak: interval keeps closure + DOM node alive
const el = document.querySelector('#x')!;
const id = setInterval(() => el.textContent = String(Date.now()), 1000);

// Cleanup
clearInterval(id);
```

**Edge case:** `setInterval` must be cleared on teardown; removing the DOM node alone does not stop the timer.

---

## 6. Node: `ref` / `unref` and process lifetime

```ts
const keepAlive = setInterval(() => heartbeat(), 30_000);
keepAlive.unref(); // don't keep Node process alive for this timer

const forceExit = setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 10_000);
forceExit.ref();   // DO keep process alive for forced shutdown
```

**Edge case:** using `unref()` for a shutdown timeout means the process may exit before the timeout fires, so force-exit timers should stay ref’d.

---

## 7. `AbortSignal` + timer (the core pattern)

```ts
function delay(ms: number, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    // 1) Already aborted before we start
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      reject(signal.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
      return;
    }

    let settled = false;
    let id: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;

    const cleanup = () => {
      clearTimeout(id);
      signal?.removeEventListener('abort', onAbort);
    };

    const finish = (fn: () => void) => {
      if (settled) return;          // 2) race-safe: only one wins
      settled = true;
      cleanup();
      fn();
    };

    const onAbort = () => finish(() =>
      reject(signal!.reason ?? new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'))
    );

    id = setTimeout(() => finish(resolve), ms);
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  });
}
```

Key edge cases handled:
- **Already aborted**: rejected synchronously.
- **Race between fire and abort**: `settled` flag ensures single resolution.
- **Listener leak**: removed on both paths; `{ once: true }` as a backstop.
- **`signal.reason`** may be any value (including non-Error) — preserve it.

---

## 8. Combining / timeout signals

```ts
// Built-in (modern runtimes)
const s = AbortSignal.timeout(5_000); // auto-aborts; reason is TimeoutError DOMException
await fetch(url, { signal: s });

// Combining two signals (newer API)
const combined = AbortSignal.any([userSignal, AbortSignal.timeout(5_000)]);
```

**Edge case:** `AbortSignal.timeout` gives no cancel handle and a fixed `TimeoutError` reason. If you need a custom reason or manual cancel, use `AbortController` + `setTimeout` (pattern above) and `clearTimeout` in `finally`.

---

## 9. fetch with manual abort timer

```ts
async function fetchWithTimeout(url: string, ms: number) {
  const ac = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(new DOMException('Timeout', 'TimeoutError')), ms);
  try {
    return await fetch(url, { signal: ac.signal });
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timer); // prevents late abort + leak
  }
}
```

**Edge cases:**
- Clear the timer on *both* success and failure.
- If the abort fires first, `fetch` rejects with the controller’s reason; distinguish timeout vs. user cancel via `signal.reason` or the error name.
- Aborting after completion is harmless but wasteful; the `finally` prevents it.

---

## 10. Reactive signals (Solid) + timers

```ts
import { createSignal, createEffect, onCleanup } from 'solid-js';

function Ticker() {
  const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);

  createEffect(() => {
    // effect may re-run when deps change -> previous timer must be cleared
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(c => c + 1); // functional update avoids stale closure
    }, 1000);

    onCleanup(() => clearInterval(id));
  });

  return () => count();
}
```

Edge cases:
- **No cleanup** = timer leak on component destroy / effect re-run.
- **Stale state**: inside `setInterval`, read the signal via functional updater or `untrack` to avoid unintended reactive subscriptions.
- Effect dependencies: a timer that should run once must not subscribe to frequently-changing signals, or wrap in `onMount`.

---

## 11. Process/OS signals + graceful shutdown timers

```ts
let shuttingDown = false;

process.on('SIGTERM', () => {
  if (shuttingDown) return; // idempotent
  shuttingDown = true;

  const force = setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 10_000);
  force.ref(); // ensure exit happens if close hangs

  server.close((err) => {
    clearTimeout(force);
    if (err) { console.error(err); process.exit(1); }
    process.exit(0);
  });
});
```

Edge cases:
- Adding a `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` handler **disables Node’s default exit**; you must call `process.exit`.
- Make handlers idempotent (signal can arrive twice).
- Use a ref’d force-exit timer so a hung connection can’t block shutdown forever, and unref’d background timers so they don’t delay exit.
- On Windows, `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT` handling differs; some signals (e.g. SIGKILL) cannot be caught.

---

## 12. Fake timers in tests

```ts
import { vi } from 'vitest';

beforeEach(() => vi.useFakeTimers());
afterEach(() => vi.useRealTimers());

test('debounce', () => {
  const fn = vi.fn();
  debounce(fn, 100)();
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(99);
  expect(fn).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(1);
  expect(fn).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

Edge cases:
- Fake timers also freeze `Date.now()` and `performance.now()`; restore with `useRealTimers()`.
- Microtasks/promises need `vi.runAllTicks()` / `flushPromises`; timers don’t drive async I/O automatically.
- Mixing real network calls with fake timers can hang; keep them separated.

---

## 13. Accuracy: prefer recursive `setTimeout` over `setInterval`

```ts
function accurateInterval(cb: () => void, ms: number) {
  let handle: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;
  const run = () => {
    cb();
    handle = setTimeout(run, ms); // schedule next AFTER work finishes
  };
  handle = setTimeout(run, ms);
  return () => clearTimeout(handle);
}
```

**Edge case:** `setInterval` can fire overlapping callbacks if the handler runs longer than the period; recursive `setTimeout` serializes execution and avoids drift buildup.

---

## 14. Worker / cross-realm timers

```ts
// In a Web Worker, setTimeout is the worker's own; the main thread isn't blocked,
// but message passing adds latency. Cleanup on 'message'/'terminate'.
```

**Edge case:** timers in Workers don’t have access to DOM; coordinate shutdown via `worker.terminate()` plus `clearInterval` inside.

---

### Quick checklist

| Concern | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Stale closure | functional updates / read lazily |
| Leaked timer | `clear*` in `finally` / `onCleanup` |
| Race with abort | `settled` guard |
| Already-aborted signal | reject synchronously |
| Invalid/extreme delay | clamp / split / recursive timers |
| Node process lifetime | `ref`/`unref` intentionally |
| Crash on exception | `try/catch` in callback |
| Test flakiness | fake timers + restore |

# Singleton Pattern in TypeScript

A singleton guarantees that only one instance of a class exists and provides a global access point to it. Below is a **complete, runnable** example (run with `npx ts-node singleton.ts` or `deno run singleton.ts`) that covers the classic form, an async-safe form, testability, and the modern module-level alternative.

## `singleton.ts`

```typescript
// ============================================================================
// 1) CLASSIC SINGLETON — eager-ish, lazily created on first call
// ============================================================================
class Database {
  // The single instance, held privately on the class itself.
  private static instance: Database | null = null;

  // Private constructor prevents `new Database()` from anywhere else.
  private constructor(
    private readonly url: string,
    private connected = false,
  ) {}

  // The global access point.
  static getInstance(url = process.env.DB_URL ?? "postgres://localhost:5432/app"): Database {
    if (Database.instance === null) {
      Database.instance = new Database(url);
    }
    return Database.instance;
  }

  connect(): void {
    if (this.connected) return;
    console.log(`[DB] connecting to ${this.url}`);
    this.connected = true;
  }

  query(sql: string): string[] {
    if (!this.connected) throw new Error("Database not connected");
    return [`result for: ${sql}`];
  }

  // Test hook: allows resetting between tests. Never expose in prod APIs.
  static __resetForTests(): void {
    Database.instance = null;
  }

  // Identity helper to prove uniqueness.
  get id(): string {
    return `Database#${this.url}`;
  }
}

// ============================================================================
// 2) ASYNC-SAFE SINGLETON — avoids duplicate creation during await
// ============================================================================
class AsyncCache {
  private static instance: AsyncCache | null = null;
  private static creating: Promise<AsyncCache> | null = null;

  private constructor(private readonly ttlMs: number) {}

  static getInstance(ttlMs = 60_000): AsyncCache | Promise<AsyncCache> {
    // Fast path: already built.
    if (AsyncCache.instance) return AsyncCache.instance;
    // Slow path: in-flight creation — reuse the same promise.
    if (!AsyncCache.creating) {
      AsyncCache.creating = (async () => {
        const inst = new AsyncCache(ttlMs);
        await inst.warmUp(); // simulate async init (I/O, schema load, etc.)
        AsyncCache.instance = inst;
        AsyncCache.creating = null;
        return inst;
      })();
    }
    return AsyncCache.creating;
  }

  private async warmUp(): Promise<void> {
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50));
    console.log(`[Cache] warmed up (ttl=${this.ttlMs}ms)`);
  }

  get(key: string): string | undefined {
    console.log(`[Cache] get(${key})`);
    return undefined;
  }

  static __resetForTests(): void {
    AsyncCache.instance = null;
    AsyncCache.creating = null;
  }
}

// ============================================================================
// 3) MODERN ALTERNATIVE — module-level singleton (preferred in TS/ESM)
// ============================================================================
// In ESM, the module itself is a singleton: evaluated once, cached by the loader.
class FeatureFlags {
  private flags = new Map<string, boolean>();

  set(name: string, value: boolean): void {
    this.flags.set(name, value);
  }
  isEnabled(name: string): boolean {
    return this.flags.get(name) ?? false;
  }
}

// A single instance, exported once. Consumers `import { featureFlags }`.
export const featureFlags = new FeatureFlags();

// ============================================================================
// 4) DEMO / SELF-TEST
// ============================================================================
function assert(cond: boolean, msg: string): void {
  if (!cond) throw new Error(`ASSERT FAILED: ${msg}`);
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  console.log("=== Classic singleton ===");
  const a = Database.getInstance();
  const b = Database.getInstance();
  assert(a === b, "same instance");
  a.connect();
  console.log("query:", a.query("SELECT 1"));
  // Prove `new` is blocked at compile time (cast needed only to demo):
  // @ts-expect-error private constructor
  // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars
  const _x = new Database("x");
  console.log("identity:", a.id, "==", b.id);

  console.log("\n=== Async singleton (concurrent calls share one instance) ===");
  AsyncCache.__resetForTests();
  const [c1, c2, c3] = await Promise.all([
    AsyncCache.getInstance(1000) as Promise<AsyncCache>,
    AsyncCache.getInstance(1000) as Promise<AsyncCache>,
    AsyncCache.getInstance(1000) as Promise<AsyncCache>,
  ]);
  assert(c1 === c2 && c2 === c3, "all three refs are the same");
  console.log("warm-up happened exactly once (see log above)");
  c1.get("user:42");

  console.log("\n=== Module-level singleton ===");
  featureFlags.set("dark-mode", true);
  console.log("dark-mode enabled?", featureFlags.isEnabled("dark-mode"));

  console.log("\n=== Test reset ===");
  Database.__resetForTests();
  const a2 = Database.getInstance();
  assert(a !== a2, "new instance after reset");
  console.log("reset works; instances differ as expected");

  console.log("\nALL CHECKS PASSED ✅");
}

main().catch((e) => {
  console.error(e);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

## Key points

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| Private constructor | Enforces single creation site (`getInstance`). |
| Static field | Holds the one instance; lazy init avoids work if never used. |
| Async init | Cache the **promise**, not just the instance, so concurrent callers await the same creation. Reset both `instance` and `creating`. |
| Testability | Provide `__resetForTests()` (or better, depend on an interface and inject a fake). |
| Thread safety | JS/TS is single-threaded; the promise cache covers async races. |
| Modern code | Prefer **module-level** `export const x = new X()` — simpler, tree-shakeable, and no static state. Reserve class singletons for cross-cutting infra (DB, cache, logger). |
| Pitfalls | Hidden global state hurts testability; singletons can become "god objects"; prefer dependency injection when instances need variation (different configs per environment). |

