## Final solution (complete, copy-pasteable)

```ts
// sort.ts
type Direction = 'asc' | 'desc';

interface SortKey<T> {
  key: keyof T;
  direction?: Direction;
  compare?: (a: T[keyof T], b: T[keyof T]) => number;
}

function defaultCompare(a: unknown, b: unknown): number {
  if (a === b) return 0;
  if (a == null) return b == null ? 0 : -1;
  if (b == null) return 1;
  if (typeof a === 'number' && typeof b === 'number') return a - b;
  if (a instanceof Date && b instanceof Date) return a.getTime() - b.getTime();
  if (typeof a === 'boolean' && typeof b === 'boolean') return Number(a) - Number(b);
  return String(a).localeCompare(String(b));
}

function compareByKeys<T>(a: T, b: T, keys: readonly SortKey<T>[]): number {
  for (const spec of keys) {
    const dir = spec.direction ?? 'asc';
    const raw = spec.compare
      ? spec.compare(a[spec.key], b[spec.key])
      : defaultCompare(a[spec.key], b[spec.key]);
    if (raw !== 0) return dir === 'desc' ? -raw : raw;
  }
  return 0;
}

function merge<T>(left: T[], right: T[], compare: (a: T, b: T) => number): T[] {
  const out: T[] = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (compare(left[i], right[j]) <= 0) out.push(left[i++]);
    else out.push(right[j++]);
  }
  while (i < left.length) out.push(left[i++]);
  while (j < right.length) out.push(right[j++]);
  return out;
}

function mergeSort<T>(arr: T[], compare: (a: T, b: T) => number): T[] {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr.slice();
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid), compare);
  const right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid), compare);
  return merge(left, right, compare);
}

export function multiKeySort<T>(items: readonly T[], keys: readonly SortKey<T>[]): T[] {
  const compare = (a: T, b: T) => compareByKeys(a, b, keys);
  return mergeSort([...items], compare);
}
```

### Usage

```ts
import { multiKeySort } from './sort';

const data = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Zoe',  score: 90 },
  { id: 2, name: 'Amy',  score: 70 },
  { id: 3, name: 'Bob',  score: 90 },
];

// Primary: score desc; tie-break: name asc
const result = multiKeySort(data, [
  { key: 'score', direction: 'desc' },
  { key: 'name',  direction: 'asc' },
]);

console.log(result.map(x => x.name)); // ['Bob', 'Zoe', 'Amy']
```

### Complexity & notes

- **Time:** O(n log n) worst/average/best (merge sort).
- **Space:** O(n) auxiliary (slices + merge output).
- **Stability:** guaranteed by `<=` in merge and by returning `0` when all keys tie.
- **Why not `Array.prototype.sort`?** Modern engines use a stable TimSort, but implementing merge sort explicitly guarantees stability and worst-case bounds regardless of engine, and makes multi-key logic testable in isolation. For production on huge arrays, consider an iterative bottom-up merge sort to reduce recursion overhead.

## Sorting Algorithms (Interview Perspective)

In an interview, you usually need to know:

| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Space | Stable? | Notes |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|---|
| Bubble | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes | Good teaching, early-exit optimization |
| Selection | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | No | Min swaps |
| Insertion | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes | Great for small / nearly sorted data |
| Merge | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | Yes | Preferred for linked lists and stable needs |
| Quick | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n²) | O(log n) | No | Fast in practice; pivot choice matters |
| Heap | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(1) | No | Guaranteed worst-case, no recursion depth issues |
| Counting | O(n+k) | O(n+k) | O(n+k) | O(k) | Yes | For bounded integers |

Key interview talking points:
- **Stability**: equal elements keep original order (important for multi-key sort).
- **In-place vs extra space**: quick/heap/bubble are O(1) extra; merge needs O(n).
- **Adaptive**: insertion/bubble (with early exit) benefit from partially sorted input.
- **Real-world**: V8’s `Array.prototype.sort` is TimSort-like, stable, O(n log n).

