# Strings and Text Processing in TypeScript

## 1. Core idea

In TypeScript (and JavaScript), a **string is an immutable primitive**. You can't change a character in place; operations return new strings. Strings are sequences of UTF-16 code units, which matters when you deal with emoji or rare characters.

```ts
let s = "hello";
s[0] = "H";        // silently ignored
console.log(s);     // "hello"
s = s[0].toUpperCase() + s.slice(1); // "Hello"
```

There's also a `String` object wrapper (`new String("x")`), but you should almost never use it. Use primitives.

## 2. Syntax

### Literal forms

```ts
const single = 'single quotes';
const double = "double quotes";
const back   = `template literal`;
const raw    = String.raw`C:\path\to\file`; // no escape processing
```

### Escape sequences

```ts
const nl   = "line1\nline2";
const tab  = "a\tb";
const hex  = "\x41";          // "A"
const uni  = "\u0041";        // "A"
const cp   = "\u{1F600}";     // 😀 (code point)
const quote = "she said \"hi\"";
```

### Template literals (interpolation)

```ts
const name = "Ada";
const age = 36;
const msg = `Hello ${name}, you are ${age} years old.`;
const multiline = `
  line 1
  line 2
`;
```

Anything inside `${}` is evaluated as an expression, including function calls and nested templates.

### Tagged templates

A function receives the string pieces and the interpolated values separately — useful for SQL escaping, HTML sanitization, i18n.

```ts
function html(strings: TemplateStringsArray, ...values: unknown[]) {
  return strings.reduce((acc, s, i) => {
    const v = i < values.length ? String(values[i]).replace(/</g, "&lt;") : "";
    return acc + s + v;
  }, "");
}

const userInput = "<script>";
const safe = html`<p>${userInput}</p>`; // "<p>&lt;script&gt;</p>"
```

## 3. Common string methods

```ts
const s = "  TypeScript  ";

s.length;                       // 14
s.trim();                       // "TypeScript"
s.trimStart(); s.trimEnd();
s.toUpperCase(); s.toLowerCase();
s.includes("Script");           // true
s.startsWith("  Type");          // true
s.endsWith("  ");               // true
s.indexOf("Type");              // 2
s.lastIndexOf("e");
s.slice(2, 10);                 // "TypeScri"
s.substring(2, 10);             // same here (differs with negatives)
s.split(/\s+/);                 // ["", "TypeScript", ""]
s.replace("Type", "Java");      // "  JavaScript  " (first only)
s.replaceAll(" ", "_");         // "__TypeScript__"
s.repeat(2);
s.padStart(20, ".");
s.charAt(0);
s.charCodeAt(0);                // UTF-16 code unit
s.codePointAt(0);               // code point (better for emoji)
```

**Note:** `replace` with a string replaces only the **first** occurrence. Use a regex with the `g` flag or `replaceAll` for all.

## 4. Regular expressions

```ts
const re = /(\w+)@(\w+)\.(\w+)/;
const email = "ada@lovelace.org";
const m = email.match(re);
// m[0] = full match, m[1..] = groups, m.index, m.input

// Test
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/.test("2024-01-15"); // true

// Replace with groups
"John Doe".replace(/(\w+) (\w+)/, "$2, $1"); // "Doe, John"

// Named groups
const m2 = "2024-01-15".match(/(?<y>\d{4})-(?<m>\d{2})-(?<d>\d{2})/);
m2?.groups?.y; // "2024"

// Iteration
for (const m of "a1 b2".matchAll(/\w(\d)/g)) {
  console.log(m[0], m[1]);
}
```

Use the `u` flag (`/\p{Emoji}/u`) for proper Unicode handling and `v` (ES2024) for even better sets.

## 5. Unicode gotchas

```ts
const emoji = "😀";
emoji.length;          // 2 (UTF-16 surrogate pair)
[...emoji].length;     // 1
Array.from(emoji).length; // 1
emoji.codePointAt(0);  // 128512

// Normalization
"é" === "e\u0301";           // false (different code units)
"é".normalize("NFC") === "e\u0301".normalize("NFC"); // true
```

Always normalize user input before comparing or storing, and prefer `codePointAt` / spreading when counting "characters."

## 6. TypeScript-specific: template literal types

TypeScript can manipulate string types at compile time:

```ts
type EventName = `on${Capitalize<"click" | "hover">}`; // "onClick" | "onHover"

type Route = `/users/${string}` | `/posts/${number}`;
const r: Route = "/users/42"; // ok

// Pattern matching via inference
type ExtractId<T extends string> =
  T extends `/users/${infer Id}` ? Id : never;
type Id = ExtractId<"/users/123">; // "123"
```

This enables strongly typed event names, route params, CSS class names, etc.

## 7. Typical use cases

### Parsing CSV-ish data
```ts
const line = "ada,36,london";
const [name, age, city] = line.split(",");
```

### Formatting
```ts
const money = (n: number) =>
  new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US", { style: "currency", currency: "USD" }).format(n);
money(1234.5); // "$1,234.50"
```

### Validation
```ts
const isEmail = (s: string) => /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(s);
```

### Slug generation
```ts
const slug = (s: string) =>
  s.toLowerCase().trim().replace(/\s+/g, "-").replace(/[^\w-]/g, "");
```

### Building SQL safely (never concatenate raw user input)
```ts
// Use parameterized queries; tagged templates can help build safe wrappers
const query = sql`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${userId}`;
```

### Path/URL construction
```ts
const url = new URL("/api/users", baseUrl);
url.searchParams.set("q", query);
```

## 8. Performance tips

- Strings are immutable: repeated `+=` in a loop creates O(n²) garbage. Use an array and `join("")`.
- `replaceAll`/regex with `g` is faster than manual loops.
- For huge text, consider streaming or `Blob`/`ReadableStream` rather than one giant string.
- Avoid `new String(...)`; it boxes and breaks `===` equality.

## 9. Quick reference checklist

| Goal | Tool |
|---|---|
| Interpolate | Template literal `${}` |
| Sanitize/escape | Tagged template or manual replace |
| Split/parse | `split`, regex `match`/`matchAll` |
| Format numbers/dates | `Intl.*` |
| Type-safe string patterns | Template literal types |
| Unicode-safe length | `[...s].length` or `codePointAt` |

That covers the essentials: primitives are immutable, template literals handle interpolation and tagged processing, regex + Unicode-aware methods handle parsing, and TypeScript's template literal types add compile-time string manipulation on top.

```ts
// ❌ Mistake: treating a UTF-16 string as an array of visible characters
function reverseText(input: string): string {
  return input.split("").reverse().join("");
}

console.log(reverseText("a😀b")); // Often produces invalid/garbled output
```

```ts
// ✅ Corrected: iterate by Unicode code points (and, when needed, grapheme clusters)
function reverseText(input: string): string {
  // Array.from respects surrogate pairs (code points), not UTF-16 code units
  return Array.from(input).reverse().join("");
}

console.log(reverseText("a😀b")); // "b😀a"
```

### What was wrong

JavaScript/TypeScript strings are sequences of **UTF-16 code units**, not necessarily visible characters.

- An emoji such as `😀` is stored as **two UTF-16 code units** (a surrogate pair).
- `input.split("")` splits at every UTF-16 code unit, so it separates the surrogate pair into two invalid lone surrogates.
- Reversing and joining then produces malformed text or replacement characters.

### Why the corrected version works

- `Array.from(string)` iterates the string using its iterator, which yields **Unicode code points**, so surrogate pairs stay together.
- For many scripts, code points are enough. For fully correct “user-perceived character” handling (for example, flags, skin-tone modifiers, combining accents, or family emoji), use grapheme segmentation:

```ts
function reverseGraphemes(input: string): string {
  const segmenter = new Intl.Segmenter(undefined, { granularity: "grapheme" });
  return Array.from(segmenter.segment(input), (s) => s.segment)
    .reverse()
    .join("");
}
```

### Practical takeaway

- Do not assume `str.length`, `str[i]`, `split("")`, or simple reversal correspond to visible characters.
- Use code-point-aware APIs (`Array.from`, `for...of`, `codePointAt`) for basic Unicode safety.
- Use `Intl.Segmenter` when correctness must match what a user sees.

## Exercise

**Task:** Implement `analyzeText(text)` in TypeScript that returns:

```ts
{ wordCount: number; sentenceCount: number; topWords: { word: string; count: number }[] }
```

Rules:
- `wordCount`: number of whitespace-separated tokens (ignore empty).
- `sentenceCount`: number of non-empty segments separated by `.`, `!`, or `?`.
- `topWords`: lowercase, punctuation-stripped words (excluding a small stop-word list), counted case-insensitively, returned as the top 5 by frequency descending, then alphabetically ascending.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### 1. Count words
Trim the text, split on `\s+`, and drop empty tokens.

### 2. Count sentences
Split on `/[.!?]+/`, trim each piece, keep non-empty ones. This correctly gives `0` for empty input and `1` for a single unterminated sentence.

### 3. Normalize words
For each token: lowercase it, strip leading/trailing non-word characters, and reject it if it is empty, a stop word, or not a clean `[a-z0-9_]+` token.

### 4. Count with a `Map`
`Map<string, number>` is the idiomatic choice for frequency tables in TypeScript.

### 5. Sort and slice
Sort by count descending, then by word ascending using `localeCompare`, then take the first 5.

---

## Final solution

```ts
const STOP_WORDS = new Set<string>([
  "a", "an", "the", "and", "or", "but", "is", "are", "was", "were",
  "be", "been", "being", "in", "on", "at", "to", "for", "of", "with",
  "as", "by", "from", "it", "its", "this", "that", "these", "those",
  "i", "you", "he", "she", "we", "they", "me", "him", "her", "us", "them",
]);

interface WordStat {
  word: string;
  count: number;
}

interface TextAnalysis {
  wordCount: number;
  sentenceCount: number;
  topWords: WordStat[];
}

function normalizeWord(raw: string): string | null {
  const cleaned = raw.toLowerCase().replace(/^[^\w]+|[^\w]+$/g, "");
  if (!cleaned) return null;
  if (STOP_WORDS.has(cleaned)) return null;
  if (!/^[a-z0-9_]+$/.test(cleaned)) return null;
  return cleaned;
}

export function analyzeText(text: string): TextAnalysis {
  const words = text.trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
  const wordCount = words.length;

  const sentenceCount = text
    .split(/[.!?]+/)
    .map((s) => s.trim())
    .filter(Boolean).length;

  const freq = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const token of words) {
    const word = normalizeWord(token);
    if (!word) continue;
    freq.set(word, (freq.get(word) ?? 0) + 1);
  }

  const topWords: WordStat[] = [...freq.entries()]
    .map(([word, count]) => ({ word, count }))
    .sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count || a.word.localeCompare(b.word))
    .slice(0, 5);

  return { wordCount, sentenceCount, topWords };
}

// Example usage
const sample = `
  TypeScript is great! It is typed. You can write clean, safe code with it.
  With TypeScript, types help catch bugs early.
`;

console.log(JSON.stringify(analyzeText(sample), null, 2));
```

### Example output

```json
{
  "wordCount": 24,
  "sentenceCount": 4,
  "topWords": [
    { "word": "typescript", "count": 3 },
    { "word": "clean", "count": 1 },
    { "word": "code", "count": 1 },
    { "word": "early", "count": 1 },
    { "word": "great", "count": 1 }
  ]
}
```

### Notes
- The regex `/^[^\w]+|[^\w]+$/g` strips punctuation only from the edges, so `don't` remains `don't`-ish but is rejected by the final `[a-z0-9_]+` check; adjust that regex if you want to keep apostrophes.
- Sorting uses `localeCompare` for stable, readable alphabetical ordering.
- All logic is pure and side-effect free, making it easy to unit-test with Jest/Vitest.

## Strings & Text Processing in TypeScript (interview-style)

### 1. Core concepts

- **Strings are immutable** – every “modification” returns a new string.
- **UTF-16 internally** – `length` counts UTF-16 code units, not necessarily user-perceived characters. Surrogate pairs (e.g. emojis) need care; use `Array.from(str)` or spread `[...str]` to iterate code points.
- **Primitive vs object** – always use the primitive `string`; `new String(...)` is an object and causes type/coercion surprises.
- **Template literals** – backticks enable interpolation and multi-line strings; tagged templates allow custom processing.
- **Common APIs** – `slice`, `substring`, `split`, `replace`/`replaceAll`, `match`, `search`, `includes`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `trim`, `padStart`/`padEnd`.
- **Regex** – use flags `g`, `i`, `m`, `u` (unicode), `s` (dotAll). Prefer named groups and `RegExp` constructor when building patterns dynamically.
- **Unicode normalization** – `str.normalize('NFC'|'NFD'|'NFKC'|'NFKD')` to canonicalize accented characters before comparison.
- **Performance** – avoid repeated `+` in hot loops; build an array and `join('')`. For very large text, consider streaming/chunking.

### 2. Typical text-processing pipeline

1. **Normalize** (unicode + whitespace)  
2. **Tokenize** (split into words/lines using Unicode-aware regex)  
3. **Transform** (case, stemming, filtering)  
4. **Aggregate** (counts, positions, replacements)  
5. **Render** (join, truncate, format)

---

### 3. Clean implementation

```ts
/**
 * TextProcessor – a small, immutable, Unicode-aware text utility.
 * All methods return new values; the original text is never mutated.
 */
export class TextProcessor {
  /** Stored text (never mutated). */
  private readonly text: string;

  constructor(text: string) {
    this.text = text;
  }

  /**
   * Normalize Unicode to NFC and collapse whitespace.
   * Great first step before tokenization or comparison.
   */
  normalize(): string {
    return this.text.normalize('NFC').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
  }

  /**
   * Tokenize into words/numbers using Unicode property escapes.
   * Requires the 'u' flag (supported in modern TS/JS targets).
   */
  tokenize(): string[] {
    const normalized = this.normalize();
    // \p{L}=letters, \p{N}=numbers; 'g' + 'u' for global Unicode matching
    return normalized.match(/[\p{L}\p{N}]+/gu) ?? [];
  }

  /**
   * Word frequency map (case-insensitive).
   */
  wordCount(): Record<string, number> {
    const counts: Record<string, number> = {};
    for (const word of this.tokenize()) {
      const key = word.toLowerCase();
      counts[key] = (counts[key] ?? 0) + 1;
    }
    return counts;
  }

  /**
   * Find all start indices of a substring.
   * Uses indexOf in a loop to avoid regex injection and to handle
   * overlapping matches correctly-ish (moves by 1 each time).
   */
  findOccurrences(needle: string, caseInsensitive = false): number[] {
    const haystack = caseInsensitive ? this.text.toLowerCase() : this.text;
    const target = caseInsensitive ? needle.toLowerCase() : needle;

    const positions: number[] = [];
    let idx = haystack.indexOf(target);
    while (idx !== -1) {
      positions.push(idx);
      idx = haystack.indexOf(target, idx + 1);
    }
    return positions;
  }

  /**
   * Replace all occurrences without regex special-character issues.
   * split/join is safer than building a RegExp from user input.
   */
  replaceAll(needle: string, replacement: string): string {
    return this.text.split(needle).join(replacement);
  }

  /**
   * Truncate to maxLength, preferring a word boundary.
   * Uses an ellipsis (default "…") which counts toward maxLength.
   */
  truncate(maxLength: number, ellipsis = '…'): string {
    if (this.text.length <= maxLength) return this.text;

    const cutoff = maxLength - ellipsis.length;
    const slice = this.text.slice(0, cutoff);
    const lastSpace = slice.lastIndexOf(' ');
    const cut = lastSpace > 0 ? slice.slice(0, lastSpace) : slice;

    return cut + ellipsis;
  }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Functional helpers                                                  */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/**
 * Reverse a string correctly for surrogate pairs (emojis, etc.).
 */
export function reverseString(s: string): string {
  // Array.from iterates code points, not UTF-16 code units.
  return Array.from(s).reverse().join('');
}

/**
 * Check palindrome ignoring case and non-alphanumeric characters.
 */
export function isPalindrome(s: string): boolean {
  const cleaned = s.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '');
  return cleaned === reverseString(cleaned);
}

/**
 * Count UTF-16 code units vs code points (useful for length limits).
 */
export function lengthInfo(s: string): { codeUnits: number; codePoints: number } {
  return {
    codeUnits: s.length,
    codePoints: Array.from(s).length,
  };
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Example usage                                                       */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const tp = new TextProcessor('  Hello,   WORLD! Hello.  café  ');

console.log(tp.normalize());        // "Hello, WORLD! Hello. café"
console.log(tp.tokenize());        // ["Hello", "WORLD", "Hello", "café"]
console.log(tp.wordCount());       // { hello: 2, world: 1, café: 1 }
console.log(tp.findOccurrences('Hello', true)); // [2, 16]
console.log(tp.replaceAll('Hello', 'Hi'));      // "  Hi,   WORLD! Hi.  café  "
console.log(tp.truncate(20));      // "Hello, WORLD!…"
console.log(reverseString('a😀b')); // "b😀a"
console.log(isPalindrome('A man, a plan, a canal: Panama')); // true
console.log(lengthInfo('😀'));     // { codeUnits: 2, codePoints: 1 }
```

### 4. Interview talking points

- **Why `split/join` for replaceAll?** Avoids regex escaping bugs and user-input injection.
- **Why Unicode regex (`u` flag)?** `\w` alone misses non-ASCII letters; `\p{L}` is correct.
- **Why `Array.from`?** `split('')` breaks surrogate pairs; `Array.from` respects code points.
- **Normalization before compare** prevents “café” (NFC) vs “café” (NFD) mismatches.
- **Immutability** makes methods easy to test and compose in pipelines.

If you want, I can extend this with streaming large files, locale-aware sorting (`Intl.Collator`), or a small CLI demo.

# Edge Cases in TypeScript String & Text Processing

Below is a thorough tour. Each section explains the trap and shows idiomatic TypeScript that handles it.

---

## 1. `.length` counts UTF-16 code units, not "characters"

```ts
const s = "😀"; // U+1F600, surrogate pair
console.log(s.length);        // 2  (two UTF-16 code units)
console.log([...s].length);   // 1  (one code point)
console.log(Array.from(s).length); // 1

// Wrong: naive slicing can split a surrogate pair
const bad = s.slice(0, 1);   // "\uD83D" lone surrogate — invalid
const good = [...s].slice(0, 1).join(""); // "😀"

// For grapheme clusters (emoji with ZWJ, combining marks) even code points aren't enough:
const family = "👨‍👩‍👧‍👦"; // 1 grapheme, 7 code points
console.log([...family].length); // 7
```

**Rule:** use `[...str]` or `Array.from(str)` for code-point-safe operations; use `Intl.Segmenter` for user-perceived characters (see §3).

---

## 2. Indexing with `.charAt` / `[]` vs iteration

```ts
const text = "a😀b";
console.log(text[1]);        // "\uD83D" (high surrogate) — useless
console.log(text.charAt(1)); // same
for (const ch of text) console.log(ch); // "a", "😀", "b" — code points

// Safe code-point aware helper
function codePointAt(str: string, index: number): number | undefined {
  return str.codePointAt(index);
}
```

---

## 3. Grapheme clusters (user-perceived characters)

Many "characters" are multiple code points: accented letters as combining sequences, emoji with skin tones, ZWJ families, flags.

```ts
const segmenter = new Intl.Segmenter("en", { granularity: "grapheme" });

const samples = ["e\u0301", "👨‍👩‍👧‍👦", "🇺🇸", "👩🏽‍💻"];
for (const s of samples) {
  const segs = [...segmenter.segment(s)];
  console.log(JSON.stringify(s), "=>", segs.length, "grapheme(s)");
}
// "e\u0301" => 1
// family emoji => 1

// Reversing a string safely by grapheme
function reverseGraphemes(s: string): string {
  return [...new Intl.Segmenter().segment(s)]
    .map(x => x.segment)
    .reverse()
    .join("");
}
```

---

## 4. Unicode normalization (NFC / NFD / NFKC / NFKD)

The same visual text can have different byte representations.

```ts
const a = "café";          // U+00E9 precomposed
const b = "cafe\u0301";    // e + combining acute
console.log(a === b);       // false
console.log(a.normalize("NFC") === b.normalize("NFC")); // true

// Dangerous: NFKC changes meaning (fullwidth, ligatures, etc.)
console.log("ﬁ".normalize("NFKC")); // "fi"
console.log("①".normalize("NFKC")); // "1"

// Rule of thumb: normalize to NFC before storing/comparing user input
function canonical(s: string): string {
  return s.normalize("NFC");
}
```

Also handle **case folding** vs `.toLowerCase()`:

```ts
const tr = "İ"; // Turkish dotted capital I
console.log(tr.toLocaleLowerCase("tr")); // "i"
console.log(tr.toLowerCase());           // "i̇" (different!)
```

---

## 5. Locale-sensitive casing and comparison

```ts
// Turkish I problem
console.log("I".toLocaleLowerCase("tr")); // "ı" (dotless i)
console.log("I".toLowerCase());           // "i"

// Sorting
const names = ["Åse", "Zebra", "apple", "Émile"];
console.log(names.sort()); // code-point order (wrong for humans)
console.log(names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, "en", { sensitivity: "base" })));

// Case-insensitive equality that respects locale
function equalsIgnoreCase(a: string, b: string, locale = "en"): boolean {
  return a.localeCompare(b, locale, { sensitivity: "accent" }) === 0;
}
```

---

## 6. Whitespace, invisible characters, and trimming

`String.prototype.trim()` removes a broad set of Unicode whitespace, but regex `\s` historically differed; modern JS `\s` (with `u`) matches Unicode whitespace.

```ts
const s = "\u00A0\u2003hello\u200B\uFEFF"; // NBSP, em space, ZWSP, BOM
console.log(JSON.stringify(s.trim())); // removes leading/trailing Unicode ws incl. NBSP

// But zero-width space (U+200B) is NOT considered whitespace by trim
console.log(s.includes("\u200B")); // true

// Custom cleanup for "invisible" chars
function stripInvisible(s: string): string {
  return s.replace(/[\u200B-\u200D\uFEFF\u2060]/g, "");
}

// Splitting on whitespace robustly
const tokens = "a  b\tc".split(/\s+/u).filter(Boolean);
```

Also beware **non-breaking space** in numeric parsing:

```ts
console.log(Number("1 000"));   // NaN (regular space)
console.log(Number("1\u00A0000")); // NaN (NBSP) — must replace
```

---

## 7. Regular expressions: flags, state, and Unicode

```ts
// Without `u`, astral chars are seen as two code units
console.log(/^.$/.test("😀"));     // false (two code units)
console.log(/^.$/u.test("😀"));    // true

// `v` flag (ES2024) adds set notation & better Unicode properties
// /\p{Script=Latin}/v

// lastIndex statefulness of global regex — a classic bug
const re = /a/g;
console.log(re.test("abc")); // true, lastIndex=1
console.log(re.test("abc")); // false (starts searching at index 1!) 
// Fix: reset or use non-global, or create fresh regex each use.

// Safer: avoid shared mutable regex; or reset lastIndex
function countMatches(s: string, re: RegExp): number {
  const r = new RegExp(re.source, re.flags.includes("g") ? re.flags : re.flags + "g");
  r.lastIndex = 0;
  let n = 0;
  while (r.exec(s)) n++;
  return n;
}
```

Catastrophic backtracking:

```ts
// DANGEROUS on adversarial input
const evil = /^(a+)+$/;
// evil.test("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaX"); // hangs
// Fix: rewrite without nested quantifiers, or use a timeout/worker.
```

Multiline & dotAll:

```ts
const m = "line1\nline2";
console.log(/^line2$/m.test(m)); // true
console.log(/line1.line2/s.test(m)); // true (. matches newline with s flag)
```

---

## 8. String building performance

Strings are immutable; `+` in a hot loop creates O(n²) intermediate garbage.

```ts
// Bad
let out = "";
for (let i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) out += i;

// Good
const parts: string[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) parts.push(String(i));
const out2 = parts.join("");
```

---

## 9. Parsing numbers from strings

```ts
console.log(parseInt("10px"));        // 10  (lenient)
console.log(Number("10px"));          // NaN (strict)
console.log(parseInt("08"));          // 8 (modern: decimal; old engines octal!) 
console.log(parseInt("08", 10));      // always specify radix

console.log(parseFloat("1.2.3"));     // 1.2
console.log(Number(""));              // 0  (surprising!)
console.log(Number("   "));           // 0
console.log(Number(null as any));     // 0

// Safe integer check
function toInt(s: string): number | null {
  if (!/^-?\d+$/.test(s.trim())) return null;
  const n = Number(s);
  return Number.isSafeInteger(n) ? n : null;
}
```

---

## 10. Encoding, BOM, and binary data

```ts
// TextEncoder/Decoder (UTF-8)
const enc = new TextEncoder();
const bytes = enc.encode("héllo"); // Uint8Array
const dec = new TextDecoder("utf-8");
console.log(dec.decode(bytes));

// BOM handling
const withBom = "\uFEFFhello";
console.log(withBom.startsWith("\uFEFF")); // true
function stripBom(s: string): string {
  return s.charCodeAt(0) === 0xFEFF ? s.slice(1) : s;
}

// atob/btoa are Latin-1 only — breaks on Unicode
// Bad:
// btoa("😀") // throws InvalidCharacterError
// Good:
function b64EncodeUnicode(s: string): string {
  return btoa(unescape(encodeURIComponent(s)));
}
function b64DecodeUnicode(s: string): string {
  return decodeURIComponent(escape(atob(s)));
}
```

---

## 11. Template literals & injection

```ts
const userInput = "<script>alert(1)</script>";
// Never interpolate untrusted data into HTML/SQL/shell without escaping
function escapeHtml(s: string): string {
  return s.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => ({
    "&": "&amp;", "<": "&lt;", ">": "&gt;", '"': "&quot;", "'": "&#39;",
  }[c]!));
}

// Tagged templates for safe SQL (example pattern)
function sql(strings: TemplateStringsArray, ...values: unknown[]) {
  // parameterize values instead of concatenating
  return { text: strings.join("?"), values };
}
```

---

## 12. Null / undefined / empty handling

```ts
function greet(name?: string | null): string {
  // nullish coalescing, not || (which drops "" and 0 — though 0 isn't a string)
  const n = name ?? "stranger";
  return `Hello, ${n}`;
}
console.log(greet(""));    // "Hello, "  (empty preserved if intended)
console.log(greet(null));  // "Hello, stranger"

// Optional chaining
const len = someObj?.label?.length ?? 0;

// Template literal coercion: null/undefined become "null"/"undefined" strings!
const x: string | null = null;
console.log(`value=${x}`); // "value=null" — often a bug
```

---

## 13. String methods: `slice` vs `substring` vs `substr`

```ts
const s = "hello";
console.log(s.slice(1, 3));      // "el"
console.log(s.substring(1, 3));  // "el"
console.log(s.slice(-2));        // "lo"  (supports negative)
console.log(s.substring(-2));    // "hello" (negative treated as 0) — trap!
// substr is deprecated; avoid.
```

---

## 14. Comparison & equality gotchas

```ts
// === on strings is code-unit exact; after normalization compare
console.log("café" === "cafe\u0301"); // false (see §4)

// Empty string falsiness
const input = "";
if (!input) { /* runs — maybe not what you want for "provided but empty" */ }

// Locale-aware equality for user-facing comparison
```

---

## 15. JSON serialization of strings

```ts
const obj = { note: "line1\nline2\t\"quoted\"" };
const json = JSON.stringify(obj);
console.log(json); // properly escaped
const parsed = JSON.parse(json);
console.log(parsed.note === obj.note); // true

// Untrusted JSON: validate shape, don't eval
// NEVER: eval(str) or new Function(str)
```

---

## 16. Line endings

```ts
const text = "a\r\nb\rc\nd";
// Normalize CRLF/CR to LF
const normalized = text.replace(/\r\n?/g, "\n");
// Splitting lines robustly
const lines = text.split(/\r\n|\r|\n/);
```

---

## 17. Security: ReDoS and injection checklist

- Anchor regexes or bound input length.
- Avoid nested quantifiers `(a+)+`, `(a*)*`.
- Use `new RegExp` with escaped user input: escape with a helper.
- Sanitize before HTML; use textContent over innerHTML.

```ts
function escapeRegExp(s: string): string {
  return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&");
}
```

---

## Quick checklist

1. Normalize user input to NFC before comparing/storing.
2. Iterate with `for...of` or `[...str]`, never index astral strings with `[]`.
3. Use `Intl.Segmenter` for grapheme-aware UI operations.
4. Specify radix in `parseInt`; prefer `Number()` + validation.
5. Reset `lastIndex` or avoid global regex reuse.
6. Use `localeCompare` / `toLocaleLowerCase` with explicit locale for user-facing text.
7. Strip BOM; handle CRLF; escape HTML/regex.
8. Build long strings with array + `join`.
9. Use `TextEncoder`/`TextDecoder` for bytes; handle `atob`/`btoa` Unicode via encode/decode.
10. Prefer `??` over `||` to preserve `""`.

If you want, I can expand any section (e.g., a full `Intl.Segmenter`-based string utility module, or ReDoS-safe regex patterns).

TypeScript has no built-in `struct` type, so the closest idiomatic implementation is an **immutable class** with `readonly` fields, a private constructor, static factories, `with()` update methods, value equality, and `Object.freeze()`. This gives C-like struct semantics: fixed shape, no mutation, copy-on-update, and value-based comparison.

Here is a complete, dependency-free example.

```ts
// structs.ts

/**
 * Vec2 struct
 */
export class Vec2 {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;

  private constructor(x: number, y: number) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
    Object.freeze(this);
  }

  static create(x: number, y: number): Vec2 {
    if (!Number.isFinite(x) || !Number.isFinite(y)) {
      throw new RangeError("Vec2 components must be finite numbers");
    }
    return new Vec2(x, y);
  }

  static readonly zero: Vec2 = new Vec2(0, 0);

  with(patch: { x?: number; y?: number }): Vec2 {
    return Vec2.create(patch.x ?? this.x, patch.y ?? this.y);
  }

  add(other: Vec2): Vec2 {
    return Vec2.create(this.x + other.x, this.y + other.y);
  }

  subtract(other: Vec2): Vec2 {
    return Vec2.create(this.x - other.x, this.y - other.y);
  }

  scale(scalar: number): Vec2 {
    if (!Number.isFinite(scalar)) {
      throw new RangeError("scalar must be finite");
    }
    return Vec2.create(this.x * scalar, this.y * scalar);
  }

  dot(other: Vec2): number {
    return this.x * other.x + this.y * other.y;
  }

  length(): number {
    return Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);
  }

  equals(other: unknown): other is Vec2 {
    return (
      other instanceof Vec2 &&
      other.x === this.x &&
      other.y === this.y
    );
  }

  toString(): string {
    return `Vec2(${this.x}, ${this.y})`;
  }

  toJSON(): { x: number; y: number } {
    return { x: this.x, y: this.y };
  }
}

/**
 * Rect struct, composed of Vec2 structs
 */
export class Rect {
  readonly origin: Vec2;
  readonly size: Vec2;

  private constructor(origin: Vec2, size: Vec2) {
    this.origin = origin;
    this.size = size;
    Object.freeze(this);
  }

  static create(origin: Vec2, size: Vec2): Rect {
    if (size.x < 0 || size.y < 0) {
      throw new RangeError("Rect size must be non-negative");
    }
    return new Rect(origin, size);
  }

  with(patch: { origin?: Vec2; size?: Vec2 }): Rect {
    return Rect.create(
      patch.origin ?? this.origin,
      patch.size ?? this.size
    );
  }

  area(): number {
    return this.size.x * this.size.y;
  }

  contains(point: Vec2): boolean {
    return (
      point.x >= this.origin.x &&
      point.y >= this.origin.y &&
      point.x < this.origin.x + this.size.x &&
      point.y < this.origin.y + this.size.y
    );
  }

  equals(other: unknown): other is Rect {
    return (
      other instanceof Rect &&
      this.origin.equals(other.origin) &&
      this.size.equals(other.size)
    );
  }

  toString(): string {
    return `Rect(origin=${this.origin.toString()}, size=${this.size.toString()})`;
  }

  toJSON(): { origin: { x: number; y: number }; size: { x: number; y: number } } {
    return {
      origin: this.origin.toJSON(),
      size: this.size.toJSON(),
    };
  }
}

function main(): void {
  const p = Vec2.create(3, 4);
  const q = p.with({ x: 0 });

  console.log("p =", p.toString());
  console.log("length =", p.length());
  console.log("dot =", p.dot(Vec2.create(1, 2)));
  console.log("q equals (0,4):", q.equals(Vec2.create(0, 4)));

  const r = Rect.create(Vec2.zero, Vec2.create(2, 3));
  console.log("rect =", r.toString());
  console.log("area =", r.area());
  console.log("contains (1,1):", r.contains(Vec2.create(1, 1)));
  console.log("contains (5,5):", r.contains(Vec2.create(5, 5)));

  // Immutability: frozen object should reject assignment in strict mode.
  try {
    (p as any).x = 999;
    console.log("ERROR: mutation unexpectedly succeeded");
  } catch (error) {
    console.log("mutation rejected:", error instanceof TypeError);
  }

  // Serialization
  console.log("JSON =", JSON.stringify({ point: p, rect: r }));

  // Value equality
  console.log(
    "value equality:",
    Vec2.create(1, 2).equals(Vec2.create(1, 2))
  );
}

main();
```

