## Two-pointer technique in TypeScript

### The idea

Instead of nested loops (often **O(n²)**), you maintain **two indices (pointers)** into a sequence and move them based on a condition. Each pointer only moves forward (or they converge), so the whole scan is **O(n)** (or **O(n log n)** if sorting first).

Common shapes:

| Shape | Pointers | Typical use |
|-------|----------|-------------|
| **Converging** (opposite ends) | `left = 0`, `right = n-1`, move inward | Sorted two-sum, palindrome, container with most water |
| **Fast/slow** (same direction) | `slow`, `fast` both start at 0; `fast` advances faster | Remove duplicates, partition, cycle detection |
| **Sliding window** | `left`, `right` both advance; window shrinks/grows | Substring problems, min/max subarray |

### Basic syntax patterns

```ts
// Converging
let left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
  // use arr[left], arr[right]
  if (condition) left++;
  else right--;
}

// Fast / slow
let slow = 0;
for (let fast = 0; fast < arr.length; fast++) {
  if (keep(arr[fast])) {
    arr[slow] = arr[fast];
    slow++;
  }
}
// result length = slow

// Swap in place
[arr[left], arr[right]] = [arr[right], arr[left]];
```

