### Key takeaways

| Concept | Behavior in TS/JS | How to get value semantics |
|---|---|---|
| Primitives | Value semantics (copied) | Free — just assign |
| Objects | Reference semantics (shared) | `deepClone` for one-off copies |
| Immutable state | Not built-in | `Value<T>` wrapper: store frozen clone, updates return new instance |
| Equality | `===` is reference equality | `deepEqual` / `Value.equals` for structural equality |

### Notes & caveats

- `deepClone` handles `Date`, `RegExp`, `Map`, `Set`, arrays, and plain objects. It does **not** clone functions, class instances with methods, `WeakMap`/`WeakSet`, or DOM nodes — those need custom handling.
- `Object.freeze` is shallow, so `deepFreeze` recurses. Note `Map`/`Set` mutation is not prevented by `freeze` (it only freezes the object's own properties), but in this design we never expose mutable handles — `get()` returns the already-frozen object, and updates go through `set`/`update`, which clone first.
- For production, prefer established libraries: **Immer** (copy-on-write proxies), **immer** + React, or **Immutable.js** / **Immer** for persistent data structures. The pattern above mirrors how those work under the hood.

In TypeScript, **value vs reference semantics** is the same as in JavaScript at runtime: **primitives are values**, **objects are references**. TypeScript adds compile-time annotations, but it does not change how data is copied or shared.

## 1. The idea

### Value semantics: primitives

Primitive values are copied **by value**. Assigning or passing a primitive creates an independent copy.

```ts
let a = 10;
let b = a; // b gets a copy of 10

b = 20;

console.log(a); // 10
console.log(b); // 20
```

Primitives in JS/TS:

- `number`
- `string`
- `boolean`
- `null`
- `undefined`
- `symbol`
- `bigint`

Strings are immutable, so “mutating” a string always creates a new string:

```ts
let s = "hello";
let t = s;

t = t.toUpperCase();

console.log(s); // "hello"
console.log(t); // "HELLO"
```

### Reference semantics: objects

Objects are copied **by reference**. The variable holds a pointer to the object in memory, not the object itself.

```ts
const user = { name: "Ada", age: 36 };
const alias = user; // same object reference

alias.age = 37;

console.log(user.age); // 37
console.log(alias === user); // true
```

This applies to:

- plain objects
- arrays
- functions
- `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `RegExp`, etc.
- class instances

## 2. The syntax and common patterns

### Reassignment vs mutation

```ts
let arr = [1, 2, 3];

// Reassignment: creates a new array binding
arr = [4, 5, 6];

// Mutation: changes the shared object
const shared = [1, 2, 3];
const view = shared;

view.push(4);

console.log(shared); // [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

`const` prevents reassignment of the binding, **not** mutation of the object:

```ts
const obj = { x: 1 };
obj.x = 2;        // allowed
// obj = { x: 3 }; // error: Cannot assign to 'obj' because it is a constant
```

### Passing to functions

Primitives are passed by value; objects are passed by reference.

```ts
function increment(n: number) {
  n += 1;
  return n;
}

let value = 5;
console.log(increment(value)); // 6
console.log(value);            // 5


function addItem(arr: number[]) {
  arr.push(99); // mutates shared array
}

const nums = [1, 2];
addItem(nums);
console.log(nums); // [1, 2, 99]
```

To avoid mutation, pass a copy or return a new value.

### Defensive copying with spread

Spread creates a **shallow copy**.

```ts
const original = { name: "Ada", skills: ["math"] };
const copy = { ...original };

copy.name = "Grace";
copy.skills.push("logic");

console.log(original.name);   // "Ada"
console.log(original.skills); // ["math", "logic"]  // nested object shared
```

For arrays:

```ts
const a = [1, 2, 3];
const b = [...a];

b.push(4);

console.log(a); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log(b); // [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

### Deep copying

For nested data, use `structuredClone` (modern browsers/Node) or a deep-clone utility.

```ts
const original = {
  user: { name: "Ada" },
  tags: ["math", "logic"],
};

const copy = structuredClone(original);

copy.user.name = "Grace";
copy.tags.push("ai");

console.log(original.user.name); // "Ada"
console.log(original.tags);      // ["math", "logic"]
```

`JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` works for JSON-safe data but loses `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `undefined`, functions, etc.

## 3. TypeScript-specific compile-time tools

TypeScript can express intent, but these are erased at runtime.

### `readonly`

```ts
type User = {
  readonly id: number;
  name: string;
};

const u: User = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };
// u.id = 2; // compile-time error
u.name = "Grace"; // allowed
```

`readonly` prevents reassignment of that property, but nested objects can still be mutated unless also readonly.

### `Readonly<T>` and `ReadonlyArray<T>`

```ts
const point: Readonly<{ x: number; y: number }> = { x: 1, y: 2 };
// point.x = 3; // error

const nums: ReadonlyArray<number> = [1, 2, 3];
// nums.push(4); // error
```

### `as const`

```ts
const config = {
  host: "localhost",
  port: 3000,
} as const;

// config.port = 4000; // error
```

This makes properties deeply `readonly` at the type level, but runtime mutation is still possible if someone casts around it.

### Deep readonly is not built in

TypeScript has no standard `DeepReadonly` in the standard library. You can define one:

```ts
type DeepReadonly<T> = T extends (infer U)[]
  ? ReadonlyArray<DeepReadonly<U>>
  : T extends object
  ? { readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepReadonly<T[K]> }
  : T;
```

## 4. Typical use cases

### React / state management

Prefer immutable updates so change detection works.

```ts
type State = { count: number; items: string[] };

function reducer(state: State, action: { type: "inc" } | { type: "add"; item: string }) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "inc":
      return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };
    case "add":
      return { ...state, items: [...state.items, action.item] };
  }
}
```

### API boundaries

Clone or validate input before mutating it.

```ts
function normalizeUser(input: { name: string; email: string }) {
  const user = { ...input };
  user.email = user.email.trim().toLowerCase();
  return user;
}
```

### Caching and memoization

Objects used as cache keys need stable identity or serialized keys.

```ts
const cache = new Map<string, number>();

function compute(key: string) {
  if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key)!;
  const result = key.length * 2;
  cache.set(key, result);
  return result;
}
```

### Avoiding accidental shared mutable state

```ts
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return {
    increment() {
      count += 1;
      return count;
    },
    get() {
      return count;
    },
  };
}

const c1 = makeCounter();
const c2 = makeCounter();

c1.increment();
console.log(c1.get()); // 1
console.log(c2.get()); // 0
```

Here, closure variables give each instance private state. The returned object is a reference, but `count` is not directly exposed.

## 5. Common pitfalls

### Nested mutation through a copy

```ts
const a = { nested: { value: 1 } };
const b = { ...a };

b.nested.value = 2;

console.log(a.nested.value); // 2
```

Fix: clone nested values or use immutable updates.

### Equality checks

```ts
const x = { id: 1 };
const y = { id: 1 };

console.log(x === y); // false, different references
console.log(x.id === y.id); // true
```

For arrays/objects, `===` checks identity, not contents.

### `const` does not mean immutable

```ts
const arr = [1, 2];
arr.push(3); // allowed
```

Use `ReadonlyArray`, `Object.freeze`, or disciplined immutable patterns if you need stronger guarantees.

### `Object.freeze` is shallow

```ts
const obj = Object.freeze({ nested: { value: 1 } });
obj.nested.value = 2; // still allowed at runtime
```

## 6. Practical rules of thumb

1. Treat primitives as safe independent values.
2. Treat objects/arrays as shared references.
3. Use `const` for bindings, but remember it does not freeze objects.
4. Use spread for shallow copies; `structuredClone` for deep copies.
5. Prefer immutable updates in state-heavy code.
6. Use TypeScript `readonly` and `as const` to catch accidental mutation at compile time.
7. Be explicit when a function mutates its argument; otherwise copy first.

### Example: mutation-safe function

```ts
function appendImmutable(items: readonly string[], item: string): string[] {
  return [...items, item];
}

const original = ["a", "b"];
const next = appendImmutable(original, "c");

console.log(original); // ["a", "b"]
console.log(next);    // ["a", "b", "c"]
```

This is the idiomatic TypeScript/JavaScript style: keep data flow explicit and avoid hidden shared mutation.

## Typical mistake: mutating a caller’s array in place

```ts
function appendAndSort(items: number[]): number[] {
  items.push(999);              // mutates the caller's array
  items.sort((a, b) => a - b); // also mutates in place
  return items;
}

const data = [3, 1, 2];
const sorted = appendAndSort(data);

console.log(sorted); // [1, 2, 3, 999]
console.log(data);   // [1, 2, 3, 999]  ← original was changed unexpectedly
```

### Corrected version

```ts
function appendAndSort(items: readonly number[]): number[] {
  const copy = [...items];      // create a new array (shallow copy is enough for primitives)
  copy.push(999);
  copy.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  return copy;
}

const data = [3, 1, 2];
const sorted = appendAndSort(data);

console.log(sorted); // [1, 2, 3, 999]
console.log(data);   // [3, 1, 2]  ← original stays unchanged
```

### What was wrong and why

- In JavaScript/TypeScript, **objects and arrays are passed by reference** (more precisely, the value of the reference is passed). So when `appendAndSort` calls `items.push(...)`, it is modifying the **same array object** that the caller passed in.
- Primitives such as `number`, `string`, and `boolean` are passed **by value**, so reassigning a parameter inside the function does not affect the caller. This asymmetry often tricks developers into thinking objects are copied too.
- The fix is to make the function treat its input as **immutable**: accept `readonly number[]` (which prevents mutating methods like `push` at compile time) and explicitly create a copy with `[...items]` or `items.slice()` before modifying.
- Note: this copy is **shallow**. If the array contains objects, the inner objects are still shared. For deep immutability you need a deep clone (e.g., `structuredClone`) or immutable data structures.

### Extra pitfall: shallow copies don’t protect nested data

```ts
const user = { name: 'Alice', roles: ['admin'] };

const copy = { ...user };          // shallow copy
copy.roles.push('editor');

console.log(user.roles);           // ['admin', 'editor'] ← mutated!
```

To avoid this, copy nested structures explicitly:

```ts
const copy = { ...user, roles: [...user.roles] };
```

## Exercise

You are given two utility functions. Both currently **mutate their inputs**, which can cause surprising bugs for callers.

```ts
interface Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

// 1) Mutates the original points in place
function shiftPoints(points: Point[], dx: number, dy: number): Point[] {
  for (const p of points) {
    p.x += dx;
    p.y += dy;
  }
  return points;
}

// 2) Mutates the original number array because Array.prototype.sort is in-place
function topScores(scores: number[]): number[] {
  return scores.sort((a, b) => b - a).slice(0, 3);
}
```

**Task:** Rewrite both functions so they:

1. Do **not** mutate the caller's `points` or `scores`.
2. Return new arrays containing the transformed values.
3. Preserve correct TypeScript types.
4. Make it obvious to callers that the inputs are treated as read-only.

---

## Step-by-step solution

### Step 1: Recognize value vs. reference semantics

- **Primitives** (`number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`) are passed **by value**. Reassigning a parameter like `x += dx` where `x` is a local number does not affect the caller.
- **Objects and arrays** are passed **by reference**. The parameter holds a reference to the same underlying object/array. Mutating a property (`p.x += dx`) or calling an in-place method (`scores.sort(...)`) changes the caller's data.

So in `shiftPoints`, `p` is a reference to an element of the caller's array; mutating `p.x` mutates the caller's point. In `topScores`, `scores.sort(...)` rearranges the caller's array.

### Step 2: Make inputs explicitly read-only

Use `ReadonlyArray<T>` (or `readonly T[]`) for array parameters. This tells TypeScript and the reader: "this function must not mutate this array."

```ts
function shiftPoints(points: readonly Point[], dx: number, dy: number): Point[] { ... }
function topScores(scores: readonly number[]): number[] { ... }
```

For object elements, you can also use `Readonly<Point>` if you want to forbid property writes on the elements:

```ts
function shiftPoints(points: readonly Readonly<Point>[], dx: number, dy: number): Point[] { ... }
```

This makes accidental mutation a compile-time error.

### Step 3: Create new objects for `shiftPoints`

Instead of mutating each point, build a new `Point` for each input. `Array.prototype.map` is ideal because it returns a new array and never mutates the source.

```ts
function shiftPoints(points: readonly Point[], dx: number, dy: number): Point[] {
  return points.map((p) => ({
    x: p.x + dx,
    y: p.y + dy,
  }));
}
```

Notes:

- `map` allocates a new array and new point objects, so the original array and points are untouched.
- If `Point` had nested objects, you would need a deeper copy; here a shallow copy of `{ x, y }` is sufficient.

### Step 4: Create a copy before sorting for `topScores`

`sort` mutates in place, so first copy the array. Use spread syntax or `slice()`.

```ts
function topScores(scores: readonly number[]): number[] {
  return [...scores].sort((a, b) => b - a).slice(0, 3);
}
```

Notes:

- `[...scores]` creates a new array with the same primitive values (numbers are copied by value, so no aliasing issue).
- `sort` mutates the copy, not the original.
- `slice` returns a new array, so chaining is safe.

### Step 5: (Optional) Add defensive copies at boundaries

If your functions are part of a public API and you cannot trust callers, copying inputs is already enough because you never mutate them. You do not need to copy outputs unless you want to prevent callers from mutating your returned arrays; but returning fresh arrays from `map`/`slice` already gives them their own arrays.

---

## Final solution

```ts
interface Point {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

// Non-mutating: returns new array of new points
function shiftPoints(
  points: readonly Point[],
  dx: number,
  dy: number
): Point[] {
  return points.map((p) => ({
    x: p.x + dx,
    y: p.y + dy,
  }));
}

// Non-mutating: copies first, then sorts the copy
function topScores(scores: readonly number[]): number[] {
  return [...scores].sort((a, b) => b - a).slice(0, 3);
}

// --- Demo / sanity checks ---
const originalPoints: Point[] = [
  { x: 1, y: 2 },
  { x: 3, y: 4 },
];
const shifted = shiftPoints(originalPoints, 10, 20);

console.log("originalPoints:", originalPoints);
// [{ x: 1, y: 2 }, { x: 3, y: 4 }]  (unchanged)

console.log("shifted:", shifted);
// [{ x: 11, y: 22 }, { x: 13, y: 24 }]

const originalScores = [5, 1, 9, 3, 7];
const top = topScores(originalScores);

console.log("originalScores:", originalScores);
// [5, 1, 9, 3, 7] (unchanged)

console.log("top:", top);
// [9, 7, 5]
```

### Key takeaways

| Concept | Value semantics example | Reference semantics example |
|---|---|---|
| Primitives | `const a = b + 1` doesn't change `b` | — |
| Objects/arrays | — | `arr.push(x)` changes caller's array |
| Safe pattern | Compute new primitive | `map`, spread + `sort` on copy, `slice` |
| Type hint | `number` | `readonly Point[]`, `Readonly<Point>` |

Use `readonly` in signatures to document non-mutation, and prefer non-mutating array methods (`map`, `filter`, `slice`, spread) over in-place ones (`sort`, `push`, `splice`) when you need to preserve inputs.

## Value vs. Reference Semantics in TypeScript

### Core idea

- **Value semantics (primitives):** `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint`.  
  Assignment copies the *value*. Two variables are independent; mutating one cannot affect the other.

- **Reference semantics (objects):** Objects, arrays, functions, dates, maps, sets.  
  Assignment copies the *reference* (pointer) to the same underlying storage. Mutating through one variable is visible through every other reference to that object.

- **Equality consequences:** `===` compares identity for objects (same reference), but value for primitives. Two distinct objects with identical contents are **not** `===`.

- **Pitfalls in practice:** shared mutable state, accidental mutation in reducers/state updates, React/Redux stale data, shallow copies that still share nested references.

- **Mitigations:** defensive cloning, immutable updates (`{ ...obj, field }`), structural sharing (Immer), `readonly` types, `Object.freeze` (shallow), `structuredClone` for deep cloning.

---

### Clean, commented implementation

Below is a compact module that (a) demonstrates the semantics, and (b) provides reusable helpers for defensive copying, deep equality, and immutable updates — the “core” you reach for in real codebases.

```ts
// value-vs-reference.ts
// Run with: npx ts-node value-vs-reference.ts  (or compile with tsc)

// ---------- 1. Primitive value semantics ----------

function valueSemanticsDemo(): void {
  let a = 42;
  let b = a;       // copy of the value
  b = 100;         // rebinds only b
  // a is still 42 — independent storage
  console.assert(a === 42, "primitive assignment must not affect source");
  console.assert(b === 100, "rebinding target must not affect source");
}

// ---------- 2. Object reference semantics ----------

interface Point { x: number; y: number }

function referenceSemanticsDemo(): void {
  const p1: Point = { x: 1, y: 2 };
  const p2 = p1;          // copy of the REFERENCE, not the object
  p2.x = 999;             // mutation visible through p1
  console.assert(p1.x === 999, "shared reference must reflect mutation");

  // Rebinding p2 does NOT affect p1 (reference itself is a value)
  const p3 = p1;
  // @ts-expect-error (demo: pretending reassignment) — in real code use let
  // p3 = { x: 0, y: 0 };
}

// ---------- 3. Defensive copying ----------

/** Shallow clone: copies top-level keys; nested objects are still shared. */
function shallowClone<T extends object>(obj: T): T {
  return { ...obj };
}

/** Deep clone using the platform's structured clone (handles cycles, Map, Set, Date, etc.). */
function deepClone<T>(value: T): T {
  // structuredClone is available in modern Node/browser runtimes.
  // Fallback for JSON-safe data only:
  if (typeof structuredClone === "function") return structuredClone(value);
  return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(value)) as T;
}

function cloningDemo(): void {
  const original = { a: { b: 1 }, arr: [1, 2] };
  const shallow = shallowClone(original);
  const deep = deepClone(original);

  shallow.a.b = 7;       // leaks into original (shared nested ref)
  console.assert(original.a.b === 7, "shallow clone shares nested refs");

  deep.arr.push(3);      // does NOT leak
  console.assert(original.arr.length === 2, "deep clone is independent");
}

// ---------- 4. Deep equality (structural) ----------

function isPlainObject(v: unknown): v is Record<string, unknown> {
  return typeof v === "object" && v !== null && !Array.isArray(v);
}

function deepEqual(a: unknown, b: unknown): boolean {
  if (a === b) return true;                 // covers primitives + same ref
  if (typeof a !== "object" || typeof b !== "object" || a === null || b === null)
    return false;
  if (Array.isArray(a) !== Array.isArray(b)) return false;

  const keysA = Object.keys(a as object);
  const keysB = Object.keys(b as object);
  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;

  for (const k of keysA) {
    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(b, k)) return false;
    if (!deepEqual((a as Record<string, unknown>)[k], (b as Record<string, unknown>)[k]))
      return false;
  }
  return true;
}

// ---------- 5. Immutable updates (value-semantics style) ----------

/** Produce a new object with a path updated; leaves input untouched. */
function setPath<T extends object>(obj: T, path: string[], value: unknown): T {
  if (path.length === 0) return obj;
  const [head, ...rest] = path;
  const clone: Record<string, unknown> = { ...(obj as Record<string, unknown>) };

  if (rest.length === 0) {
    clone[head] = value;
  } else {
    const child = (obj as Record<string, unknown>)[head];
    clone[head] = setPath((child ?? {}) as object, rest, value);
  }
  return clone as T;
}

function immutableUpdateDemo(): void {
  const state = { user: { name: "Ada", tags: ["math"] } };
  const next = setPath(state, ["user", "name"], "Grace");
  console.assert(state.user.name === "Ada", "original must be untouched");
  console.assert(next.user.name === "Grace", "new tree must reflect update");
  console.assert(next.user.tags === state.user.tags, "unchanged branches may be shared");
}

// ---------- 6. Readonly guard (compile-time only) ----------

function readonlyDemo(): void {
  const cfg: Readonly<{ host: string; port: number }> = { host: "localhost", port: 3000 };
  // cfg.port = 80; // ❌ compile error: cannot assign to readonly property
  void cfg;
}

// ---------- Entry point ----------

function main(): void {
  valueSemanticsDemo();
  referenceSemanticsDemo();
  cloningDemo();
  immutableUpdateDemo();
  readonlyDemo();

  // Equality demo
  const a = { x: 1 };
  const b = { x: 1 };
  console.assert(a !== b, "distinct objects are not ===");
  console.assert(deepEqual(a, b), "deepEqual sees structural equality");

  console.log("All assertions passed ✅");
}

main();
```

### Interview talking points to pair with the code

1. **`===` vs. structural equality** — objects compare by identity; explain why `{} === {}` is `false`.
2. **Shared mutation** — why `const arr2 = arr1; arr2.push(x)` mutates `arr1`; contrast with `const arr2 = [...arr1]`.
3. **Shallow vs. deep clone** — `{...obj}` only fixes the first level; nested refs remain shared.
4. **Immutability in state management** — React relies on new references to trigger re-renders; mutating in place breaks change detection.
5. **`readonly` is compile-time** — it prevents reassignment but not runtime mutation of nested objects; pair with cloning/freezing for real safety.
6. **Performance trade-off** — deep cloning is O(n) and breaks identity; structural sharing (Immer) keeps unchanged subtrees reference-equal.

# Value vs. Reference Semantics in TypeScript — Edge Cases

## 1. Primitives are values, everything else is a reference

```ts
let a = 5;
let b = a;        // copy of the value
b = 10;
console.log(a);   // 5 — unaffected

const arr = [1, 2];
const arr2 = arr; // same reference
arr2.push(3);
console.log(arr); // [1, 2, 3] — mutated through alias
```

**Edge case:** `string`, `number`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint` are value types. `object`, `array`, `function`, `Date`, `RegExp`, `Map`, `Set` are references.

## 2. Shallow copy is not deep copy

```ts
const user = { name: "Ada", address: { city: "London" } };
const copy = { ...user };          // shallow
copy.address.city = "Paris";
console.log(user.address.city);    // "Paris" — nested still shared

// Fix: structuredClone (modern)
const deep = structuredClone(user);
deep.address.city = "Berlin";
console.log(user.address.city);    // "Paris" — independent
```

**Edge cases for cloning:**
- `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` drops `undefined`, functions, `Date` (becomes string), `Map`/`Set` (becomes `{}`), and throws on circular refs or `BigInt`.
- `structuredClone` cannot clone functions, DOM nodes, or `Error` with non-enumerable props; handles `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `ArrayBuffer`, circular refs.

## 3. Default parameters share one mutable object across calls

```ts
function append(item: string, list: string[] = []) {
  list.push(item);
  return list;
}
console.log(append("a")); // ["a"]
console.log(append("b")); // ["b"]? NO — ["a", "b"]? Actually each call gets fresh default? 

// Wait: default params are evaluated per call in TS/JS, so each gets fresh [].
// But if you do:
const shared: string[] = [];
function f(x = shared) { x.push("x"); }
f(); f();
console.log(shared); // ["x", "x"] — aliasing bug
```

**Edge case:** default parameters are re-evaluated each call (safe), but a *mutable default defined outside* is shared.

## 4. Function arguments are passed by value of the reference

```ts
function reassign(obj: { n: number }) {
  obj = { n: 99 };   // rebinds local param only
}
const o = { n: 1 };
reassign(o);
console.log(o.n);    // 1 — caller unaffected

function mutate(obj: { n: number }) {
  obj.n = 99;        // mutates shared object
}
mutate(o);
console.log(o.n);    // 99
```

## 5. `const` vs `readonly` — different guarantees

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
arr.push(4);          // OK — const only prevents rebinding the variable
// arr = [5];         // Error

interface Point { readonly x: number; }
const p: Point = { x: 1 };
// p.x = 2;           // Error at compile time
// But at runtime, readonly is erased:
(p as any).x = 2;     // works — no runtime protection
```

**Edge case:** `readonly` is compile-time only. For runtime immutability use `Object.freeze` (shallow) or `Object.freeze` + recursive freeze.

## 6. `Object.freeze` is shallow

```ts
const cfg = Object.freeze({ db: { host: "localhost" } });
cfg.db.host = "evil";   // succeeds! nested not frozen
// cfg.db = {} as any;  // throws in strict mode

function deepFreeze<T>(o: T): T {
  Object.freeze(o);
  for (const k of Object.keys(o as any)) {
    const v = (o as any)[k];
    if (v && typeof v === "object" && !Object.isFrozen(v)) deepFreeze(v);
  }
  return o;
}
```

## 7. Closure capturing mutable references (classic loop bug)

```ts
const fns: (() => number)[] = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  fns.push(() => i);   // all capture same `i`
}
console.log(fns.map(f => f())); // [3, 3, 3]

// Fix: let (block scope) or IIFE
const fns2: (() => number)[] = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  fns2.push(() => i);
}
console.log(fns2.map(f => f())); // [0, 1, 2]
```

## 8. `Date` is mutable by reference

```ts
const d = new Date("2024-01-01");
const d2 = d;          // alias
d2.setDate(15);
console.log(d.toISOString()); // changed!

// Comparison is by reference, not value
new Date("2024-01-01") === new Date("2024-01-01"); // false
// Use getTime() for value equality
```

## 9. `NaN` — value that is never equal to itself

```ts
NaN === NaN;            // false
Object.is(NaN, NaN);    // true
[NaN].includes(NaN);    // true (uses SameValueZero)
[NaN].indexOf(NaN);     // -1 (uses strict equality)
```

## 10. `-0` vs `0`

```ts
0 === -0;               // true
Object.is(0, -0);       // false
1 / 0;                  // Infinity
1 / -0;                 // -Infinity
```

## 11. `undefined` vs `null`

```ts
let x;                  // undefined
let y = null;           // null (explicit absence)
x == y;                 // true (loose)
x === y;                // false
```

## 12. `String` object vs string primitive

```ts
const s1 = "hi";
const s2 = new String("hi");
s1 === s2;              // false
typeof s1;              // "string"
typeof s2;              // "object"
// Avoid `new String/Number/Boolean`
```

## 13. `Symbol` uniqueness

```ts
Symbol("x") === Symbol("x");       // false
Symbol.for("x") === Symbol.for("x"); // true (global registry)
```

## 14. `Map`/`Set` use SameValueZero for keys (reference for objects)

```ts
const m = new Map();
const key = { id: 1 };
m.set(key, "v");
m.get({ id: 1 });      // undefined — different reference
m.get(key);            // "v"

// Arrays as keys: same reference issue
const arr = [1, 2];
const s = new Set([arr]);
s.has([1, 2]);         // false
```

## 15. Array mutation methods vs non-mutating

```ts
const a = [3, 1, 2];
a.sort();              // mutates a
const b = a.slice().sort(); // non-mutating copy (pre-ES2023)
// ES2023:
const c = a.toSorted(); // non-mutating
const d = a.toReversed();
```

**Edge case:** `sort`, `reverse`, `splice`, `push`, `pop`, `shift`, `unshift`, `fill`, `copyWithin` mutate; `slice`, `map`, `filter`, `concat`, `flat` don't. (TS types don't always reflect this — `ReadonlyArray` does.)

## 16. `ReadonlyArray` and `as const`

```ts
const arr = [1, 2, 3] as const;  // readonly tuple
// arr.push(4);                   // Error
// arr[0] = 9;                    // Error

const obj = { a: 1 } as const;
// obj.a = 2;                     // Error
```

## 17. React / state: mutation doesn't trigger updates

```tsx
// Bad
state.items.push(newItem);
setState(state);          // same reference → no re-render

// Good
setState(prev => ({ ...prev, items: [...prev.items, newItem] }));
```

## 18. Closure over mutable state in callbacks

```ts
let count = 0;
const inc = () => count++;
// Multiple closures share `count` by reference through scope
```

## 19. `RegExp` with `g` flag mutates `lastIndex`

```ts
const re = /a/g;
re.test("abc");    // true, lastIndex = 1
re.test("abc");    // false (starts at index 1) — stateful!
// Fix: remove `g` or reset lastIndex = 0
```

## 20. Typed arrays share underlying `ArrayBuffer`

```ts
const buf = new ArrayBuffer(8);
const u8 = new Uint8Array(buf);
const i32 = new Int32Array(buf);
u8[0] = 255;
console.log(i32[0]); // 255 (shared memory view)
```

## 21. `Proxy` can fake value/reference semantics

```ts
const target = { x: 1 };
const p = new Proxy(target, {
  get(t, k) { return (t as any)[k] * 2; }
});
p.x;  // 2, but target.x is 1 — virtual reference
```

## 22. Circular references & serialization

```ts
const a: any = {};
a.self = a;
JSON.stringify(a);              // throws TypeError
structuredClone(a);             // works
```

## 23. Weak references don't keep objects alive

```ts
let obj: any = { data: "big" };
const wr = new WeakRef(obj);
obj = null;                      // now eligible for GC
// wr.deref() may return undefined later
```

## 24. `instanceof` vs `constructor` (cross-realm)

```ts
const arr = [];
arr instanceof Array;            // true
// In iframe, Array !== iframe's Array
Object.prototype.toString.call(arr); // "[object Array]" — more reliable
```

## 25. Type assertions lie about runtime

```ts
interface User { id: number; name: string; }
const u = JSON.parse('{"id":1}') as User;
u.name;  // undefined at runtime, but TS thinks string — reference exists but property missing
```

## Summary checklist

| Pitfall | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Nested mutation after spread | Shallow copy | `structuredClone` / deep clone |
| Loop closure capturing index | `var` / shared scope | `let` / IIFE |
| `NaN` comparisons | IEEE 754 | `Object.is` / `Number.isNaN` |
| Date mutation | Mutable object | clone with `new Date(d)` |
| Default mutable arg | Shared reference | factory default or clone inside |
| Frozen but nested mutable | Shallow freeze | recursive freeze |
| State not updating (React) | Same reference | immutable update |
| RegExp stateful | `lastIndex` mutation | avoid `g` or reset |
| Typed array aliasing | Shared buffer | copy buffer |

