### Step 5 — Optional: an async/await-like helper (educational)

You can wrap a goroutine in a Future-like channel, but this is usually overkill in Go:

```go
func AsyncFetch(ctx context.Context, url string) <-chan string {
	ch := make(chan string, 1)
	go func() {
		defer close(ch)
		req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, nil)
		resp, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
		if err != nil {
			ch <- "ERROR: " + err.Error()
			return
		}
		defer resp.Body.Close()
		b, _ := io.ReadAll(resp.Body)
		ch <- string(b)
	}()
	return ch
}

// "await"
body := <-AsyncFetch(ctx, "https://example.com")
```

This shows the mechanical mapping, but idiomatic Go code tends to use channels directly or `errgroup` rather than inventing Future types.

