## Using the standard library

Go’s `sort` package provides optimized, tested binary search.

### `sort.SearchInts` / `sort.SearchFloat64s` / `sort.SearchStrings`

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func main() {
	a := []int{1, 3, 5, 7, 9}

	// insertion index of 5
	i := sort.SearchInts(a, 5)
	fmt.Println(i) // 2

	// exact check
	if i < len(a) && a[i] == 5 {
		fmt.Println("found at", i)
	}

	// not present: returns insertion point
	fmt.Println(sort.SearchInts(a, 4)) // 1
}
```

### `sort.Search` (custom types / predicates)

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

type User struct {
	ID   int
	Name string
}

func main() {
	users := []User{{1, "alice"}, {3, "bob"}, {5, "carol"}}
	target := 3

	i := sort.Search(len(users), func(k int) bool {
		return users[k].ID >= target
	})

	if i < len(users) && users[i].ID == target {
		fmt.Println("found:", users[i].Name) // bob
	} else {
		fmt.Println("not found, insert at", i)
	}
}
```

### Searching a sorted slice of structs with `sort.Interface`

If you implement `sort.Interface`, you can use `sort.Search` with a custom less function, or sort first then search:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

type ByID []User

func (u ByID) Len() int           { return len(u) }
func (u ByID) Less(i, j int) bool { return u[i].ID < u[j].ID }
func (u ByID) Swap(i, j int)      { u[i], u[j] = u[j], u[i] }

func main() {
	u := ByID{{5, "carol"}, {1, "alice"}, {3, "bob"}}
	sort.Sort(u) // must be sorted before searching

	i := sort.Search(len(u), func(k int) bool { return u[k].ID >= 3 })
	fmt.Println(u[i]) // {3 bob}
}
```

